// Plain words for this work, by passage key. See ./index.ts.

export const PLAIN_HALL: Record<string, string> = {
  "modern.hall.dedication": "Hall dedicates the book to the Rational Soul of the World.",
  "modern.hall.preface.p1": "Hall says that many books have been written about the secret philosophies of the ancient world, but they have usually been poorly presented; this book tries to give those sages a volume worthy of them, and the cost will be justified by its effect on the reader. He began writing on 1 January 1926 and worked for over two years, after collecting material from 1921 and planning the book from 1924. He dropped footnotes and worked quotations into the text, added a bibliography for further study and a detailed index. He claims neither to be infallible nor original: the ancient writings are fragmentary, and being dogmatic about them would be foolish. Though some of it may sound fantastic, he has tried not to speculate wildly and to give the spirit of the original authors, taking responsibility only for his own mistakes. Having no 'ism' of his own to push, he has not bent the sources to fit his ideas or forced different systems to agree.",
  "modern.hall.preface.p2": "Hall says the whole book runs against modern ways of thinking, since it deals with subjects that twentieth-century sceptics mock; its aim is to introduce the reader to a way of life beyond materialist religion, philosophy and science. The material cannot be perfectly organised, but related topics are grouped. English lacks good words for abstract philosophy, so the reader needs some intuition to grasp the ancient Mystery teachings. Most books in the bibliography are in his own library, but he thanks the libraries of San Francisco, Los Angeles, the Scottish Rite, the University of California, the Mechanics' Library and the Krotona Theosophical Library, and a list of people who helped. Translation was the biggest task: Alfred Beri did the German unpaid over nearly three years, Homer P. Earle the Latin, Italian, French and Spanish, and Rabbi Jacob M. Alkow edited the Hebrew.",
  "modern.hall.preface.p3": "Hall thanks those who made the book: his editor C. B. Rowlingson, the printer's staff at H. S. Crocker Company, M. M. Saxton who took down the whole manuscript by dictation and made the index, the illustrator J. Augustus Knapp for the colour plates, the printer Frederick E. Keast, the designer John Henry Nash, and C. E. Benson of the Los Angeles Engraving Company. He says the advance sale was unprecedented: the first edition of 550 copies sold out a year before printing, and the 'King Solomon' edition of 550 and the 'Theosophical' edition of 200 sold before the book arrived, thanks to Maud F. Galigher and friends from his lectures. He thanks the hundreds of subscribers whose advance payments, made on trust, paid for the book. He hopes readers will gain from it as he has, and ends with a few lines from John Bunyan. Signed Manly P. Hall, Los Angeles, 28 May 1928.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p1": "Hall says philosophy is the science of weighing values: it decides what is more important by asking what remains when everything secondary is taken away, and its task is to relate visible things to their invisible first cause. He quotes Sir William Hamilton's list of definitions of philosophy given by thinkers from Cicero and Hobbes to Kant, Fichte, Schelling and Hegel. He then lists the six usual branches of philosophy: metaphysics (being, the cosmos and God), logic (the rules of right thinking), ethics (morality and the good), psychology (the mind), epistemology (the nature of knowledge) and aesthetics (beauty).",
  "modern.hall.intro.p2": "Hall says Plato thought philosophy the greatest gift of the gods, but in the twentieth century it has become a heap of clashing opinions and keeps drifting toward materialism, as when Laplace told Napoleon he had 'no need' of God in his astronomy. He quotes Bacon, that a little philosophy leads to atheism and a deep one back to religion, and Aristotle, that all people naturally want to know. Humans have searched both outer space and their own inner selves for the link between the one and the all, cause and effect, mind and its source, illusion and reality. An ancient saying ranks people by what they know: those who know nothing are brutes, those who know human affairs are men, and those who know divine things are gods among men. So a person's rank depends on the quality of his thinking. He ends with Cicero's praise of philosophy as life's guide and the founder of cities.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p3": "Hall says that today 'philosophy' has split into rival 'isms' so busy disproving each other that the great questions of divine order and human destiny are neglected. Philosophy ought to steady human thought, but philosophers have often wandered further than the untrained minds they should lead. He will sketch only a few of the main schools of the last 2,600 years. Greek philosophy began with the Seven Sages listed by Diogenes Laertius. Thales held water to be the first principle, with the earth floating on it like a ship and earthquakes caused by its movements; his Ionic school ran through Anaximander, who made the boundless infinite the source of all things, Anaximenes, who made air the first element and said souls and even God were made of it, Anaxagoras and Archelaus.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p4": "Hall continues his survey. Anaxagoras held God to be an infinite, self-moving mind that set confused matter in order. Archelaus made mind and air the two principles and thought the stars were burning iron. Heraclitus taught constant change and made fire the first element into which the world will return. Pythagoras founded the Italic school, whose followers included Empedocles, Archytas, Philolaus and Eudoxus; he held mathematics the most sacred science, required arithmetic, music, astronomy and geometry of his pupils, founded a community of mutual help, taught self-examination and silence, and first taught the music of the spheres. Pythagoras and Empedocles both believed in transmigration; Empedocles said he had been a boy, a girl, a plant, a bird and a fish. Archytas is said to have invented the screw and the crane, and called pleasure a plague.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p5": "Hall describes the Eleatic school. Xenophanes attacked Homer's and Hesiod's stories of the gods and taught that God is one, bodiless, round, all sight and hearing, eternal and unchanging, and that the world has no beginning or end. Parmenides held that the senses are unreliable and only reason judges truth, and was the first to say the earth is round and divided into hot and cold zones. Melissus and Zeno of Elea denied that empty space exists; Zeno also rejected motion and held God to be one, eternal and spherical. Leucippus said the universe is part full and part empty, with tiny bodies falling into the void and forming spheres. Democritus developed this: all things are atoms and void, atoms differing endlessly in shape and size; the soul too is made of atoms and dies with the body. Aristotle hints that Democritus took his atoms from the Pythagorean idea of the Monad.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p6": "Hall says Socrates did not impose his views but made others state theirs by questioning them. He held that the soul existed before the body and knew everything, was dulled on entering it, and could be reawakened by discussion; the subject of his philosophy was man, and its aim was to contemplate God and to free the soul from the senses. He named three principles, God, matter and ideas, and said of God, 'What He is I know not; what He is not I know.' Among his followers were Xenophon, Aeschines, Crito and Cebes, though Zeller has called Xenophon's writings on him forgeries. When Aristophanes' comedy The Clouds mocked him, Socrates stood up in the theatre so the audience could compare his face with the actor's mask.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p7": "Hall describes three minor Socratic schools. The Elean school was founded by Phaedo, a young nobleman Socrates had freed from slavery; its later head Menedemus said he was 'free' of opinions, argued that 'to benefit is not the same as good, so good does not benefit', and often came home bruised from debates; under the name Eretrian the school accepted only simple affirmative statements. The Megarian school was founded by Euclid of Megara, who dressed as a woman to visit Socrates at night despite an Athenian death penalty, and sheltered Socrates' pupils after his death; it taught that virtue is wisdom, that the good is absolute unity, that change is an illusion and that evil does not exist. Its later members included Eubulides and Diodorus, who is said to have died of grief at failing to answer Stilpo, and who argued that nothing can move.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p8": "Hall describes the Cynics, founded by Antisthenes: extreme individualists who gave up all possessions and lived on the simplest food, reasoning that those with fewest needs are nearest the gods; Antisthenes said philosophy had taught him to talk with himself. Diogenes lived for years in a tub in Athens; when a youth bored holes in it, the city gave him a new one. He argued that all things belong to the wise, since all things belong to the gods, the gods are friends of the wise, and friends share everything. The Cyrenaics, founded by Aristippus, taught hedonism: Socrates failed to reform him, and he lived by his rule that pleasure is life's chief aim. They held that all we know of anything is the feeling it rouses in us, so the most pleasant feeling is the greatest good; feelings are pleasant, harsh or mean, ending in pleasure, grief or nothing.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p9": "Hall continues with the Cyrenaics: they held that physical pleasure is the true goal of life, that only the present moment counts, that grief is the worst disease, and that a philosopher lives free of envy, love and superstition in constant pleasure, and would live the same way if all laws were abolished. He then turns to Plato's Academy, which had an old, a middle and a new phase. Plato studied under Socrates, travelled widely, was initiated by the Egyptians into Hermetic philosophy, and drew much from the Pythagoreans. His philosophy covered ethics, physics and dialectic, and he saw the good as threefold: in the soul, in the body and in the outer world. Speusippus defined God as a being who lives immortally by himself alone; for Plato the One is the best name for the Absolute, since the whole comes before its parts and the One comes even before being.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p10": "Hall says Plato's philosophy rests on three orders of being: what moves others without being moved, what moves itself, and what is moved by another. Self-moving things cannot be dissolved, so they are immortal; things moved by another can be separated from their source and so are mortal; above both is the unmoved Mover, the divine permanence on which all depends. Plato taught that learning is remembering what the soul knew in an earlier existence, and over his school was written, 'Let none ignorant of geometry enter here.' After his death his followers split into the Academics and the Peripatetics, who followed Aristotle to the Lyceum. Plato called Aristotle 'the mind of the school'. Hall quotes Thomas Taylor's praise of Aristotle's range and accuracy, and Taylor's summary that Aristotle's ethics aims at perfection through virtue and his contemplative philosophy at union with the one principle of all things.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p11": "Hall says Aristotle divided philosophy into practical (ethics and politics) and theoretical (physics and logic). For him the soul is what makes a person live, feel and understand, with nourishing, sensing and thinking powers; wisdom is knowledge of first causes; and God is the First Mover, the best of beings, unmoving, bodiless and indivisible. Plato reasoned from principles, Aristotle from experience. Aristotle taught Alexander that a day without a good deed was a day he had not reigned. Hall then describes the Sceptics of Pyrrho and Timon: Sextus Empiricus says that those who think they have found truth are Dogmatists, those who think it cannot be found are Academics, and those still searching are Sceptics. Because every argument has an equal counter-argument, they refused to dogmatise, doubted all claims about God, and aimed at calm in matters of opinion, moderation in impulses and suspended judgement.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p12": "Hall describes the Stoics, founded by Zeno of Citium, a pupil of the Cynic Crates, and continued by Cleanthes, Chrysippus, Posidonius and, at Rome, Epictetus and Marcus Aurelius. They held that the world is God, its reason spread through it like seed; their philosophy was materialist and taught willing acceptance of natural law. Chrysippus said good and evil each need the other. The soul was a body spread through the physical body, made of eight parts, and not truly immortal. As an example of Stoic meekness, when a listener spat in Diogenes' face during a talk against anger, he said he was not angry, only unsure whether he ought to be. Epicurus founded a school like the Cyrenaic but with higher ethics: pleasure is a calm, dignified state reached by giving up the passions that cause pain, the mind's joys exceed the body's, and rest gives pleasure as well as action. He took his physics from Democritus' atoms and summed up his philosophy in four rules.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p13": "Hall gives Epicurus' four rules: the senses never deceive; opinion is added to sensation and can be true or false; an opinion confirmed or not contradicted by the senses is true; one contradicted or unconfirmed is false. He then criticises Eclecticism, the habit of picking doctrines from rival schools and combining them: since the schools reason from different premises, the mixture rests on contradictions, and it has been called the layman's cult. Most Roman thinkers, Cicero above all, were eclectics; the habit began when people doubted that ultimate truth could be found, and it produced a shallow broad-mindedness. The Neo-Pythagorean school of first-century Alexandria, with Apollonius of Tyana and Moderatus of Gades, linked the old philosophies to Neo-Platonism, stressed number and asceticism, and may have known more of Pythagoras' real teaching than survives. But Greek philosophy had passed its peak, and interest in physical life was growing toward what Hall calls modern materialism and commercialism.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p14": "Hall says Neo-Platonism really began with Plotinus, not Ammonius Saccas, and counts Porphyry, Iamblichus, Sallustius, the Emperor Julian, Plutarch and Proclus among its members. He calls it the last great effort of dying paganism to publish and preserve its secret, unwritten doctrine. It dealt almost entirely with higher metaphysics and recognised a hidden doctrine kept since the earliest civilisations in the rites, symbols and allegories of religions. To outsiders it may look like wild speculation, but that ignores the Mysteries, into which nearly all the early philosophers were initiated. The attempt to revive pagan thought by unveiling its mystical truths seemed to fail, yet many of its ideas passed into early Christian thought. In short, Neo-Platonism treats every outward body of doctrine as the shell of a spiritual truth that can be found by meditation and mystical exercises, and puts little weight on outward religion or material science.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p15": "Hall says 'Patristic' means the philosophy of the early Church Fathers, divided into the periods before and after the Council of Nicaea. Before it, Fathers such as Irenaeus, Clement of Alexandria and Justin Martyr attacked paganism, defended Christianity and put faith above reason, though some tried to reconcile pagan truths with revelation. After it, Athanasius, Gregory of Nyssa and Cyril of Alexandria developed Christian philosophy along Platonic lines in long, rambling, unsound writings. The Patristics placed man above all creation, a special divine work for whose benefit nature was made. Their thought culminated in Augustine's Christian Platonism, which opposed the Pelagian view that people save themselves and made the Church infallible until the Reformation. Hall also mentions Gnosticism, known mostly from its enemies such as Irenaeus, and Manichaeism, a Persian dualism of eternal war between Good and Evil, in which Christ the redeeming principle is set against Jesus the man.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p16": "Hall says the death of Boethius in the sixth century ended Greek philosophy, and Scholasticism arose in the ninth to reconcile philosophy with theology. He names John of Salisbury, Bernard of Clairvaux, Bonaventure, Abelard and Meister Eckhart, the Arab Aristotelians Avicenna and Averroes, and its high point in Albertus Magnus and Thomas Aquinas, whose Thomism was Aristotelian with faith as an extension of reason; Duns Scotus stressed the will instead. Scholasticism tried to force all European thought into Aristotle's mould and sank into word-chopping, which Francis Bacon mocked. Bacon's inductive method, gathering facts by observation and testing them by experiment, opened the way to modern science. His sometime secretary Thomas Hobbes held mathematics the only exact science, matter the only reality, and understanding the grasp of how words relate to things.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p17": "Hall lists the many schools of modern philosophy after the Reformation: Humanism (man the measure of all things), Rationalism, political philosophy, Empiricism, Moralism, Idealism, Realism and Phenomenalism, and the newest, Behaviourism and Neo-Realism, which he says means the end of idealism. Spinoza held God to be a self-existent substance known through its two attributes, extension and thought; the human mind and body are modes of these, and by reason a person can rise above the senses into union with God, though critics say Spinoza made God the same as the universe. Leibniz, the founder of German philosophy, found Descartes' idea of extension inadequate and held that matter dissolves into countless units of power he called monads; God is the greatest monad, the human spirit an awakened one, and lower beings have half-asleep monads.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p18": "Hall says Kant examined the powers and limits of understanding in his three critiques, and, as Will Durant put it, rescued mind from matter: the mind sorts sensations using categories such as time, space, quality and causation. Kant held that we cannot know things in themselves by reason, but morality proves free will, immortality and God. Hall blames German 'superintellectualism' on an overemphasis of Kant's view that mind rules over sensation. Fichte tried to unite Kant's practical and pure reason and held that nothing is real to us except the contents of our own consciousness. Schelling, his successor at Jena, made infinite Mind the cause of all, reachable by intellectual intuition, treated space and time as positive and negative, and described the Absolute developing in three movements: the Infinite entering the finite, the return from the finite, and reason, where the two meet.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p19": "Hall says Hegel rejected Schelling's intuition and built a philosophy on pure logic, starting, it is said, from nothing and showing how everything followed. For Hegel God is an endless process of unfolding that never finishes, all things exist through their opposites, and opposites are really the same; so only the relation between opposites exists, and creation is a ceaseless flow of divine consciousness. Hall says this undermines belief in a personal God and allows immortality only to the ever-flowing Deity. Herbart reacted against Fichte and Schelling with a realism based on the stream of appearances in the mind; since much of it is unreal, he broke appearances into elements to find reality, and wrestled with the contradictions in the ideas of thing, matter and mind, as when matter, reduced to its smallest units, turns out to occupy no space.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p20": "Hall says Schopenhauer's subject is the will and his aim is to raise the mind to control it. He pictures the will as a strong blind man carrying the intellect, a weak lame man who can see; every part of nature is a product of will, and the mind mostly builds arguments to justify what the will wants. Genius is the state in which intellect rules the will. He found Christianity's strength in its pessimism and conquest of the will, was close to Buddhism, saw Nirvana as the will subdued, called life a misfortune, and thought the wise would refuse to have children. Nietzsche, said to have announced that God died of pity, taught eternal recurrence and the will to power, and held that existence should produce the superman, bred apart from the mediocre mass, with love sacrificed to that end and an aristocracy ruling. Hall says that if power is understood as virtue, self-control and truth, Nietzsche's ideal makes sense; read superficially, it is a heartless creed of survival of the fittest.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p21": "Hall says there is no room for the other German schools, the newest being Freud's psychoanalysis and Einstein's relativity. Descartes heads French philosophy and shares with Bacon the founding of modern science: Bacon observed outer things, Descartes inner ones. Descartes first set everything aside and then restored only what existence requires, held that a clear and distinct idea must be true, and built his system from the simplest premises without relying on authority. Comte's Positivism teaches that the mind passes through three stages, theological, metaphysical and finally positive, in which it gives up the search for absolutes, origins and causes and studies only the laws of how things follow and resemble each other. Hall notes that this has been called a vast materialism, and that for Comte the heavens now declare the glory of Newton and Laplace rather than of God.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p22": "Hall lists more French schools: Traditionalism, the Sociological school, the Encyclopedists, Voltaire's scepticism toward Christianity, and Neo-Criticism. Bergson, whom he calls the greatest living French philosopher, offers a mystical theory of creative evolution, seeing God as life struggling against matter and even imagining life's victory over death; his popularity comes from people's revolt against hopeless materialism. Locke treated everything in the mind as a valid object of study, held the mind blank until experience writes on it, and thought God can only be inferred by reason; Hume was his most powerful follower. Bishop Berkeley turned Locke's premises into idealism: ideas are the real objects of knowledge, matter cannot be proved to exist, the universe is governed by mind, yet it would be madness to doubt our perceptions, since then we could know nothing.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p23": "Hall says Hartley and Hume made the association of ideas the key to psychology: a repeated sensation tends to recur when something associated with it appears. The Utilitarians, Bentham, Paley and the Mills, held the greatest good to be what is most useful to the most people; John Stuart Mill also thought intuition or reason could know the true substance of things. Darwinism is natural selection and physical evolution; Darwin, it is said, tried to banish spirit and make Mind the same as impersonal Nature. Agnosticism holds ultimates unknowable; Neo-Hegelianism revived Hegel in England and America. Herbert Spencer, famous for his First Principles, described evolution as growth from simplicity to complexity toward balance, followed by dissolution, which happens so that things can be rebuilt at a higher level. Hall quotes his definition of God as infinite intelligence, endlessly diversified through infinite time and space.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p24": "Hall says Giordano Bruno is the leading Italian philosopher: he accepted Copernicus, called the sun a star and every star a sun, and was put to death for teaching many universes in a cosmos too big for any one creed. Vico held that God governs the world through natural law, and that human law comes from a spiritual source in people that is in touch with divine law. Gioberti's Ontologism makes God the only true Being and the source of all knowledge. Benedetto Croce, the most important modern Italian thinker, is a Hegelian idealist who rejects theology and immortality and wants ethics and aesthetics to replace religion. Hall also names Sensism, Criticism and Neo-Scholasticism, a Church-backed revival of Thomism.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p25": "Hall says the two main American schools are Emerson's Transcendentalism, which puts the spiritual above the physical and shows Eastern influence, and Pragmatism, popularised by William James, which judges the meaning of things by their results; for James 'the true' is only what is useful in thinking. John Dewey applies an experimental attitude to all of life and sets no final goals. George Santayana, a Spaniard long resident in America, uses scepticism to lead people toward what he calls the life of reason. Hall lists the histories of philosophy he used for this survey. He then argues that philosophy did not begin with the Greeks: as Thomas Stanley admits, it came from the East, from the Hindu, Chaldean and Egyptian schools, and Thales, Pythagoras and Plato brought back eastern learning from their travels.",
  "modern.hall.intro.p26": "Hall argues that philosophy grew out of the religious Mysteries of the ancient world and only separated from religion after they decayed. The Mysteries claimed a knowledge so deep that it could be safely given only to selfless people, and the greatest ancient philosophers were initiated and vouched for it. Little is known of them because initiates were sworn to secrecy on pain of death, and over time the teachings became attached to the names of the philosophers who spread them. The Mysteries used symbols as their language, because a symbol can reveal to the wise and hide from the ignorant. Knowing that civilisations rise and fall, the sages hid their knowledge in mountains, giant statues, myths and temple architecture. Hall says the book is built on the idea that a secret doctrine about the inner mysteries of life lies hidden in ancient symbols, allegories and rituals, kept by a small group of initiates since the beginning of the world, and that whoever finds its lost keys can open a treasury of philosophical, scientific and religious truth.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig1": "The caption says the picture shows the atom as imagined by Dr Edwin D. Babbitt in his Principles of Light and Color. Hall says many people since Democritus, scientists included, have tried to picture the structure of atoms, and calls Babbitt's design the most remarkable of the last century. Babbitt also devised a way these atoms could fit together in order to form molecules. The reader is reminded that this apparently massive structure is actually too small to analyse.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig2": "The caption, under a portrait of Plato from a French book of ornamental figures, says Plato's real name was Aristocles. When he was brought to Socrates, Socrates said he had dreamed the night before of a white swan, a sign that this pupil would become one of the world's enlightened. There is also a tradition that Plato was sold as a slave by the king of Sicily.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig3": "The caption explains a diagram from Kircher's Ars Magna Sciendi, in which eighteen objects in two columns are used to count the ways things can be combined. Kircher calculated that fifty objects can be arranged in an enormous number of ways, which Hall gives in full. Hall draws the lesson that, since the parts of the universe can be related in countless ways, infinite variety and individuality must result, and life can never become monotonous.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig4": "The caption, for an illustration from Dryden's translation of Virgil's Aeneid, says that Virgil describes part of the ritual of a Greek Mystery, possibly the Eleusinian, in his account of Aeneas going down to the gate of hell with the Sibyl. Hall quotes the passage: an elm hung with empty dreams stands in the road, and monsters such as centaurs, the Hydra, Briareus, Gorgons, Geryon and the Chimaera crowd the door. Aeneas draws his sword in fear, but the Sibyl stops him and tells him they are only bodiless phantoms of empty air.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig5": "The caption, for an old print of Ptolemy's universe, says modern astronomers who mock Ptolemy's earth-centred system miss its philosophical meaning. Hall says the diagram is not astronomy in the modern sense but a map of how the divine and elemental parts of every creature relate. The three zodiac rings stand for the threefold spiritual nature of the universe, the planetary orbits for the Governors of the World, and the four elemental spheres in the middle for the physical make-up of both man and the universe. He calls it a cross-section of the universal aura, whose planets and elements are not those of modern astronomy.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig6": "The caption, for a tree diagram from Hort's The New Pantheon, says that to understand the deeper meaning of Greek myth one must first put the gods and lesser powers in order. Proclus, in his commentary on Plato's theology, gives the key to how the deities follow from the First Cause. Arranged this way, the divine ranks look like a great tree: its roots are in unknowable Being, its trunk and main branches are the higher gods, and its twigs and leaves are the countless beings that depend on the first, unchanging Power.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig7": "The caption, for a picture from Hone's Ancient Mysteries Described, says that Christians trying to picture the Trinity needed an image in which Father, Son and Holy Ghost were separate yet one, and so made figures with three faces on one head, which can be seen in various parts of Europe. Hall calls this a legitimate symbol with a sacred meaning, but adds that, given such Christian images, people of other faiths should not be called benighted for having a three-faced Brahma or a two-faced Janus.",
  "modern.hall.intro.fig8": "The caption, for a picture from Bryant's Analysis of Ancient Mythology, says the symbol of the Orphic Mysteries was an egg with a serpent coiled round it, meaning the cosmos surrounded by the fiery creative spirit. The egg also stands for the soul of the philosopher and the serpent for the Mysteries: at initiation the shell breaks and the person emerges from the embryo stage of physical life in which he grew toward philosophical rebirth.",
  "modern.hall.1.p1": "Hall says strong minds meet a problem calmly by gathering facts, while immature minds are overwhelmed and must be led like sheep and taught simply; as Paul said, the weak need milk and the strong meat. Since few minds are mature, the pagan religions were split into two levels: the inner, spiritual teaching for the few, and the literal, outer teaching for the many. To make nature's laws simple, its forces were turned into gods and goddesses; the crowd made offerings to Priapus and Pan, while the wise saw the statues as symbols of abstract truths. Every ancient city had public temples and also groups of philosophers and mystics, the most important of which were the Mysteries. Many great minds were initiated by strange, sometimes cruel rites. Hall quotes Alexander Wilder's definition of the Mysteries as sacred dramas held at set times, the best known being those of Isis, Sabazius, Cybele and Eleusis, and says Plato was criticised for revealing many of their secrets.",
  "modern.hall.1.p2": "Hall says every pagan nation had a public state religion and another open only to a philosophical elite. Some of these cults vanished with their secrets, but others survived, and much Masonic ritual is based on the ordeals that ancient candidates faced. He quotes the Masonic historian Robert Macoy, who credits the Mysteries with all ancient progress in civilisation, philosophy, science and art, and says their chief aim was to teach one God, the soul's resurrection to eternal life and the dignity of the human soul. When virtue declined, the Mysteries became corrupt: sorcery replaced divine magic and orgies such as the Bacchanalia crept in, and often only an empty shell remained. Quoting Thomas Taylor, that man is a religious animal, Hall says the pagan Mysteries attacked early Christianity for not requiring virtue for salvation, and introduces a quotation from Celsus.",
  "modern.hall.1.p3": "Hall quotes Celsus: the heralds of other mysteries invite only the pure in hand, wise in speech and free from wickedness, but the Christian mysteries invite sinners, fools and the miserable, so whom would a herald summon if he were gathering robbers? Hall says Celsus was attacking not the true faith of early Christian mystics but corruptions already creeping in, that early Christian ideals rested on the moral standards of the pagan Mysteries, and that the first Christians in Rome worshipped in underground temples of Mithras, from which the modern church borrowed much of its priestly ritual. The ancients held that no one can live wisely without knowing nature's laws. The Mysteries were more moral and philosophical than religious: they taught patience, courage and truthfulness, and that a worthy life is the best sacrifice to God and the body is His altar.",
  "modern.hall.1.p4": "Hall says sun worship was central to nearly all the early Mysteries, which he takes as a sign that they came from Atlantis, whose people worshipped the sun. The sun god was usually a beautiful golden-haired youth killed by evil men and brought back to life by rites of purification and rebirth, becoming the saviour of his people. His resurrection stood for the inner training by which a person masters his lower nature and awakens the spiritual powers sleeping in his soul (Hall points to Wagner's Siegfried). Ancient secret societies were mostly philosophical and religious, medieval ones religious and political, and modern Western ones mostly political or fraternal, though Masonry keeps the old principles. There were scores of such schools; some, like those of Pythagoras and the Hermetists, show Eastern influence, and the Rosicrucians said they learned from Arabian mystics. Even very primitive peoples have secret rites that Hall says have a Masonic flavour.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p1": "Hall quotes Meyrick and Smith on how the ancient Britons reorganised their priesthood: the instructor once called Gwydd was divided into the Druid, or superior teacher, and the Ovate, or lesser teacher, both called Bards, and the order grew into three classes, Druids, privileged Bards and Ovates. The origin of the word Druid is disputed: Max Müller links it, like the Irish drui, to oak trees and the Greek dryads; others call it Teutonic, Welsh or Gaelic (druidh, a wise man or sorcerer), and Sanskrit dru means timber. At the Roman conquest the Druids held great power in Britain and Gaul; armies are said to have sheathed their swords at their command, and nothing important was begun without them. They studied geography, natural science, theology and astrology, knew herbal medicine and used simple surgical tools. Hall then introduces a statement by Eliphas Levi.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p2": "Hall quotes Eliphas Levi: the Druids were priests and healers who cured by magnetism and charged amulets with their influence; their universal remedies were mistletoe and serpents' eggs, which attract the astral light, and a future medicine will understand such growths as mistletoe, mushrooms and truffles. Hall says mistletoe was sacred both as the universal remedy and because it grew on the oak, through which the Druids worshipped the Supreme God. At times set by the sun, moon and stars, the Arch-Druid climbed the oak and cut it with a golden sickle, catching it in white cloths so it would not touch the earth, and a white bull was often sacrificed. The Druids had a secret school like the Greek and Egyptian Mysteries, with unwritten teachings passed on orally. Robert Brown thinks they learned from Phoenician tin traders; Thomas Maurice writes of such tin voyages; others think the Druid Mysteries came from the East, possibly from Buddhism.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p3": "Hall suggests that Britain's nearness to the lost Atlantis may explain Druid sun worship. Artemidorus says Ceres and Persephone were worshipped on an island near Britain with rites like those of Samothrace, and Caesar was surprised that the Britons and Gauls worshipped gods he identified as Mercury, Apollo, Mars and Jupiter; Hall thinks the Druid Mysteries came from an older civilisation. The Druid school had three grades, whose teachings Hall likens to the three degrees of Blue Lodge Masonry. Ovates, an honorary grade, wore green, the colour of learning, and knew some medicine, astronomy, poetry and music. Bards wore sky-blue for harmony and truth, memorised much of the twenty thousand verses of sacred poetry, played a harp said to be strung with human hair, and often taught candidates, who wore striped robes of blue, green and white. Druids, who served the people's religious needs, wore white for purity and the sun, and had first to become privileged Bards.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p4": "Hall says that to become Arch-Druid a priest had to pass six degrees, marked by coloured sashes over white robes; the post was probably elective rather than hereditary, given for virtue and learning. James Gardner says Britain usually had two Arch-Druids, on Anglesey and the Isle of Man, carrying golden sceptres and crowned with oak leaves. Young Druids were clean-shaven and plainly dressed, older ones bearded and adorned with gold, and British Druid schools were so good that young Gauls were sent to them. Eliphas Levi says the Druids lived austerely, studied nature, kept strict secrecy and admitted members only after long probation; many lived in monastery-like groups or as hermits in caves and forest huts. Hall quotes James Freeman Clarke: the Druids believed in three worlds, a happier one above, a miserable one below and this one, where good and evil are balanced so that people can freely choose, and taught that souls pass between them to be punished, rewarded and purified. He adds sayings from the Welsh Triads, such as that darkness, falsehood and death keep shrinking while light, life and truth keep growing.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p5": "Hall says the Druids, like most Mystery schools, taught a simple moral code to everyone and a deeper doctrine only to initiated priests. Candidates had to be of good family and character and were tested and tempted before being trusted. The Druids taught the immortality of the soul and transmigration, apparently reincarnation; people would even borrow in one life promising to repay in the next. They believed in a purifying hell followed by union with the gods, and that all would be saved, though some must return many times to overcome their own evil. Sworn to secrecy, initiates were taught in forests and caves about creation, the gods, nature's laws, occult medicine, the heavens, and magic. Feast days fell on the new and full moon and the moon's sixth day, initiations probably at solstices and equinoxes, and the sun god's birth was celebrated at dawn on 25 December. Hall says their teachings are thought to show Pythagorean influence; they had a virgin mother with a child, and a sun god who rose again at the time of Easter. The cross, made by cutting an oak into a T, and the serpent were sacred; they worshipped the sun, moon and stars, honoured Mercury, perhaps in the form of a stone cube, and made offerings to nature spirits.",
  "modern.hall.1.s1.p6": "Hall quotes Charles Heckethorn: Druid temples, where the sacred fire was kept, stood on hills and in oak groves and took symbolic shapes, circular for the universe, oval for the world egg, serpent-shaped for Hu, cruciform for rebirth or winged for the Divine Spirit; the chief gods were a great father and mother, Hu and Ceridwen, like Osiris and Isis. Godfrey Higgins says Hu, the first settler of Britain, came from the Summer Country, which he places at Constantinople, and Albert Pike says Masonry's Lost Word is hidden in the name Hu. Hall says the Druid initiations resembled those of Greece and Egypt: Hu the sun god was killed and brought back to life. There were three degrees, and few passed them all. The candidate was buried in a coffin to represent the sun god's death, and the final test was being set adrift at sea in an open boat, which many did not survive; Taliesin describes it. Those who passed were said to be 'born again', learned the ancient hidden truths, and often became leaders in British religion and politics.",
  "modern.hall.1.s2.p1": "Hall says the Persian Mysteries of Mithras spread fast in southern Europe, especially among Roman soldiers, who carried them across the empire; at least one emperor was initiated, and the order met in caves under Rome. He quotes C. W. King that Mithraic carvings are common in the old western provinces, including Germany, France and Britain along Hadrian's Wall and at Bath. Alexander Wilder says Mithras is the Zend name for the sun and has a male and a female side: as Mithras he is the radiant lord of the sun, as Mithra the receptive, earthly nature that bears fruit only in sunlight. The cult simplifies the teachings of Zarathustra. The Persians held that two principles existed from eternity: Ahura-Mazda or Ormuzd, the Spirit of Good, from whom came ranks of good spirits, and Ahriman, also once a pure spirit, who after Ormuzd made light grew jealous, rebelled, became the Spirit of Evil, and produced destructive beings to harm Ormuzd's work.",
  "modern.hall.1.s2.p2": "Hall says that Ahriman entered the coarser parts of the earth Ormuzd made, put evil into each of his good works, and finally took up residence in man's lower nature, so that good and evil fight within every person. The Persians described four periods of 3,000 years: Ormuzd ruled the heavens in light, then ruled mankind in wisdom, then Ahriman's power began and the struggle for man's soul goes on, and in the last period Ahriman will be destroyed, good restored, death defeated, and the Spirit of Evil will bow before Ormuzd. Mithras, god of intelligence, mediates between them, and some compare him to the alchemists' mercury, which dissolves and reconciles opposites. Hall notes many likenesses between Mithraism and Christianity, partly because both spread in Italy in the first century, and quotes the Encyclopaedia Britannica's list, which includes shepherds bringing gifts, Sunday and 25 December, bell, candle, holy water and communion, a mediating Logos, an atoning sacrifice, the last judgement and the resurrection of the flesh; such resemblances, it says, helped Mithraism resist Christianity.",
  "modern.hall.1.s2.p3": "Hall says Mithraic rites were held in caves; Porphyry says Zoroaster first consecrated a cave to God because a cave stands for the dark lower world. He quotes John P. Lundy that the cave was marked with Cancer and Capricorn, the solstice gates through which souls come down into life and go up to the gods. Godfrey Higgins reports that the so-called chair of St Peter in Rome, perhaps once used in a pagan Mystery, was found in 1662 to bear the Twelve Labours of Hercules, and later the Muslim confession of faith in Arabic. Mithraic initiation seems to have had three degrees, prepared for by self-purification, mental training and control of the animal nature. In the first the candidate received a crown on a sword point, probably standing for his own spiritual nature; Hall interprets Mithras as the soul mediating between spirit (Ormuzd) and animal nature (Ahriman). In the second he was armed with intelligence and purity and sent into dark pits to fight the beasts of lust and passion; in the third he received a cape painted with the zodiac. He was then hailed as risen from the dead, called a Lion, and marked on the forehead with the Egyptian cross. The ritual told of the sun god's birth, sacrifice, death, resurrection and intercession for all humanity.",
  "modern.hall.1.s2.p4": "Hall says Mithraism never reached Zarathustra's philosophical heights but once spread over almost all Europe, and its influence lasted. He suggests the 'Lion' and the 'Lion's Paw' grip in the Master Mason's degree, and the Masonic ladder of seven rungs, may come from it; Faber thinks the Mithraic ladder was once a seven-step pyramid. Women were never admitted, though boys were, and Hall suggests Masonry's exclusion of women may rest on the Mithraic reason. Like other such cults, its legends picture the sun's journey: Mithras born from a stone is the sun rising from the horizon at the spring equinox. John O'Neill disagrees, arguing that Mithra was first a title of the supreme sky god, the all-seeing, sleepless one with ten thousand eyes, and that this explains the Masonic Eye. Hall warns not to confuse the Persian Mithra with the Vedic Mitra, and quotes Alexander Wilder that the Mithraic rites replaced those of Bacchus and became the foundation of the Gnostic system.",
  "modern.hall.1.fig1": "The caption, for an engraving from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says it shows Cybele, here called the Syrian Goddess, dressed as a hierophant, a priest of the Mysteries. Montfaucon describes her mitre adorned with towers and a crescent, a surplice and tunic like a bishop's, a cope bordered with the twelve signs of the zodiac, lions on either side, and many objects in her hands, including a tympanum, sistrum, distaff, caduceus and thunderbolt, with animals, fruit, a bow, a torch and a scythe. The statue is lost; Montfaucon's copy comes from drawings by Pirro Ligorio.",
  "modern.hall.1.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from Wellcome's Ancient Cymric Medicine, describes the Arch-Druid's regalia. His breastplate of judgement was said to strangle anyone who lied while wearing it, and Godfrey Higgins says it was put on witnesses. His tiara with points like sun rays made him a figure of the rising sun. A brooch on his belt held a white stone said to draw down the gods' fire, which Hall explains as a burning glass used to light altar fires. Other emblems were the golden sickle for the mistletoe and a crescent sceptre standing for the moon's sixth day and for Noah's ark. An early initiate said entry to the midnight rite was by a glass boat, a symbol of the moon carrying the seeds of life on the waters of eternity.",
  "modern.hall.1.fig3": "The caption, for a ground plan of Stonehenge from Maurice's Indian Antiquities, says Druid places of worship were unlike those of other nations: ceremonies were mostly at night, in oak groves or at open-air altars of huge uncut stones, moved by means still unexplained. The most famous is the stone ring of Stonehenge in south-western England, laid out on astronomical lines.",
  "modern.hall.1.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Lundy's Monumental Christianity, says Mithras is usually shown kneeling on a bull and driving a sword into its throat. Hall explains that the sword is the sun's rays, which at the spring equinox release the earth's life force, the bull's blood, to fertilise living things. Dogs were sacred to Mithras as symbols of loyalty; the serpent stood for Ahriman, to whom water rats were sacred. Esoterically the bull is the constellation Taurus and the serpent its zodiacal opposite, Scorpio.",
  "modern.hall.1.fig5": "The caption, for a picture from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says Mithras was born from a rock that split open in a dark underground chamber. Hall compares the Church of the Nativity at Bethlehem, which places Jesus' birth in a cave, and cites Dupuis's claim that Mithras was crucified and rose on the third day.",
  "modern.hall.2.p1": "Hall says the history of Gnosticism is obscure because little of its large literature survives: the Church, once in power, destroyed its records. The name comes from the Greek gnosis, knowledge; Gnostics claimed to know the secret doctrines of early Christianity, read them through pagan symbolism, and taught them only to a few initiates. Simon Magus is often called the founder, which would make Gnosticism one of the first branches of Christianity. Even his enemies admitted his supernatural powers but said they came from demons. The best-known legend tells of his contest with Peter in Rome: Simon was lifted into the air by invisible powers to prove his superiority, Peter ordered the demons to let go, and Simon fell to his death. Hall calls this story invented, since accounts of Simon's death disagree, and adds that growing evidence that Peter was never in Rome removes its last trace of authenticity.",
  "modern.hall.2.p2": "Hall says Simon Magus was clearly a philosopher, and quotes a passage attributed to him by Hippolytus: from one root, the invisible silence, spring two endless shoots, the male Great Power or Universal Mind above, and the female Great Thought below; their union produces the 'Middle Distance', the boundless Air, in which the Father sustains all things. Hall explains this as creation arising from a positive and negative principle acting on each other in a middle plane of balance, the pleroma, a substance blending spiritual and material aeons. From the pleroma came the Demiurge, the 'immortal mortal' responsible for our bodily existence and its suffering. In the system three pairs of opposites, or syzygies, came from the Eternal One, making seven with Him: Mind and Thought, Voice and Name, Reason and Reflection; from these came the aeons or angels who formed the lower worlds under the Demiurge. Hall then turns to how Simon's and his disciple Menander's teaching was expanded and often distorted by later followers.",
  "modern.hall.2.p3": "Hall says Gnosticism had two main branches, the dualistic Syrian school, largely following Simon, and the more pantheistic Alexandrian school, founded on the ideas of Basilides, an Egyptian Christian who claimed to have been taught by the apostle Matthew. Gnosticism as a whole rests on the idea of emanations linking Absolute Spirit and Absolute Substance, which it held to have existed together from eternity. Basilides mixed Hermetic, Eastern, Chaldean and Persian teachings and tried to unite Christianity with the pagan Mysteries. He is credited with the god Abraxas: Godfrey Higgins shows that the letters of Abraxas, like those of Mithras, add up to 365, and Basilides taught that the universe has 365 aeons, whose sum is the Supreme Father. Abraxas is usually shown with a human body, a cockerel's head and serpents for legs. Hall quotes C. W. King, citing Irenaeus, that for Basilides the eternal Father first brought forth Mind, then Word, Intelligence, Wisdom and Strength.",
  "modern.hall.2.p4": "Hall quotes C. W. King citing Bellermann: the Abraxas figure is a Gnostic image of the Supreme Being, with the five emanations shown by symbols, the serpent legs for Mind and Word, the cockerel's head for Intelligence or foresight, and the shield and whip for Wisdom and Strength. The Gnostics disagreed about the Demiurge, who made the lower world with six sons, perhaps the planetary angels: some thought him evil for imprisoning souls in bodies, others a servant of the hidden God. Some identified him with the Jewish God Jehovah, an idea Hall says influenced medieval Rosicrucians, who saw Jehovah as lord of the material world rather than the Supreme God; he compares Odin, a god both supreme and mortal. The Gnostics claimed to have real pictures of Christ, which Hall thinks were idealised from images of pagan sun gods. For them Christ was the divine Mind, who entered Jesus at his baptism and left before the crucifixion; Christ could not die, and Simon of Cyrene was crucified in his place, made to look like Jesus. Hall introduces a quotation from Irenaeus.",
  "modern.hall.2.p5": "Hall quotes Irenaeus' report of Gnostic belief: seeing mankind's corruption, the unnamed Father sent his firstborn, Mind, as Christ to free believers from the makers of the world, and he appeared as the man Jesus and worked miracles. The Gnostics divided people into three kinds: those who worship visible nature, those who, like the Jews, worship the Demiurge, and those, like themselves, who worship Mind and the true spiritual light. After Basilides, Valentinus led the movement and multiplied its details, raising the emanations to fifteen pairs and stressing the Virgin Sophia, or Wisdom; the Pistis Sophia contains much of this doctrine. Hall quotes James Freeman Clarke on their wide influence in the Church and claims some of their scientific ideas have been confirmed. Sects such as the Valentinians, Ophites and Adamites branched off, but Gnosticism faded after the third century, and medieval revivals failed for lack of records. Hall says Gnostic ideas survive under other names and have entered Church doctrine.",
  "modern.hall.2.s1.p1": "Hall says the true nature of Serapis, the Greek name for the Egyptian Asar-Hapi, was revealed only to his initiates, and no authentic account of his rites survives. In an oracle to the king of Cyprus, Serapis described himself as a god whose head is the starry sky, whose body is the sea, whose feet are the earth, whose ears are the air and whose eyes are the sun's rays. Godfrey Higgins derives the name from Soros, stone coffin, and Apis, the sacred bull, 'tomb of the bull', which Hall finds unlikely. Macrobius and others called Serapis the sun because of the halo round his head, and Julian wrote 'One Jove, one Pluto, one Sun is Serapis'; Hall links the name to Hebrew saraph, to blaze, and the Seraphim. The most common view derives it from Osiris-Apis. Hall rejects the Egyptologists' idea that Serapis was simply the dead Apis bull, arguing that the wise priests used him for the world soul: Apis was nature's material body, and Serapis the soul bound to it in life and released at death.",
  "modern.hall.2.s1.p2": "Hall says C. W. King thinks Serapis came from India, his name a Greek form of titles of Yama, the Hindu god of death, which fits a legend that Bacchus drove Serapis, as a bull, from India to Egypt. Other suggested meanings are 'the sacred bull', 'the sun in Taurus', 'the soul of Osiris', 'the sacred serpent' and 'the retiring of the bull', the last referring to the drowning of the Apis bull in the Nile every twenty-five years. The great statue in the Alexandrian Serapeum seems to have been brought from Sinope, and another legend makes Serapis an early king who founded Egyptian learning and was deified. Hall retells Plutarch's story: Ptolemy Soter dreamed of a huge statue that told him to bring it to Alexandria; the traveller Sosibius said he had seen it at Sinope; after three years the king's envoys stole it, claiming it had walked to the ship by itself. In Egypt the initiates Timotheus and Manetho declared it Serapis, equal to Pluto, which gave Greeks and Egyptians a god in common and united their religions.",
  "modern.hall.2.s1.p3": "Hall says ancient writers describe statues of Serapis that look more Greek than Egyptian, some wrapped in a serpent, others combining Osiris and Apis. The likeliest description is of a tall, strong figure with both manly strength and womanly grace, a pensive, rather sad face, long curls on the chest and shoulders, a heavy beard, and heavy robes that initiates believed hid an androgynous body. The statues were made of stone, metal, or plates of different metals; one thirteen-foot statue was said to be a single emerald, which modern writers think was green glass. Clement of Alexandria describes one made of ground metals and precious stones mixed with dye left from the funerals of Osiris and Apis, coloured indigo. When a Christian soldier struck the Alexandrian Serapis under Theodosius' edict, his axe shattered and sparks flew. Serapis was called the god of the seven-letter name, his priests sang the seven vowels, and he sometimes wore seven rays, which Hall reads as the seven divine intelligences in sunlight. He was the only god consulted for the dying Alexander.",
  "modern.hall.2.s1.p4": "Hall says the Egyptian Mysteries were divided into the Lesser, sacred to Isis, and the Greater, sacred to Serapis and Osiris; Wilkinson thinks only priests entered the Greater, and even the heir to the throne only once crowned. Much of what is known of the higher rites comes from the passages where they were held: under the temple of Serapis destroyed by Theodosius were found machines built for nightly initiations, showing hard tests of courage, after which survivors were brought before a statue of Serapis lit by hidden lights. Labyrinths were part of his rites, and Budge shows a bull-headed, Minotaur-like Serapis. Hall reads the labyrinth as the illusions of the lower world, through which the soul searches for truth while the bull-headed animal man tries to destroy it; Serapis is here the tester of souls. The maze may also stand for the solar system with the sun at its centre. Through Gnosticism Serapis became linked with early Christianity: Hall cites a letter of Hadrian (dated 'A.D. 24' in the text) saying Serapis worshippers in Egypt were Christians and that bishops and even the Patriarch worshipped him.",
  "modern.hall.2.s1.p5": "Hall quotes C. W. King, who thinks the grave, pensive head of Serapis gave the model for the traditional face of Christ, since Jewish-born Christians would not have portrayed Jesus until long after his lifetime. Serapis gradually displaced the other Egyptian and Greek gods and stayed supreme until the fourth century. In 385 Theodosius ordered his image destroyed; the Christian soldiers at first feared to touch it lest the earth swallow them, then smashed it, sacked the Serapeum and burned its library. Hall notes the church historian Socrates' report that Christ's monogram was found under the temple's foundations. He ends with two quotations linking Serapis to other Mysteries: Payne Knight cites Varro that the great gods of Samothrace were the same as the later Egyptians' Serapis and Isis, and Pike quotes Martianus Capella's hymn to the sun, worshipped as Serapis on the Nile, Osiris at Memphis, Mithras in Persia, Atys in Phrygia, Ammon in Libya and Adonis at Byblos.",
  "modern.hall.2.s2.p1": "Hall says the Odinic Mysteries were founded around the first century BC or AD. He quotes Robert Macoy: Sigge, chief of an Asian tribe, migrated from the Caspian and Caucasus into northern Europe, setting his sons over Russia, the Saxons, the Franks and Denmark, then went to Sweden, built Sigtuna, gave new laws, took the name Odin, and founded twelve priests called Drottars to conduct the secret worship, judge and prophesy; their rites celebrated the death of the beautiful Balder, the grief of gods and men, and his return to life. After his death the historical Odin merged with the god of wisdom, whose cult replaced that of Thor; his burial mound is shown near his temple at Uppsala. Hall says the twelve Drottars stood for Odin's twelve holy names and the signs of the zodiac, kept the arts and sciences, and taught them to those who passed the initiations. The Mysteries, modelled on Greek, Persian and Brahmin rites, were destroyed by Christianity, but their real downfall was the corruption of their priests.",
  "modern.hall.2.s2.p2": "Hall says myth is nearly always the ritual and symbolism of a Mystery school, and summarises the Odinic drama. The invisible creator was All-Father, and Odin, the one-eyed god, was his regent in nature. Ymir, the frost giant, formed from mists rising from Ginnungagap, the chasm into which frost and fire giants had hurled snow and fire, was killed by Odin, Vili and Ve, who made the world from his body. Odin built Asgard on a mountain for the twelve gods, and Valhalla, where heroes who died fighting fought and feasted every day, their wounds healed nightly and their boar renewed as fast as it was eaten. Balder the Beautiful, Odin's beloved son and, Hall says, the Scandinavian Christ, brought peace and joy to the gods; but as Jesus had Judas, one of the twelve, Loki, was false and made the blind god Hothr shoot Balder with a mistletoe arrow. Grief-stricken, the gods sought a way to bring him back, and so the Mysteries were founded.",
  "modern.hall.2.s2.p3": "Hall says the Odinic Mysteries were held in nine underground chambers representing the Nine Worlds. The candidate, who called himself a wanderer, was set the task of raising Balder from the dead, without realising he himself played Balder; the priests stood for the sun, moon and stars, and the three chief initiators, the Sublime, the Equal to the Sublime and the Highest, Hall likens to the Master and Wardens of a Masonic lodge. After hours in the passages he was brought before a statue of Balder in a hall roofed with shields, with a seven-flowered plant for the planets; there he swore secrecy on a naked sword, drank mead from a skull, and after many trials was allowed to unveil the mystery of Odin, or wisdom. He received the order's ring, was hailed as reborn, and was said to have died and risen without passing through death. Hall says Wagner's Ring cycle, from Das Rheingold to Gotterdammerung, though taking liberties, is based on these Mystery rituals and keeps the majesty of the original sagas.",
  "modern.hall.2.fig1": "The caption, for a woodcut from the Nuremberg Chronicle, says it shows Simon Magus being carried up by demons after calling on the spirits of the air, while St Peter orders them to let him go; they obey, and Simon is killed by the fall.",
  "modern.hall.2.fig2": "The caption, for a Gnostic gem from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says its serpent body stands for the sun's path and its lion head for the sun's power at its height in the constellation Leo.",
  "modern.hall.2.fig3": "The caption, for a labyrinth from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says many ancient cults used labyrinths and mazes for initiation. Remains have been found among Native Americans, Hindus, Persians, Egyptians and Greeks; some are paths lined with stones, others miles of dark tunnels under temples or in mountains. Hall says the labyrinth of Crete, home of the bull-headed Minotaur, was certainly a place of initiation into the Cretan Mysteries.",
  "modern.hall.2.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from a Dutch history of the Hebrew church, says Serapis is often shown standing on a sacred crocodile, holding a rule to measure the Nile flood, and balancing a three-headed animal wrapped in a serpent: a lion's head for the present, a wolf's for the past and a dog's for the future. He is sometimes accompanied by Cerberus, Pluto's three-headed dog, and, like Jupiter, wears a basket of grain on his head.",
  "modern.hall.2.fig5": "The caption says the Norse Mysteries were given in nine chambers that the candidate passed through in order, standing for the nine worlds into which the Drottars divided the universe: Asgard, home of the gods; Alfheim, of the bright elves; Niflheim, cold and dark, in the north; Jotunheim, of the giants, in the east; Midgard, the human world in the middle; Vanaheim, in the west; Muspellsheim, of fire, in the south; Svartalfheim, of the dark elves, underground; and Helheim, cold home of the dead, at the very bottom. All but Midgard are invisible, but during initiation the candidate's soul, freed by the priests' secret power, was believed to wander among them. Hall links them to the nine spheres the Eleusinian initiates passed through.",
  "modern.hall.3.p1": "Hall says the most famous ancient Mysteries were the Eleusinian, held at Eleusis in honour of Ceres (Demeter) and her daughter Persephone, and their initiates were known for fine philosophy and high morals; the rites later spread to Rome and Britain. They are usually said to have been founded by Eumolpos about 1400 BC, and their principles survive through Plato's philosophy. They absorbed many smaller schools; Heckethorn sees in them a form of the rites of Isis and Osiris, and Hall believes all ancient secret schools were branches of one philosophical tree, rooted in heaven, through which one light was spread. The rites had Lesser Mysteries in spring at Agrae, probably yearly, and Greater Mysteries in autumn at Eleusis or Athens, probably every five years, and understanding them needed a deep, esoteric reading of Greek myth. The Lesser Mysteries were dedicated to Persephone; Hall quotes Thomas Taylor that they signified the state of the impure soul wrapped in an earthly body and a material nature.",
  "modern.hall.3.p2": "Hall says the Lesser rites used the story of Pluto, lord of the underworld, seizing Persephone as she picked flowers and carrying her off to be his queen. Many initiates probably thought this was only about the seasons. Candidates swore on the skins of sacrificed animals to die rather than reveal the secrets, but some teachings survive indirectly. The soul, symbolised by Persephone, is spiritual and truly alive only in the higher worlds; the physical, human nature is a tomb, a swamp and the source of all sorrow, and Plato calls the body the soul's grave. The gloom of the Lesser Mysteries showed the soul's agony at being trapped in human limits and illusions. The key teaching was that a person is no wiser after death than before: whoever stays ignorant here wanders for ever repeating his mistakes, and whoever keeps material desires carries them into the next world where they can never be satisfied. Hall compares Dante's Inferno, and says those whose souls slept through life lay in rows in Hades sleeping for ever.",
  "modern.hall.3.p3": "Hall says that for the Eleusinian philosophers birth into the body was a true death, and the only real birth was the spiritual soul rising out of the flesh; he cites Longfellow's 'the soul is dead that slumbers'. Like Narcissus, who died trying to embrace his reflection in the water, a person who takes the body seen in nature's mirror for his real self loses the chance to unfold his immortal self. The saying 'the living are ruled by the dead' means most people are ruled by their senseless animal natures, not their living spirits. The Mysteries taught transmigration and reincarnation in an unusual way: at midnight the invisible worlds come nearest, souls enter material existence then, and some sleeping spirits slip through and take the forms of creatures, so many rites were held at midnight. They warned that a great sorrow comes to those who take their own lives. The Lesser Mysteries, held underground with a dramatic portrayal of the horrors of Hades, ended with the title Mystes, one who sees through a veil, which would be torn away in the higher degree; Hall thinks the word mystic comes from it.",
  "modern.hall.3.p4": "Hall says the Greater Mysteries, open only to some who had passed the Lesser, were sacred to Ceres and showed her wandering the world with two torches, intuition and reason, in search of her stolen daughter, the soul. She found Persephone near Eleusis, taught the people there to grow corn, and founded the Mysteries. Pluto at first refused to release Persephone because she had eaten the pomegranate, the fruit of mortality, but agreed that she could spend half the year above and half with him. The Greeks took Persephone as the sun's energy, under the earth in winter and back in summer, and the flowers were said to die of grief when she left; only the priests understood the deeper meaning. Hall quotes Thomas Taylor that the Greater Mysteries showed in splendid visions the happiness of the purified soul. The Lesser Mysteries dealt with the soul's descent into the body over nine months; the Greater taught how to free the higher nature, chained like Prometheus to the personality. The nine days of initiation stood for the nine spheres of descent, and since the rites are said to have closed with the words 'Konx Om Pax', Hall thinks they resembled Brahmin teachings.",
  "modern.hall.3.p5": "Hall suggests that Persephone's half-year with Pluto may mean that the Eleusinians knew the soul leaves the body in sleep, or could train it to: by day the soul is queen of Pluto's realm, the body, and in sleep it rises to the spiritual worlds. The initiate learned to free his soul from his material nature, and once freed it kept for ever the qualities that would be its vehicle in the heaven world. Since the gods were said to live on mountain tops such as Olympus, the candidate passed through ever brighter rooms and finally into a vaulted hall with a brightly lit statue of Ceres, where the hierophant and robed priests taught him the highest secrets. He was then called an Epoptes, 'one who has seen', so initiation was called autopsy, and he received sacred books, probably in cipher, and inscribed stone tablets. Hall cites John A. Weisse for the officials: male and female hierophants, torchbearers, altar attendants and a herald, who according to Porphyry stood for the Creator, the sun, the moon and Hermes.",
  "modern.hall.3.p6": "Hall says the rites were reportedly accompanied by strange, seemingly supernatural events, and many initiates claimed to have seen the gods; whether from ecstasy or real unseen powers is unknown. He quotes Apuleius' Golden Ass, probably describing his own initiation: he came to the edge of death, passed through all the elements, saw the sun shining at midnight, and came near to and worshipped the gods below and above. Women and children were admitted and initiates numbered thousands, so the highest teachings went only to a few able minds. Socrates refused initiation because, knowing its principles already, membership would have silenced him. Hall says the praise of Pindar, Plato, Cicero and Epictetus shows the Mysteries rested on great truths. Initiation garments were kept for years as almost sacred. Candidates came unclothed, as souls without wisdom, and received first an animal skin and later a consecrated robe. They passed through two gates, one down into the lower world of ignorance and one up into a room lit by hidden lamps with the statue of Ceres. Strabo says the temple held twenty to thirty thousand people. Zoroaster's caves also had two doors, for birth and death.",
  "modern.hall.3.p7": "Hall quotes Porphyry, via Ouvaroff's essay on Eleusis, on the meaning of sacred symbols: God is a light hidden in the finest fire, invisible to those who do not rise above material life, so transparent things like crystal, marble and ivory recall divine light and gold recalls its purity; a black stone may mean God's invisibility; the gods are shown in beautiful human form, of every age and both sexes; spheres stand for the universe, sun and moon, circles for eternity, crescents for the moon's phases, pyramids and obelisks for fire and the heavenly gods, a cone for the sun, a cylinder for the earth, and the phallus and triangle for generation. Heckethorn says the Eleusinian Mysteries outlasted all others until Theodosius suppressed them almost four hundred years after Christ, killing those who would not accept Christianity. Cicero said they taught people not only how to live but also how to die.",
  "modern.hall.3.s1.p1": "Hall says Orpheus, the Thracian bard and great initiator of the Greeks, came to be honoured as a god centuries before Christ. He quotes Thomas Taylor: almost nothing certain is known of his life, except that he founded Greek theology and morals, was the first prophet and prince of poets, son of a Muse, taught the Greeks their rites and Mysteries, and was the source of Homer's poetry and of the theology of Pythagoras and Plato. Orpheus founded the Greek myth system as a vehicle for his philosophy, which may have come from the Brahmins; some legends make him a Hindu. He was initiated in Egypt, learning magic, astrology and medicine, and into the Cabiric Mysteries of Samothrace. Hall retells the story of Eurydice, killed by a snake while fleeing a seducer: Orpheus charmed Pluto and Persephone with his music and was allowed to lead her back if he did not look round, but he did, and she was lost. Accounts of his death differ, but most say Ciconian women whose advances he spurned tore him apart; Plato's Republic says his soul later chose to be a swan rather than be born of woman. His head floated with his lyre down the Hebrus to the sea and gave oracles from a rock, and the gods made the lyre a constellation.",
  "modern.hall.3.s1.p2": "Hall says Orpheus' seven-stringed lyre moved the gods, gathered birds and beasts, and made trees pull up their roots to follow him; he is one of the Immortals who gave themselves so that mankind could have divine wisdom, and some say the gods, fearing he would overthrow them, destroyed him. In time the man merged with his doctrine. Hall interprets the myth: Orpheus, son of Apollo (divine truth) and Calliope (harmony), is the secret doctrine revealed through music. Eurydice is humanity, killed by the serpent of false knowledge and held in the underworld of ignorance; Orpheus as theology wins her back but fails because it distrusts the understanding within the soul. The women who tear him apart are quarrelling theological factions that destroy the body of truth once their noise drowns his harmony. His living head is the esoteric doctrine, which goes on speaking after the cult is destroyed; the lyre is his secret teaching and its seven strings the seven keys of universal knowledge. The differing deaths are the many ways wisdom is killed, and his rebirth as a swan, the emblem of initiates, means his truths will be taught by initiates in all ages.",
  "modern.hall.3.s2.p1": "Hall says the Bacchic rite centres on the young Bacchus (Dionysus or Zagreus) being torn apart by the Titans, who distracted him with his reflection in a mirror, then boiled and roasted the pieces. Pallas saved his heart, so he could rise again. Jupiter struck the Titans with thunderbolts and burned them to ashes, and from these ashes, which held some of Bacchus' flesh, mankind was made, so every person carries part of the Bacchic life. For this reason the Mysteries forbade suicide, since the body is indirectly Bacchus' tomb. Hall interprets Bacchus as the rational soul of the lower world and chief of the Titans who build the world's spheres. The Bacchic state is the rational soul unified and self-aware; the Titanic state is that soul scattered through creation, unaware of its oneness. The mirror is the sea of illusion: Bacchus mistakes his reflection for himself and ensouls it, and so plants in the irrational universe the urge to become like its rational source. Hence the ancients said the gods are known not by logic but by realising their presence within.",
  "modern.hall.3.s2.p2": "Hall continues: when Bacchus followed his reflection into matter, the world soul was broken up and spread through the world by the Titans, though they could not scatter its heart. Boiling the pieces means immersion in the material world, and roasting them the later rise of the spirit out of form. Jupiter, Bacchus' father and the world's maker, killed the Titans so the divine idea would not be wholly lost, and made from their ashes mankind, whose purpose is to preserve and finally free the rational soul. As the third person of the creative triad he is lord of death, which exists only in the lower world; his thunderbolts show that death serves to rescue the rational soul from the irrational nature, so that things break down in order to be rebuilt at a higher level. Man is therefore double, his lower nature made of the Titans and his higher of Bacchus, and can live either way. Hesiod's twelve Titans correspond to the zodiac, and the murdering Titans to the zodiacal powers distorted by matter; Bacchus is the sun dismembered by the signs, from whose body the world is made, losing its sense of wholeness. His heart, saved by Pallas and lifted out of the four elements into the ether, is the immortal centre of the rational soul.",
  "modern.hall.3.s2.p3": "Hall says the Bacchic Mysteries were founded to free the rational soul from the irrational Titanic nature and to raise it from separateness to unity: when all the scattered rational parts of Bacchus are gathered, he rises again. The rites of Dionysus were much like those of Bacchus, and many treat the two as one god; statues of Dionysus were carried in the Eleusinian Mysteries, and Dionysus seems to be Bacchus' solar aspect. Hall then describes the Dionysiac Architects, an ancient secret society of builders much like modern Freemasonry, bound by secret knowledge of how earthly and divine architecture correspond. They are said to have been employed by Solomon to build the Temple although they were followers of Bacchus and Dionysus, not Jews. They raised many great ancient monuments, had a secret language, a system of marking stones, yearly gatherings and sacred feasts. Their doctrines are unknown, and Hiram Abiff is believed to have been one of them.",
  "modern.hall.3.fig1": "The caption, for a picture of Pluto seizing Persephone, says Pluto stands for the body's intelligence, and the rape of Persephone for the divine nature attacked by the animal soul and dragged down into Hades, meaning the material world. Hall then gives, from James Christie, Meursius' account of the nine days of the Greater Eleusinian rites: a general meeting to question candidates; a procession to the sea; the sacrifice of a mullet; the bringing of the sacred basket to Eleusis; a torch race; a procession with a statue of Iacchus; athletic contests; a repeat of the rites for latecomers; and on the ninth day the deepest teachings, when an urn, the symbol of Bacchus, was shown as an emblem of supreme importance.",
  "modern.hall.3.fig2": "The caption, for a Pompeian wall painting, says Ceres or Demeter was the daughter of Kronos and Rhea and mother of Persephone by Zeus. She is better understood as the protector of farming, especially corn, than as the earth goddess. The poppy was sacred to her and she is often shown with a garland of poppies; in the Mysteries she was pictured riding in a chariot drawn by winged serpents.",
  "modern.hall.3.fig3": "The caption, for an illustration from Ovid's Metamorphoses, says that in the Bacchic Mysteries the candidate played Bacchus, was set upon and killed by priests dressed as Titans, and was restored to life amid rejoicing; the rites were held every three years in two degrees, and initiates wore crowns of myrtle and ivy. Hall quotes Godfrey Higgins, who presents Bacchus as an early pagan form of the Christ story: born of a virgin on 25 December, he worked miracles, including turning water into wine, rode in triumph on an ass, was killed by the Titans, rose on 25 March, was called the Saviour, and was shown to the people as an infant, as Christ is at Rome on Christmas morning. Hall adds that Bacchus is also a solar power, raised with Apollo's help, and that his resurrection means freeing the soul from the world's Titanic make-up, shown by smoke rising from the burned Titans. The rites were once lofty but later became degraded, as the famous Bacchanalia show.",
  "modern.hall.4.p1": "Hall says the Smithsonian Institution's annual report for 1915 printed notes of a 1912 lecture on Atlantis given at the Oceanographic Institute by Pierre Termier, a member of the French Academy of Sciences and director of the Geological Map of France. He quotes Termier: after long scorn, scientists are again asking whether Plato preserved a page of real history; nothing can yet be affirmed, but it seems more and more likely that a large land, a continent or great islands, collapsed west of Gibraltar in the not very distant past, and oceanography is needed to settle the question. Hall summarises the evidence Termier gives: the uneven floor of the Atlantic, lava dredged from 3,000 metres along a line from the Azores to Iceland, and the volcanic nature of the Atlantic islands, which he takes as supporting Plato's account of volcanic destruction; also the zoologist Louis Germain's view that an Atlantic continent joined Iberia and North Africa. Termier ends with a vivid picture of the continent sinking.",
  "modern.hall.4.p2": "Hall summarises Plato's Critias. In the first ages the gods divided the earth, and Poseidon received the sea and the island continent of Atlantis. On a mountain there lived Evenor, his wife Leucippe and their daughter Cleito; after her parents died Poseidon courted her and fathered five pairs of twin sons. He divided the land among the ten, made the eldest, Atlas, overlord, and named the country and the ocean after him. He had already made rings of land and sea round the central island, two of land and three of water, as perfect as if turned on a lathe, and gave the island a warm spring and a cold one. Atlas' descendants ruled wisely and the land grew rich, with metals, tamed animals and perfumes from its flowers. The Atlanteans built palaces, temples and docks, bridged the rings of sea, and dug a deep canal from the ocean to the central island, where Poseidon's palaces and temple outshone everything, with a network of bridges and canals joining the kingdom.",
  "modern.hall.4.p3": "Hall continues Plato's account. The Atlanteans built with white, black and red stone from beneath the island and walled each ring of land, the outer wall covered with brass, the middle with tin and the inner, round the citadel, with orichalch. In the citadel stood a shrine to Cleito and Poseidon inside a golden wall, where the first ten princes were born, and Poseidon's temple, silver outside with golden pinnacles, ivory, gold, silver and orichalch within, holding a huge statue of the god in a chariot of six winged horses surrounded by a hundred Nereids on dolphins, with golden statues of the first kings and queens outside. There were hot and cold springs, temples, gymnasia, baths, a racecourse, forts and a great harbour full of foreign ships. The coast was high and steep, but round the city lay a plain sheltered by mountains, giving two harvests a year and divided into districts that supplied soldiers and chariots. The ten kings each ruled their own realm but were bound by laws engraved on an orichalch pillar in Poseidon's temple. Every five and six years in turn they met there, sacrificed, renewed their oath, judged in blue robes, and wrote their verdicts on a golden tablet. They were not to fight each other, were to help one another, and could not put a kinsman to death without a majority.",
  "modern.hall.4.p4": "Hall says Plato ends by telling how this empire, its kings corrupted by ambition, attacked the Greek states; Zeus called the gods together to judge them, and there the unfinished Critias breaks off. He quotes the Timaeus, where an Egyptian priest tells Solon that earthquakes and floods came, the Athenian army sank into the earth in a single day and night, Atlantis vanished under the sea, and the sea there is still blocked by mud. Thomas Taylor quotes Marcellus' History of Ethiopia that there were seven islands in the Atlantic sacred to Proserpine and three huge ones sacred to Pluto, Ammon and Neptune. Crantor said the Egyptian priests claimed the story was written on pillars still standing around 300 BC, and Ignatius Donnelly thought the Atlanteans first tamed horses, which is why horses were sacred to Poseidon. Hall argues the story is both history and allegory: Origen, Porphyry, Proclus, Iamblichus and Syrianus saw a philosophical mystery in it, though they disagreed on its meaning. For Hall, Atlantis stands for the threefold nature of the universe and the body; the ten kings are the numbers one to ten, born as five pairs of opposites and ruled by the Monad, the One.",
  "modern.hall.4.p5": "Hall continues his reading of Atlantis: the ten kings ruled seven small and three great islands, which stand for the threefold power of God and the seven regents before his throne. If Atlantis is the archetypal world, its sinking means rational consciousness descending into the realm of mortal ignorance; like the Biblical fall of man, it signifies spiritual involution, which must come before conscious evolution. Either Plato used the story for two purposes, or the Egyptian records were altered to hide the secret doctrine; this does not make Atlantis pure myth, but it explains the fantastic details of its size and its destruction in 9600 BC. The high mountain in the centre, casting a shadow five thousand stadia long and touching the ether, is the world's axis and a symbol of the head rising above the body's four elements; it gave rise to Olympus, Meru and Asgard. The City of the Golden Gates is the original of the Holy City of many religions, and of the New Jerusalem. Hall quotes Ignatius Donnelly that Atlantis is the key to Greek mythology and that the Greek gods were human rulers given divine honours.",
  "modern.hall.4.p6": "Hall says Donnelly supports this by noting that the Greek gods were seen as regents over the universe, not its makers. Eden, guarded by a flaming sword, may recall an earthly paradise west of Gibraltar destroyed by volcanoes, and the Flood may recall the Atlantean deluge. Hall asks whether the knowledge of the ancient priesthoods came from Atlantis. Atlantean sun worship survives in Christian and pagan rites, and the cross and serpent were Atlantean symbols of wisdom. In the Popol Vuh the divine ancestors of the Maya and Quiche lived within the radiance of Gucumatz, the plumed serpent, six sky-born sages united by a seventh; the title plumed serpent was given to Quetzalcoatl or Kukulcan. Hall pictures the centre of Atlantean religion as a pyramid temple in the City of the Golden Gates, from which the 'Initiate-Priests of the Sacred Feather' went out across the world. Many peoples tell of gods who came out of the sea: Native American holy men dressed in feathers and wampum, the Chaldean Oannes who taught writing, farming, medicine, astronomy, government and the Mysteries, and Quetzalcoatl, whom some Christians identify with St Thomas, who taught the Maya and sailed away on a raft of serpents to escape Tezcatlipoca.",
  "modern.hall.4.p7": "Hall asks whether these demigods from the sea were Atlantean priests, remembered only for their gold, wisdom and symbols, the cross and serpent, while the ships they came in were forgotten. He claims that wherever they went they built pyramids and temples like the one in their capital, and that this explains the pyramids of Egypt, Mexico and Central America and the mounds of Normandy, Britain and North America. When the catastrophes began, the priests never returned, and only stories of gods from the sea remained. He quotes Blavatsky's Isis Unveiled: led by their demon Thevetat, the Atlanteans became wicked magicians, war followed, and the land was submerged, a story echoed in the tales of Cain, the giants and Noah, and in the floods of Babylon and the Bible, with Xisuthrus and Noah escaping like the father in the Popol Vuh and the Hindu Vaivasvata. Hall says the world inherited from Atlantis its arts, sciences, philosophies and religions, but also hatred and war; the Atlanteans started the first war, and all later wars try in vain to justify it. Before the end, the enlightened initiates left with the secret doctrine for Egypt, where they became its first 'divine' rulers, and most of the world's great creation myths are based on their Mystery rites.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p1": "Hall says the myth of Tammuz and Ishtar is one of the oldest dying-god stories, probably earlier than 4000 BC, known only in fragments from damaged tablets. The Babylonians saw Tammuz as a god of farming or vegetation, though Hall calls him the esoteric sun god; he was also a guardian of the underworld gates and, like other saviour gods, a 'shepherd'. He was both son and husband of Ishtar, the mother goddess of Babylonia and Assyria, sacred to the planet Venus and probably the same as Ashtoreth, Astarte and Aphrodite. Tammuz died in midsummer, in the month named after him, and was mourned; how he died is unknown, though Izdubar (Nimrod) blamed Ishtar. His resurrection was celebrated and he was hailed as redeemer. Hall retells Ishtar's descent: she flies down to the house of Irkalla, the land of no return, dark, where the dead eat dust and mud. She threatens to break the gates and raise the dead, and is admitted on the same terms as everyone else. At each of the seven gates she loses something: her crown, earrings, necklace, breast ornaments, girdle, bracelets and finally her robe. The queen of the underworld then strikes her with diseases and imprisons her.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p2": "Hall continues: with Ishtar, the spirit of fertility, gone, nothing on earth ripens, as in the story of Persephone. The gods demand her release, she is sprinkled with the water of life, and she goes back up through the seven gates, receiving her things at each one; there is no record that she brought back the water that would revive Tammuz. Hall reads this as the spirit's descent through the seven planetary spheres, losing its spiritual adornments until it is imprisoned in the body, the true Hades; the water of life, the secret doctrine, cures ignorance, and the spirit regains its adornments as it rises again. Another Babylonian Mystery was that of Merodach, who kills a monstrous dragon and makes the universe from her body, which Hall thinks is the source of St George and the Dragon. The Mysteries of Adonis were held in Egypt, Phoenicia and Byblos; the name means 'Lord', a title of the sun later used by the Jews for God. His mother Smyrna was turned into a tree, from which he was born, in one version freed by a boar's tusks. He was born at midnight on 24 December and was gored to death by a boar sent by Mars in the month Tammuz, and his death was lamented in the Adoniasmos.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p3": "Hall notes Ezekiel 8:14, where women weep for Tammuz at the north gate of the Temple in Jerusalem, and quotes Frazer citing Jerome that Bethlehem was shaded by a grove of Adonis, where he was mourned where Jesus was born. A boar's image is said to have been set over a gate of Jerusalem and Adonis' rites held in the Nativity grotto; Hall says Adonis as the 'gored' or 'god' man is a key to Francis Bacon's boar symbol. Adonis was first an androgynous god of the sun's power, killed in winter by the boar of cold; after three days (or months) in the tomb he rose on 25 March to cries of 'He is risen!' He was born from a myrrh tree, and myrrh, used in embalming and so a symbol of death, was a gift of the Magi. In his Mysteries the candidate passed through the god's death and was 'raised' by the priests. Most authors think him a vegetation god, pointing to the 'gardens of Adonis', baskets of quick-dying seedlings thrown into the sea with his images. In Phrygia Atys, or Attis, often equated with Adonis, was also born on 24 December; he was gored, or castrated himself under a pine and died. Cybele kept his incorrupt body in a cave, and the pine, given his immortality, is the origin of the Christmas tree; he rose after three days at Easter time, conquering death for his initiates.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p4": "Hall quotes Julius Firmicus: in the Phrygian Mysteries a pine is cut each year and the image of a youth tied inside it; in those of Isis a pine trunk is hollowed and an image of Osiris made from it is buried; in those of Proserpine a tree is made into the image of a virgin, mourned forty nights and burned on the fortieth. The Mysteries of Atys included a sacred meal eaten from a drum and drunk from a cymbal; after baptism in bull's blood the initiate was fed only milk as a philosophical infant, and Hall wonders whether Paul's milk for spiritual babes is connected. Sallust explains that Cybele is the life-giving power of the universe and Atys the spiritual intellect between the divine and animal worlds: Cybele gave him a starry cap, but he loved a nymph, the lower nature, and lost his divinity; so his Mysteries are about regaining the starry cap. The rites of Sabazius were much like those of Bacchus, and the two are usually identified. They were held at night, and a live snake was drawn across the candidate's breast, the 'god gliding over the breast' of Clement. A golden serpent was his emblem, for the sun's yearly renewal of the world; Hall says the Jews took the name Sabaoth from these rites, and that the cult later influenced Christian symbolism.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p5": "Hall says the Cabiric Mysteries of Samothrace were second only to the Eleusinian in fame; Herodotus says the Samothracians got their teachings, especially about Mercury, from the Pelasgians. Little is known of them because of their secrecy. Some count seven Cabiri, the 'seven spirits of fire before the throne of Saturn', or the seven planets. The drama centres on four brothers, three of whom, Aschieros, Achiochersus and Achiochersa, murder the fourth, Cashmala or Cadmillus; Dionysidorus identifies them with Demeter, Pluto, Persephone and Hermes, and Wilder says Cadmillus includes Cadmus, Thoth, Hermes and Aesculapius. Hall sees here again the story of Osiris, Bacchus, Adonis, Balder and Hiram Abiff. The rites included the worship of Atys and Cybele and a pine-tree cult: the pine was trimmed into a cross and cut down in honour of the murdered god found at its foot. He quotes Clement: the Corybantes killed their third brother, wrapped and crowned the head, carried it on a spear and buried it under Olympus, so these mysteries are 'murders and funerals'; their priests forbade parsley on the table, thinking it grew from the spilled blood, as women at the Thesmophoria avoided fallen pomegranate seeds. Hall inserts a comment that Clement ignores that Jesus too was betrayed and killed.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p6": "Hall says the Cabiric Mysteries had three degrees: the murder of Cashmala by his brothers, the laborious gathering of his scattered body, and his joyful resurrection and the saving of the world. The Samothracian temple held strange misshapen figures of nature's elemental powers, perhaps the Titans. Children were initiated like adults, criminals who reached the sanctuary were safe, and the rites concerned seafaring, honouring Castor and Pollux; on Orpheus' advice the Argonauts stopped to be initiated. Herodotus says Cambyses laughed at seeing in the temple a man standing upright facing a woman standing on her head, but Hall says he was looking at the key to universal balance. He quotes Voltaire, via Mackey, asking who these 'sacred Freemasons' of Samothrace were and where they came from. Clement calls the rite 'the sacred mystery of a brother slain by his brethren', and the 'Cabiric death' was a secret symbol. Hall sees in all this the Self murdered by the not-self, and says the philosophical death and resurrection are the Lesser and the Greater Mysteries.",
  "modern.hall.4.s1.p7": "Hall describes the Hanged Man form of the dying-god myth, chiefly in Odin, who hung himself for nine nights from the World Tree and pierced his own side with a spear; hanging over Niflheim, he found by meditation the runes, the letters in which his people's records were kept. So Odin is sometimes shown on a gallows and was patron of those who died by the noose. Esoterically, the Hanged Man is the human spirit hanging from heaven by one thread: by suspending itself above the world of illusion and meditating on its unreality, the soul gains wisdom and self-realisation, not death. Hall concludes that the dying god was a universal mystery of the ancient schools and lives on in Christianity in the crucifixion of Jesus. Its secret meaning must be rediscovered if Christianity is to reach the heights of pagan philosophy, for this allegory is the key to universal and individual redemption, and those who do not understand it cannot call themselves wise or truly religious.",
  "modern.hall.4.fig1": "The caption, for a diagram from Cartari's book on the images of the ancient gods, describes the Graeco-Roman universe as layers: rising from the fiery sphere of Hades through the spheres of water, earth and air, the heaven of the moon, then Mercury, Venus, the sun, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn, which holds the zodiac. Above the arch of Saturn dwell the powers that rule the universe. The supreme council of twelve gods, six male and six female, matches the positive and negative signs of the zodiac: Jupiter, Vulcan, Apollo, Mars, Neptune and Mercury, and Juno, Ceres, Vesta, Minerva, Venus and Diana. Jupiter rides an eagle as ruler of the world, and Juno sits on a peacock for her pride and glory.",
  "modern.hall.4.fig2": "The caption, for an image from Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus, says Pan was honoured as the founder of sacred dances imitating the movements of the heavenly bodies. Human above except for his horns and a goat below, he stands for natural energy, and though phallic should not be confused with Priapus. His pipes mean the harmony of the spheres, and he symbolises Saturn, which rules Capricorn, the goat. The Egyptians had Mysteries of Pan, seen as an aspect of Jupiter the world-maker; he represented the sun's fertilising power, led the rustic gods and satyrs, and governed the lower worlds. Hall ends with the story that at Christ's birth the oracles fell silent after a last cry, 'Great Pan is dead!', which he says was invented.",
  "modern.hall.5.p0": "Hall opens the chapter with an imagined initiation scene. Amid thunder and lightning the temple veil tears, and the initiator in blue and gold points into the darkness: 'Behold the Light of Egypt!' A glowing haze fills the air, and a figure appears in a greenish light, larger than human and partly transparent, so that his heart and brain can be seen shining; the heart becomes an ibis and the brain an emerald, and he holds a winged staff twined with serpents. The initiator hails him as Thoth Hermes, Thrice Greatest, Prince of Men, who stands on the head of Typhon. A flaming dragon appears, part serpent, part crocodile and part hog; Hermes strikes it with his staff, it falls, and he sets his foot on its skull. Then, in a blinding blaze that throws the candidate back against a pillar, Hermes passes through the chamber and vanishes in green mist.",
  "modern.hall.5.s1": "Hall says Iamblichus credited Hermes with twenty thousand books and Manetho with over thirty-six thousand, too many for one person. Hermes is said to have given mankind medicine, chemistry, law, art, astrology, music, rhetoric, magic, philosophy, geography, mathematics, anatomy and oratory, as the Greeks said of Orpheus. He quotes Francis Barrett that if God ever appeared in a man he appeared in Hermes, whose books and Pymander gave posterity divine knowledge. Hermes was identified with many early sages: Bryant equates Cadmus with Thoth, and some think the Jews knew him as Enoch. The Greeks took him into their myths and the Romans made him Mercury, linked to the planet nearest the sun, since Hermes was nearest to God, the messenger of the gods. In Egyptian drawings Thoth holds a writing tablet and records the weighing of souls in the hall of Osiris. Hall says Hermes matters to Masons because Masonic rituals and most Masonic symbols came from his Mysteries. Hermes is also said to have lengthened the year from 360 to 365 days. He was called Thrice Greatest as the greatest philosopher, priest and king, and Hall notes that Longfellow's last poem was an ode to Hermes.",
  "modern.hall.5.s2.p1": "Hall quotes James Campbell Brown's History of Chemistry: the early Egyptian books written on papyrus are attributed to Hermes Trismegistus, who may have been a real scholar or a name for many writers; he is identified with the Greek Hermes and the ibis-headed Egyptian moon god Thoth, god of wisdom, letters and the reckoning of time. Because the alchemists revered him, chemical writings were called 'hermetic', and 'hermetically sealed' still means closing glass by melting it; the same root appears in Paracelsus' hermetic medicines and the hermetic freemasonry of the Middle Ages. Hall says the two most famous writings credited to Hermes are the Emerald Table and the Divine Pymander, or Shepherd of Men. He notes that the early Christians did not attack Hermes; some Church Fathers even said he would have been truly great had he lived late enough to learn from them. Clement of Alexandria's Stromata gives nearly all that is known of the original forty-two books of Hermes, and Hall introduces his account of an Egyptian procession.",
  "modern.hall.5.s2.p2": "Hall quotes Clement of Alexandria's description of an Egyptian sacred procession. First comes the Singer, who must know two books of Hermes, the hymns of the gods and the rules for the king's life; then the Astrologer, with a clock and palm, who knows the four astronomical books; then the sacred Scribe, with feathers on his head and book, ink and reed, who knows hieroglyphs, cosmography, geography, the planets, the land of Egypt and the Nile, and the priests' equipment and rites; then the Stole-keeper, with the cubit of justice and libation cup, who knows the ten books on training, sacrifice and worship; and last the Prophet, carrying the water vessel, who as head of the temple learns the ten Hieratic books on laws, the gods and priestly training, and oversees revenues. Thirty-six books hold all Egyptian philosophy, and six medical books, on the body, diseases, instruments, drugs, eyes and women, are learned by the image-bearers. Hall calls the loss of almost all forty-two books one of philosophy's greatest tragedies, blaming the burning at Alexandria, since Romans and later Christians knew they could not subdue Egypt while the books survived. He claims that those that escaped were buried in the desert, at places known only to a few initiates.",
  "modern.hall.5.s3": "Hall says that while Hermes lived he gave his chosen successors the Book of Thoth, which held the secret methods for regenerating humanity and was the key to his other writings. Its pages were said to be covered with strange figures that gave power over the spirits of the air and the underworld. Hall says that when certain parts of the brain are stimulated by the secret methods of the Mysteries, consciousness expands to see the Immortals, and the book described how; so it was the 'Key to Immortality'. It was kept in a golden box in the temple's inner sanctuary, with one key held by the Master of the Mysteries. When the Mysteries decayed, initiates carried it sealed to another land. Hall asserts that it still exists and still leads disciples to the Immortals, that the succession from the first hierophant initiated by Hermes is unbroken, and that those fit to serve may find it by sincere searching. Some say the Book of Thoth is the Tarot, a book of seventy-eight leaves kept by the gypsies, whom secret histories make Egyptian priests driven from the Serapeum. Hall says the several secret schools still able to initiate nearly all lit their fires from Hermes' torch, and that the wise of every age have reached immortality by the 'One Way' he revealed.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p1": "Hall says the Divine Pymander is one of the earliest surviving Hermetic writings; probably reworked in the first Christian centuries and badly translated since, it still contains many original Hermetic ideas. It consists of seventeen fragments put together as one work. Its second book, Poimandres or The Vision, is thought to describe how divine wisdom was first revealed to Hermes, after which he began to teach; it is the most famous Hermetic fragment and sets out Hermetic cosmology and the Egyptian science of the soul's unfolding. It was once wrongly called 'The Genesis of Enoch'. Hall lists his sources: Everard's 1650 translation, Walter Scott's Hermetica (1924), Schure's Hermes (1925) and Mead's Thrice-Greatest Hermes (1906). He says he has added commentary from Egyptian esoteric philosophy and amplifications from other Hermetic fragments and the 'secret arcanum', and has turned the dialogue into a narrative in modern wording, so what follows is his own retelling.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p2": "In Hall's retelling, Hermes, meditating in a desolate rocky place, frees his higher consciousness from his senses and sees a terrifying Great Dragon whose wings span the sky, streaming light; Hall notes the Mysteries pictured universal life as a dragon. It calls Hermes by name and says it is Poimandres, the Mind of the Universe and absolute ruler of all; Hall notes that Schure identifies it with Osiris. Asked to reveal the nature of the universe, it tells Hermes to hold its image in mind. It becomes a pulsing radiance into which Hermes is 'raised', and the material world fades. Then a darkness swallows the light, and a swirling watery substance gives off vapour and inarticulate moaning; Hermes understands that the light is the spiritual universe and the darkness material substance. A holy Word comes out of the light and stands on the waters, rising like a pillar, followed by fire and air while earth and water stay below. So the waters of light, from which the upper worlds are made, are divided from the waters of darkness, from which the lower are made; earth and water mingle, and the Word, called Reason, moves on them, stirring endless turmoil.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p3": "In Hall's retelling, Poimandres explains: he is the Light and Mind that existed before spirit and substance were divided, and the Word that rose like a flaming pillar is the Son of God, called Reason, born of Mind, which will divide light from darkness. What sees and hears in Hermes is the Word of God in him, and divine light lives in mortal darkness inseparably; the union of Word and Mind is Life. As the darkness outside is divided, so is the darkness within: the rising fire is the divine man, and what does not rise is the mortal man who cannot be immortal; the secret of immortality lies in understanding Mind. The Dragon and Hermes gaze at each other, the heavens open, and Hermes sees countless fiery powers, the spirits of the stars, all made visible by the Word. Poimandres goes on: the mould of the universe, the Archetype, existed in the Supreme Mind before creation; delighting in it, the Mind used the Word as a hammer to carve out space, cast the spheres in that mould and sow the seeds of life, and the darkness became an ordered universe. The male-female Mind brought forth the Word, and the Word brought forth a second Mind, the Workman or Master-Builder.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p4": "In Hall's retelling, Poimandres says the Word, moving through space, struck fire by friction, so fire is called the Son of Striving; the Workman whirled through the universe and formed the Seven Governors, the planetary spirits, who rule the world by Destiny. Once Chaos was ordered, the Word rose out of matter, leaving the elements without Reason, and joined the Workman, and together they turn the heavenly wheels from an endless beginning to an endless end. The elements, now without Reason, produced unreasoning creatures: birds from the air, fish from the water, and beasts, dragons and monsters from the earth. Then the Father, being Light and Life, made a heavenly Universal Man in his own image and loved him and gave him rule over creation. The Man wished to create too, and the Seven Governors each gave him a share of their nature. Longing to understand the One on the Eternal Fire, he leaned down through the seven spheres and showed himself to Nature below. Seeing his shadow on the earth and his reflection in the waters, he smiled, fell in love with his own image and wished to live in it; at that moment he united with the unreasoning form.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p5": "In Hall's retelling, Poimandres says Nature wrapped herself round the Man she loved, and the two mingled, so earthly man is double: an immortal Sky Man within and mortal Nature without. Suffering comes from the immortal Man giving up reality for love of his shadow; immortal, he has the Governors' powers and the Life, Light and Word, but mortal, he is ruled by Fate through their rings. The immortal Man is male and female and never sleeps, like his Father. Nature and the Sky Man produced seven upright, two-sexed men, each embodying one Governor; these are the seven races. Earth was the female element, water the male, fire and ether gave them spirit, and from the Dragon's Life came their soul and from its Light their mind. They reproduced from themselves for an age, until God untied the knot of Destiny and all creatures, man included, were divided into male and female. Then God told his creatures to increase and multiply, and said that whoever has Mind should know he is immortal, that love of the body causes death, and that whoever knows himself enters the Good.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p6": "In Hall's retelling, Providence with the Seven Governors joined the sexes and all things multiplied; whoever loves his body stays wandering in darkness and suffers death, but whoever sees the body as the soul's tomb rises to immortality. Hermes asks why ignorance alone should cost immortality; Poimandres answers that the ignorant, knowing only the mortal body, believe in death because they worship the substance that causes it. Asked how the wise reach God, he says the Father is Life and Light, of which man is made, and whoever understands Life and Light passes into them. The way is for a person with Mind to study himself and separate himself from his not-self. When Hermes asks whether all people have Mind, Poimandres warns him: he is Mind, the eternal teacher and Father of the Word, the redeemer, and the Word takes flesh in the wise; as Thought (Thoth) he comes only to the holy, pure, merciful and pious, who then know all things and worship the Father, and who learn before death to renounce the senses as enemies of the soul.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p7": "In Hall's retelling, Poimandres says he guards those who love him like a doorkeeper, keeping out evil senses, emotions and thoughts, but does not come to the wicked, envious and greedy, who cannot understand Mind; he leaves them to the avenging demon they make in themselves, for evil grows daily until it destroys itself, and the punishment of desire is the agony of not being satisfied. At death the body returns to the elements, the divine man rises to the Eighth Sphere, evil goes to the demon, and the passions return to the Seven Governors. The soul climbs the seven rings and gives back to each its lower power: to the Moon growth and decrease, to Mercury scheming and craft, to Venus lust, to the Sun ambition, to Mars rashness and impious daring, to Jupiter the urge to amass riches, and to Saturn, at the gate of Chaos, falsehood and plotting. Stripped of all this, it reaches the Eighth Sphere of fixed stars and sings praise to the Father. The Milky Way is the seed-bed of souls, from which they fall into the rings and to which they return past Saturn; but some cannot climb the seven-runged ladder and wander below in darkness.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p8": "In Hall's retelling, Poimandres says the path to immortality is hard and few find it; the rest wait for the Great Day when the universe stops and the sparks escape matter, and they must return unknowing to the seed-bed of stars to await a new beginning. Those saved by this mystery return to the Father in the White Light, are absorbed into it and become powers in God. He blesses Hermes and sends him to guide those in darkness and to found his Mysteries, which cannot fail while Mind lasts, then vanishes into the heavens; Hermes dedicates his life to the Great Light. Hermes then preaches, urging people born of the elements but holding the divine within to wake from ignorance, realise their home is the Light, repent, give up corruption and prepare to climb the seven rings. Some mock him and go to the Second Death; others beg to be taught, and he goes out with his staff teaching, taking no praise for himself. At the end of his life he tells his disciples to keep his teaching pure for all ages, and writes down the Vision so that all who seek immortality can find the way.",
  "modern.hall.5.s4.p9": "In Hall's retelling, Hermes ends his account of the Vision by saying that the body's sleep is the mind's sober watchfulness, that closing his eyes shows him the true light, and that his silence is full of life and good. He says this is a faithful account of what he received from Poimandres, his true Mind, which gave birth in him to the Word, Reason, by which he was redeemed. He then praises God in a litany: holy is God, Father of all, before the first beginning, whose will is done by his own powers, who wishes to be known and is known by his own; holy is he who made all things by his Word, whose image is all nature, who is stronger than all powers and better than all praise. He asks God to accept his pure offering, calls him unspeakable and praised in silence, prays not to fall from knowledge of him and to enlighten his ignorant brothers, and goes in trust into God's Light and Life. Hall closes by saying that the Vision, like most Hermetic writings, is an allegory of philosophical and mystical truths, understood only by those 'raised' into the presence of the true Mind.",
  "modern.hall.5.fig1": "The caption, for a portrait of Hermes from a book on prophetic gods, lists his titles: master of all arts, sciences and crafts, ruler of the three worlds, scribe of the gods and keeper of the books of life. The Egyptians saw Thoth Hermes Trismegistus, the Thrice Greatest, as the embodiment of the Universal Mind. Hall thinks a great sage named Hermes probably lived, but says the historical man cannot now be separated from legends that identify him with the cosmic principle of thought.",
  "modern.hall.5.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from Wilkinson's book on ancient Egyptian customs, says the Egyptian Thoth may not originally have been Hermes, but the two have long since merged. Thoth was called Lord of the Divine Books and Scribe of the Company of the Gods, and is usually shown as a man with an ibis head. Hall says the symbolic meaning of the ibis has never been discovered, and suggests that studying the shape of its head and beak would be revealing.",
  "modern.hall.5.fig3": "The caption, for a Greek figure of Hermes from Bryant's Mythology, says the name Hermes comes from 'Herm', a form of Chiram, the universal life principle, usually shown as fire. Hall says Hermes was worshipped as Odin by the Scandinavians, Wotan by the Teutons and Buddha or Fo by some Eastern peoples. One account says he was taken up to God without dying, like Enoch; another that he was buried in the Valley of Ebron with a treasure of books and sacred learning. The Egyptians pictured humanity as a flock of sheep, with the Supreme Father as shepherd and Hermes as sheepdog; Hall traces the religious shepherd's crook to Egyptian ritual, where it was one of the Pharaoh's three sceptres.",
  "modern.hall.6.p1": "Hall praises the Great Pyramid of Giza as the supreme wonder of antiquity, a 'sermon in stone' left by an unknown civilisation, and asks who designed and built it. He then quotes Herodotus' account, the earliest and best known: the pyramid was built in steps, and stones were raised from step to step by machines made of short wooden planks, either one machine per step or one moved up as the work rose; the top was finished first and the bottom last. Herodotus says an inscription recorded that 1,600 talents of silver were spent on radishes, onions and garlic for the workers, and he wonders how much more must have gone on iron tools, food and clothing over the ten years of building, besides quarrying, transport and digging the underground rooms.",
  "modern.hall.6.p2": "Hall argues that Herodotus, whom he suspects was an initiate sworn to secrecy, invented this story to hide the pyramid's real origin and purpose. He rejects the accepted view that it was Cheops' tomb: Manetho, Eratosthenes and Diodorus disagree with Herodotus and each other about the builder; the burial vault was never finished; and it lacks the carvings, inscriptions, cartouches and paintings typical of Egyptian royal tombs. The only hieroglyphs are a few builders' marks in the sealed relieving chambers first opened by Howard Vyse, painted before the stones were set, some upside-down or damaged; Hall doubts Cheops would have let his name be treated so. Against them he sets sea shells at the base that a Mr Gab takes as evidence the pyramid is older than the Flood, a view he says Arab traditions support: one Arab historian said the Egyptian sages built it as a refuge from the Flood, another that it was the treasure house of the antediluvian king Sheddad Ben Ad. The hieroglyph panel over the entrance dates only from 1843, cut by Lepsius in honour of the King of Prussia.",
  "modern.hall.6.p3": "Hall tells how Caliph al-Mamun came from Baghdad in AD 820 with workmen to open the pyramid in search of treasure. The casing stones were still in place and he could find no entrance, so his men cut into the north face with crude tools and vinegar, tunnelling a hundred feet through limestone, near mutiny. Just as they were about to give up, they heard a stone fall nearby, dug toward the sound and broke into the descending passage; they cut round a fallen portcullis stone and removed the granite plugs that kept sliding down the ascending passage. When the way was clear they found no treasure. To calm his men, al-Mamun, son of Harun al-Rashid, had money brought from Baghdad and secretly buried near the entrance, then had them dig there, and they were delighted at the ancient king who had so exactly provided their wages. Hall adds that in the ninth century the polished casing made each face a dazzling triangle of light, but all except two casing stones have since gone, recut into the walls of mosques and palaces in and around Cairo.",
  "modern.hall.6.s1.p1": "Hall quotes Piazzi Smyth asking whether the pyramid was built before hieroglyphs and the Egyptian religion existed, and suggests the upper chambers were sealed before the Egyptian empire, though marks in the underground chamber show the Romans got in. He reports W. W. Harmon's calculation, based on the secret philosophy of the initiates, that the first ceremony in the pyramid took place 68,890 years ago, when the star Vega first shone down the descending passage, after ten to fifteen years of building; Hall admits Egyptologists will mock such figures. He doubts that weathering marks or salt on the inner stones prove the pyramid was once under water, since the casing was intact in the ninth century and this stone exudes salt naturally. He describes the building as limestone and granite combined in a significant way, its stones precisely trued, its cement as hard as stone, the limestone sawn with bronze saws set with diamonds or other jewels, and the chips piled against the north side as a buttress. He says it is perfectly oriented and squares the circle: a circle whose radius is the pyramid's height has a circumference equal to the four sides of its base.",
  "modern.hall.6.s1.p2": "Hall argues that if the passage to the upper chambers was sealed thousands of years before Christ, later initiates must have entered by underground galleries now unknown, perhaps under or in the Sphinx, a nearby temple or the side of the plateau, since the only surface entrance was covered by casing stones. He discusses the granite plugs that al-Mamun had to break up. Piazzi Smyth notes they were placed from above, so the workmen who placed them must have left the upper chambers some other way; he thinks they went down the well, dropping a ramp stone behind them, and that later robbers broke through that stone to enter. The architect Dupre, who studied the pyramids for years, disagrees: he thinks the well itself is a robbers' tunnel, the first successful way into the upper chambers from the underground chamber, which was then the only open part of the pyramid.",
  "modern.hall.6.s1.p3": "Hall gives Dupre's reasons: the well is a rough hole and the grotto an irregular chamber with none of the building's precision; the well's width shows it was dug upward from below, and the grotto was needed for air; and the builders would never have broken their own ramp stone and left a hole in an otherwise perfect gallery. If so, robbers may explain why the pyramid was empty when al-Mamun entered and why the coffer lid is missing, and the unfinished underground chamber, their likely base, might show traces of them or their rubble. Hall describes a niche in the north wall of the Queen's Chamber that guides call a shrine, but whose stepped walls suggest it was meant as a passage; Dupre thinks the workmen left that way after dropping the plugs. He mocks Bible scholars who have called the pyramid Joseph's granary, despite its tiny capacity, a tomb for the Pharaoh of the Exodus whose body was lost in the Red Sea, and proof of the prophecies of the Authorized Version.",
  "modern.hall.6.s2.p1": "Hall says that while the Great Pyramid follows an antediluvian style found worldwide, as Donnelly argues, the Sphinx is typically Egyptian. The stele between its paws says it is an image of the sun god Harmakhis, probably modelled on the reigning Pharaoh; Thutmose IV restored and dug it out after the god appeared in a vision complaining of the weight of the sand. Its broken beard was found between the paws, the steps, temple and altar there are later, probably Roman, additions, and a hollow in its head, once thought to lead to a passage to the pyramid, held a lost headdress. Metal rods driven into it found no chambers. Most of it is one stone, though the front paws are built of smaller blocks; it is about 200 feet long, 70 high and 38 wide at the shoulders. Some think the stone was brought from far quarries, others that it is native rock already somewhat sphinx-shaped; the theory of artificial stone is abandoned, and the limestone is made of tiny sea creatures, nummulites. Hall says the popular idea that the Sphinx was the true entrance to the pyramid has never been proved, and introduces P. Christian's version of it, partly based on Iamblichus.",
  "modern.hall.6.s2.p2": "Hall quotes P. Christian: the Sphinx was the entrance to the underground chambers where initiates were tested, between its forelegs, once closed by a bronze gate with a secret spring known only to the Magi and protected by religious awe; galleries cut through its body led to the pyramid, so cunningly crossed that anyone without a guide always came back to the start. Hall says no trace of the bronze door has been found, though the original opening may have been closed over the centuries. Most students believe there are chambers under the pyramid, and he quotes Robert Ballard that the priests at Giza probably had underground residences like those at Lake Moeris, that the pyramids' limestone may have come from these caverns, and that a diamond drill would settle the matter. Hall replies that the builders would not have put over five million tons of stone on anything but solid foundations, so any chambers beneath must be small, like the internal ones, which take up less than one sixteen-hundredth of its volume.",
  "modern.hall.6.s2.p3": "Hall says the Sphinx was surely built by the priests for symbolic purposes, and calls theories that its uraeus was a sundial pointer, or that it and the pyramid measured time and precession, ingenious but unconvincing. If it was built to block the old passage into the pyramid's underground temple, its symbolism would be fitting. Next to the pyramid it is almost insignificant; its face, with traces of red paint, is battered, its nose broken off by a Muslim zealot to prevent idolatry, and its construction and the repairs needed to keep the head on suggest it cannot be as old as the pyramid. To the Egyptians the Sphinx meant strength and intelligence and was androgynous, showing that initiates and gods share both creative powers. He quotes Gerald Massey that the Egyptian sphinx is male in front and female behind, like Sut-Typhon and like the Pharaohs who wore an animal tail, uniting both sexes in one person. Most investigators, he says, have ignored the Sphinx for the greater mystery of the pyramid.",
  "modern.hall.6.s3.p1": "Hall says 'pyramid' is popularly derived from the Greek for fire, making it a symbol of the one divine flame; John Taylor thinks it means 'a measure of wheat', and Piazzi Smyth prefers a Coptic meaning, 'a division into ten'. The initiates took the pyramid as the ideal symbol of the secret doctrine and its schools; pyramids and mounds copy the Holy Mountain at the centre of the earth, and Lundy links the pyramid to Olympus and its passages to Hades. The square base means wisdom rests on nature's laws; Pike says the Gnostics built on a square of Silence, Depth, Intelligence and Truth. The sides face the four directions, which Eliphas Levi links to heat, cold, light and darkness, and the base stands for the four elements of the body, from each of which rises a triangle for the threefold divine nature. Hall adds numbers: twelve edges and sixteen hypothetical squares make 28, the number of the lower world, plus 21 for the sun makes 49, the square of seven. The twelve edges are the zodiac, Ezekiel's four beasts sit on the faces, the three chambers match the heart, brain and generative system, and the shape resembles the meditation posture. Divine energy was said to descend on the apex, like an inverted tree, and stream down the sides to the world.",
  "modern.hall.6.s3.p2": "Hall says no trace of a capstone survives, and builders of great temples often left them unfinished, since only God is complete. A capstone would itself be a little pyramid, capped by a smaller one and so on for ever, an epitome of the whole: the pyramid is the universe and the capstone man; mind is man's capstone, spirit the mind's, and God the spirit's. Man is a rough block from the quarry, shaped by the Mysteries into a perfect capstone, and the temple is complete only when the initiate becomes the living apex through which divine power flows. W. Marsham Adams calls it 'the House of the Hidden Places'. Linked by the Egyptians with Hermes, it was, Hall says, not a lighthouse, observatory or tomb but the first temple of the Mysteries, an image of microcosm and macrocosm and, by the secret teaching, the tomb of Osiris, whose house stands for the universe in which the sun's power is entombed and crucified. Initiates entered it as men and left as gods; deep within lived an unseen hierophant, 'the Initiator', robed in blue and gold, lion-faced, holding the sevenfold key of eternity, whom only the prepared could see, and Hall says Plato met him there.",
  "modern.hall.6.s3.p3": "Hall asks who the Master of the pyramid was, whom only the 'born again' could see, and laments that he has gone, the hymns are silent, and men now call the House of God a tomb. He says this Master taught the initiate to know his guardian spirit and to separate his body from his divine vehicle, and finally revealed the secret name of God, by which man and God become consciously one; the initiate then became a pyramid himself, in whose soul others might be enlightened. In the King's Chamber the 'second death' was enacted: the candidate, 'crucified' on the cross of solstices and equinoxes, was laid in the great coffer, in a strange, deathly cold, while his soul flew like a human-headed hawk through the heavens and learned that life, light and truth are eternal and death, darkness and sin illusions. The pyramid was a gate through which a few passed to completion. Hall adds that the coffer, when struck, gives a note belonging to no known musical scale, which may have helped make the chamber fit for the highest degree.",
  "modern.hall.6.s3.p4": "Hall says modern scientists and theologians wonder what drove such an enormous labour, and answers that only the desire to know and to exchange mortal narrowness for divine understanding could have done so. People call the pyramid the most perfect building, the source of weights and measures, Noah's ark or the origin of alphabets, but few see it as the gateway to the eternal. The modern world knows a million secrets that breed death, selfishness and greed, while the ancient world knew one secret that gives life, light and truth. He predicts that the secret wisdom will again lead the world's religion and philosophy, that dogma is doomed, and that the Mysteries will rise like a phoenix from dead creeds, since no religion since their fall could have satisfied Plato. Spiritual unfolding is as exact a science as astronomy or medicine, and religion was founded for it, with science, philosophy and logic as its tools. He ends: the dying god will rise, the secret room will be found, and the Master of the Hidden House will again wait for the person who throws off dogma and seeks only truth.",
  "modern.hall.6.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Levi's book on the Kabbalah, links the Egyptian Sphinx to the Greek story of Oedipus. The sphinx, a winged lion with a woman's head, asked travellers to Thebes what walks on four feet in the morning, two at noon and three in the evening, and killed those who failed; Oedipus answered 'man', who crawls as a child, walks upright as an adult and uses a stick in old age, and the sphinx threw herself from a cliff. Hall offers another answer from Pythagorean numbers: 4, 2 and 3 add up to 9, the number of man and the lower worlds; 4 is the ignorant man, 2 the intellectual man and 3 the spiritual man, who adds the staff of wisdom to his mind. The sphinx is the mystery of nature and the secret doctrine; those who cannot solve her riddle perish, and to pass her is to gain personal immortality.",
  "modern.hall.6.fig2": "The caption, for a cross-section from Piazzi Smyth's Life and Work at the Great Pyramid, describes the pyramid's measurements and layout. Hall says it stands on a limestone plateau once reached by the Nile flood, and, by John Taylor's figures, would be about 486 feet high with its capstone, with sides of 764 feet, covering over 13 acres. It is the only Egyptian pyramid known to have chambers within its body, which Hall says refutes Lepsius' rule that pyramids cover underground royal tombs. He describes the King's Chamber (about 39 by 17 by 19 feet, roofed with nine huge stones), with five low relieving chambers above, the lowest bearing the supposed Cheops marks, the coffer, and two air shafts, which he says prove it was not a tomb; the antechamber with its grooved walls and hanging slab; the Grand Gallery, 157 feet long and 28 high, its walls narrowing in seven overlaps; the horizontal passage to the peaked Queen's Chamber, with a niche Hall thinks may be a lost entrance; the well, dropping 59 feet to the grotto and 133 feet more to the descending passage; the low, rough subterranean chamber with a blind tunnel; and the tunnel cut by al-Mamun's men.",
  "modern.hall.7.p1": "Hall begins his study of Hermetic symbols with Isis of Sais, whose temple bore the inscription: 'I, Isis, am all that has been, that is or shall be; no mortal man hath ever me unveiled.' Plutarch says some called her the daughter of Hermes and others of Prometheus, probably allegorically, and translates her name as 'wisdom'. Godfrey Higgins derives it from Hebrew and Greek words meaning 'to save', while Payne Knight thinks it northern, from Isa, ice, water in its most passive state. Under many names she is the principle of natural fertility in nearly all ancient religions, the goddess of ten thousand names, and Christianity turned her into the Virgin Mary, since Isis bore all living things, chief of them the sun, yet stayed a virgin. Hall quotes Apuleius' Golden Ass, where the goddess says she is Nature, mother of all things, queen of the elements, first of the gods, ruler of heaven, sea and underworld, worshipped everywhere under many names, as Mother of the Gods, Minerva, Venus, Diana, Proserpine, Ceres, Juno, Hecate and others, but called by her true name, Queen Isis, by the Ethiopians and the learned Egyptians.",
  "modern.hall.7.p2": "Hall reports Le Plongeon's theory that the Isis myth goes back to the Maya: Queen Moo, widow of Prince Coh (Osiris), fled to Mayan colonies in Egypt and was made queen there as Isis. Hall replies that any historical queen matters little beside the symbolic World Virgin, and her presence among so many peoples argues against her being a real person. Sextus Empiricus says the Trojan War was fought over a statue of the moon goddess, a lunar Helen. Some writers make Isis, Osiris, Typhon, Nephthys and Thoth grandchildren of Noah through Ham, but since Noah's ark is itself a cosmic allegory, this makes them even less likely to be historical. Robert Fludd says the sun has three properties, life, light and heat, which enliven the spiritual, intellectual and material worlds, 'from one light, three lights', the first three Master Masons. Hall takes Osiris as the third, material aspect of the sun's activity, which enlivens plants and animals: not the sun itself, but the vital principle of nature the sun symbolises, hence his emblem the open eye. Against this active fiery principle stands nature's passive, receptive principle.",
  "modern.hall.7.p3": "Hall says science has shown that everything from solar systems to atoms has a positive centre surrounded by negative bodies living on its emanations. This is the meaning of Solomon and his wives: Solomon is the sun and his wives the planets and moons, while Isis, the dark maid of the Song of Songs, is receptive, watery nature, which brings forth all things once the sun has made her fruitful. The ancient year had 360 days; the five extra days became the birthdays of five gods, Osiris on the first, Typhon on the third and Isis on the fourth, a number linking her to the earth. Typhon, the Adversary, is shown as a crocodile or crocodile and hog; Plutarch says his name means insolence and pride, the enemies of wisdom, which Isis stands for. Osiris, as the sun, ruled Egypt, then travelled to enlighten other nations; the Greeks identified him with Dionysus. While he was away Typhon, like Loki, plotted with seventy-two accomplices: he made a splendid chest to Osiris' measure and offered it to whoever fitted it, and when Osiris lay in it they nailed and leaded it shut and threw it in the Nile. Plutarch dates this to 17 Athyr, with the sun in Scorpio, the sign of treachery, the same season in which Noah entered the ark.",
  "modern.hall.7.p4": "Hall says Plutarch tells that the Pans and Satyrs, nature spirits, first discovered the murder and raised the alarm, giving us the word 'panic'. Isis, told by children who had seen the murderers, put on mourning and searched. The chest had floated to Byblos and lodged in a tree that grew round it; the king had the tree made into a pillar for his palace. Isis recovered the body, but Typhon stole it again and cut it into fourteen pieces, scattering them over the earth. Isis found thirteen; the fourteenth, the phallus, had been eaten by a fish in the Nile, so she made one of gold. Osiris' son later killed Typhon. Some Egyptians thought the gods' souls became stars, Isis the Dog Star and Typhon the Bear, though this may not have been widely held. Isis is often shown wearing the empty throne of her husband. Hall says Egyptian headdresses, like Christian halos, represent the auras of superhuman beings, and notes Frank C. Higgins' observation that some are tilted back at the angle of the earth's axis. Priestly robes and jewels stood for spiritual energies radiating from the body, and Hall claims science is rediscovering how to judge a person's mind, soul and health from these emanations, citing Walter Kilner's The Human Atmosphere.",
  "modern.hall.7.p5": "Hall says Isis is sometimes symbolised by a cow's head or a whole cow, like the Norse cow Audhumla who licked the first gods out of ice and stood for nourishment and fertility, and sometimes by a bird; she often holds the ankh, symbol of eternal life, and a flowered sceptre of authority. Thoth Hermes gave the ancient priests secrets preserved in myths and emblems, which hide formulas for spiritual, mental, moral and physical regeneration, the 'mystic chemistry of the soul', or alchemy. Initiates learned these truths, while the uninitiated worshipped the statues that symbolised them. Egypt's secrecy is summed up in the Sphinx, and the mysteries of Hermeticism in the veiled Virgin Isis, who reveals herself only to tested initiates who may tear away nature's veil and face the divine reality. Hall says his explanations of her symbols follow, unless noted, a free translation of a section of the Bibliotheque des Philosophes Hermetiques, with his own additions. Her statues bore the sun, moon, stars and earthly emblems; she stood for Universal Nature, mother of all, usually as a partly naked, often pregnant woman, loosely robed in green or black, or in black, white, yellow and red.",
  "modern.hall.7.p6": "Hall quotes Apuleius' description of Isis as she appeared to him: long hair falling over her neck; a crown of many flowers with a shining disc like a mirror on her forehead, showing she was the moon, with vipers on either side and ears of corn above; a robe of fine linen shifting between white, saffron yellow and rosy red; and over it a dazzling black cloak, gathered over her left shoulder like a shield boss, fringed, scattered with stars, with a full moon in the middle breathing out flame, and bordered with a garland of flowers and fruit. In her right hand she held a bronze sistrum, whose rods made a sharp triple sound when shaken, and from her left hung a boat-shaped vessel with an asp rising from its handle; her feet were shod with palm leaves. Hall explains the colours: green is the vegetation that clothes the earth, nature's robe; black is death and decay as the way to new life, as in 'Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God'; and white, yellow and red are the three chief colours of the alchemists' universal medicine once its black stage of putrefaction is over.",
  "modern.hall.7.p7": "Hall says the ancients named one of their secret medicines Isis, so her description is partly chemical. Her black cloak also means that the moon, the 'lunar humidity' or philosophical mercury of the alchemists, has no light of its own but takes it from the sun. Isis stood for the alchemists' Great Work: the Philosopher's Stone, the Elixir of Life and the Universal Medicine. She sometimes wears a cap of cypress, for mourning her husband and for the death every creature must undergo to gain new life; sometimes a gold crown or olive garland, as queen of the universe. The gold crown also stands for the sun's 'sulphurous' vital fire, which she gives to all through the circulation of the elements, symbolised by her sistrum, also a symbol of purity. A serpent biting its tail among the olive leaves means this fire is soiled by earthly corruption and must be purified by seven planetary circulations, which Hall compares to the circuits of a Masonic lodge, the march round Jericho and the circling of the Kaaba. Three horns of plenty rise from her crown, for nature's abundance springing from one heavenly root.",
  "modern.hall.7.p8": "Hall continues the symbolism of the Isis statue: she represents the vital powers of the mineral, plant and animal kingdoms. The moon at one ear and the sun at the other show that these are the mother and father principles through which nature works on all things. On the back of her neck are the planetary signs and the zodiac, meaning that the heavens govern the seeds of all earthly things and make them little worlds in the image of the great one. In her right hand is a small ship with a spindle for a mast and a jug with a serpent handle: Isis steers the troubled bark of life over the sea of time, and spins and cuts the thread of life. These also mean that she nourishes bodies with moisture, protecting them from the sun's heat; but this moisture, the life ether, is tainted by decay and must be purified by nature's hidden fire to become a universal medicine. The serpent shedding its skin stands for spiritual renewal and for spring. In her left hand she holds a sistrum and a square cymbal that sounds nature's keynote, Fa, and sometimes an olive branch, for the harmony by which she renews life through death and change according to the four elements.",
  "modern.hall.7.p9": "Hall says Dr Sigismund Bacstrom believed that a physician who could harmonise earth, fire, air and water into a stone, the Philosopher's Stone, symbolised by the six-pointed star, could cure all disease, and that nature's universal fire 'does all and is all in all', working on matter by attraction, repulsion, heat, evaporation, coagulation and similar processes; whoever understands this in all three kingdoms is a true philosopher. Grapes at the right breast of Isis and a golden ear of corn at the left show nature feeding all life, the gold hinting that the first seed of life is hidden in sunlight, or spiritual gold. Her girdle, fastened by four gold plates in a square, means that Isis, the alchemists' first matter, is the essence of the four elements, and its stars show the influence of the heavens by night as the sun's by day. She is the Virgin of the constellation Virgo, with a serpent under her feet and a crown of stars, holding grain and sometimes the infant sun god. Her statue stood on a dark pedestal with rams' heads and trod on venomous reptiles: nature can purify corrosive and corrupt things, life is best generated when the sun is in Aries, and nature tends to heal by expelling impurity.",
  "modern.hall.7.p10": "Hall quotes the old axioms: nature contains nature, rejoices in nature, overcomes nature, and can only be improved through her own nature; without their hidden sense, he says, the statue is an unsolvable riddle. From her left arm hangs a box of burning coals and incense, the sacred fire kept by the Vestal Virgins, nature's immortal flame, fed by the 'inconsumable oil' or balsam of life. From her right hangs a pair of scales, for nature's exact weights and measures, making her a symbol of justice. She sometimes stands between two pillars, Masonry's Jachin and Boaz, showing that nature is productive through polarity and that understanding lies at the point of balance, truth often being crucified between two apparent contradictions. The gold in her dark hair means that though lunar, her power comes from the sun, like the woman clothed with the sun in Revelation. Apuleius saw her rising from the sea, and Hall says the ancients knew life began in water, quoting H. G. Wells to show modern science agrees. The universal seed was thought to come from warm, moist vapour; the veiled Isis stands for this moisture carrying the sun's seed, the child in her arms, and the sea, receiving the rays of the heavenly bodies, was its breeding ground, which is why she is sometimes shown pregnant.",
  "modern.hall.7.p11": "Hall says Isis was often accompanied by a black and white ox, Osiris as Taurus or the Apis bull, a reminder of nature's patient labour, and by Harpocrates, god of silence, finger to his lips, warning that the secrets of the wise must be kept from the unfit. The Druids knew her Mysteries and worshipped her as the moon, which Higgins thinks a mistake, the moon being chosen for its rule over water; they called the sun the father and the moon the mother of all things. Isis also stands for the magical arts: one myth says she made Ra tell her his secret name, which Hall equates with Masonry's Lost Word, by which a magician can command higher powers, and her priests, he claims, knew hypnotism and mesmerism long before the modern world. He quotes Plutarch that a true follower of Isis is not made by shaving or linen robes but by searching out the hidden truths in the gods' stories through reason and philosophy. Medieval troubadours sang of the most beautiful woman in the world, who was really Sophia, the Virgin of Wisdom. Isis represents the mystery of motherhood, and only those who unveil her can solve the mysteries of life, death, generation and regeneration.",
  "modern.hall.7.s1": "Hall quotes Servius that the Egyptians embalmed their dead so the soul would stay linked with the body for a long time, while the Romans burned theirs so the spark of life would return at once to its element. No full record of Egyptian teaching on spirit and body survives, but Hall thinks Pythagoras' doctrine of transmigration partly restates it, and rejects the popular view that mummies were preserved for a physical resurrection. Porphyry describes the Egyptian practice of removing the abdominal organs into a separate chest and gives a prayer in which the dead man tells the Sun and the gods that he honoured the gods and his parents, killed and cheated no one, and if he ate or drank anything forbidden, it was the fault of 'these', pointing to the chest; removing the seat of the appetites purified the body. Early Christians, by contrast, preserved bodies in salt water for the resurrection and avoided embalming cuts that might prevent the spirit's return. Hall quotes S.S.D.D.'s Egyptian Magic, which suggests only initiates were mummified, since mummification prevented reincarnation, which only imperfect souls needed, and the initiate's body was kept as a talisman for the soul's work on earth. At first mummification was limited to the Pharaoh and royalty, who shared the nature of the mummified Osiris.",
  "modern.hall.7.fig1": "The caption, for an engraving from a Dutch history of the Hebrew church, quotes an inscription that Diodorus says was carved on a column at Nysa in Arabia, in which Isis speaks: she is queen of the country, taught by Mercury; no one can undo her laws; she is the eldest daughter of Saturn, wife and sister of King Osiris, mother of King Horus; she first taught mortals the use of wheat; the city of Bubastis was built in her honour; and she calls on Egypt, the land of her birth, to rejoice. Hall cites the quotation from Albert Pike's Morals and Dogma.",
  "modern.hall.7.fig2": "The caption quotes Plutarch's Isis and Osiris on the sistrum: it shows that everything must be kept moving and shaken when it grows sluggish, for its sound drives away Typhon, meaning that as decay halts nature's course, generation through motion restores it. Its curved frame stands for the sphere of the moon, within which all birth and decay happen, and its bars, four in Plutarch though only three are shown, for the four elements whose combinations cause all change. On top is carved a cat with a human face, and below the bars the faces of Isis and Nephthys, standing for generation and decay.",
  "modern.hall.7.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Lenoir's book on Freemasonry, says Thoth, also called Aroueris, one of the five immortals, protected the infant Horus from Typhon after Osiris' murder, and reformed the calendar from 360 to 365 days. He was called 'the Dog-Headed' for his faithfulness and integrity, and is shown with a solar halo, holding the ankh, symbol of eternal life, and a serpent-twined staff as counsellor of the gods.",
  "modern.hall.7.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Lenoir's book on Freemasonry, shows Isis holding her son Horus, crowned with the moon's disc and horns. Horus, son of Isis and Osiris, was the god of time, hours and days, and of the short span of mortal life; Hall thinks his four sons probably stand for the four kingdoms of nature. Horus finally avenged his father by killing Typhon, the spirit of evil.",
  "modern.hall.7.fig5": "The caption says Osiris is often shown with his lower body in a mummy case or bandages. Hall explains that the human spirit has three parts, only one of which takes bodily form, and the body is its tomb; so Osiris, the incarnating self, is shown half mummified as the living spirit enclosed in matter. From the union of God's active principle and nature's passive principle comes rational creation: from the Father man inherits his immortal spirit, the fire of aspiration, and from the Mother his body, ruled by nature's laws, with his personality, appetites and emotions. The Egyptians also saw Osiris as the Nile and Isis as the land it floods and makes fruitful, and the Nile's dark water was thought to explain why Osiris was shown black.",
  "modern.hall.8.p0": "Hall says sun worship was one of the earliest and most natural religions, and modern theologies are only elaborations of it; early peoples revered the sun as the stand-in for the Supreme God. He quotes Albert Pike: the sun was to them the fire of nature, the cause of life and all generation, everywhere present, its absence most feared, and so it was identified with the Good; Brahma, Mithras, Atum, Amun, Ptah, Osiris, Bel, Adonai, Adonis and Apollo were all personifications of it. Altars and temples to the sun survive everywhere, such as the pyramids of Yucatan and Egypt, Native American serpent mounds, Babylonian ziggurats, Irish round towers and the stone rings of Britain and Normandy, and Hall thinks the Tower of Babel was probably an observatory. Many early priests and prophets knew astronomy and astrology and are best read in that light; as knowledge grew, gods were given planetary thrones and the stars grouped into constellations through which the sun and planets move.",
  "modern.hall.8.s1.p1": "Hall says the sun, supreme among visible bodies, was given to the highest god and stood for the Creator's authority, and that the doctrine of the Trinity grew from reflecting on it. Persians, Hindus, Babylonians and Egyptians all had trinities, each the threefold form of one Intelligence; Masonry shows God as an equilateral triangle with a small flame, the Hebrew Yod, and Jakob Bohme calls the Trinity the Three Witnesses. The sun's three daily phases, rising, noon and setting, gave the three stages of growth, maturity and decay. God the Father is the dawn, coloured blue from the morning mist; God the Son is the radiant noonday sun, the Lion of Judah and golden-haired Saviour, coloured yellow; God the Holy Ghost is the red sunset, sinking into darkness to wander the lower worlds and rise again. For the Egyptians the sun meant immortality, dying each night and rising each dawn. It also makes a yearly journey through the twelve houses of the zodiac, thirty days in each, and a third, the precession of the equinoxes, moving backward through the signs at one degree every seventy-two years.",
  "modern.hall.8.s1.p2": "Hall quotes Robert Hewitt Brown that the sun was said to take on the nature of, or conquer, each zodiac sign it entered: a bull in Taurus, worshipped as Apis or Baal; Hercules the lion-slayer in Leo; an archer in Sagittarius; a fish-god, Dagon or Vishnu, in Pisces. Hall says pagan priests served the solar energy and their supreme deity was always divine light personified, and quotes Godfrey Higgins that all ancient gods resolve into the solar fire, either as God or as the emblem of the Creator. Egyptian priests wore lion skins because the sun is strongest in Leo, once the keystone of the heavenly arch. Hercules is a sun god, his twelve labours the sun's work in the twelve signs, and he too wore a lion skin. Samson, as his name shows, is solar: his fight with the lion, his battles with the Philistines, the powers of darkness, and his carrying off the gates of Gaza are aspects of the sun's activity. Many peoples had several sun gods, and all gods were thought to share the sun's light.",
  "modern.hall.8.s1.p3": "Hall says priests' gold ornaments and kings' crowns refer to the sun; crowns once had outward points like rays, which have since been bent inward and topped with an orb or cross, and ancient sceptres often bore a radiant sun. Earthly kingdoms copied the heavenly one, with the sun as king, the planets as council and nature as subjects. The Greeks linked Apollo, Bacchus, Dionysus, Sabazius, Hercules, Jason, Ulysses, Zeus, Uranus and Vulcan with the sun; the Norse, Balder and the one-eyed Odin; the Egyptians, Osiris, Ra, Anubis, Hermes, Ammon, Isis as mother of the sun, and even Typhon, with the solar myth finally centring on Serapis; and in Central America Tezcatlipoca and Quetzalcoatl were solar gods too. In Masonry, Solomon's name, Sol-Om-On, is said to be 'light' in three languages, and Hiram Abiff is a sun god whose murder by the ruffians Hall explains later. He quotes George Oliver that the Worshipful Master sits in the east where the sun rises, to warm the brethren to their work, and adds that the gold sunbursts on Catholic vestments mark the priest as a representative of Sol Invictus.",
  "modern.hall.8.s2": "Hall says the writers of Jesus' life chose, for their own reasons, to turn him into a sun god, so that the historical Jesus was forgotten and nearly every Gospel event matches a movement of the heavens. The picture of a blue-eyed, golden-haired Sun God in white, carrying the Lamb of the spring equinox, combines Apollo, Osiris, Orpheus, Mithras and Bacchus. The Greeks and Egyptians showed the sun in four forms through the year: at the winter solstice a helpless infant escaping the powers of darkness, with a single hair, sometimes suckled by a goat since it is born in Capricorn; at the spring equinox a youth with golden curls; at the summer solstice a strong bearded man; and at the autumn equinox a bent old man going into winter. In autumn it enters, like Samson, the house of Delilah (Virgo), loses its rays and its strength; in Masonry the three winter months are the three murderers of the God of Light. Its coming was celebrated and its departure mourned. Hall says this light that raises the dead, feeds the multitude, calms the storm, dies and rises is what Christendom calls Christ.",
  "modern.hall.8.s3.p1": "Hall says the pagans kept 25 December as the birthday of the Solar Man, with feasts, processions and offerings, since winter darkness was over; the old sun god had pulled down the house of the Philistines, the spirits of darkness, and the new sun was born from the depths of the earth among the beasts of the lower world. He quotes an anonymous Oxford author, Mankind, Their Origin and Destiny: the Romans held games for the birth of the sun on the eighth day before the kalends of January, 25 December, which Servius calls the day of the new sun; some Church Fathers under Leo I said Christmas was venerable less for Christ's birth than for the sun's rebirth; the Roman calendars of Constantine's and Julian's time mark the birth of the Invincible Sun on that day; and 'Invictus' was also the title of the Persian Mithras, born in a cave, as Christ is shown born in a stable.",
  "modern.hall.8.s3.p2": "Hall quotes the same author on the Assumption: in mid-August the sun, passing through the eighth sign, absorbs the Virgin in its rays and she disappears, which gave rise to the feast in which Mary is taken up to join her son; Columella's calendar marks the disappearance of Virgo then, the feast was once called the Passage of the Virgin, and the Greeks and Romans placed the assumption of Astraea on the same day. Hall links this to the Sais inscription of Isis, 'The fruit which I have brought forth is the Sun', and argues that the ancients knew it was the constellation Virgo, not the moon, that gave birth to the sun on 25 December; he cites Albertus Magnus that Virgo was rising at the moment of Christ's birth. Some Arab and Persian astronomers called Orion's belt the Magi, and the author notes that Cancer, at the meridian at midnight, holds the Manger and the Asses, while the stars of the Bear were called Martha and Mary and the coffin of Lazarus. Hall concludes that pagan esoteric teaching was absorbed into Christianity but its keys were lost, and the Church follows ancient customs blindly, ignoring that every religion rests on its predecessor's secret doctrine.",
  "modern.hall.8.s4.p1": "Hall says the sages divided the sun, like man, into three: the spiritual sun, the 'soular' or intellectual sun and the material sun, shown in Masonry by three candles and corresponding to Father, Son and Holy Spirit and to man's spirit, soul and body. The alignment of the three suns was one explanation offered for elliptical planetary orbits. Pagan priests saw the solar system as a Grand Man with three centres matching the brain, heart and generative system, which Hall finds in the three tabernacles of the Transfiguration. He suggests the idea may also come from sightings of three suns at once, recorded in AD 51 and 66, two in 69, and, according to William Lilly, twenty times between 1156 and 1648. He quotes Paracelsus: there is an earthly sun whose heat even the blind can feel, and an eternal sun of wisdom that the spiritually awake can see and others feel through intuition. Some Rosicrucians named the spiritual sun Vulcan, the soular and intellectual suns Christ and Lucifer, and the material sun Jehovah; Lucifer is the intellect without spiritual light, the 'false light', redeemed by the true light of Christ, and the secret transformation of the one into the other is the alchemists' turning of base metal into gold.",
  "modern.hall.8.s4.p2": "Hall quotes Franz Hartmann's definition of the sun in alchemy: the symbol of wisdom and the heart of things; every living being has a centre of life that can grow into a sun, and in the regenerated it illuminates the mind; the earthly sun reflects an invisible heavenly one from which it gets its power. Most ancient religions held the visible sun to be a reflector, not a source; the Norse Frey carries it as a shield. The physical world is a realm of effects, the spiritual world of causes, and the soul world mediates between them, which is why Christ, the higher soul, is called the Mediator, through whom alone one comes to the Father. As the sun is to the solar system, so the spirit is to man, whose organs and faculties are like planets living on its emanations. Man's threefold spirit is shown by a downward triangle and his threefold lower nature, body, emotions and mind, by an upward one; joined they make the six-pointed star, the Star of David or Seal of Solomon, uniting spirit and matter in man, whose animal nature is of the earth, divine nature of heaven and human nature the mediator.",
  "modern.hall.8.s5": "Hall says the Rosicrucians and Illuminati described angels as small suns, centres of energy with streamers of 'Vril' force, which gave rise to the idea of wings: fans of light that move them through the finer worlds. True mystics, he says, deny that angels have human shape, which would be useless in the ether. He argues that objections to life on other planets ignore nature's law of adaptation; the ancients held that life came from the sun, and one idea pictured the planets as embryos joined to the sun by ethereal umbilical cords. He reports that some secret orders taught that the sun is inhabited by beings with bodies of radiant ether, unharmed by its heat, looking like small golden-white suns about the size of a dinner plate, some much larger, with four long, moving streamers of Vril and a rippling pulse. The greatest and brightest is the Archangel Michael, and the whole order of these solar beings is what Christians call archangels or spirits of light.",
  "modern.hall.8.s6": "Hall says gold, the sun's metal, has been called crystallised sunlight, and 'gold' in alchemical texts may mean the metal or the sun itself; sulphur was also linked with the sun for its fieriness. Since gold stood for spirit and base metals for the lower nature, some alchemists were pictured as miners digging for the finer qualities buried in materialism; a diamond in black carbon meant the same, and the Illuminati used a pearl in an oyster on the sea floor, so the seeker became a pearl-fisher diving into the sea of illusion for the 'Pearl of Great Price'. When alchemists said everything contains the seeds of gold, they meant even sand has a spiritual nature. He quotes the Rosicrucian rule that a seed is useless without its proper matrix, and Hartmann's comment that a soul cannot develop without a body. Alchemy did not create gold from nothing but made the seed already present grow; likewise regeneration is not adding something new but unfolding the divinity already in man so that it shines like a sun.",
  "modern.hall.8.s7": "Hall recalls Apuleius' words 'at midnight I saw the sun shining', and says the midnight sun in alchemy meant the spirit shining through the darkness of the body, and the spiritual sun, which a mystic can see at midnight as at noon, since the earth cannot block it. The lights in Egyptian temples at night were said to be its reflections gathered by the priests' magic, and he suggests the light seen deep underground in the Masonic allegory Etidorhpa refers to it. Early ideas of good and evil often came from day and night: medieval black magic was practised at night, and its servants were black magicians, those of the good white. Typhon was part crocodile and part hog for their earthy grossness; creatures of the dark such as cats, bats, toads and owls became linked with witchcraft, and from the belief that black magicians took the form of wolves came werewolf stories. Serpents, living in the earth, were tied to darkness, but since the battle of good and evil turns on the use of generative forces, winged serpents stand for the regenerated animal nature. Egyptian sun rays often end in hands, which Hall links to the Masonic Lion's Paw that raises the dead with its grip.",
  "modern.hall.8.s8": "Hall claims there are really seven primary colours, not three, though the eye can judge only three; a true green exists that is not a mixture, and Helmholtz found that spectral orange passed through a second prism stays orange. Blue, yellow and red stand for consciousness, intelligence and force; blue is soothing, yellow vitalising and red agitating and heating, and he says minerals and plants affect the body according to their colour, a yellow flower acting like yellow light or the note mi. Spirit is blue, mind yellow and body red, so heaven is blue, earth yellow and hell red, the red sphere of passion in the Greek Mysteries; Indian gods such as forms of Vishnu are blue to show their divinity, and esoterically blue is the sun's true colour, which looks orange-yellow only because its rays pass through the illusory world. In early Christian art colours were strictly regulated: white for life, purity and joy; red for Christ's passion, martyrdom and divine love; blue for heaven and contemplation; gold for glory and goodness; green for youth and prosperity; violet for humility and sorrow; black for death and humiliation; saints' robes showed martyrdom or their work. Much meaning has since been lost. Beyond the visible spectrum lie countless colours too high or low to see, which Hall says man will one day explore.",
  "modern.hall.8.fig1": "The caption, for an emblem from Maurice's Indian Antiquities, says the sun rising over a lion's back, or astrologically in the lion's back, has always meant power and rule; a similar emblem appears on the flag of Persia, whose people have always worshipped the sun. Hall notes that kings and emperors often linked their power with the sun's and took the sun or one of its animals as their emblem, such as the lion of the Great Mogul and the eagles of Caesar and Napoleon.",
  "modern.hall.8.fig2": "The caption, for a winged globe from Maurice's Indian Antiquities, says this emblem over the gateways of many Egyptian temples and palaces stands for the three persons of the Egyptian Trinity: the wings, the serpents and the sun disc belong to Ammon, Ra and Osiris.",
  "modern.hall.8.fig3": "The caption, for an illustration from William Lilly's astrological predictions for 1648 to 1650, quotes a letter by Jeremiah Shakerley of Lancashire describing what he saw on 28 February 1648: two mock suns rose with the sun, one on each side about ten degrees away, keeping the same height, with bright rays coming from them and green and red colouring on the sides facing the sun. Above them was a faint rainbow whose ends seemed to touch the mock suns, and higher still, near the zenith, a bright many-coloured rainbow, back to back with the first. They vanished about the time of the full moon, leaving those who saw them terrified and amazed.",
  "modern.hall.8.fig4": "The caption, for a figure from Moor's Hindu Pantheon, quotes Moor's description of a nine-inch cast of the sun god Surya holding the emblems of Vishnu, seated on a seven-headed serpent, his chariot drawn by a seven-headed horse and driven by the legless Arun, personification of the dawn.",
  "modern.hall.8.fig5": "The caption, for a sun face from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says the sun's corona is drawn as a lion's mane, a reminder that the summer solstice once fell in Leo, the celestial lion.",
  "modern.hall.9.p1": "Hall says it is hard today to appreciate how deeply the study of the planets and stars shaped ancient religion, philosophy and science; the Persian Magi were called star gazers, and the Egyptians were famous for reckoning the heavens' influence on nations and individuals. Ancient observatories exist everywhere, often unrecognised by archaeologists. Without telescopes, ancient astronomers made remarkable calculations with instruments of stone, brass and copper, still used in India, such as the huge stone sundials at Jaipur, and the bronze instruments on the wall of Peking, including a lensless sighting tube. The pagans thought the stars were alive and shaped the fates of people and nations, and the Bible shows the early Hebrews thought so too: 'the stars in their courses fought against Sisera.' Chaldeans, Phoenicians, Egyptians, Persians, Hindus and Chinese all had similar zodiacs, and each has been called the cradle of astrology; Central and North American peoples had zodiacs with different signs. The word zodiac comes from Greek for 'circle of animals', a band of stars about sixteen degrees wide round the sky; Robert Hewitt Brown traces it to Egyptian roots meaning 'life' and 'a being'.",
  "modern.hall.9.p2": "Hall says the Greeks divided the zodiac into twelve houses, each sixteen degrees wide and thirty long, through which the sun passes in a year, and drew mostly animal figures in their star groups, which became the signs. He rejects the theory that shepherds invented the figures while watching their flocks, unless the shepherds were the shepherd-priests of old, and argues that the creatures were not taken from the shapes of the star groups but symbolise the quality and strength of the sun's power in each part of the year. He quotes Payne Knight that the star groups look nothing like the animals they are named after, and are only conventional signs marking parts of the sky consecrated to the attributes those animals stood for. Some think the zodiac first had ten houses: there were once a solar year of ten months of thirty-six days plus five days sacred to the gods, and a lunar year of thirteen months of twenty-eight days plus one, and the solar zodiac then had ten houses of thirty-six degrees each.",
  "modern.hall.9.p3": "Hall says the first six signs were thought good because the sun is in them while in the northern half of the sky, matching the 6,000 years in which, for the Persians, Ahura-Mazda ruled in peace; the second six, the winter months of the Greeks, Egyptians and Persians, were thought evil, like the 6,000 years of suffering caused by Ahriman. Those who think the zodiac once had ten signs say Libra, the scales, was added by splitting a single sign Virgo-Scorpio, setting the balance between the rising and falling halves of the year; he cites Hargrave Jennings and quotes Isaac Myer, who thinks the ten signs once formed a huge androgynous man, then became eleven with Virgo and Scorpio separated, then twelve with Libra. He explains the precession of the equinoxes: each year the sun crosses the equator a little behind where it did the year before, losing about one degree every seventy-two years, so that it moves back through one sign in about 2,160 years and through the whole zodiac in about 25,920 years, a Great or Platonic Year, though authorities disagree on the figures.",
  "modern.hall.9.p4": "Hall says the ancients pictured the sun in the form of the constellation in which it stood at the spring equinox: for nearly the last 2,000 years Pisces, the fishes; for 2,160 years before that Aries, the ram; and before that Taurus, the bull, probably given that animal because ploughing with oxen took place when the sun was in that part of the sky. He quotes Albert Pike that in Zoroaster's cave the sun was shown emerging from the back of Taurus, and notes that Taurus contains the Pleiades, the Seven Stars of Masonry at the top of the sacred ladder. In Egypt, during the age of Taurus, the Apis bull was sacred to the sun god, which is the meaning of the saying that the celestial bull 'broke the egg of the year with his horns'. Sampson Arnold Mackey thinks a shift of the earth's poles changed the angle between the equator and the zodiac, which he believes was once at right angles to it, with Cancer at the north pole and Capricorn at the south; Hall suggests the Orphic serpent coiled round an egg may picture this. Mackey cites the Cretan labyrinth, Abraxas and the word abracadabra as evidence.",
  "modern.hall.9.p5": "Hall quotes Mackey, who reads ABRACADABRA as 'the Bull, the only Bull', from Ab'r, bull, and achad, 'the only one', a name of the sun, and says writing the word with one letter fewer on each line until it vanishes records the gradual disappearance of the Bull from the spring equinox; he links it to Serapis and to a three-headed figure wrapped in a serpent of eleven coils, matching the eleven lines of the charm. Hall says almost every religion shows astrological influence: the Old Testament, shaped by Egypt, is full of astronomical allegories, and most Greek and Roman myths can be traced in the stars; some think the twenty-two Hebrew letters came from star groups, with fixed stars as consonants and planets as vowels spelling out future events. The zodiac also explains the seasons, and ancient calendars were based on the equinoxes and solstices: the year began at the spring equinox on 21 March, when the sun crosses the equator northward; the summer solstice, its most northerly point, fell on 21 June, the autumn equinox on 21 September and the winter solstice on 21 December.",
  "modern.hall.9.p6": "Hall says four signs are permanently tied to the equinoxes and solstices; although the signs no longer match the star groups they were named after, astronomers still use them. The spring equinox is said to be in Aries, and fittingly a ram leads the heavenly flock. Godfrey Higgins says this constellation was called the 'Lamb of God', 'Saviour' and 'Lord', who takes away the sins of the world, and was addressed in a litany asking for mercy and peace; so the Lamb of God is a title of the sun reborn each spring in the Ram, though because of the drift it actually rises in Pisces. The summer solstice is said to be in Cancer, which the Egyptians represented by the scarab beetle, sacred to them as a symbol of eternal life; the crab suits this sign because after passing through it the sun 'walks backward' down the zodiac. Cancer is the house of the moon, mother of all things and patron of generation. Hall quotes Payne Knight that the moon, by raising the tides and affecting women's bodies, was seen as queen of moisture and of nourishment and birth, and was symbolised by the sea crab, which can shed a damaged limb and grow a new one.",
  "modern.hall.9.p7": "Hall says the autumn equinox seems to fall in Libra, the scales, when the sun turns toward winter; the scales stand for the power of choice. Long ago, he says, man was like the angels, knowing neither good nor evil, until the gods gave him the seed of mind; from his experience he distils what will restore his lost state with individual intelligence added. He quotes Paracelsus: the body comes from the elements, the soul from the stars and the spirit from God. Capricorn, where the winter solstice theoretically falls, was the House of Death, when life is at its lowest; it is part goat and part fish, and since the sun begins to grow stronger after it, the Greeks said Jupiter was suckled by a goat. He quotes John Cole's different reading: the goat rising from a fish is the city of Babylon rising from its marshes, its two horns Nineveh and Babylon on the Tigris and Euphrates under one ruler. The 2,160-year period in each sign is called an age, named from the sign of the spring equinox, the Taurian, Aryan (Arian), Piscean and Aquarian ages, and in each, worship takes the form of that sign, as a spirit takes a body; the twelve signs are the jewels of the sun's breastplate.",
  "modern.hall.9.p8": "Hall says this explains why religious symbols changed with the ages: in the age of Taurus the sun god took the form of Apis and the bull was sacred to Osiris; in the age of Aries the lamb was sacred, priests were called shepherds, sheep and goats were sacrificed and a scapegoat bore Israel's sins; he refers to Max and Augusta Foss Heindel's The Message of the Stars. In the age of Pisces the fish became the sign of divinity and the sun god fed the multitude with two small fishes; Inman shows Isis with a fish on her head, and Krishna in one incarnation came from a fish's mouth. Jesus is the Fisher of Men, and Hall quotes Lundy and Augustine that the initials of the Greek words for 'Jesus Christ, Son of God, Saviour' spell 'fish', because Christ lived without sin in the depths of mortality as in water. Many Christians eat fish on Friday, the day of Venus, and a fish drawn in the sand once told one Christian that another was near. Aquarius is the water bearer, the man with the water jug in the Gospels, shown as an androgynous angel pouring from an urn, and in the East often as a jar alone; Edward Upham describes it as a blue-yellow pot, house of Saturn. After Herschel discovered Uranus, half of Aquarius was given to it. The water of Aquarius is the 'water of eternal life'. Thus the sun's path governs the form of worship.",
  "modern.hall.9.p9": "Hall says there are two astrological systems. The Ptolemaic is geocentric, with the earth at the centre; it is wrong astronomically but has worked for thousands of years when applied to earthly matters. The heliocentric puts the sun at the centre where it belongs, but is too new to have had its effects catalogued; geocentric astrology deals with earthly nature, heliocentric with the higher mental and spiritual faculties. He claims that when the ancients said the sun was in a sign, they meant it was really in the opposite sign, casting its ray into the one named: 'the sun in Taurus' means the sun is in Scorpio. This produced an outer, geocentric school and an inner, heliocentric one: the crowd worshipped the house of the sun's reflection, the Bull, while the wise honoured its true dwelling, the Scorpion or Serpent, symbol of hidden spiritual mystery. Scorpio has three symbols: the scorpion, the 'backbiter', meaning deceit; the serpent, meaning wisdom; and, rarest, the eagle, the highest form of this sign of occult initiation, rising above the venomous insect. Because Scorpio and Taurus are opposite, their symbols mix; Hall quotes E. M. Plunket that the Scorpion joins Mithras in attacking the Bull, with the genii of the two equinoxes present, one joyful and one mourning.",
  "modern.hall.9.p10": "Hall says the Egyptians, Assyrians and Babylonians, who knew the sun as a bull, called the zodiac a set of furrows through which the heavenly ox dragged the plough of the sun, and the people led garlanded steers through the streets with priests, temple dancers and musicians. The philosophers did not take part but approved of such rites for the mass of people; their deeper understanding was shown by the serpent of Scorpio, the uraeus, on their foreheads. He quotes Robert Hewitt Brown on Leo: at the summer solstice the constellation Leo, thirty degrees ahead of the sun, seems to lead the way and lift the sun with its paw to the top of the heavenly arch, which is why the ancients revered it; astrologers called Leo the sun's only house and said the world was created with the sun in it, the lion was worshipped by Egyptians and Mexicans, and the chief Druid was called a lion. Hall adds that, by the geocentric-heliocentric reversal he has described, the sun will be in Leo when the Aquarian Age is fully established, and then the secret religions will again raise initiates by the Grip of the Lion's Paw, as Lazarus was raised.",
  "modern.hall.9.p11": "Hall says the age of the zodiac is disputed, and it is a great mistake to date it only a few thousand years before Christ: it must go back to when its signs matched the actual constellations and the animals fitted the sun's activity in each month. One author put it at five million years old, and Hall thinks it probably came from Atlantis or Lemuria. Around 10,000 BC, he claims, there was a long era in which knowledge was suppressed and the records of earlier civilisations destroyed, leaving only some copper knives, arrowheads, cave carvings and a few giant monuments such as those on Easter Island. Science counts mankind's age in tens of thousands of years, occultism in tens of millions, and 'Mother Earth has shaken many civilisations from her back'. He says the ancient occultists understood evolution: grains of sand are becoming human in consciousness though not in form, humans are becoming planets, planets solar systems, and solar systems cosmic chains; between solar system and cosmic chain comes the zodiac, so a solar system eventually breaks up into a zodiac whose houses become thrones for twelve heavenly hierarchies, or ten divine orders. Pythagoras held ten the most perfect number and showed it as the tetractys, ten dots in a triangle.",
  "modern.hall.9.p12": "Hall says the early astronomers made the three brightest stars of each constellation joint rulers of its house, divided each house into three decans of ten degrees, and halved these into seventy-two divisions of five degrees; the Hebrews set an angel over each, giving the seventy-two sacred names of the Kabbalah, which correspond to the seventy-two ornaments on the Tabernacle candlestick and the seventy-two elders chosen from the twelve tribes. Gemini is usually two children said to be born from eggs, perhaps those the Bull broke with his horns; the stories of Castor and Pollux and of Romulus and Remus may grow from them, the Arabs used a peacock, two of its stars still bear the names Castor and Pollux, and it was thought the patron of phallic worship, like the two pillars before temples and churches. Sagittarius is a centaur aiming an arrow at the stars: it stands for man's spiritual evolution, the human rising out of the beast, and for aspiration, since everyone aims higher than he can reach. Hall quotes Albert Churchward on the twelvefold division found in the zodiac, the tribes of Israel, the gates of heaven in Revelation, the twelve portals of the Great Pyramid, the apostles and the twelve points of Masonry.",
  "modern.hall.9.p13": "Hall says the ancients took literally the idea that man is made in God's image: the universe is an organism like the human body, every part of it has a counterpart in man, and the law of analogy was the most precious key given to initiates, which made astronomy a sacred science. He says the Pythagoreans were wrongly blamed for teaching transmigration: the popular version was a cover for the truth that the spirit descends from the Milky Way, the seed-ground of souls, through one of the twelve gates of the zodiac, and so is said to be born in the form of that sign's creature, a ram for Aries, a bull for Taurus. Transmigration applies not to the body but to the spirit moving through the forms of the zodiac animals in its evolution. He quotes Julius Firmicus Maternus that, according to Aesculapius and Anubius, the 'birth chart of the world' placed the sun, moon, Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Venus, Mercury and the ascendant each at the fifteenth degree of a sign, Leo, Cancer, Capricorn, Sagittarius, Scorpio, Libra, Virgo and Cancer, and that human destinies are arranged in keeping with it. The seven ages of man are ruled by the moon, Mercury, Venus, the sun, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn, from infancy to decay.",
  "modern.hall.9.fig1": "The caption explains a chart linking the human body with the outer universe. Its decorated border lists animal, mineral and plant substances, and dotted lines connect each to the part of the body it relates to, with words in capitals naming the organ, limb or disease concerned. Zodiac signs, each divided by crosses into three decans, show the best times of year, and planetary signs on either side of the figure add to this.",
  "modern.hall.9.fig2": "The caption to a diagram says the plane of the zodiac crosses the celestial equator at an angle of about 23 degrees 28 minutes, and the two points where they cross, marked A and B, are called the equinoxes.",
  "modern.hall.9.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from the Margarita Philosophica, says the pagans thought of the zodiac as the body of the Grand Man of the universe, the Macrocosm, divided into twelve parts each ruled by one constellation. Believing the whole universe is summed up in man, the Microcosm, they assigned a sign to each of twelve parts of the human body, giving the familiar 'cut-up man in the almanac'.",
  "modern.hall.9.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from John Cole's treatise, says the oldest known circular zodiac is the one from Tentyra (Dendera) in Egypt, now owned by the French government. Cole describes it: a medallion four feet nine inches across in French measure, inside a larger circle of hieroglyphs within a square with sides of seven feet nine inches; the constellations, mixed with others, run in a spiral beginning with Leo, who tramples a serpent while a woman holds his tail, followed by Virgo with an ear of corn, the scales with Harpocrates above them in a medallion, Scorpio, a winged two-faced Sagittarius, Capricorn, Aquarius, Pisces, Aries, Taurus, Gemini, and ending with Cancer.",
  "modern.hall.9.fig5": "The caption explains a diagram called the Hieroglyphic Plan of the Ancient Zodiac, attributed to Hermes. In the centre is the sign of Hemphta, a three-formed god of all shapes. Around it six rings show, from the inside out: the numbers of the zodiac houses in figures and words; their modern names; the Greek or Egyptian names of the Egyptian gods assigned to them; full figures of these gods; the ancient or modern zodiac signs, sometimes both; and the number of decans, or subdivisions, in each house.",
  "modern.hall.10.p1": "Hall quotes a manuscript of Thomas Taylor saying that Plato was initiated into the Greater Mysteries at forty-nine in an underground hall of the Great Pyramid, with the Isiac Table as the altar; after three days he was received by the hierophant, given the highest teachings with their symbols, and after three months sent out to do the order's work like Pythagoras and Orpheus. Hall then traces the table's known history. It is first mentioned at the sack of Rome in 1527, when a locksmith sold it to Cardinal Bembo; after Bembo's death in 1547 it went to the house of Mantua until Ferdinand II's troops took the city in 1630. It was not melted down for its silver, as some claimed, but passed through Cardinal Pava to the Duke of Savoy and the King of Sardinia, was taken to Paris by the French in 1797, shown at the Bibliotheque Nationale according to Lenoir in 1809, and returned to Italy; Baedeker places it in the Museum of Antiquities at Turin. Aeneas Vicus of Parma engraved a copy in 1559. Kircher gives its size as five palms by four, Westcott as 50 by 30 inches; it is bronze with enamel and silver inlay, the figures outlined in silver, and the silver bases have been torn away.",
  "modern.hall.10.p2": "Hall says those who know Hermetic philosophy will see in the Isiac Table the key to Chaldean, Egyptian and Greek theology. Montfaucon admitted he could not understand it, doubted it meant anything, and mocked Kircher as more obscure than the table; Pignorius reproduced it in 1605 with timid explanations that showed his ignorance. Kircher, in his Oedipus Aegyptiacus of 1654, with years of study and a team of scholars, went far toward explaining it, but, as Eliphas Levi noted, its master secret escaped him. Hall quotes Levi: Kircher guessed it held the key to sacred alphabets but could not explain it; it has three panels, the twelve houses of heaven above, the year's labours below, and in the middle twenty-one sacred signs matching the letters of the alphabet; in the centre sits the all-formed Iynx, emblem of universal being, like the Hebrew Yod from which all letters came, surrounded by the Ophite triad, the three mother letters, with other triads on either side standing for active and passive, fire and water; these make three groups of seven, the number of the three worlds and of the primitive letters, plus one extra sign like zero.",
  "modern.hall.10.p3": "Hall suggests that Levi's hint means the twenty-one central figures are the twenty-one major Tarot trumps. If so, he asks, is the disputed zero card the nameless crown of the Supreme Mind, symbolised by a hidden triad above the central throne, and is the first emanation of that Mind the juggler or magician with the rod, sword, cup and coin of the four lower worlds on his table? Then the zero card belongs nowhere among the others but is the point from which they all come, broken up into the twenty-one cards, or letters, which together make the zero; the circle on that card, the emblem of the higher sphere, supports this. He says Westcott gathered the scanty theories in a rare book of 1887, the only detailed English account since Humphreys translated Montfaucon's 'worthless' description in 1721, and quotes his summary of Levi's diagram: the upper region holds the four seasons with three signs each and the four letters of the divine name assigned to Aquarius, Taurus, Leo and Typhon, matching the four cherubic forms, with the fourth shown as Scorpion or Eagle according to good or evil intent; the lower region holds the twelve simple Hebrew letters and the four quarters, as in the Sepher Yetzirah.",
  "modern.hall.10.p4": "Hall continues Westcott's account: the central region belongs to the sun and planets, with the sun, marked Ops, above a Solomon's seal and cross; one triad receives Venus, Mercury and Mars round an upright triangle for fire, the other Saturn, Moon and Jupiter round an inverted triangle for water, the female principle; the old planet signs are built from cross, disc and crescent, 'deep mysteries'; Levi swapped Serapis and Hecate in his plate; the seven double letters belong to the planets, and the three mother letters for air, water and fire are pictured round the central Iynx by two serpents and a sphinx; Ops is the earth goddess Cybele, crowned with towers and holding a key. Hall says Lenoir's 1809 essay, which treats the table as a calendar, gives little real information, and that since all later writers depend on Kircher he has had Kircher's eighty pages of Latin translated; the plate is Kircher's reproduction, and his letters and numbers are used in what follows. A. E. Waite, echoing Wilkinson, called the table very late and a forgery, while Winckelmann defended its antiquity. Hall concludes that whoever made it, Egyptian or not, was an initiate of the highest order who knew the deepest Hermetic teachings.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p1": "Hall says his explanation of the table digests Kircher, supplemented from Chaldean, Hebrew, Egyptian and Greek mystical writings. Egyptian temples were designed so that rooms, decoration and utensils were all symbolic: beside the central altar was a cistern of Nile water fed by hidden pipes, with images of the gods in order and magical inscriptions, and here neophytes were taught the priests' secrets. The Table of Isis was originally an altar or table dedicated to Isis. Tables for the gods were made of materials matching their rank, gold for Jupiter and Apollo, silver for Diana, Venus and Juno, marble for other high gods, wood for lesser ones, or of the metal of the god's planet, and as food is laid on a banquet table, so these altars held symbols that feed man's invisible nature. He quotes Kircher's summary: the table teaches the threefold world, archetypal, intellectual and sensible; it shows the Supreme Divinity, the Father Mind, as a threefold symbol moving from centre to circumference and animating all things, with three triads called the Foundation; above are the Governors of the worlds with their fiery, ethereal and material emblems; below the Fathers of Fountains, who preserve the principles of things and the laws of nature; and also the gods of the spheres and the wandering powers, of both sexes, facing their superior deity.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p2": "Hall suggests the table's three horizontal panels may show the ground plan of the rooms where the Isiac Mysteries were given: the middle panel has seven compartments and the lower one a gate at each end. There are forty-five main figures in fifteen triads, four in the upper panel, seven in the middle and four in the lower, and he lists them by Kircher's letters and names: the Mendesian, Ammonian, Momphtaean and Omphtaean triads above; the Isiac, Hecatine, Ibimorphous, Ophionic, Nephtaean, Serapaean and Osirian in the middle; and the Horaean, Pandochaean, Thaustic and Aeluristic below. Kircher says the figures differ in eight ways: form, position, gesture, action, dress, headdress, staff and the hieroglyphs around them; Hall links these to eight spiritual senses and to the Buddhists' eight-spoked wheel and eightfold path. The decorated border of birds, animals, reptiles, people and composite creatures may stand for the four elements and their spirits, or for the archetypal spheres holding the patterns of future forms. The four flowers at the corners, which turn to follow the sun, stand for the part of man that delights in facing its Creator.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p3": "Hall says the Chaldean secret doctrine divides the universe into four planes, archetypal, intellectual, sidereal and elemental, each revealing the others, the higher ruling the lower. The archetypal plane is the mind of the triune God, in which everything that is, was or will be exists as divine thought-forms, shown on the table by a chain of hidden likenesses. In the middle of the table is the personified spiritual essence containing all forms, from which come the lower worlds as nine emanations in three triads, like the nine Sephiroth issuing from Kether, the Crown. The twelve Governors of the upper panel distribute creative influence under the direction of the archetypes, and the Father Fountains of the lower panel, keepers of the gates of the universe, pass these influences down to the lower worlds. For the Egyptians, he says, goodness comes first and is the cause of causes; spreading itself, it exists in everything. The table shows that all is in God and God in all, that the invisible is revealed by its works, and so the Egyptians made images of earthly things to stand for invisible powers, showing that this world is the shadow of God and the outward picture of the paradise within.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p4": "Hall says the creative Mind poured out its powers from highest to lowest, first through its Paternal Foundation and then through lesser gods called Intelligences; in their phallic symbolism the Egyptians used the seed for the spiritual spheres, since each contains all that comes from it. Everything that results dwells in its cause and turns toward it, as the lotus to the sun. So the Supreme Intellect first made light, the angelic world; from it the invisible hierarchies called stars; and from the stars the four elements and the sensible world. All are in all after their kind: bodies in the stars and stars in bodies, stars in angels and angels in stars, angels in God and God in all; as the seed is the tree folded up, the world is God unfolded. He quotes Proclus that every divine property pervades all creation. The Supreme Mind gives every creature the power to reproduce after its kind; this one power appears as existence in minerals, vitality in plants, sensation in animals, motion in the heavens, thought in human souls, intelligence in angels and superessence in God, so that all forms are of one substance and all life of one force.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p5": "Hall says Plato first taught this doctrine and quotes a passage he attributes to Aristotle: if this sensible world, an image of another, is alive, the other is far more so; beyond the stars are other heavens, brighter and vaster, not separated because they are bodiless; there is an earth there too, full of life, with plants, gardens, flowing water and nobler animals, and air and life, all immortal, unchanging and intellectual; these things exist without being externalised, suffer no decay, are full of energy and joy, and keep their own character without mixing or multiplying as bodies do. In the middle of the table sits a female figure on a covered throne, Isis, here called the Pantomorphic Iynx, which Mead defines as 'a transmitting intelligence' and others as universal being. Above her is a triple crown for the triune Supreme Mind, which the Zohar calls hidden and unrevealed, and below her the house of material substance. Hall compares the Kabbalistic tree, whose upper three Sephiroth, Crown, Wisdom and Understanding, are beyond comprehension while the lower seven can be grasped; the seven triads of the middle panel stand for these lower seven, all flowing from the hidden crown.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p6": "Hall quotes Kircher: the throne shows the threefold Supreme Mind spreading through the three worlds, from which comes the sensible universe that Plutarch calls the 'House of Horns' and the Egyptians the 'Great Gate of the Gods'; the top of the throne is surrounded by serpent-like flames, showing the Mind full of eternal light and life, untouched by matter, and the divine fire passes to the lower spheres through Nature, the World Virgin Isis, here called the Iynx, the many-formed universal Idea. Hall explains 'Idea' in Plato's sense, quoting Hamilton: eternal, immaterial forms of all things, the patterns by which God made the visible world. Kircher then describes the twenty-one central figures as seven triads for seven higher worlds: the Ophionic or Iynx triad, the fiery Aetherium; the Ibimorphous triad, of moisture; and the Nephtaean triad, of fertility, these three being the ethereal worlds of the Father Foundation; then the sidereal triads of Osiris and Isis, sun and moon, marked by two bulls; and the Hecatine and Serapaean triads of the sublunary and underground worlds. He cites Psellus quoting Zoroaster that the Egyptians and Chaldeans taught seven corporeal worlds, one of fire, three ethereal and three material, the last, below the moon, being earthly and hostile to light; with the invisible crown these make eight.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p7": "Hall continues quoting Kircher: Plato says a philosopher must know how the Egyptians arranged the seven circles below the first; the first triad is fire and life, the second water under the Ibimorphous gods, the third air under Nephthys; from fire came the heavens, from water the earth, and air mediates. The Sepher Yetzirah says the three produce the seven, the six directions and the holy temple in the centre, which Kircher identifies with the throne of the Spirit of Nature in the middle of the table. Psellus says the Egyptians worshipped the triad of faith, truth and love and seven fountains, which Kircher matches to the sun, moon, Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, Venus and Mercury, all shown in the middle panel. Hall then explains the upper panel as the twelve signs in four triads. The middle figure of each is a fixed sign, Aquarius, Taurus, Leo and Scorpio, called the Fathers, the man, bull, lion and eagle, known in the Far East as the four Maharajas at the corners of creation; the cardinal signs, Capricorn, Aries, Cancer and Libra, are the Powers; and the common signs, Pisces, Gemini, Virgo and Sagittarius, the Minds of the Four Lords. This explains the Egyptian winged globe: the four fixed signs, Ezekiel's cherubim, are globes and the signs on either side their wings.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p8": "Hall says the Egyptians also showed each heavenly triad as a globe (the Father) with a serpent (the Mind) and wings (the Power). These twelve forces build the world, and from them come the twelve sacred animals, which are the twelve parts of the world and of the human body. He suggests the twelve upper figures may stand for twelve convolutions of the brain and the twelve lower ones for the twelve zodiacal organs and limbs, which the powers in the brain govern. A deeper reading concerns two zodiacs, the fixed and the movable. The fixed zodiac is a vast dodecahedron whose twelve faces are the outer walls of space; from each face a spiritual power radiates inward and becomes one of the hierarchies of the movable zodiac, the band of so-called fixed stars, within which lie the planets and elements. Hall compares this to breathing: the fixed zodiac is the atmosphere, the movable zodiac the lungs, and the worlds below the body; the cosmic lungs breathe in the divine energies and distribute them through the twelve sacred animals, then breathe out the poisons of the lower worlds to be purified by the twelve eternal hierarchies.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p9": "Hall says the table can be read in many ways. If the border is the spiritual source, the central throne is the physical body and the whole table the human aura, with the border as the shell of the auric egg. If the throne is the spiritual sphere, the border is the elements and the panels the worlds flowing from the divine source. Physically, the throne is the generative system and the table shows embryology applied to the making of worlds; anatomically, the throne is the heart, the Ibimorphous triad the mind, the Nephtaean triad the generative system, and the surrounding signs the body's parts. In terms of evolution the central gate is both entrance and exit, and the table shows initiation, in which the candidate finally comes before his own soul, which only he can unveil. In cosmology the middle panel is the spiritual worlds, the upper the intellectual and the lower the material; the middle may also be the nine invisible worlds, with the creature marked T as the physical nature, the footstool of Isis. Alchemically, the throne holds the Universal Mercury, its flaming canopy the divine Sulphur and the cube below the Salt. The three central triads are the three invisible parts of man, the 'Silent Watchers', and the panels on either side his fourfold lower nature; the twenty-one central figures belong to the sun, three powers with seven attributes, and reduce to three.",
  "modern.hall.10.s1.p10": "Hall predicts that the table will be shown to be connected with Egyptian Gnosticism, since a Gnostic papyrus in the Bodleian Library mentions twelve Fathers with twelve Fountains beneath them. The lower panel's two gates, east and west, show it is the underworld, through which, in Chaldean theology, the sun passes at night. Since Plato studied for thirteen years with Egyptian Magi, his philosophy is full of their triads, and Hall calls the table a diagram of Platonic cosmology, best interpreted through Proclus' commentary on Plato's theology and the Chaldean Oracles. He mentions Hesiod's Theogony and the Orphic world egg from which Phanes sprang, which Taylor equates with Plato's mixture of limit and the unlimited and which is the symbol of the Demiurge, whose aura is the egg of the lower universe. Eusebius, citing Porphyry, says the Egyptians worshipped the Creator as Cneph, a dark-blue man with a girdle, sceptre and plume, an egg coming from his mouth. Hall says the rectangular table philosophically represents the Orphic egg, and that breaking this egg, the supreme achievement, equals the Eastern Nirvana. He notes Samuel Boyse's plates add nothing, and quotes the Abbe Banier's opinion that the table was a votive offering to Isis for some favour received.",
  "modern.hall.10.fig1": "The caption to Levi's key to the table quotes Westcott's summary of Levi: the Isiac Table is a key to the Book of Thoth, which survives in the Tarot cards. The Book of Thoth summed up Egyptian esoteric learning, which after Egypt's decline was preserved in pictures as the Tarot; once misunderstood, the cards passed to fortune-tellers and card players. The modern pack has 78 cards: 22 picture trumps and 56 others in four suits of ten numbers and four court cards, swords, cups, wands and coins, matching spades, hearts, clubs and diamonds and said to stand for the military, the priesthood, agriculture and commerce. The 22 trumps descend from the hieroglyphs and correspond to the letters of the Hebrew alphabet, divided into three mothers, seven doubles and twelve simple letters, or three groups of seven plus one apart.",
  "modern.hall.10.fig2": "The caption quotes Kircher on the magical purpose of the table. Egyptian priests believed a great spiritual power was called down by complete, exact rites, and that, as Iamblichus says, one missing detail spoiled everything; so they kept manuals, and the table showed initiates the nature of the gods and the right forms of sacrifice, since each order of gods had its own symbols, gestures and dress. Their temples pictured the universe: the floor showed the physical world with stones and little streams, the walls the stars, the dome the world of spirits, and the central altar the outflowing of the Supreme Mind. Priests dressed like the gods, partly bare, chaste, shaven, veiled and wearing the gods' emblems, so as to become one with the intelligence they sought; standing before the central figure with its symbols and singing hymns, they believed they drew the god's attention. Kircher compares this to a plucked string making nearby strings sound: their acts came into tune with the divine Idea, which returned to them as prophecy, since in the Supreme Mind the future is present. Most of the table's figures are amulets; the priests believed they could cure illness, receive healing dreams, and get answers from the gods by signs while asleep or awake.",
  "modern.hall.10.fig3": "The caption to Westcott's key says Zoroaster declared that the number three shines throughout the world, and the table shows this through its series of creative triads. Hall quotes Alexandre Lenoir: as art the table is cold and lifeless, its figures sketchy and mechanically arranged, but once its purpose is understood it is seen to be an image of the heavens divided into parts for teaching, probably the introduction to a collection used in the Mysteries of Isis, engraved on copper for the initiation ceremony.",
  "modern.hall.11.p1": "Hall says the Egyptians, Greeks and Romans often sealed lighted lamps in tombs as offerings to the god of death, perhaps to light the dead through the underworld; later clay models were buried too. Some lamps were sealed in protective vessels, and oil has reportedly been found in them unchanged after 2,000 years; many were lit when the tombs were closed, and it is claimed some were still burning when opened centuries later. Medieval writers argued over whether a fuel could renew itself as fast as it burned, and Hall thinks it possible the priest-chemists made lamps that burned for very long periods. Westcott counts over 150 writers on the subject and Blavatsky 173; most admit the lamps existed, a few say they burned for ever, many that they might last centuries, some that they were priestly tricks, and many that the Devil made them to deceive. Kircher, Hall says, contradicts himself: in Oedipus Aegyptiacus he says many such lamps in pagan tombs were the Devil's work, set there to win belief for false worship.",
  "modern.hall.11.p2": "Hall says Kircher, after attributing the lamps to the Devil, also called them impossible, like perpetual motion and the Philosopher's Stone, and then offered a different solution: Egypt has asphalt and petroleum, so the priests may have piped oil secretly to lamps with asbestos wicks, which would burn for ever. Montfaucon agrees that the temple lamps were mechanical tricks, and says the tomb stories arose because smoke-like fumes came out of newly opened vaults, and people who later found lamps on the floor assumed they were the source. Hall then gives examples. In a tomb on the Appian Way, opened under Pope Paul III, a lamp was said to have burned for nearly 1,600 years; a contemporary account says the body of a beautiful golden-haired girl lay floating in an unknown clear liquid, perfectly preserved, and the lamp went out in the draught when the door was opened and could not be relit. Kircher gives an epitaph, 'To Tulliola my daughter', which Montfaucon says never existed, though the body was generally thought to be Cicero's daughter Tulliola.",
  "modern.hall.11.p3": "Hall says perpetual lamps have been reported from the Mediterranean, India, Tibet, China and South America, and lists examples. Plutarch mentions a lamp over the door of the temple of Jupiter Ammon that the priests said had burned for centuries without fuel. Augustine describes one in an Egyptian temple of Venus that wind and water could not put out, which he thought the Devil's work. Under Justinian a lamp found in a niche over the gate of Edessa or Antioch was dated over 500 years old, and was destroyed by soldiers. In the early Middle Ages a lamp burning since the third century was found in England, in what was thought to be the tomb of Constantine's father. The Lantern of Pallas, found near Rome in 1401 in the tomb of Pallas, son of Evander, had supposedly burned for 2,000 years. In 1550 a vault on the island of Nesis near Naples held a lamp lit before Christ. Pausanias describes a golden lamp in Minerva's temple that burned a year without refilling. The Fama Fraternitatis says Christian Rosenkreutz's tomb, opened 120 years after his death, was lit by a perpetual lamp. And Numa Pompilius kept a perpetual light in a temple built for an elemental being.",
  "modern.hall.11.p4": "Hall tells of an English tomb, thought to be a Rosicrucian's, in which a countryman found a lamp burning; as he stepped on certain floor stones, an armoured figure rose and smashed the lamp with an iron club, so its fuel was never discovered. Lights are also said to have been found in sealed chambers near Memphis and in Brahmin temples, going out and their fuel evaporating when exposed to air. The wicks are now thought to have been asbestos, which alchemists called salamander's wool, and the fuel a product of alchemy; Kircher spent two years trying in vain to extract an everlasting oil from asbestos. Several fuel recipes survive, and Hall reprints one that Blavatsky took from Trithemius and Korndorf: sulphur and burnt alum are sublimed into flowers, mixed with Venetian borax, and repeatedly digested in strong spirit of wine until the sulphur melts like wax without smoke; asbestos threads are soaked in this sulphur in a glass set in hot sand for twenty-four hours; the wick is placed in a shell-shaped glass with part above the sulphur, which is melted onto it, and once lit it is said to burn for ever.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p1": "Hall says the worship of Apollo included oracles through which the gods spoke and revealed the future, and early Greek history is full of talking trees, rivers, statues and caves inhabited by nymphs or daemons. Christian writers said the Devil spoke through oracles but could not deny oracles as such, since their own scriptures contain them: if the high priest's onyx stones revealed God's will by flashing, a dove might speak at Ammon's temple, and if the witch of Endor could call up Samuel for Saul, a priestess of Apollo might call up her god. The most famous oracles were Delphi, Dodona, Trophonius and Latona, with Dodona's talking oaks the oldest, and many oracle caves were sacred before Greek culture arose. Wilder derives Delphi from delphos, womb, from the shape of the cave; it was first called Pytho after the serpent Python, born from the slime of the flood that left only Deucalion and Pyrrha, which Apollo killed and threw into the cleft, thereafter giving oracles there, sharing Delphi with Dionysus. Python's spirit stayed as Apollo's agent, and the fumes, said to come from its rotting body, put the priestess, the Pythia, in touch with the god. The Greeks called Delphi the navel of the earth, showing they saw the planet as a great being; Hall says the link between oracles and the navel is a Mystery secret.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p2": "Hall says the oracle is older than this story, which the priests probably invented for the curious; some think a Hyperborean priest found the cleft, but it was sacred as far back as records go, and people came from all over Greece to consult the daemon in its vent. He tells the discovery legend: shepherds on Parnassus saw their goats leaping and crying strangely near a chasm; one went to look, breathed the fumes, and began to dance, sing, babble and foretell the future, and others did the same, so the place became famous. Some, seized with a madman's strength, broke free and leapt into the cleft, so a wall was built and a prophetess appointed as go-between. Later a golden tripod carved with Apollo as the serpent Python was set over the cleft, with a seat designed so that she would not fall off while entranced. Hall adds that the story that the fumes came from Python's body was circulating just before then, and suggests the oracle may have revealed its own origin.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p3": "Hall says that for centuries virgins, called Phoebades or Pythiae, served the oracle, probably because women's sensitive natures responded more fully to 'the fumes of enthusiasm'. For three days before a consultation the priestess purified herself: she bathed in the Castalian spring, fasted, drank only from the spring of Cassotis piped into the temple, and chewed bay leaves before mounting the tripod. Some say the water was drugged, or that the priests piped an intoxicating gas into the shaft, but neither theory is proved or explains the accuracy of the predictions. Robed and seated over the fumes, she gradually changed as if another spirit had entered her: she struggled, tore her clothes and cried out, then grew calm and majestic and, rigid and staring, spoke the prophecy, usually in hexameter verse, often ambiguous or unintelligible. Five holy men, the Hosii, appointed for life from the descendants of Deucalion, recorded every sound and movement. Then she struggled again, the spirit left her, and she was carried to rest until the ecstasy passed.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p4": "Hall quotes Iamblichus: whether the Delphic prophetess prophesies through a fiery spirit rising from the cave or seated on the tripod, she gives herself wholly to the divine spirit and is lit by divine fire; the fire surrounds her and fills her with splendour, the seat attunes her to the god's prophetic power, and she is possessed by the god, who is distinct from the fire, the spirit, the seat and all the visible apparatus. Hall tells how Apollonius of Tyana visited Delphi with his disciple Damis, crowned with laurel, went behind Apollo's statue into the cave, and asked whether he would be remembered; the priestess said yes, but always slandered. He left angry, but Hall says she was right, since the Church Fathers made him an Antichrist. The priestess's words went to the oracle's philosophers to interpret and then to poets who put them into verse for the public. Serpents were prominent: the tripod's base was three twisted snakes, and some say the priestess was made to gaze into a serpent's eyes until, hypnotised, she spoke with the god's voice.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p5": "Hall says the early Pythian priestesses were young maidens, but a later law required women over fifty, who dressed as girls and went through the same rites, probably because priestesses had been assaulted. At first the god spoke only on Apollo's seventh birthday, but demand forced monthly sessions, with dates and questions chosen by lot or by vote of the Delphians. The oracle's influence on Greek culture was generally good: he quotes James Gardner that it exposed tyrants and foretold their fates, saved and guided many, encouraged useful institutions and discoveries, and supported virtue and civil liberty. At Dodona, Jupiter spoke through oak trees, birds and brass vessels, and many have compared its rites to those of the Druids. Its famous dove, perched in the sacred oaks, spoke Greek on philosophy and religion and answered visitors' questions. The priests, after solemn purification, went into the grove and asked the trees, which answered in human voices; some say only one oak or beech in the centre spoke, which is why Jupiter was called Phegonaeus, dweller in the beech.",
  "modern.hall.11.s1.p6": "Hall describes the 'talking' brass vessels of Dodona, which rang for hours when struck; some say a row of them echoed one another into a din, others that a single vessel on a pillar was struck by metal-tipped cords hanging from a whip held by a statue of a child, blown by the wind, and the priests read the strikes and echoes as oracles. When the original priests, the Selloi, disappeared, three priestesses served for centuries, reading the vessels and consulting the trees at midnight; visitors brought offerings. The Cave of Trophonius had an opening so small it seemed impossible to enter: after offerings and robing, the consultant sat at the edge holding a honey cake and put his feet in, and his whole body was suddenly drawn into a space the size of an oven; afterwards he was thrown out feet first, usually delirious. He first drank from two nearby springs, one of forgetfulness of sorrow and one of Mnemosyne, memory, so he could later recall what he experienced. The cave, marked by two brass obelisks and walled with white stones in a sacred grove, looked unimpressive, but visitors had to record their experiences at the temple. The prophecies came as dreams and visions with severe headaches, some never fully recovering; Hall thinks the priests may have used an unknown herb, but calls their interpretation almost supernatural. A ram was sacrificed first, and the priest judged by its entrails whether the time was right.",
  "modern.hall.11.s2.p1": "Hall says many ancient sculptors and architects were initiates of the Mysteries, especially the Eleusinian, and that stoneworkers and woodworkers have always formed a divinely guided caste. Their buildings and statues show knowledge of mathematics, astronomy and anatomy that no one today equals, proving they were masters steeped in the Mysteries; thus arose the Guild of Builders, ancestors of the Freemasons. When they built palaces, temples or tombs, or carved statues, they hid the secret doctrine in their work, and Hall says they were worthy of a Master Mason's wages. He claims the Seven Wonders of the World, though built for various apparent reasons, were monuments to the secrets of the Mysteries, symbolic structures placed in significant spots whose purpose only initiates can sense. Eliphas Levi noted their correspondence with the seven planets: they were built by 'Widow's sons' in honour of the seven planetary spirits, and their symbolism is the same as that of the seven seals and seven churches of Revelation.",
  "modern.hall.11.s2.p2": "Hall describes the Wonders. The Colossus of Rhodes, a brass statue about 109 feet high that took over twelve years to make, was by the initiate Chares of Lindus; the idea that it straddled the harbour with ships sailing between its legs has never been proved. It stood only fifty-six years before an earthquake threw it down in 224 BC, and its pieces lay for over 900 years until sold to a Jewish merchant who carried the metal off on 700 camels. With its crown of rays and raised torch it stood for the Sun Man of the Mysteries, the universal saviour. The temple of Diana at Ephesus, south of Smyrna, planned in the fifth century BC by the architect Hall calls Ctesiphon for the Ionian cities, was of marble with 127 columns, each 60 feet high and over 150 tons. Hall says it was destroyed by black magic around 356 BC, with the madman Herostratus as the tool blamed; it was rebuilt without its symbolism, and the original, a model of the universe, was dedicated to the moon, symbol of generation. Phidias, exiled from Athens, made the colossal Zeus at Olympia, known only from a few coins: ivory flesh, robes of beaten gold, Victory on a globe in one hand and an eagle sceptre in the other, a bearded head with an olive wreath, on an ornate throne, dedicated to the planet Jupiter.",
  "modern.hall.11.s2.p3": "Hall says Eliphas Levi puts Solomon's Temple among the Wonders in place of the Pharos of Alexandria, built by Sostratus of Cnidus under Ptolemy (283 to 247 BC): white marble, said to be over 600 feet high, costing nearly a million dollars, with fires on top visible far out to sea; it fell in a thirteenth-century earthquake but remains were seen until 1350, and as the tallest it was assigned to Saturn. The Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, built by Queen Artemisia for King Mausolus, designed by Satyrus and Pythis and decorated by four great sculptors, was 114 by 92 feet, in five sections (the senses) topped by a 24-step pyramid (man's spiritual nature) with a statue of Mausolus in a chariot, 9 feet 9 and a half inches tall; destroyed by earthquake and never satisfactorily reconstructed, it was sacred to Mars. The Hanging Gardens of Babylon, in Nebuchadnezzar's palace near the Euphrates, rose in terraces with a reservoir on top, built about 600 BC by an unknown designer, and stood for the planes of the invisible world, sacred to Venus. The Great Pyramid, supreme temple of the Mysteries, must, by its astronomy, be about 70,000 years old; it was the tomb of Osiris, said to be built by the gods, perhaps designed by Hermes, and is the monument of Mercury, symbol of wisdom and letters.",
  "modern.hall.11.fig1": "The caption, for a carving from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says the coiled bodies of these three serpents formed the base of a tripod and their three heads held up its three feet. Hall adds that nothing reliable is known about the shape and size of the famous Delphic tripod; theories about it rest mostly on small ornamental tripods found in various temples.",
  "modern.hall.11.fig2": "The caption, for a reconstruction from Beaumont's Gleanings of Antiquities, says Beaumont thought this the most authentic surviving form of the Delphic tripod, though it must have changed over the oracle's long life. He divided it into four parts: a three-legged frame, a resonating bowl set in it, a flat plate on which the Pythia sat, and a cone-shaped cover that hid her completely, so that her voice came out hollow and strange. Some have compared it to the Ark of the Covenant: the frame to the Ark, the plate to the Mercy Seat and the cover to the Tabernacle tent. Hall says this differs from the popular picture but shows a valuable likeness between Jewish and Greek symbolism.",
  "modern.hall.11.fig3": "The caption, for a figure from a book on prophetic gods, says Apollo, twin of Diana and son of Jupiter and Latona, was born fully grown and was held to be the first physician, the inventor of music and song, and the father of archery. His temple at Delphi was rebuilt five times: first of laurel branches, then something similar, then of brass, and lastly probably twice in marble. At its height no Greek oracle matched Delphi's splendour, with statues of solid gold and silver and precious offerings from kings and princes who came from all over the known world.",
  "modern.hall.11.fig4": "The caption says Jupiter was called Dodonean after Dodona in Epirus, near which an oak-covered hill had been sacred to him from ancient times; dryads, fauns, satyrs and nymphs were thought to live in the grove, and chains of small bronze bells hung from the trees, ringing in the wind. Some say the talking dove of Dodona was really a woman, since in Thessaly prophetesses and doves were both called Peleiades. The first temple there was supposedly built by Deucalion and the survivors of the flood, which is why Dodona was thought the oldest oracle in Greece.",
  "modern.hall.11.fig5": "The caption tells the story of Trophonius and his brother Agamedes, famous architects who left a loose stone in a treasury they built so they could rob it. The owner set a trap and caught Agamedes; Trophonius cut off his brother's head to prevent discovery and fled to the grove of Lebadea, where the earth swallowed him, and his spirit gave oracles there afterwards. His name is said to mean 'to be agitated'; visitors to his caves were said never to smile again. Bees appear with him because they led the first envoys from Boeotia to the site. The picture is said to copy a statue of him set on the hill above the oracle and surrounded by sharp stakes so no one could touch it.",
  "modern.hall.12.p1": "Hall tells how Mnesarchus, a merchant, and his wife Parthenis consulted the Delphic oracle about their voyage home; instead the priestess told them she was pregnant with a son who would surpass all men in beauty and wisdom and greatly benefit mankind. Mnesarchus renamed his wife Pythasis after the priestess, and when the boy was born at Sidon they named him Pythagoras. Legends say he was a god in human form, and he is among the ancient sages credited with an immaculate conception. Hall quotes Godfrey Higgins' comparison with Jesus: both born in Syria (Sidon and Bethlehem), both fathers told of a son who would benefit mankind, both born while their parents were travelling; the mother of Pythagoras was visited by an apparition of Apollo, which told her husband to abstain from her during the pregnancy, like the story of Joseph and Mary; so Pythagoras too was called the son of God and thought divinely inspired.",
  "modern.hall.12.p2": "Hall says Pythagoras was born between 600 and 590 BC and lived nearly a hundred years. He knew Eastern and Western esoteric teaching, learned the secret traditions of Moses from the rabbis, and, Hall says, the Essenes later existed largely to interpret his symbols; he was initiated into the Egyptian, Babylonian and Chaldean Mysteries, and may have studied under a Zoroaster, though probably not the one the Parsees revere. He quotes Frank C. Higgins: after learning all the Greeks could teach and probably being initiated at Eleusis, he was at last initiated into the Mysteries of Isis at Thebes, then into those of Adonis in Phoenicia and Syria, studied with the Chaldeans near Babylon, and spent years with the Brahmins of Elephanta and Ellora, whose records still name him Yavancharya, the Ionian Teacher. Pythagoras coined the word 'philosopher', modestly calling himself one who is trying to find out rather than a sage who knows. Returning, he founded a school at Croton in southern Italy; at first distrusted, he was soon consulted by leading men, and taught a few disciples the secret wisdom along with occult mathematics, music and astronomy, the triangular foundation of all arts and sciences.",
  "modern.hall.12.p3": "Hall says that at about sixty Pythagoras married a disciple and had seven children; his wife inspired him and spread his teachings after his death. His frankness made enemies, and a man refused initiation turned the people against him; a mob burned the school's buildings and killed him. Accounts differ: some say he died with his disciples, others that he escaped and was burned in a house where he sheltered, or that his disciples threw themselves into the flames to make a bridge for him and he later died of a broken heart at the apparent failure of his work. His surviving followers were persecuted, and little remains. They never called him by name but 'the Master' or 'That Man', perhaps because the letters of his name had sacred meaning; Hall cites an article by T. R. Prater claiming he initiated candidates by a formula hidden in his name. His school broke up, but his memory was revered and he came to be seen as a god, and Hall introduces a story from Schure's Pythagoras and the Delphic Mysteries illustrating the bond among his followers.",
  "modern.hall.12.p4": "Hall quotes Schure: a sick, poor Pythagorean was cared for by an innkeeper and, before dying, drew signs (no doubt the pentagram) on the door, saying a brother would pay his debts; a year later a stranger saw the signs, said he was a Pythagorean, and paid. He then quotes Frank C. Higgins' summary for Masons: Pythagoras' teachings represent what all the leading philosophers of his day agreed on, and his firm defence of one God supports the tradition that God's unity was the supreme secret of the ancient initiations. His school was a series of initiations, and pupils did not meet him personally until the higher grades. There were three degrees: Mathematicus, for mastery of mathematics and geometry, the foundation of all knowledge; Theoreticus, for applications of the exact sciences; and Electus, admitting the candidate to the fullest light he could absorb. Pupils of the outer grades were called exoterici and those past the third degree esoterici, and silence, secrecy and unconditional obedience were its cardinal rules.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p1": "Hall says Pythagoras required geometry, music and astronomy as essential to understanding God, man and nature, and dismissed applicants ignorant of them. He taught moderation, holding that even excess of virtue is a vice, and liked to say that one must cut away sickness from the body, ignorance from the soul, luxury from the belly, sedition from the city, discord from the family and excess from everything; he thought no crime equal to anarchy. Since people know what they want but not what they need, he told disciples not to pray for things for themselves. His God was the Monad, the One that is everything: the Supreme Mind spread through the universe, cause, intelligence and power of all things, moving in a circle, with a body of light and a nature of truth. He held that eating meat clouds the reason, and though he did not wholly abstain, said judges should avoid it before a trial. When he withdrew to the temple to meditate he took special food, a mixture of poppy and sesame seeds, squill skin, daffodil, mallow leaves and barley and pea paste with honey, and a drink of cucumber seeds, raisins, coriander flowers, mallow and purslane seeds, cheese, meal and cream with honey, which he said Ceres gave Hercules in the Libyan desert.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p2": "Hall says the Pythagoreans healed mainly with poultices and knew the magic properties of many plants; Pythagoras prized the sea onion and is said to have written a lost book on it. He found music had great healing power and composed harmonies for different diseases, apparently experimented with colour, and had certain lines of Homer read to the sick. He opposed all surgery and cauterising, since disfiguring the body was sacrilege against the gods' dwelling. He taught that friendship is the truest relationship and exists throughout nature, between gods and men, soul and body, reason and unreason, citizens, strangers, husband and wife, children and servants, and that bonds without friendship are chains. Relationships are mental rather than physical, so a like-minded stranger is closer than a disagreeing relative. Knowledge is gathered mainly by observation; wisdom is understanding the cause of all things, reached by raising the mind until it intuitively grasps the invisible behind the visible and attunes itself to the spirit of things; its ultimate object is the Monad. Man and the universe are both made in God's image, so knowing one gives knowledge of the other, and they constantly interact.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p3": "Hall says Pythagoras held that all heavenly bodies are alive, their visible forms housing souls, minds and spirits as the human body houses the real person, and he revered the planets as great deities, though all are subordinate to the One First Cause, in which they exist for a time as mortality exists within immortality. The Pythagorean letter Y stood for choice, the 'Forking of the Ways': the young candidate walking the stem comes to the fork and must choose between the left-hand path of earthly wisdom, following his lower nature into folly and ruin, and the right-hand path of divine wisdom, through integrity, industry and sincerity to reunion with the immortals. Hall thinks this came from Egyptian rites in which the candidate met two women, one in white temple robes urging him to learning and the other, jewelled and carrying grapes, the false light, tempting him to dissipation. The symbol survives in the Tarot as the Forking of the Ways; the forked stick has meant life to many peoples and was set up in the desert to mark water.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p4": "Hall says opinions differ on Pythagoras' doctrine of transmigration. One view is that people who became like certain animals in life returned as those animals, the timid as rabbits or deer, the cruel as wolves, the cunning as foxes; but Hall thinks this was meant allegorically, to show that people become beastly when ruled by their lower desires, and that what is meant is reincarnation, which Pythagoras must have met in India and Egypt. He quotes a note in Levi's History of Magic that Pythagoras said he had been Aethalides, son of Mercury; Euphorbus, killed by Menelaus at Troy; Hermotimus, a prophet of Clazomenae; a humble fisherman; and finally the philosopher of Samos. Pythagoras also taught that God gave each species a seal, and each body is its impression in the wax of matter. He believed man will eventually cast off his gross nature and live in a body of spiritualised ether always beside his physical form, which may be the eighth sphere or Antichthon, and from there rise to the realm of the immortals where he belongs.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p5": "Hall says Pythagoras taught that everything divides into three and that no one can be wise who does not see every problem as a triangle: 'Establish the triangle and the problem is two-thirds solved' and 'All things consist of three.' He divided the universe into the Supreme World, a subtle spiritual essence pervading everything, the plane of the all-present God; the Superior World, home of the immortals and the archetypes or seals, known only through the shadows they cast on the deep; and the Inferior World, of beings involved with matter, the mortal gods, the angels who work with men, earthly daemons, mankind and the lower kingdoms, who can rise above it by reason and philosophy. The Pythagoreans did not count 1 and 2 as numbers, since they stand for the two higher worlds; numbers begin with 3, the triangle, and 4, the square, which with 1 and 2 make 10, the archetype of the universe. The three worlds were receptacles of principles, intelligences and quantities. He begins a quotation from Redgrove on the regular solids: a perfectly regular solid has equal regular faces meeting equally at each corner, and Pythagoras may have discovered that there are only five.",
  "modern.hall.12.s1.p6": "Hall continues quoting Redgrove: the Greeks thought the four elements' particles had the shapes of the regular solids, earth the stable cube, fire the light tetrahedron, water the icosahedron and air the octahedron; the dodecahedron, hardest to construct, was the most mysterious, and Plato said God used it in planning the universe. Hall adds that Redgrove leaves out the fifth element, ether, the Hindu akasa, which pervades and unites the others, and says the twelve-faced solid also refers to the Twelve Immortals and to twelve convolutions of the brain. Pythagoras practised divination, perhaps by numbers, but his methods are unknown; he is said to have had a predicting wheel, learned divination by water from the Egyptians, and thought brass oracular because brass bowls always hum. A river once greeted him by name, and he could make daemons stir water to give signs; one of his teachers foretold an earthquake after drinking from a spring. Hall thinks he had hypnotic power over people and animals, turning a bird's flight, stopping a bear's raids and changing a bull's diet by thought, and had second sight, seeing distant things and future events.",
  "modern.hall.12.s2.p1": "Hall says Iamblichus collected and explained thirty-nine symbolic sayings of Pythagoras, translated by Thomas Taylor, and gives ten with their hidden meanings. Leave the public roads and walk unfrequented paths: wisdom is sought in solitude. Govern your tongue before all else, following the gods: words often misrepresent us, so when in doubt be silent. When the wind blows, adore the sound: God speaks in the elements, and all nature shows his attributes through harmony and order. Help a man raise a burden but not lay it down: help the diligent, but do not encourage idleness. Do not speak of Pythagorean matters without light: do not try to interpret divine mysteries without spiritual and intellectual illumination. Having left home, do not turn back, or the Furies will follow you: anyone who begins the search for truth and then gives up and returns to vice will suffer greatly, for it is better to know nothing than to learn a little and stop. Feed a cock but do not sacrifice it, for it is sacred to the sun and moon: life is sacred and should not be sacrificed, and the body, sacred to God and nature, must be preserved; Pythagoras also warned against suicide.",
  "modern.hall.12.s2.p2": "Hall continues the sayings. Do not let a swallow into your house: keep drifting thoughts out of your mind and shiftless people out of your life. Do not readily offer your right hand to anyone: do not offer wisdom to those unable to value it, for many who do not want it will bite the hand held out to them, and only time can redeem the ignorant. On rising, roll up the bedclothes and smooth out the body's imprint: those who wake from ignorance should erase their former darkness and leave no form that others might use as a mould for idols. The most famous Pythagorean fragment is the Golden Verses, ascribed to him though their authorship is uncertain, which sum up the teaching of Croton, the Italic School: they urge love of God, reverence for heroes and respect for daemons, careful thought about daily life, preferring treasures of mind and soul to wealth, and promise that whoever rises above his lower nature and masters himself will be reunited with the gods and share their immortality. Hall notes that Plato paid a great price for some of Pythagoras' manuscripts saved from the destruction at Croton.",
  "modern.hall.12.s3.p1": "Hall says Pythagoras held that each body's place in the universe matches its dignity. Against the common view of a flat, square earth at the centre, he taught that fire is the noblest element and the centre the noblest place, so that, like the hearth fire of Vesta in every home, a sphere of divine fire lies at the centre of the universe, called the Tower of Jupiter, the Globe of Unity, the Grand Monad and the Altar of Vesta. Since ten means completeness, he divided the universe into ten spheres: the central fire, the seven planets, the earth and an invisible counter-earth, Antichthon. Clement thought Antichthon the mass of the heavens, others the moon; Hall thinks it the mysterious dark eighth sphere, moving in the earth's orbit but always hidden behind the sun, and asks whether it is the Lilith of astrologers. Isaac Myer says the Pythagoreans held each star a world with its own ether-filled atmosphere. They revered Venus as the only planet bright enough to cast a shadow; as evening star it was Vesper, as morning star Lucifer, the light-bearer, and it was identified with Astarte, Aphrodite, Isis and the Mother of the Gods. Hall suggests its crescent phase may sometimes be seen with the naked eye, explaining the crescents of ancient goddesses. He says Pythagoras learned his astronomy in Egyptian temples, whose priests knew the true arrangement of the heavens thousands of years before the rest of the world.",
  "modern.hall.12.s3.p2": "Hall quotes the anonymous author of The Canon on Kepler's 1596 attempt to prove Plato's idea that the universe was built from the five regular solids: Kepler fitted a dodecahedron round the earth's orbit to get Mars, a tetrahedron round Mars for Jupiter, a cube round Jupiter for Saturn, an icosahedron inside the earth for Venus and an octahedron inside Venus for Mercury. The author says this cannot be taken as the real proportions of the cosmos, since it does not match Copernicus' ratios, yet Kepler valued it more than the Electorate of Saxony, Tycho and Galileo approved it, and Kepler never explained how it should be understood. Hall says Platonic astronomy was not concerned with the physical make-up of the heavens but treated stars and planets as focal points of divine intelligence: physical astronomy was the science of shadows, philosophical astronomy the science of realities.",
  "modern.hall.12.fig1": "The caption, for a portrait from a book on prophetic gods, says that in his youth Pythagoras studied with Pherecydes and Hermodamas and was famous in his teens for his clear thinking. He was over six feet tall, built like Apollo, majestic and awe-inspiring, and grew stronger with age, so that near a hundred he was in his prime. A word of praise from him filled his disciples with ecstasy, and one disciple killed himself because the Master was briefly irritated with him; Pythagoras was so shaken that he never again spoke unkindly to or about anyone.",
  "modern.hall.12.fig2": "The caption to a drawing of the regular solids says the sphere (1), the most perfect form, is added to the five Pythagorean solids: the tetrahedron (2), with four equilateral triangles; the cube (3), with six squares; the octahedron (4), with eight equilateral triangles; the icosahedron (5), with twenty equilateral triangles; and the dodecahedron (6), with twelve regular pentagons.",
  "modern.hall.12.fig3": "The caption says Pythagoras taught that the point stands for the power of the number one, the line for two, the surface for three and the solid for four.",
  "modern.hall.12.fig4": "The caption says Theon of Smyrna called the tetractys, the ten dots of Pythagoras arranged in a triangle, a symbol of the greatest importance, revealing the mystery of universal nature to the discerning. The Pythagoreans swore by 'him who gave to our soul the tetractys, which hath the fountain and root of ever-springing nature'.",
  "modern.hall.12.fig5": "The caption explains that joining the ten dots of the tetractys makes nine triangles; six of them form a cube and, drawn differently, a six-pointed star with a dot in the centre, using only seven dots. Kabbalistically, the three unused corner dots are the threefold invisible cause of the universe, the seven used dots are the Elohim, the spirits of the seven days of creation, and the central dot is the Sabbath.",
  "modern.hall.13.p0": "Hall says much has been speculated about the secret meaning of numbers, but the key to Pythagoras' science was lost at his death; his chief secrets were taught orally to a few disciples who did not reveal them, and for nearly 2,500 years philosophers have failed to recover them. Some secret schools today may continue the ancient Mysteries, but their writings of the last five centuries show no more knowledge than the later Greeks, who talked much, knew little and hid their ignorance behind hints. He quotes Plutarch: the Pythagoreans gave numbers and figures the names of gods, calling the equilateral triangle Minerva, one Apollo, two strife and three justice, as the mean between doing and suffering injury, and the number thirty-six, the sum of the first four odd and even numbers, their tetractys, Kosmos, their most solemn oath; and that the triangle belongs to Pluto, Bacchus and Mars, the square to Rhea, Venus, Ceres, Vesta and Juno, the dodecahedron to Jupiter and a fifty-six-angled figure to Typhon. Plutarch did not explain these, thinking them drawn from Egyptian temple images. Albert Pike confessed he did not know why seven was Minerva or the cube Neptune, and thought the meanings lost by Plutarch's time. Hall concludes that definite statements are unwise and offers only a collection of points from scattered records.",
  "modern.hall.13.s1": "Hall explains how to find the numerical value of a word: it must be taken back to its original Hebrew or Greek in its oldest full spelling, Old Testament names in Hebrew and New Testament words in Greek. Jehovah in Hebrew is Yod-He-Vau-He, worth 10+5+6+5=26, a synonym for the name; English letters would give the wrong answer. Abraxas in Greek adds up to 365, the days of the year, the key to his nature as the 365 spirits of the days combined; since he is fivefold and the circle has 360 degrees, each part is 72, a sacred number in the Bible and Kabbalah. Larger numbers reduce to one of the first ten by adding digits: 666 gives 18 and then 9, and the 144,000 saved in Revelation also give 9, showing both refer to man, whose number is 9. Hall says nothing in Pythagoras justifies changing one's name to improve character or fortune. He describes a modern English system, which assigns 1 to 9 to A to I, J to R and S to Z (so 'man' is 4+1+5=10), but says it has no ancient basis, is not Pythagorean, uses the imperfect 9 rather than 10, and does not fit the Greek and Hebrew alphabets, though experiment with it may prove useful.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p1": "Hall says this outline paraphrases the opening of Thomas Taylor's Theoretic Arithmetic, the rarest and most important collection of Pythagorean mathematical fragments. The Pythagoreans called arithmetic the mother of the mathematical sciences, since geometry, music and astronomy depend on it but not it on them: remove geometry and arithmetic remains, remove arithmetic and geometry goes. Astronomy depends on geometry for the size, shape and motion of the heavenly bodies and on music for their harmony, so those two come before it, and arithmetic before all. Mathematics deals with multitude, the parts of a thing, and magnitude, its size. Magnitude is stationary or moving, multitude related to itself or to other things; arithmetic belongs to multitude in itself, music to multitude in relation, geometry to stationary magnitude and spherics, or astronomy, to moving magnitude, all bounded by mind. Hall adds that atomic theory shows size to be the result of number, since a mass is made of tiny units. He then sets out to clarify the terms number, monad and one.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p2": "Hall explains that the monad means the all-including One, 'sire of gods and men'; any whole considered as a unit, so that the universe is a monad and so is each planet in relation to its parts; and the seed from which the numbers branch like a tree. He says Leibniz, whom he calls an initiate of a secret school, drew his theory of monads from it, and that some Pythagoreans equate the monad with the one. Number covers all numerals and their combinations, though strictly some exclude 1 and 2; Pythagoras defined it as the extension and energy of the generative reasons in the monad, and the followers of Hippasus as the first pattern used by the Demiurge. The Platonists called the one 'the summit of the many'; the monad is the parts taken as a whole, the one is each part. There are odd and even numbers. An odd number cannot be halved because the unit in its middle cannot be split, and divided into two parts it always gives one odd and one even; odd numbers, modelled on the monad, were definite and masculine. Unity was disputed, since adding it to even makes odd and to odd makes even, so it was called androgynous, 'evenly-odd'. Odd numbers of offerings were made to the higher gods and even numbers to goddesses and underworld spirits.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p3": "Hall explains that an even number always splits into two parts that are both odd or both even, as 10 into 5+5, 6+4, 7+3 or 8+2; even numbers, modelled on the duad, were indefinite and feminine. Odd numbers are sorted, by the method called the Sieve of Eratosthenes, into incomposite (prime) numbers such as 3, 5, 7 and 11, divisible only by themselves and one; composite numbers such as 9, 15 and 21, which have other divisors; and incomposite-composite pairs such as 9 and 25, each divisible but with no common divisor. Even numbers are evenly-even, evenly-odd and oddly-odd. The evenly-even numbers double from one, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16 up to 1,024, and can be halved again and again back to one; and the sum of all the terms before any term equals that term minus one, as 1+2+4+8 equals 16 minus 1.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p4": "Hall explains more number properties. In a series of evenly-even numbers, the first times the last equals the last, the second times the second-from-last equals the last, and so on, with the middle term squared also equal to the last when the series is odd in length: in 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 1x16, 2x8 and 4x4 all give 16. Evenly-odd numbers are odd numbers doubled, 2, 6, 10, 14, 18, 22, which can be halved only once; divided by an odd number they give an even quotient, and by an even number an odd one, and each is half the sum of its neighbours, as 10 is half of 6 plus 14. Oddly-odd numbers lie between the two: they can be halved more than once but not down to one, and are made by multiplying odd numbers above one by evenly-even numbers above two, as 3x4 gives 12, which halves to 6 and then 3, which the Pythagoreans would not divide further.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p5": "Hall says even numbers are also superperfect, deficient or perfect. Superabundant numbers have parts that add up to more than themselves, as the parts of 24 add to 33; deficient numbers have parts adding to less, as those of 14 add to 10; perfect numbers have parts adding exactly to themselves, as 14+7+4+2+1 makes 28. Perfect numbers are rare: one below 10 (6), one below 100 (28), one below 1,000 (496) and one below 10,000 (8,128). They are found by adding the doubling numbers 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on until the sum is prime and multiplying that sum by the last number added: 1+2=3 and 3x2=6; 1+2+4=7 and 7x4=28; and so on without end. Doubling a perfect number gives a superabundant one and halving it a deficient one. Hall says the Pythagoreans drew their philosophy from such number lore and introduces an example from Taylor's Theoretic Arithmetic.",
  "modern.hall.13.s2.p6": "Hall quotes Taylor's Theoretic Arithmetic: perfect numbers are images of the virtues, which are means between excess and defect, not extremes. Evil is opposed to evil, but both are opposed to one good, so good is always opposed to two evils at once: timidity and audacity, both lacking true courage, are opposed to fortitude; craft and folly, both lacking intellect, to prudence; extravagance and avarice to liberality. Perfect numbers also resemble virtues in being few and produced in a fixed order, while superabundant and deficient numbers, like the vices, are countless, disorderly and indefinite.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p1": "Hall says this table of the ten numbers paraphrases Nicomachus, Theon of Smyrna, Proclus, Porphyry, Plutarch, Clement, Aristotle and others. The monad, 1, is so called because it always stays the same, apart from multitude. It is called mind, for its stability; hermaphrodite, being male and female; odd and even, since added to even it makes odd and to odd even; God, as the beginning and end of all, itself without either; good; and the receptacle of matter, since it produces the material duad. It was also called chaos, darkness, chasm, Tartarus, Styx, abyss, Lethe, Atlas, axis, Morpho, and the Tower or Throne of Jupiter, for the power at the centre round which the planets turn; germinal reason; Apollo, for the sun; Prometheus, for bringing light; one who lives in fire; substance; cause of truth; and harmony. It is the equal between greater and lesser, the middle, the mean, and 'now' in time; Jupiter, father of the gods; Vesta, the hearth fire at the centre; form; love, concord and piety, being indivisible; and ship, chariot, Proteus, Mnemosyne and 'many-named'. The duad, 2, being divided and two, each part opposed to the other, was given many names, among them genius, evil, darkness, inequality, instability, boldness, contention, matter, dissimilarity, defect, opinion, error, otherness, impulse, death, motion, generation, division, marriage, soul and science.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p2": "Hall quotes Westcott that the duad was called 'audacity' for being the first to separate from the divine One. As the monad is the father, the duad is the mother, sharing qualities with Isis, Rhea, Cybele, Ceres, Diana (the moon being forked), Venus, Juno (wife and sister of Jupiter) and Maia. The monad stands for wisdom and the duad for ignorance, since it holds the sense of separateness, though ignorance always gives birth to wisdom. The Pythagoreans revered the monad and scorned the duad as the symbol of polarity: by it the deep was made opposite the heavens, a mere reflection and so a symbol of illusion, maya, the sea and void, which is why the Persian Magi carried mirrors. From the duad came strife until the monad was set between its two parts and balance restored by the saviour god, crucified between two thieves. The triad, 3, is the first truly odd number and the first balance of units, which is why Apollo spoke from a tripod and libations were poured three times. Its keywords are friendship, peace, justice, prudence, piety, temperance and virtue; it belongs to Saturn, Latona, Hecate, Pluto, Triton and the Fates, Furies and Graces, among others. It is wisdom, organising the present, foreseeing the future and learning from the past, and the number of knowledge, music, geometry and astronomy; its cube was said to have the power of the lunar circle.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p3": "Hall says the triad is sacred because it combines the monad, the divine father, and the duad, the great mother, and so is androgynous and stands for God bringing forth the worlds from himself; the duad is the womb in which the world was incubated. The tetrad, 4, was revered as the first-born number, root of all things and fountain of nature; God was called a tetrad, the Number of Numbers, because 1+2+3+4 make the decad; it is the middle of the week and the first solid. Pythagoras said the soul is a tetrad of mind, science, opinion and sense, and the tetrad connects all beings, elements, numbers and seasons, the cause of all things; Plutarch reads the tetractys as 36, the first four odd numbers (16) plus the first four even (20). Its keywords include impetuosity, strength, virility, 'key keeper of nature', harmony and first depth, and it belongs to Hercules, Mercury, Vulcan, Bacchus and Urania. The triad stands for the primary colours and major planets and the tetrad for the secondary colours and minor planets; a triangle and a square together make the seven spirits and the Masonic apron. The pentad, 5, unites odd and even (3 and 2); the Greeks took the pentagram as a sign of light, health and vitality, and of ether, the fifth element; it is called balance because it halves ten.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p4": "Hall says the pentad stands for nature, because 5 multiplied by itself ends again in 5, as seed grows into seed; only 5 and 6 do this. It represents all higher and lower beings and is called the hierophant, from its link with the spiritual ethers; its keywords include reconciliation, marriage, immortality, providence and sound, and it belongs to Pallas, Nemesis, Bast and Venus. The four elements plus the monad make the pentad, and since ether permeates the elements as the basis of life, the five-pointed star became the sign of vitality and health. Philosophers hid the element earth under the image of a dragon; heroes told to slay it drove their sword, the monad, into the dragon, the tetrad, making the pentad, the victory of spirit over matter. The four rivers of Eden are the elements, ruled by Ezekiel's cherubim. The hexad, 6, stood, as Clement says, for the creation of the world; it is the perfection of parts, sacred to Orpheus, Lachesis and Thalia, the form of forms and maker of the soul. Harmony and the soul were thought alike, and the hexad is marriage, formed of two triangles, male and female; it is also called time, panacea (health being balance), the world (made of harmonised opposites), self-sufficient (since 3+2+1=6) and unwearied.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p5": "Hall says the heptad, 7, was 'worthy of veneration', the number of religion, since man is ruled by seven heavenly spirits, and of life, since babies born in the seventh month were thought to live and those in the eighth often to die. It was called the motherless virgin Minerva, born from the head of the father, the monad; its keywords include fortune, occasion, custody, government, judgement, dreams and voices, and it belongs to Osiris, Mars and Clio. Many nations held it sacred: the Elohim of the Jews were seven, the archangels of the planets, and with the three spirits of the threefold sun make the sacred ten. The tetractys stands for the stages of creation, and the truth that all things are regenerated through ten survives in Masonic grips made with ten fingers. The 3 of spirit, mind and soul descending into the 4 of the world make the 7 of man's mystic nature, shown by the cube, whose six faces are the six directions and whose hidden seventh point is man at the centre, from whom six pyramids radiate: 'The center is the father of the directions, the dimensions, and the distances.' Seven is the number of law, of the Seven Spirits before the throne. The ogdoad, 8, was sacred as the number of the first cube's corners and the only evenly-even number below ten, halving back to one; its keywords include love, counsel, prudence and law, and it belongs to Rhea, Cybele, Neptune, Themis and Euterpe.",
  "modern.hall.13.s3.p6": "Hall says the ogdoad was a mysterious number in the Eleusinian and Cabiric Mysteries, called the little holy number, its shape taken from the twisted snakes of Hermes' caduceus, the serpentine motion of the heavens, and perhaps the moon's nodes. The ennead, 9, the first odd square, was linked with failure because it falls one short of ten; it is the number of man, from nine months in the womb, and is called ocean, horizon, boundary and the sphere of air, as it encloses all the numbers; it belongs to Prometheus, Vulcan, Juno, Proserpine, Hyperion and Terpsichore, among others. Nine was thought evil as an inverted six; in the Eleusinian Mysteries it was the number of spheres the soul passes through to birth, and its shape, like a spermatozoon, linked it to germinal life. The decad, 10, is the greatest number, being the tetractys and containing all arithmetic and harmonic ratios; all peoples count up to it and then return to one. It is called heaven and the world, is applied to age, power, faith, necessity and memory, is unwearied like God, contains odd and even, moving and unmoving, good and ill, and belongs to Atlas, who carries the numbers on his shoulders, Urania, Mnemosyne, the sun, Phanes and the One God. Hall thinks the decimal system began with counting on the fingers.",
  "modern.hall.13.fig1": "The caption, for a table from Higgins' Celtic Druids, explains its columns: the names of the Hebrew letters; Samaritan letters; Hebrew and Chaldean letters; their numerical values; capital and small Greek letters, with asterisks marking those Cadmus brought to Greece from Phoenicia; the names of the Greek letters; and their nearest English equivalents. A note says that at the end of a word certain Hebrew letters take higher values, from 440 to 900, and that a marked Alpha or Aleph equals 1,000.",
  "modern.hall.13.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram redrawn from Taylor's Theoretic Arithmetic, explains the Sieve of Eratosthenes, devised about 230 BC to separate composite from prime odd numbers. The odd numbers are listed in order, and then every third number from 3 is struck out as divisible by 3, every fifth from 5, every seventh from 7, and so on; what remains are the 'incomposite' numbers, divisible only by themselves and one, shown in the bottom panel. Hall cites David Eugene Smith that Eratosthenes, one of Alexandria's greatest scholars, was called 'the second Plato', and notes that he cleverly computed the earth's size, his diameter being only 50 miles off the modern polar figure, proving the Greeks knew the earth was round and could estimate its size and its distance from sun and moon. He adds that Aristarchus of Samos, around 250 BC, showed that the earth goes round the sun, so Copernicus only restated him seventeen centuries later.",
  "modern.hall.14.p1": "Hall says the human body is the oldest, deepest and most universal symbol. The Greeks, Persians, Egyptians and Hindus thought the study of man's threefold nature essential to religious training, and the Mysteries taught that everything in the universe has its counterpart in man. The universe is too vast for human understanding, and even the gods see only part of their source; a person in divine ecstasy may briefly glimpse the light in which creation is bathed, but cannot hold a perfect image of it. So the early philosophers turned from the inconceivable God to man, in whom they found all the mysteries of the outer spheres, and built a secret theology in which God is the Grand Man and man the little god, the universe a man and man a little universe. The great universe was called the Macrocosm, ruled by the Macroprosophus, and man's body the Microcosm, ruled by the Microprosophus. The Mysteries chiefly taught the relationship between them, between God and man, and the key to the correspondences between their organs and functions was the initiates' most prized possession.",
  "modern.hall.14.p2": "Hall quotes Blavatsky's Isis Unveiled: man is a little world inside the great one, suspended like a fetus by his three spirits in the womb of the universe; his body is in sympathy with the earth, his astral soul with the world soul, and his divine spirit is a ray from the Highest Cause; Proclus calls the three the eternal God, eternity and the pattern of the universe. Hall says that before idolatry the early priests placed a statue of a man in the sanctuary, symbolising divine power in all its workings, and studied man as their textbook. He suggests it may have been like an anatomical model, covered with hieroglyphs and perhaps opening to show the organs, bones and nerves, its measurements a standard for measuring the cosmos, a composite emblem of all the sages' knowledge. Then the Mysteries decayed, the secrets were lost, and the figure came to be worshipped as a god, the One in whose image man was made; the priests, no longer understanding it, worshipped it until their temples fell and the statue crumbled with the civilisation that had forgotten its meaning.",
  "modern.hall.14.p3": "Hall says the ancient initiates built theology on the human body, taking literally that man is made in God's image, and that today's religions do not know that biology is the source of their doctrines; many laws thought to be revelations came from long study of the human constitution. Anatomical analogies run through the sacred books, especially their creation myths: anyone who knows embryology and childbirth will see the basis of Adam and Eve and Eden, the nine degrees of Eleusis, the incarnations of Vishnu, the world egg, the Norse Ginnungagap and the fish as an emblem of fatherhood. 'The proper study of mankind is man.' Both God and man have an invisible upper part, a visible lower part and an intermediate sphere where they meet; man's spirit stands to his body as God to the visible universe. The Mysteries taught that spirit comes before form and contains it, so it is wrong to say the spirit is inside the body: spirit first marks out an area and then manifests in it, so the body is within spirit, as the whole of nature and the stars are within God, the Universal Spirit.",
  "modern.hall.14.p4": "Hall says the ancients held that every body, spiritual or material, has three centres, upper, middle and lower. He admits an apparent contradiction: what is called 'above' is really in the centre and superior to both the others, which are both 'beneath'. To make this clearer he defines 'up' as nearness to the source at the centre and 'down' as distance from it toward the circumference: centre is spirit, circumference matter, like the apex of a cone seen from above as a point in the middle of its base. These three centres are three suns, or three aspects of one sun, with counterparts in the three chief centres of the human body, itself a work of the Demiurge. He quotes Thomas Taylor: the first is like light in its source, the sun; the second like the light coming from the sun; and the third like the brightness that light gives to other things.",
  "modern.hall.14.p5": "Hall says the highest, spiritual centre, being in the middle, corresponds to the heart, the most spiritual and mysterious organ; the second centre, the link between higher and lower, to the brain, in the most dignified position; and the lower centre, of least dignity but greatest physical importance, to the generative system. The heart is the source of life, the brain unites life and form through reason, and the generative system produces bodies. A person's ideals depend on which centre dominates: the lower in the materialist, the brain in the intellectual, while in the initiate the heart bathes both in spiritual light and governs mind and body. As light bears witness to life, the mind bears witness to the spirit and the generative system to the mind; so the spirit is symbolised by a heart, the intellect by an open eye, standing for the pineal gland or 'Cyclopean eye', the two-faced Janus, and the generative system by a flower, staff, cup or hand. The Mysteries often deliberately used the heart in the outer sense, as the emotions, making the body, heart and brain the physical, emotional and mental bodies, and only after the lower degrees told initiates that the brain stood in for the spiritual flame in the heart; Hall calls this substitution one of the ancients' 'blinds'.",
  "modern.hall.14.p6": "Hall says the three degrees of the Mysteries were usually given in rooms representing the three centres, and where possible the temple was built in the shape of a body, entered between the feet. The first degree, of the generative centre, was the material mystery, raising the candidate through concrete thought; the second, in the heart chamber but representing the mental link, taught abstract thought; the third, in the brain chamber, highest in position but heart-like in dignity, gave the heart mystery, where the initiate finally understood 'As a man thinketh in his heart, so is he.' He adds that there are seven hearts in the brain and seven brains in the heart, but says little can yet be said of this. He quotes Proclus: as Socrates says in the Alcibiades, the soul turning into herself sees all things and God; laying aside multiplicity she rises to the highest watchtower; as in the Mysteries initiates first meet many-shaped forms but inside the temple receive divine light, so the soul looking outward sees shadows and images, but turning inward finds intellect and the orders of beings, and in her innermost sanctuary, with eyes closed, perceives the gods and the unities of beings, for all things are in us in a psychic way.",
  "modern.hall.14.p7": "Hall says the initiates warned that an image is not the reality but the outward form of an idea: statues of the gods were reminders of invisible powers, not objects of worship, and the body is not the person but his house, as a temple is God's. In grossness the body is the tomb or prison of the divine; regenerated, it is the sanctuary of the God who made it. 'Personality is suspended upon a thread from the nature of Being.' Man is immortal and only his bodies are born and die; reality dwells in unreality, freed for a time by death and permanently by illumination. The pagans were called polytheists only because they personified the attributes of one God, so their pantheons, like the hierarchies of the Kabbalists, catalogue his attributes, and all the gods, elements, planets and constellations have analogues in the body: four body centres for the elements, seven vital organs for the planets, twelve parts for the zodiac, the invisible parts for higher deities, and the hidden God in the marrow of the bones. Each person is a universe through which mineral, plant, animal and invisible spiritual life is evolving; as cells are tiny units in man, man is a tiny unit in the universe.",
  "modern.hall.14.p8": "Hall says man has five extremities, two legs, two arms and a head that rules them, so five is the number of man; a pyramid's four corners and apex show the same, one rational power over four irrational corners. The feet stand for earth and water, the hands for fire and air, and the brain for ether, which unites them; with feet together and arms outstretched, man forms a cross with the intellect at its head. The toes are the Ten Commandments of physical law and the fingers those of spiritual law; the four fingers of each hand are the elements and their three joints divide each, giving twelve parts for the zodiac, while the two joints and base of the thumb stand for the threefold God, creating, preserving and generating or destroying; two hands together give the twenty-four Elders and the six days of creation. The body is divided into a right half of light and a left of darkness; the uninformed call the right spiritual and the left material, but light is posterior to life, and darkness, which precedes it, is the only symbol of life and veils Being itself. Men fought with the right arm and shielded the vital organs with the left, so the right was thought offensive and masculine and the left defensive and feminine, which some think explains right-handedness; and the spiritual nature is on the dark side, since the heart is on the left.",
  "modern.hall.14.p9": "Hall says the false association of darkness with evil led some nations to use the right hand for good work and the left only for unclean tasks, to call black magic the left-hand path, and to put heaven on the right and hell on the left. Some philosophers held that writing left to right, away from the heart, was exoteric, while writing right to left, toward the heart, as in Hebrew, was esoteric. The secret doctrine says every part of the body is summed up in the brain and all in the brain is summed up in the heart. The head stands for intelligence and self-knowledge, and the whole body, earth's most perfect product, for divinity; artists showing God often paint only a hand coming from a cloud, the cloud for his unknowability and the hand for his activity, the only part of him the senses can know. The face is a trinity: the eyes, the comprehending spiritual power; the nostrils, the preserving power of the creative breath; and the mouth and ears, the creative Word of the lower world. By the Word the world was made, and its seven vowel sounds became the seven Elohim who organised it. God is sometimes symbolised by an eye, ear, nose or mouth, meaning divine awareness, interest, vitality and command.",
  "modern.hall.14.p10": "Hall says the ancients held that righteousness and reason make people spiritual, not the other way round, and the Mysteries taught that illumination comes only by raising the lower nature to a standard of efficiency and purity, following fixed rules that lift consciousness until it understands its own make-up and purpose. This knowledge of how to regenerate man's nature quickly was the secret doctrine. Certain apparently physical organs are sheaths of spiritual centres, but how to unfold them was never told the unworthy, since a person who knows how a system works can produce effects he cannot control; hence long probations. To preserve the knowledge it was hidden in myths meaningless to outsiders. Hall says the whole New Testament is a concealed account of the processes of regeneration, its characters personifying processes in the body as a person frees himself from ignorance and death. The gods' clothing is also a key, since clothing meant form, its quality showing the degree of spirituality; the body is the robe of the spirit. Clothing was first for ornament, and the Mysteries taught that man's only lasting adornments are his virtues: white for purity, red for sacrifice and love, blue for altruism and integrity, and mental or moral flaws were shown as bodily deformities.",
  "modern.hall.14.p11": "Hall says the philosophers took the human body as the measure of the universe, so that all things resemble it in constitution: the Greeks called Delphi the earth's navel, seeing the planet as a giant curled into a ball, and the pagans held the earth and stars to be living beings with their own intelligence. They even saw each kingdom of nature as one being: the animal kingdom as a single prototype beast containing all animals, as mankind is contained in the prototype Adam. Races, nations, religions and cities are likewise composite beings, each with an individuality summing up its members, a religion's organisation its body and its members its cells, and all pass through Shakespeare's seven ages. The secret doctrine says man, by refining his vehicles, is gradually freeing himself from matter, and when his physical evolution is complete the empty shell will serve other life waves; at the depth of materialism he is furthest from himself. Not all of man's spirit incarnates: it is shown as a downward triangle whose lowest point, much less than a third in dignity, descends into matter for a short time, while the rest, the Hermetic Anthropos or Overman, never does; Hall likens it to the guardian daemon of the Greeks, Bohme's angel and Emerson's Oversoul.",
  "modern.hall.14.p12": "Hall says that at birth only a third of man's divine nature leaves its immortality and takes on the dream of physical life, animating a body made of earthly elements; at death it wakes from the dream and rejoins its eternal state. This repeated descent of spirit into matter is the wheel of life and death, which the philosophers discuss under metempsychosis. Through initiation and a process called operative theology, this law can be transcended: during life the part of the spirit asleep in form is awakened without death, 'the inevitable Initiator', and consciously reunited with the Anthropos, its overshadowing self. This, he says, is both the main purpose and the highest achievement of the Mysteries: that a person should become aware of and reunite with his divine source without dying.",
  "modern.hall.14.fig1": "The caption, for an image from Bohme's Libri Apologetici, says the four letters of God's name are arranged as a tetractys inside an inverted human heart, and below it the name Jehovah becomes Jehoshua (Jesus) by inserting the shining Hebrew letter Shin. The whole represents the throne of God and his hierarchies in the human heart. Hall quotes Bohme: all people share one book that points to God, within themselves, the precious name of God, whose letters are the flames of his love revealed in the name of Jesus; read these letters in your heart and you have books enough, for all the writings of God's children point to that one book, which is Christ in you.",
  "modern.hall.14.fig2": "The caption, for an old print of a hand, says the twelve joints of the fingers bear the Apostles, each with his emblem, often the instrument of his martyrdom: Andrew a cross, Thomas a javelin or builder's square, James the Less a club, Philip a cross, Bartholomew a knife, Matthew a sword or spear or purse, Simon a club or saw, Matthias an axe and Jude a halberd. Peter carries two keys, gold and silver, James the Great a pilgrim's staff and scallop shell, and John a cup from which poison left as a serpent. Hall notes that Christ on the second joint of the thumb does not follow the pagan system, in which the Father should be on the second joint, the Son on the first and the Holy Spirit on the base, and that the Virgin should be on the base of the thumb, which is sacred to the moon.",
  "modern.hall.14.fig3": "The caption, for diagrams redrawn from Gichtel's Theosophia Practica, says Johann Georg Gichtel, the most enlightened disciple of Jakob Bohme, circulated them secretly among a few friends; he republished Bohme's works with many figures, and the plates in William Law's edition of Bohme seem to follow his. They show the anatomy of the inner, divine man in its human, infernal and divine states; Gichtel gave no explanation, so the translated German labels are the only clue. The two end figures are front and back of the 'Third Table', man regenerated in spirit, soul and body; another is the 'Second Table', man fallen and corrupt; and Hall notes that one plate does not match Law's First Table, which shows man before the Fall. He quotes Law that the three tables show man before the Fall in purity and glory, after it in pollution, and rising again through regeneration, and says students of Eastern teaching will recognise the Hindu chakras in the symbols.",
  "modern.hall.14.fig4": "The caption, for the back view of a figure from Law's edition of Bohme, says that while the front shows man's divine principles regenerated, the back shows their inferior or 'night' condition. A line rises from the sphere of the astral mind through the sphere of reason to the sphere of the senses; the astral mind and the senses are filled with stars to show their nocturnal state, and in the sphere of reason higher and lower are reconciled, reason in mortal man corresponding to illumined understanding in spiritual man.",
  "modern.hall.14.fig5": "The caption, for the front view of a figure from Law's edition of Bohme, describes a tree rooted in the heart, rising from the Mirror of the Deity through the sphere of understanding to branch out in the sphere of the senses. Its roots and trunk are man's divine nature, his spirituality; its branches the separate parts of his divine constitution, his individuality; and its short-lived leaves his personality, which has none of the permanence of its divine source.",
  "modern.hall.15.p1": "Hall tells the Masonic version of how Solomon's Temple was built. When Solomon became king he set out to build a temple to God and a palace. Hiram, King of Tyre, his father David's friend, offered help; Josephus says copies of their letters could still be seen in Jerusalem and Tyre. The two kings stayed friends, even though Hiram did not think much of the twenty Galilean towns Solomon gave him, and they sent each other riddles. Solomon paid Tyre's masons and carpenters in grain, wine and oil, and Hiram sent cedar logs floated by sea to Joppa. Hiram also sent CHiram Abiff, a widow's son and the head of the Dionysiac Architects, the finest craftsman alive. A 1771 Masonic handbook praises him as a master of every craft, who designed and oversaw all the temple's furnishings and was made Solomon's deputy; Hall notes that modern Masonic writers disagree about that last claim.",
  "modern.hall.15.p2": "Hall describes the temple. George Oliver called it a small building, smaller than many churches, but the buildings around it and its gold and jewels made the site immensely rich. At its centre was the Holy of Holies, a perfect cube of twenty cubits each way, which Hall sees as showing Egyptian influence. Tradition gives it 1,453 marble columns and 2,906 pilasters, a porch facing east, room for 300,000 people, and gold-lined inner rooms. Building began in Solomon's fourth year and ended in his eleventh, 480 years after the crossing of the Red Sea. The stones were shaped in quarries below Mount Moriah and the metal was cast in clay between Succoth and Zeredatha, so that the building went up without the noise of tools. Anderson's Constitutions of 1723 counts 3,600 master masons, 80,000 stone-cutters and 70,000 labourers, plus 30,000 men working in Lebanon; Daniel Sickels gives slightly different numbers and puts the cost at nearly four billion dollars.",
  "modern.hall.15.p3": "Hall notes that the Masonic story differs from the Bible: in Scripture CHiram goes home, while in Masonry he is murdered. A. E. Waite says the meaning lies in the allegory, not in the history. In the story CHiram divides his workers into three grades, Entered Apprentices, Fellow-Craftsmen and Master Masons, each with its own passwords and signs. Three Fellow-Craftsmen, Jubela, Jubelo and Jubelum, want the Master's password they have not earned. They wait at three gates of the temple while CHiram prays at noon. At the south gate Jubela strikes his throat with a measuring rule, at the west gate Jubelo strikes his chest with a square, and at the east gate Jubelum kills him with a blow of a maul between the eyes. They bury him on Mount Moriah, mark the grave with a sprig of acacia, try to flee to Ethiopia, are caught and executed. Search parties find the grave; the Apprentices and Fellow-Craftsmen fail to raise him, and the Master Mason raises him with the 'Lion's Paw' grip.",
  "modern.hall.15.p4": "Hall says that to initiated Masons the name CHiram Abiff means 'my Father, the Universal Spirit, one in essence, three in aspect', so the murdered Master stands for the dying god honoured in Mysteries everywhere. He quotes von Welling, from the papers of the Rosicrucian Bacstrom, reading the Hebrew letters of CHiram as Cheth (the sun's light, the invisible fire of Nature), Resh (spirit or air, which carries and gathers that light into heat) and Mem (water, or a 'radical humidity'); together they make the universal fire of Nature. Albert Pike gives other forms of the name, one ending in the Hindu syllable OM, links the three murderers to stars in Libra, and finds the god Bal in each of their names. Hall says the legend needs Pythagorean and Kabbalistic number lore to read. For example, 33: the first temple stood untouched for 33 years before being plundered and later destroyed by Nebuchadnezzar in 588 BC; David reigned 33 years in Jerusalem; Masonry has 33 degrees; the spine has 33 segments; and Jesus died in his 33rd year.",
  "modern.hall.15.p5": "Hall says the legend in its present form is fairly modern, but its ideas are very old. Masonic scholars generally agree it is based on the Egyptian rites of Osiris, whose death and rising stood for the soul's death and rebirth through initiation; CHiram is also linked with Hermes through the Emerald Tablet. So CHiram is a model of humanity reborn, just as Adam after the Fall is the model of humanity fallen. Hall then gives other readings. One ties CHiram to Jacques de Molay, last Grand Master of the Templars, burned in Paris in 1314; Jennings reports a tradition that de Molay summoned the Pope and the king to God's judgment, and that both died within the time he named. On this reading the three murderers are the Pope, the king and the executioners. Another reading ties CHiram to the beheaded Charles I and credits Elias Ashmole, probably a Rosicrucian, with inventing the legend, with England becoming a 'widow' and her people 'Widow's Sons'.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'The legend in its present form is comparatively modern' may mean only the Masonic wording is modern, not that the story's details are; Hall leaves this open.",
  "modern.hall.15.p6": "Hall gives a Christian reading: CHiram is Christ, who raised the temple of his body in three days, and the three murderers are Caesar's agent (the state), the Sanhedrin (the church) and the stirred-up crowd (the mob). Read inwardly, CHiram is man's higher nature and the murderers are ignorance, superstition and fear. Right thinking, right feeling and right action are the three gates through which the Christ power works in the world; their opposites kill it and set up a false kingdom, so that in the material world evil always seems to win. Daniel Sickels says the story repeats through history, in Orpheus, Socrates and every persecuted good, but that justice in the end sweeps evil away and truth rises again. Hall adds that if modern Masonry grew out of Francis Bacon's secret society, as he suspects, it carries Bacon's ideals of universal education and universal democracy, whose enemies are ignorance, superstition and fear, and the crown, the papal tiara and the torch. CHiram stands for the freedom that selfishness keeps sacrificing, and for beauty, which modern practicality wrongly gives up.",
  "modern.hall.15.p7": "Hall says Orville Ward Owen claimed to find most of the first 32 Masonic degrees hidden in the First Shakespeare Folio, and that Masonic emblems appear on the title pages of almost every book Bacon published. Bacon's New Atlantis is full of Masonic symbols, and Joseph Fort Newton says its 'Temple of Solomon' is an ideal society, not a building. Hall suggests the present form of the legend may draw on Bacon's life, who he says went through a 'philosophic death' and was raised in Germany. An old manuscript claims Masonry was founded by alchemists protecting their secrets from people greedy for gold-making, and Hall says the legend does contain an alchemical formula: building the temple is completing the Great Work, which needs CHiram, the universal agent, to turn human ignorance into spiritual gold. He also likens CHiram to the Hindu Kundalini, the spirit fire raised up the 33 segments of the spine into the skull, to the pituitary (Isis) and the pineal gland (Ra). He says the pineal gland is the ancients' sacred eye, the pine cone seen in Egyptian papyri and on the thyrsus of Bacchus, which opens only when that fire is raised through the seven centres called the Seven Churches in Asia.",
  "modern.hall.15.p8": "Hall describes an Eastern painting with three sunbursts on a human figure: Brahma with four heads over the head; Vishnu on a lotus over the heart and upper belly, sheltered by a seven-headed serpent; and Shiva over the genitals, with the Ganges flowing from his head. He says Christian legends can be mapped onto the body in the same way. For Masonry, the three sunbursts are the three gates where CHiram was struck; there is no north gate because the sun never shines from the north. The north stands for the body, which has no light of its own, just as the moon only reflects light. CHiram is the 'fiery, airy water' that must rise through the three great centres and the seven plexuses beside the spine. The nine fused segments of the sacrum and coccyx have ten openings through which the roots of the Tree of Life pass. Early Kabbalists called the lower body the 'Land of Egypt'; Moses, the illumined mind, led the twelve tribes, the twelve faculties, out of it by raising the brass serpent on a Tau cross. Hall says CHiram and most pagan god-men personify the spirit fire in the spinal cord.",
  "modern.hall.15.p9": "Hall turns to astronomy: the sun acts out CHiram's tragedy each year as it passes through the zodiac. Albert Pike says the sun's journey through the twelve signs gave rise to the labours of Hercules, the incarnations of Vishnu and Buddha, and the murder of Khurum by the three winter signs, Capricorn, Aquarius and Pisces, with the other nine signs as the searchers who find, bury and raise him. Other writers make Libra, Scorpio and Sagittarius the murderers, since Typhon, who killed Osiris, ruled Scorpio, and Judas's thirty silver pieces match its thirty degrees. On this reading the sun, struck by Libra (the state), Scorpio (the church) and Sagittarius (the mob), is carried secretly through the dark winter signs and buried on a hill at the spring equinox. Capricorn is Father Time with his scythe; with the Weeping Virgin as Virgo, the 90 degrees between them hold the three murderers. The urn of CHiram's ashes stands for the heart. Saturn, the old man of the north pole who brings an evergreen at the new year, is Santa Claus.",
  "modern.hall.15.p10": "Hall continues: the dead sun is found by Aries at the spring equinox and finally raised by Leo, the Lion of Judah, once the keystone of heaven's arch. The precession of the equinoxes changes which signs play the murderers over the ages, but the pattern stays the same. CHiram is the fiery architect who takes the Lost Word to the grave, the word that when spoken raises all life; in Christian mysticism it is found in a stable among beasts under a star. Robert Hewitt Brown describes the sun weakening after Leo, slain by the three autumn months, lying dead through winter and raised by spring, every year. Hall says CHiram is called dead because in most people the creative forces show only in physical, materialistic ways. The physical world is the winter solstice of the spirit; ignorance is philosophy's winter and understanding its summer. As the sun seems to stand still three days at midwinter before turning north, initiation is the spirit's spring equinox, when the CHiram in man passes into eternal life; the autumn equinox is the fall of man into earthly illusion.",
  "modern.hall.15.p11": "Hall says CHiram, charged with decorating the temple, embodies the principle of beauty, which Plotinus says refines the soul. The Mysteries taught that people are partly shaped by their surroundings, so everyone should live among things that call out noble feelings. They held that well-proportioned bodies came from souls that lived among well-proportioned things, and noble thoughts from minds surrounded by nobility; an ugly, badly proportioned building would breed ignoble deeds, misshapen children and discordant lives in the town around it. Ancient thinkers saw their great philosophers as products of the ideals of architecture, music and art of their time. Hall calls the replacement of harmony by the merely fantastic one of the great tragedies of any civilization. The saviour-gods were beautiful and worked to save people by awakening a love of beauty, and a new golden age needs beauty restored to every part of life. The Dionysiac Architects tried to raise their master, cosmic beauty, by putting up buildings so harmonious that they worked like magical formulas.",
  "modern.hall.15.p12": "Hall says the three Grand Masters of the Jerusalem lodge stand for the threefold spirit of man, which lives on three levels, the Pythagoreans' supreme, superior and inferior spheres. At the gate of the lowest, the underworld, stands the three-headed dog Cerberus, the counterpart of the three murderers. CHiram is the third part, the spirit that takes on flesh and builds living temples; he is cut down at the gates of matter and buried in the elements, yet like Thor he sets the atoms moving and brings order from chaos. He waits as unused power in every soul until life's rituals turn it into divine strength. The three murderers are the laws of the lower world: birth, growth and decay. For most people bodily birth is CHiram's death and bodily death his rising; the initiate rises spiritually without dying. Hall adds that S. A. Zola read the symbols at the base of Cleopatra's Needle in New York as Masonic, and that masons' marks appear on buildings in Europe and Asia, including the Taj Mahal and the Qutb Minar. On one view CHiram led a worldwide guild of builders based in Tyre, who built their knowledge of cosmic law into their buildings and marked each stone for the Great Architect.",
  "modern.hall.15.p13": "Hall quotes Robert Freke Gould: masons' marks are found everywhere, from the chambers of the Great Pyramid and the walls under Jerusalem to Pompeii, India, Mexico, Peru and the ruins of Europe. Seen this way, CHiram's story describes how the divine secrets of architecture are built into real buildings. The builders 'kill' the Grand Master with their tools by squeezing the boundless spirit of beauty into fixed form and 'bury' him in the structure; a Master Mason who meditates on the building can 'raise' those buried principles again. The building is the tomb of the creative idea it shadows. Hall also reads the legend as the story of philosophy. The pagan Mysteries built civilization, but fell to the state, envious of their wealth, the early church, afraid of their wisdom, and the mob stirred up by both. As the raised CHiram whispers the lost Master's Word, a revival of the ancient Mysteries would recover the secret teaching without which civilization stays spiritually confused.",
  "modern.hall.15.p14": "Hall says that when the mob rules, people are ruled by ignorance; when the church rules, by superstition; when the state rules, by fear. To live in harmony these must become wisdom, enlightened faith and love. Despite denials, he calls Masonry a religion, one that seeks to unite God and man by raising initiates to see the Great Architect at work. He describes the age-old ideal of a perfect civilization with a great university at its centre, where sacred and secular knowledge are freely taught to all who live the philosophic life, without creed or dogma, and where the wisest rule and everyone does the work they are best fitted for. Its grades would be entered by initiation, and its deepest subject would be symbolism: every thing, thought and feeling is the symbol of an eternal principle, and CHiram, truth, lies buried in every atom. Through education people would free living truths from dead forms, and earthly government would be modelled on the divine order. Then people would find happiness within, buried virtues would rise, beauty and goodness would again lead the work, and sages would rule with gods walking among men.",
  "modern.hall.15.fig1": "The caption, for an early hand-painted Masonic apron, says that strictly the apron should be a plain square of white lambskin, and quotes Albert Pike that silk, cotton or decoration spoils its symbolism; yet many aprons carry elaborate pictures. It says astrologers gave Mars, the planet of cosmic energy, Aries by day and Scorpio by night; the uninitiated are 'dead from the sting of a scorpion', and through the Paschal Lamb or the Golden Fleece they are raised to the creative power of Mars in Aries. Worn over the lower body, the pure lambskin means the purifying of the sexual forces, and its 144 square inches make it a sign of salvation. This apron shows a beehive for the lodge, a trowel, mallet and trestle-board, rough and finished stones, pyramids and the hills of Lebanon, pillars, the temple and its chequered floor, the blazing star and the tools of the craft. In the middle are the compasses and square, for the universe and man, with a black-and-white serpent of astral light; below are an acacia branch with seven sprigs for the life centres, and a skull and crossbones meaning the spirit is freed only after the 'philosophical death' of the sensual self.",
  "modern.hall.15.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says a hand covered in symbols was held out to new initiates as they entered the Temple of Wisdom. Understanding the symbols on the hand brought divine power and rebirth, so these symbolic hands were said to raise the candidate from the dead.",
  "modern.hall.15.fig3": "The caption, for a picture of Diana of Ephesus from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says she wears a triple, tower-like crown and her body is covered with creatures standing for her spiritual powers. She stood for the source of the lasting teaching that flows from the many-breasted mother as spiritual food for those who devote their lives to contemplating reality. As the body is fed by the Earth Mother, the spirit is fed by the unfailing springs of truth flowing from the unseen worlds.",
  "modern.hall.16.p0": "Hall opens the chapter on music and colour. Great philosophers hold harmony to be the first condition of beauty: a thing is beautiful only when its parts fit together harmoniously. The world is beautiful and its Creator is called the Good because good, acting by its own nature, produces harmony, and beauty is harmony showing itself in form. The universe runs in steps of good from matter, the least good, to spirit, the greatest. Man's highest nature recognizes good most easily because the good outside him matches the good in his soul. What people call evil is just the least degree of good, and ugliness is the least harmonious arrangement of things that are harmonious in themselves. Since everything together makes up the Good, ugliness is unnatural, and harmony is how the will of the eternal Good shows itself.",
  "modern.hall.16.s1.p1": "Hall says the Greek initiates probably learned the philosophy and healing use of music from the Egyptians, who credited Hermes with inventing it; one legend says he made the first lyre by stretching strings across a turtle shell. Isis and Osiris were patrons of music and poetry, and Plato said Egypt's sacred songs were at least ten thousand years old and so lofty that only gods or godlike men could have written them. In the Mysteries the lyre stood for the human being: the body was the instrument, the nerves the strings, the spirit the player, whose music became discord if the person was corrupted. Many ancient peoples used music in worship, but Pythagoras raised it to a science by showing its basis in mathematics, and he is credited with discovering the diatonic scale, though he was said not to be a musician. Hall says the story of how he did it is invented: passing a smithy, he noticed the different notes of large and small hammers, then went home and hung four equal strings from a wooden arm with weights of 12, 9, 8 and 6 pounds, matching the hammers.",
  "modern.hall.16.s1.p2": "Hall says Pythagoras found that the first and fourth strings sounded together made an octave, a ratio of 2 to 1, since doubling the weight works like halving the string. The first and third, and the second and fourth, gave the fifth (diapente), a ratio of 3 to 2. The first and second, and the third and fourth, gave the diatessaron, a ratio of 4 to 3, which Hall calls the interval of the third. Iamblichus says the second and third strings were in the ratio 8 to 9. The key to these ratios is the tetractys, the triangle of dots made from 1, 2, 3 and 4. Hall adds that, although the ratios are true, it has since been shown that hammers on metal do not produce these notes, so Pythagoras probably worked out his theory on a monochord, a single string between two pegs with movable frets.",
  "modern.hall.16.s1.p3": "Hall says that for Pythagoras music depended on mathematics and its harmonies were fixed by mathematical proportion; number came before harmony because it was the law governing every harmonic ratio. Pythagoras taught these ratios as the highest secret of his school. He divided creation into many planes, giving each a note, an interval, a number, a name, a colour and a shape, and he tested his ideas on every level from abstract logic to solid geometry; where all the methods agreed, he held that a natural law had been found. He applied the law of harmonic intervals to everything in nature, even the planets, stars and elements. Hall gives a modern parallel: John Newlands, listing the elements by atomic weight, found that properties repeated at every eighth element, the 'law of octaves' in chemistry. The Pythagoreans called themselves Canonics, judging harmony by reason and mathematics, against the Harmonic school, which trusted taste and instinct. Still, knowing how strongly music works on feeling, Pythagoras used what he called 'musical medicine'.",
  "modern.hall.16.s1.p4": "Hall says Pythagoras so preferred stringed instruments that he warned his followers against flutes and cymbals, and taught that solemn songs with the lyre could cleanse the soul of irrational influences. He found that the seven Greek modes could stir up or calm particular emotions. One night he met a drunken, jealous young man piling firewood against his lover's door to burn the house, egged on by a flute-player in the stirring Phrygian mode; Pythagoras had the player switch to the slow Spondaic mode, and the youth calmed down and went home. Empedocles is said to have saved his host Anchitus from a sword-wielding man by quickly changing the mode of his music, and Asclepius is said to have cured sciatica and nerve diseases with a loud trumpet. Pythagoras treated ailments of spirit, soul and body with specially written music or by reciting Hesiod and Homer. At Crotona his followers began each day with songs to wake the mind and ended it with soothing ones, and at the spring equinox they sang in a circle around a leader playing the lyre.",
  "modern.hall.16.s1.p5": "Hall quotes Iamblichus: Pythagoras devised melodies as remedies for grief and despondency, for rage and anger, for every disorder of the soul, and against desire. The Pythagoreans probably linked the seven modes with the planets; Pliny says Saturn moves in the Dorian mode and Jupiter in the Phrygian. Temperaments and passions also match modes, so anger, a fiery passion, can be fed by a fiery mode or cooled by a watery one. Hall quotes Emil Naumann on Plato: music should teach love of the noble and hatred of the mean; a new, softening scale could endanger a whole nation, and changing a mode shakes the foundations of the state; lawmakers should ban weak and lewd music and encourage pure, dignified music, bold tunes for men and gentle ones for women. Music without words needed special care, since its meaning was uncertain, and popular taste for showy effects deserved contempt. Hall adds that martial music still works powerfully in war, and religious music, though no longer built on the ancient theory, still moves worshippers.",
  "modern.hall.16.s2.p1": "Hall calls the Pythagorean idea of the music of the heavens the most sublime and least understood of their teachings; only Pythagoras was said to hear it. The Chaldeans seem to have first imagined the stars singing together; Job speaks of the morning stars singing, and Shakespeare's Merchant of Venice says every orb sings like an angel as it moves. Little of the theory survives. Pythagoras saw the universe as a huge one-stringed instrument, its string running from pure spirit above to pure matter below. Some say he divided it into nine parts, others into twelve: the sphere of the fixed stars, home of the immortals; then Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the sun, Venus, Mercury and the moon; then fire, air, water and earth. Hall likens the seven planets, with the sun in the middle, to the Jewish seven-branched candlestick. Macrobius says the names of the notes came from the planets' speed and size, each sphere sounding a tone as it moved through the ether. The Canon says Saturn, the farthest, gave the deepest note and the moon the highest, and that the seven planets, the fixed stars and the counter-earth were the nine Muses, whose joint song was called Mnemosyne.",
  "modern.hall.16.s2.p2": "Hall says Greek initiates linked the seven planetary heavens with the seven sacred Greek vowels, Alpha, Epsilon, Eta, Iota, Omicron, Upsilon and Omega, from the first heaven to the seventh, and that together they sing a perfect harmony of praise to the Creator, citing Irenaeus. He thinks the heavens probably count upward from the moon. Many early instruments had seven strings, and Pythagoras is generally credited with adding an eighth to Terpander's lyre; the strings were matched to the planets and to parts of the body, and the names of God were thought to be made from the planetary harmonies. The Egyptians allowed only the seven primary sounds in their temples, and one hymn praises God with the seven tones, while in another God calls himself the great lyre of the world. The Pythagoreans held that everything has a voice and that all creatures forever sing the Creator's praise. People cannot hear it because their souls are caught in material illusion; once freed, they would hear the music of the spheres again, as in the Golden Age, and join in it.",
  "modern.hall.16.s2.p3": "Hall says the Greek Mysteries taught a link between music and form: the parts of a building were like musical notes and modes, and a building combining them was like a chord, harmonious only if it met the mathematics of harmonic intervals, which is why Goethe called architecture 'crystallized music'. Early priests understood vibration and built special sound chambers for their chants, where a whisper could swell into a roar that shook the building, and the wood and stone eventually absorbed the sounds of the rites so that they gave back the same tones when struck. Every element has its own keynote, and a compound structure has a chord which, if sounded, would break it apart. Likewise every person has a keynote that could destroy them, and Hall reads the fall of Jericho's walls at the trumpets as an allegory of this.",
  "modern.hall.16.s3.p1": "Hall quotes Edwin Babbitt praising light: it reveals the world's beauty and is itself the most beautiful thing; it shows things as they are; it reaches us from immensely distant stars and through the microscope shows things too small to see; it is gentle yet powerful; without it all life would die; and the more we study it, the more it appears as a store of power to heal and refine. Hall says light is the basic physical form of life, and what we call light is really a rate of vibration acting on the optic nerve. Our senses fence us in: there is far more light than anyone has seen, kinds that no instrument will ever detect, and countless colours, sounds, smells, tastes and substances beyond our senses. We are surrounded by a world beyond the senses that we know nothing of, because our organs of perception are not yet developed enough to respond to its finer vibrations.",
  "modern.hall.16.s3.p2": "Hall says peoples of every kind have used colour as a language for religious and philosophical ideas. Herodotus describes Ecbatana with seven walls coloured for the seven planets, and the ziggurat of Nebo at Borsippa rose in seven stages, each painted the colour of a planet, which Hall takes to show Babylonian knowledge of the spectrum and the seven creative powers. A Mughal emperor built a seven-level fountain whose water changed through every colour as it fell. Tibetan artists use colour for mood: L. A. Waddell says white and yellow faces mean gentle forms, red, blue and black fierce ones, light blue sometimes just heavenly, and that gods are usually white, goblins red and devils black. Plato in the Meno calls colour 'an effluence of form, commensurate with sight', and in the Theaetetus says no colour exists by itself: each arises between the eye and the right motion, belongs to each perceiver, and may look different to a dog than to us.",
  "modern.hall.16.s3.p3": "Hall says the Greek ideas about colour and music are set out in the Pythagorean tetractys. Its first three dots are the threefold white light, the Godhead, which holds all sound and colour in potential; the other seven are the colours of the spectrum and the notes of the scale, the creative powers flowing from the First Cause. The seven split into three, the spiritual side of the universe, and four, the lower, irrational world. In the Mysteries the seven creative lords stream from the mouth of the Eternal One, like the spectrum drawn out of white light. The Jews called these seven makers of the lower spheres the Elohim; the Egyptians called them the Builders, shown with great knives carving the universe. Planet worship rests on seeing the planets as the seven creative attributes of God, said to live inside the sun, which like white light holds all tones and colours. Hall prefers matching colours to notes by the octave: red is do, orange re, yellow mi, green fa, blue sol, indigo la and violet ti, with the eye seeing only seven colours against many octaves of sound. He finds proof in the match of the first, third and fifth notes with the primary colours red, yellow and blue, and of the weak seventh note with purple.",
  "modern.hall.16.s3.p4": "Hall quotes Edwin Babbitt: just as C is the lowest note, made by the coarsest air waves, red is the lowest colour, made by the coarsest waves of ether; the top of each scale vibrates at roughly twice the bottom, and when one octave of colour ends in violet another, invisible octave begins at double the rate. Hall then matches the colours to the zodiac like the spokes of a wheel: Aries red, Taurus red-orange, Gemini orange, Cancer orange-yellow, Leo yellow, Virgo yellow-green, Libra green, Scorpio green-blue, Sagittarius blue, Capricorn blue-violet, Aquarius violet and Pisces violet-red. He gives H. P. Blavatsky's table linking colours to the seven principles of man and seven states of matter, from violet (the etheric double, ether) down to red (the seat of animal life, ice). This scheme needs a different order of planets: do is Mars, re the sun, mi Mercury, fa Saturn, sol Jupiter, la Venus and ti the moon.",
  "modern.hall.16.fig1": "The caption, for a diagram from Stanley's History of Philosophy, says that in the Pythagorean music of the spheres the span from the earth to the fixed stars was an octave, the most perfect interval. The usual scheme runs: earth to moon a tone; moon to Mercury a half-tone; Mercury to Venus a half-tone; Venus to the sun a tone and a half; sun to Mars a tone; Mars to Jupiter a half-tone; Jupiter to Saturn a half-tone; Saturn to the fixed stars a half-tone; adding up to the six whole tones of an octave.",
  "modern.hall.16.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram from Fludd's De Musica Mundana, says it shows the steps of energy and substance between earth and pure, unconditioned force. From the top there are fifteen spheres: limitless eternal life; the upper, middle and lower empyrean; the seven planets; and the four elements. Fludd draws energy as a pyramid with its base at the top and substance as a pyramid with its base on the earth's sphere, showing how much of each is in every level. The substance pyramid touches but does not pierce the highest sphere, and the energy pyramid touches but does not pierce the lowest. The sun's level is the 'sphere of equality', where neither prevails. The world-monochord is an imagined string running between the bases of the two pyramids.",
  "modern.hall.16.fig3": "The caption, for another chart from Fludd's De Musica Mundana, sums up his theory of universal music. The span from earth to the highest heaven is a double octave, so the two extremes are in harmony; the highest heaven, the sun and the earth sound the same note at different pitches, the sun an octave below heaven and the earth an octave below the sun. In the lower octave substance outweighs energy and its harmonies are coarser; in the upper octave energy outweighs substance. Fludd writes that the monochord struck in its spiritual part gives eternal life, and in its material part passing life. Some elements, planets and heavens stand in harmonic ratios, which Fludd offered as the key to the sympathies and antipathies in nature.",
  "modern.hall.16.fig4": "The caption, for a diagram from Fludd's De Musica Mundana, says two interlocking pyramids stand for fire and earth, showing that neither enters into the other. Earth is four parts earth; water three parts earth and one fire; the 'sphere of equality' two of each; air three parts fire and one earth; and fire four parts fire. So earth to water and fire to air are 4 to 3, a fourth (diatessaron), and water and air to the sphere of equality are 3 to 2, a fifth (diapente). A fourth plus a fifth makes an octave, so fire and earth are each an octave from the sphere of equality and a double octave from each other.",
  "modern.hall.16.fig5": "The caption, for a diagram from Fludd's De Musica Mundana, says Fludd splits each of the four elements into three levels: the coarsest, mixed with the element below it (except earth, which has nothing below); the element in its fairly pure state; and the finest, mixed with the element above. Water, for example, is muddy at the bottom, salty as in the sea in the middle, and fresh at the top. The lowest and middle levels are each a tone apart and the top level a half-tone, so each element spans two and a half tones, and Fludd stresses that the fourth (diatessaron) is the ruling interval of the elements.",
  "modern.hall.17.p1": "Hall says every ancient people honoured the creatures of water, air and land. Seeing bodies as symbols of unseen forces, they worshipped divine power through the simpler creatures, which respond most readily to the gods, and they held that God is best understood through his works in nature. Each creature shows some part of God's mind or power, and an animal was picked as a symbol when its traits displayed an invisible principle in action. Fish, insects, animals, reptiles and birds appear in nearly every religion because their forms, habits and surroundings tie them to nature's powers of generation. Knowing that life began in water, and that fish breed in huge numbers, early thinkers made the fish the symbol of the seed of life, and guessed, without microscopes, that sperm looked like a fish. Fish were sacred to Aphrodite. Hall sees eating fish on Friday as a pagan survival: the day is named after Freya, the Norse Venus, goddess of beauty and fertility, and Friday is also holy to Muslims.",
  "modern.hall.17.p2": "Hall says the word nun means both fish and growth, and quotes Inman that the Jews were led by Joshua, the 'son of Nun', whose name is the same as Jesus, while 'nun' is still used for a female religious. Early Christians used three fish for the Trinity, and the fish is one of Buddhism's eight sacred signs. The dolphin was sacred to Apollo and Neptune and was said to carry drowned sailors to heaven; Christians took it as an emblem of Christ, and the French heir, the Dauphin, may owe his title to it. Converts were likened to fish who at baptism went back into the 'sea of Christ'. Hall says old books of zoology are full of composite beasts, and that priests of the Persian, Greek and Egyptian Mysteries dressed as such creatures to stand for sides of human nature. They gave their gods animal emblems with imaginary habits that hid spiritual truths: the phoenix nests in incense and flame, the unicorn has a horse's body, elephant's feet and boar's tail, the centaur is half man and half horse, and the Hermetic pelican feeds its young from its own breast.",
  "modern.hall.17.p3": "Hall says that, apart from the pelican, these creatures never existed outside the symbols of the Mysteries, though they may have begun as rumours of real animals: the mantichora resembles the hyena, and the unicorn may be the rhinoceros. In the temples they stood for parts of human nature and for forces in the unseen worlds, a point he says writers on medieval monsters miss. Legends tell of a race of composite creatures, destroyed by the gods, before humans appeared. Hall says the temples kept records, never shown to outsiders, that man evolved from an amphibian-like being with gills and some scales, which the human embryo partly echoes; so the fish was seen as humanity's ancestor and was worshipped by Chaldeans, Phoenicians and Brahmins. American Indians tell of 'Water Indians' living in lakes and seas. The fish could mean damnation, but in China it meant good fortune. Plutarch says that when Set cut Osiris into fourteen pieces, one was eaten by three kinds of fish in the Nile, so Egyptians would not eat them. As a sign of evil, the fish stood for the body and the tomb, as Jonah spent three days in the fish, like Christ in the tomb.",
  "modern.hall.17.p4": "Hall says some church fathers saw Jonah's whale as God the Father, taking the prophet into himself until he was safe. He reads the story as an initiation: the great fish is the darkness of ignorance that swallows a person thrown overboard (born) into the sea (life). He suggests it might also come from fish-shaped ships, so Jonah may simply have been picked up by another vessel, or more likely from the hippocampus, half horse and half dolphin, which early Christian carvings show instead of a real whale. The sea serpents that Maya and Toltec legends say brought the gods to Mexico may have been dragon-shaped Viking or Chaldean ships. Blavatsky thought cetus, the whale, came from a name of the fish god Dagon, and that Jonah was kept in a cell inside a huge Dagon statue. Leviathan, a huge sea serpent, stood for man's lower nature: serpent forms mean solar energy, here trapped in water and in the body as writhing greed and lust. The Gnostics split the Redeemer into Jesus, a mortal man, and the Christos, cosmic mind, which used Jesus's body for three years; ugly creatures that hide splendid forms, like the caterpillar hiding the butterfly, were used to show this.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p1": "Hall says Heinrich Khunrath's Amphitheatre of Eternal Wisdom, published in 1609 and said by Eliphas Levi to hide all the secrets of magic, has a plate showing the Hermetic sciences attacked by narrow-minded scholars, whom Khunrath drew as composite beasts with donkey ears and tails, while the air above is full of dragonflies, winged frogs and human-headed birds spewing gossip and slander, to no effect. Poisonous insects often stood for the deadly power of the tongue. Insects also stood for nature spirits and daemons, which were thought to live in the air, and medieval pictures show conjured spirits in part-insect form. Early thinkers saw a plague as one living being rather than many germs and, since it came through the air, gave it the shape of an insect or bird. Good powers got beautiful forms and evil ones twisted shapes: the Devil had a rooster's feet in medieval belief and Typhon a hog's body in Egypt. The ant stood for hard work and foresight, and locust swarms for passion, disease and strife, which leave the soul a desert. The insects honoured worldwide are the scarab, the scorpion, the butterfly and the bee.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p2": "Hall calls the Egyptian scarab one of the most remarkable symbols ever devised: from a simple beetle the priests made a sign of bodily strength, the soul's resurrection and the Creator as lord of the sun. Budge says early Egyptians pictured the sky as a meadow across which a giant beetle, the sky god, pushed the sun as its egg, as the scarab rolls a ball; Fabre later showed the ball holds dung to feed the young, not eggs. Initiates were sometimes called scarabs, or lions and panthers. Heart scarabs of stone, about three inches long, were placed in the chest of the dead when the heart was removed, or by some accounts wrapped in the bandages, and the Book of the Dead orders a green stone scarab laid on the breast to perform the 'opening of the mouth'. As creator, Ra took the form of Khepera, the scarab-headed god of new life. Coffins usually had a winged scarab over the breast, and small scarabs were common jewellery. The wings hidden under the shell stood for the soul hidden in the body, and soldiers bore the scarab because all scarabs were thought to be male and so signs of strength and courage.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p3": "Hall says Plutarch noted that the scarab rolls its ball backwards while facing the other way, which suited it as a sun symbol, since in Egyptian astronomy the sun really moved west to east though it seemed to go the other way. An Egyptian story says dawn comes when the scarab spreads its coloured wings and night when it folds them; Khepera sails the sky in the Boat of the Sun. The scorpion stands for both wisdom and self-destruction. The Egyptians called it accursed, and the sun's entry into Scorpio began Typhon's rule; it was matched with Judas among the twelve apostles. Stinging with its tail, it was called a backbiter, and Calmet calls it an emblem of the wicked and of persecution. Typhon's hot desert winds carry its sting. It also stood for the spinal fire, which destroyed a person if it gathered at the base of the spine. Antares, the red heart of the celestial scorpion, was the worst star in the sky, Mars's deputy, and was thought to cause blindness at birth, perhaps a memory of blinding sandstorms. Yet the scorpion's fire can enlighten as well as burn: the Greater Mysteries were held under Scorpio, and in the Book of the Dead the soul says 'I am that scorpion'. Scorpions were linked with Selk, guardians of the sun's gate, and the seven who went with Isis.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p4": "Hall cites George Smith's Chaldean Account of Genesis, where the hero Izdubar (Nimrod) meets the terrifying scorpion men who guard the sun at its rising and setting, their crowns at heaven's lattice and their feet in hell, which Hall glosses as the spine. The Romans also had a war machine called the scorpion, which shot arrows with a beam like a scorpion's tail. The butterfly, as Psyche, the winged maiden, is the soul because of the stages it passes through to gain flight, which match the three degrees of the Mysteries: the egg-to-larva stage is the ignorant, unreborn person; larva-to-pupa, inside the chrysalis or 'tomb', is the disciple in meditation; and pupa-to-butterfly is the enlightened initiate rising from his lower nature. Night moths stand for secret wisdom hidden in darkness, and some for death, like the death's-head moth with its skull-like mark; the death-watch beetle was thought to tick a warning of death. Opinions differ on the spider: its shape suits the nerve centres, some Europeans fear to kill one as the Devil's agent, and Paracelsus taught it carried an evil force used by black magicians.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p5": "Hall says some plants, minerals and animals were held sacred everywhere because they are especially sensitive to the 'astral fire', a force science meets as electricity and magnetism; lodestone, radium and some parasitic plants respond to it strongly. Medieval magicians kept bats, spiders, cats, snakes and monkeys to draw on their life force. Some ancient schools taught that poisonous creatures are bred from human evil and will vanish when people stop breeding hate. American Indians tell of a Spider Man whose web joined heaven and earth, and in India the gods who held the young universe together with threads of force were called Spider Gods, ruled by the Great Spider. In Masonry the beehive means that happiness comes from working for the common good; the bee is wisdom, gathering it from daily life as it gathers pollen. Mystics say the bee, like wheat and bananas, came to earth from Venus millions of years ago, which is why their origins cannot be traced, and its rule by queens makes it a feminine symbol. In India the life-god Prana is shown ringed by bees; the bee stands for generative power, and it was the emblem of the French kings.",
  "modern.hall.17.s1.p6": "Hall says the fly stands for a tormentor. The Chaldean god Baal was called Baal-Zebul, god of the dwelling, and since zebub means fly the name became Beelzebub, loosely 'Jupiter's fly'. The fly was also seen as a form of divine power, because it clears away rotting matter. Inman thinks the name comes from its buzzing, so that the Jews' mocking 'Lord of Flies' really meant 'Lord who hums', and compares the singing statue of Memnon in Egypt, which hums when the wind blows; the Jews made Beelzebub prince of devils by reading daemon as demon. Hall adds that Naudé, defending the poet Virgil from charges of sorcery, ended up giving plenty of evidence against him, including the tale that Virgil set a brass fly over a gate of Naples after secret rites, so that no flies entered the city for over eight years.",
  "modern.hall.17.s2.p1": "Hall says the serpent heads the reptile family and has been honoured almost everywhere: Indian serpent mounds, carved snakes in Central and South America, Indian cobras, the Greek Python, the Druids' serpents, the Norse Midgard serpent, the Nagas of South-East Asia, the bronze serpent of the Jews, the serpents around the Delphic tripod, those kept in Egyptian temples and the uraeus on the Pharaoh's brow. The snake-wound staff stood for the physician, and Hermes's staff is still medicine's emblem. Most ancient peoples saw the serpent as wisdom or salvation; Christian dislike of it comes from a misunderstood Eden story. Hall says the serpent, true to wisdom, tempted man to know himself, against the will of the Demiurge, Jehovah, and asks how it got into a garden God had called good. The tree in the middle of the garden is the spinal fire, and knowing how to use it is the serpent's gift. He argues that the serpent is the model of the universal saviour, giving creation self-knowledge, and points to Moses's bronze serpent on a cross, which saved those who looked at it, and to Christ telling his disciples to be wise as serpents.",
  "modern.hall.17.s2.p2": "Hall says the idea that the serpent is evil cannot be proved. It has long meant immortality and rebirth, since it sheds its skin and seems to return in a new body; people believed snakes die only by violence and that they swallow themselves, which made them a sign of the Creator who takes the universe back into himself. He quotes Blavatsky: before the earth was round it was a trail of fire-mist writhing like a serpent, the Spirit of God moving on chaos, until it took the shape of a serpent biting its tail, eternity in spirit and the world in matter. The seven-headed snake is God working through his seven spirits, the Elohim. Its coils stand for the orbits of the heavenly bodies, and the serpent around the egg for the sun's apparent path and the bands of astral light around the earth. Electricity, which moves like a snake across a spark gap, was called the Great Snake, and as universal force the serpent means both good and evil, since force can destroy as fast as it builds. The serpent biting its tail is eternity, its head and tail the poles of the life circuit. Initiates were called serpents, and 'Winged Serpents', perhaps the Seraphim, was the name of an unseen order that helped shape the early earth.",
  "modern.hall.17.s2.p3": "Hall says a legend tells that winged serpents ruled the early earth, probably the demigods before recorded history. The sun-serpent link was seen in the belief that a snake cut into pieces stays alive until sunset. The Hopi think snakes are close to the Earth Spirit, so at their yearly snake dance they bless many snakes and release them to carry the tribe's prayers into the earth. Lizards, being fast, were linked with Mercury. Hall quotes H. E. Santee's anatomy: reptiles have two pineal bodies, the front one forming a primitive single eye, which in the tuatara of New Zealand pokes through the skull with a rough lens, retina and nerve fibres. The Egyptians saw the crocodile as Typhon and also as the supreme god, because, Plutarch says, it can see under water through a thin membrane, and because it always laid its eggs just where the next flood would reach. The large, fierce crocodile was hated as Typhon, who waited to devour those who failed the judgment of the dead; the smaller, tamer kind stood for good and, Anthony Todd Thomson says, was fed daily, given mulled wine, and decorated with gold earrings and bracelets.",
  "modern.hall.17.s2.p4": "Hall says the turtle meant long life to the Chinese; in a Singapore temple sacred turtles have their ages carved on their shells. American Indians read the ridge on its back as the divide between life and death. It stands for wisdom because it withdraws into itself for protection, and also has a phallic sense. Hindus pictured the world on four elephants standing on a giant turtle crawling through chaos. The Egyptian sphinx, the Greek centaur and the Assyrian man-bull are composites of man and beast that stand for man's mixed nature and for the heavenly orders guiding humanity, the twelve holy animals now called constellations, which are only symbols of spiritual forces. Chiron the centaur, teacher of men, stands for the beings of Sagittarius, keepers of the secret teaching while the sun passed through Gemini. The five-footed, eagle-winged, man-headed bull shows that man's unseen nature has a god's wings, a man's head and a beast's body. The sphinx at the temple gate barred outsiders and so stood for the secret teaching itself. Hall says children's fairy tales full of monsters draw on this old mystical lore.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig1": "The caption, for a picture of Vishnu's first incarnation as Matsya from Picart's Religious Ceremonials, says the fish is often linked with world saviours. Vishnu, who takes ten forms to save the universe, comes out of a fish's mouth in his first. Isis nursing Horus often wears a fish on her headdress, and Oannes, the Chaldean saviour, is shown as a fish with a man's body showing through. Jesus was often symbolized by a fish, told his disciples to be 'fishers of men', and the fish was the first Christian monogram; the Greek ICHTHYS, a name for Jesus, means 'fish'. Church fathers accepted the fish as a sign of Christ; Augustine compared Christ to a broiled fish whose flesh feeds the righteous.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from Redgrove's Bygone Beliefs, calls the mantichora the most remarkable of allegorical beasts. Ctesias describes it with a flame-coloured, lion-like body, three rows of teeth, a human head and ears, blue eyes, a tail of spikes and scorpion-like stings, and a voice like trumpets. It found its way into medieval natural histories and was taken seriously, but no one had seen it, because it was said to live in places no one could reach.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig3": "The caption, for a royal scarab from H. R. Hall's British Museum catalogue, says the flat underside of a scarab usually bears an inscription of the dynasty in which it was made, and some were used as seals. They were cut from ordinary or precious stone or made of glazed clay, most small ones are pierced like beads, and some are hard enough to cut glass. The picture shows the scarab from above and the side, and its underside with the name Men-ka-Ra in a cartouche.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig4": "The caption, for the fleur-de-lis, says Charlemagne used the bee as a sign of royalty, and that the French lily is probably a stylized bee rather than a flower. It adds an old Greek legend that the nine Muses sometimes took the form of bees.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig5": "The caption, for a scorpion talisman from Paracelsus' Archidoxes of Magic, says scorpions often appear on medieval charms and were thought able to cure disease. This one was made of several metals under certain positions of the planets, and Paracelsus advised wearing it for disorders of the reproductive organs.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig6": "The caption, for the uraeus from Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus, says the spinal cord was symbolized by a snake, and the serpent coiled on the foreheads of Egyptian initiates stood for the divine fire that had climbed like a snake up the Tree of Life.",
  "modern.hall.17.fig7": "The caption, for a drawing from Maurice's Indian Antiquities, says Mithras and Serapis are both shown with serpents coiled around their bodies. The drawing shows the Persian good and evil powers, Ahura Mazda and Ahriman, fighting over the Egg of the Earth, each trying to tear it from the other's teeth.",
  "modern.hall.18.p1": "Hall says pagans and Christians alike used birds as emblems: the buzzard for cruelty, the eagle for courage, the pelican for self-sacrifice and the peacock for pride. Because birds rise toward the light, they stand for aspiration, purity and beauty, and wings were added to earthly creatures to suggest something higher. Living in the sacred trees of old forests, birds were thought to be messengers of the tree spirits and nature gods, who spoke through their song. Many myths explain bright feathers, like the eyes of Argus set in Juno's peacock's tail, and a Navaho story says the wild turkey's tail lost its colour when it trailed in the flood waters. Gravity pulls toward matter and 'levitation' toward spirit; since birds seem to defy gravity, they were thought to be of a higher nature, and feathers became signs of divinity, courage and achievement, like the eagle feathers of American Indians. Angels have wings because, like birds, they go between gods and men. In the Norse Mysteries the sky was a skull and birds were God's thoughts, so Odin's ravens were Hugin and Munin, thought and memory.",
  "modern.hall.18.p2": "Hall says the eagle was Jupiter's bird for Greeks and Romans and stood for the swift forces of the Demiurge, the earthly lord of birds, while the phoenix stood for the heavenly ruler. The eagle meant the sun in its material side and the fixed law all mortals must obey. To Hermetists it was sulphur and the hidden fire of Scorpio, which Hall calls the most meaningful sign of the zodiac; as one of Scorpio's three symbols it stood, like the Goat of Mendes, for the art of turning the scorpion's hellish fire into the gods' spiritual light. Some American Indian tribes honour the thunderbird, whose wingbeats make thunder and whose eyes flash lightning. Birds also meant the breath of life: Egyptian art shows human-headed hawks holding symbols of immortality over mummies, as the freed soul. The hawk was the sun's bird, and Ra, Osiris and Horus often have hawk heads. The cock stood for Casmilus in the Samothracian Mysteries, was sacred to the sun and to Ares, and meant watchfulness; on a weather vane it is the sun amid the four corners of the world, and Greeks sacrificed one on entering the Eleusinian Mysteries. Hall asks whether Bacon's death after stuffing a fowl with snow hints at his initiation.",
  "modern.hall.18.p3": "Hall says the peacock and the ibis were honoured because they killed poisonous snakes, thought to be agents of the underworld gods. The many 'eyes' in its tail made the peacock a symbol of wisdom, and it was often confused with the phoenix; a belief that its flesh does not rot made it a sign of immortality, like the incorruptible spirit. The Egyptians gave the ibis divine honours, and killing one, even by accident, was a grave crime. They said it could live only in Egypt, would die of grief abroad, protected the crops, and drove out the winged serpents of Libya blown in by the wind. It was sacred to Thoth, and with its head tucked under its wing it looked like a human heart; the black-and-white ibis belonged to the moon, and all kinds were honoured for eating crocodile eggs. Night birds stood both for sorcery, which works only in darkness, and for the secret sciences, whose masters see through the dark; so owls and bats meant witchcraft or wisdom. The goose stood for the first substance, the cosmic goose having laid the world-egg; the black crow for chaos before creation; and the swan for the grace and purity of the initiate, so gods taking swan form meant secret wisdom entering the initiate.",
  "modern.hall.18.p4": "Hall says scavengers like the vulture, buzzard and condor stand for the divine power that cleans up what would harm life, doing good while seeming to destroy, though some religions wrongly call them evil. Parrots and ravens were honoured because, imitating human speech, they seemed to link humans and animals. The dove, the Christian sign of the Holy Spirit, is a very old pagan feminine emblem. In many Mysteries it stood for the third person of the creative trinity, the maker of the world, and it was sacred to Astarte, Cybele, Isis, Venus, Juno, Mylitta and Aphrodite. Gentle and devoted to its young, it stood for motherhood, and also for wisdom, divine order and God's will at work. The name 'dove' was given to oracles and prophets: Hall quotes Bryant that its name was Ionah, and that Jonah, John the Baptist and John the Apostle all bear it. In Masonry the dove means purity and innocence. In the pagan Mysteries the dove of Venus was crucified on a four-spoked wheel, foreshadowing the crucified Lord of Love. Muhammad drove the doves from the Mecca temple, yet is sometimes shown with one on his shoulder as inspiration; doves topped sceptres as signs of divine favour, and in medieval art meant blessing.",
  "modern.hall.18.s1.p1": "Hall quotes Clement, an early church father: the phoenix is the only one of its kind and lives five hundred years. When it is about to die it builds a nest of frankincense, myrrh and spices, enters it and dies. As its flesh rots, a worm grows from it, feeds on its juices and grows feathers; when strong, it carries the nest with its parent's bones from Arabia to Heliopolis in Egypt, lays them on the altar of the sun in full view, and flies home, and the priests find from their records that exactly five hundred years have passed. Herodotus, admitting he had never seen one, gives a version he did not believe: the bird makes a ball of myrrh as heavy as it can carry, hollows it out, puts its dead parent inside, seals it with more myrrh so it weighs the same, and takes it to the temple of the sun. Herodotus and Pliny both say the phoenix looked like an eagle, and Hall says the modern Masonic eagle was almost certainly a phoenix at first. Its body was glossy purple, its long tail feathers alternately blue and red, its head pale with a golden collar, and it had a tuft at the back of its head that most writers overlook.",
  "modern.hall.18.s1.p2": "Hall says the phoenix was sacred to the sun, and its lifespan of 500 to 1,000 years served as a measure for the motions of the heavens and for the cycles of time taught in the Mysteries. No one knew what it ate; some said it lived on air, others that it ate rarely and never where people could see. He points out to Masons that it was said to build its nest from acacia sprigs. The phoenix, which he identifies with the Persian roc, is also a southern constellation, and was probably the swan of the Greeks, the eagle of the Romans and the peacock of the Far East. As it rose again from its own dead self seven times seven, so man's spirit rises again and again from the dead body. Medieval Hermetists made it the symbol of completed alchemical transmutation, the same as human rebirth, and an alchemical formula bore its name. Hall says the pelican of the Rose Croix degree, feeding its young from its breast, is really a phoenix, since its head is eagle-like and lacks the pelican's pouch. Initiates were called phoenixes, the twice-born: after 'nine degrees in the womb of the Mysteries' they were born into spiritual awareness, which Hall links with Christ's words, 'Except a man be born again, he cannot see the kingdom of God.'",
  "modern.hall.18.s1.p3": "Hall claims that European mysticism was still alive when the United States was founded, and that the Mysteries had a hand in it, as shown by the Great Seal. He says the seal is full of occult and Masonic symbols, chief of them the 'American eagle', a bird Benjamin Franklin thought unworthy of the nation, which Hall says is really a stylized phoenix, plain on the original seal. He cites Gaillard Hunt's history of the seal as supporting a phoenix on the front and the Great Pyramid on the back, and notes that William Barton's 1782 design showed a phoenix on a nest of flames. He quotes Harvard's Charles Eliot Norton that the adopted design of the unfinished pyramid and the all-seeing eye could hardly look like anything but 'a dull emblem of a Masonic fraternity'. Hall says the eagles of Napoleon and Caesar and the eagle of Scorpio are really phoenixes, the sign of spiritual victory, and that Masonry's single and double-headed eagles are phoenixes standing for the regeneration of creative energy, the Great Work; the two-headed bird foreshadows a future androgynous human with two spinal cords. He asserts that many founders were Masons, helped by a secret body in Europe for a purpose known only to a few, and that the unfinished pyramid sets out the nation's task.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p1": "Hall says the lion is king of the animals and, like the head of every kingdom, sacred to the sun, its mane standing for the sun's rays. The Mysteries' saying that the lion opens the secret book means that solar power opens the seed and frees the life within. The ancients believed the lion slept with its eyes open, so it stood for vigilance, and lions beside doorways mean divine protection; Solomon was often pictured as a lion. Priests in several Mysteries, especially the Egyptian, wore the skins of big cats. Hercules and Samson, both sun figures, killed the lion of Leo and wore its skin, showing they stood for the sun at its height. At Bubastis stood the temple of the cat goddess Bast. Egyptians honoured cats, especially three-coloured ones or those with odd eyes; priests kept them for the 'astral fire' they gave off, and a sleeping cat curled head to tail stood for eternity. Cats were sacred to Diana. Buddhists say the cat alone missed the Buddha's death because it stopped to chase a mouse, which Hall finds fitting for a symbol of the lower astral forces. He quotes Herodotus that cats leap into fires, that households shave their eyebrows when a cat dies, and that dead cats were embalmed and buried at Bubastis.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p2": "Hall calls the Apis bull of Memphis the most important symbolic animal, the sacred vehicle in which the soul of Osiris was reborn. It was said to be conceived by lightning, and its choosing and consecration were among Egypt's most impressive rites. Herodotus says it had to be black with a square white spot on its forehead, an eagle (probably a vulture) on its back, a beetle under its tongue and tail hairs lying two ways; others list twenty-nine sacred marks, a spotted body and a white crescent on its right side. It was kept in a stable beside the temple and led through the streets on solemn days, and any child it breathed on would become famous. At twenty-five it was drowned in the Nile or a sacred spring amid public mourning, which lasted until a new Apis was found and Osiris was said to be reborn. Bull worship was widespread: Nandi, Shiva's white bull, is still honoured in India, and Persians, Jews, Assyrians, Phoenicians, Chaldeans and Greeks honoured the bull; Jupiter became a white bull to carry off Europa. It stood for the fatherly creative power of the Demiurge. Dead bulls were mummified and buried like gods, and over sixty bull tombs have been found in the Serapeum at Memphis.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p3": "Hall says the sign rising at the spring equinox is the sun's body for the year; when that was Taurus, the bull was the sun-man and was called the opener of the year, often shown breaking the world-egg with its horns. The Apis shows that the divine mind takes on an animal body, so man's lower personality is the Apis in which Osiris lives, and the result is Serapis, the soul ruling, and bound in, the body. After twenty-five years, five squared, the Apis is drowned, which stands for washing away the material nature in the baptismal waters of light and truth: its death is death, and Osiris's return in a new bull is eternal renewal. The white bull was the emblem of initiates, the purified body of man and nature. When the spring equinox moved into Aries, the ram became the sun's vehicle, and the sun rising in the Celestial Lamb defeats the serpent of darkness. The lamb, gentle and white, stands for purity, for the universal saviour in many Mysteries and for Christ; early church paintings show a lamb on a hill with four streams, the four Gospels, flowing from its feet. The blood of the lamb is solar life pouring into the world through Aries.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p4": "Hall says the goat is a phallic symbol and also stands for courage and aspiration, being sure-footed on the highest peaks, and to alchemists its head meant sulphur. The Jewish scapegoat, loaded with the people's sins, is an allegory of the sun-man, on whom the sins of the twelve houses of heaven are laid. Truth is the divine lamb slain for the world's sins, and every saviour god has personified it. The Golden Fleece sought by Jason is the Celestial Lamb, the spiritual sun, and the secret teaching itself, the rays of the sun of truth; Suidas says it was really a book on skin giving formulas for making gold by chemistry. The Mysteries existed to turn ignorance into enlightenment, and the dragon guarding the fleece is ignorance, the darkness of the old year fighting the sun at the equinox. Deer were sacred in the Bacchic Mysteries, whose women wore fawnskins; linked with the moon goddess and night rites, the deer's grace and speed made it the symbol of ecstatic abandon, and herds are still kept at temples in Japan. The wolf, with its mournful howl and fierceness, stands for evil: the Fenris Wolf, Loki's son, devoured Odin as Asgard burned, standing for the mindless forces of nature that overthrew the first creation.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p5": "Hall calls the unicorn a strange creation of the ancient initiates. He quotes Thomas Boreman: a beast many doubt, said to have one rich horn in the middle of its forehead, a deer's head, an elephant's feet, a boar's tail and a horse's body; a horn a foot and a half long, hard as iron, rough as a file, twisted like a flaming sword and black except at the tip, thought to drive out poison and cure diseases; a voice like a lowing ox, yellowish hair, and no taste for prey. The Bible mentions it, but there is no proof it existed, and museum drinking horns said to be unicorn horn were probably tusks or rhinoceros horn. J. P. Lundy reads the horn as the 'horn of salvation' in Luke, and medieval mystics made the unicorn a sign of Christ, so it means the spiritual life in man; its single horn may be the pineal gland, the third eye. In the Mysteries it stood for the initiate's enlightened spirit, its horn the flaming sword of spiritual teaching. The Hermetic Book of Lambspring shows a deer and a unicorn in a wood: the forest is the body, the unicorn the spirit, the deer the soul, and whoever can tame and join them is a master.",
  "modern.hall.18.s2.p6": "Hall says the Egyptian devil Typhon was shown as the Set animal, an obscure creature with a snout and pointed ears, perhaps a stylized hyena, which lived in sandstorms spreading evil; the hyena's night howl sounded like a lost soul in Typhon's grip, yet it also guarded the dead against grave robbers. The hippopotamus was Typhon's too, sacred to Mars, who rules Scorpio, Typhon's house, and so was the ass: Jesus riding an ass into Jerusalem means the same as Hermes standing on Typhon, and early Christians were accused of worshipping an ass's head. The sow, sacred to Diana, stood for the occult art, as the boar that killed Atys shows. The monkey stands for man before the rational soul entered him; some saw it as unsouled, others as man fallen, and the ancients saw it as a branch that split from the main line of progress rather than man's ancestor. The dog-headed ape was the Egyptian sign of writing and companion of Thoth, as the moon follows Mercury. The faithful dog stands for the disciple and master, its tracking for the philosopher following truth, and Sirius foretold the Nile flood. The horse stands for body and spirit carrying each other; the winged horse and magic carpet for the secret teaching; and the Trojan horse and the siege of Troy for the soul's capture and rescue.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Lycosthenes' chronicle of prodigies, calls the phoenix the most famous of the symbolic creatures the Mysteries invented to hide the truths of their philosophy. Modern naturalists call it a myth, but Pliny says one was caught and shown in the Roman Forum in the reign of Claudius.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig2": "The caption, for two birds' heads, compares the bird on the first Great Seal of the United States (1782) with that on the 1902 seal. Hall says the first bird looked very different from today's eagle: a longer neck, a clear tuft at the back of the head, a beak unlike an eagle's, a thinner body and shorter wings. He says it takes little imagination to see the ancient phoenix in it, that a phoenix suits a new country rising from an old one, and that Benjamin Franklin noted the eagle was not a bird of good moral character.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Wilkinson's Manners and Customs of the Ancient Egyptians, says the Egyptians sometimes showed the phoenix with a man's body and a bird's wings, a tuft on its head and arms raised in prayer. Since the phoenix stood for rebirth, Hall suggests the tuft may stand for the working of the pineal gland, the third eye, whose hidden role he says the priests understood.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig4": "The caption, for the front and back of the Great Seal of the United States from Hunt's history, says the number 13 on it means more than the thirteen colonies. Thirteen stars form the initiates' sacred emblem over the 'eagle'; E Pluribus Unum and Annuit Coeptis each have 13 letters; the bird holds a branch with 13 leaves and 13 berries and a bundle of 13 arrows; and the pyramid, without its dated panel, has 72 stones in 13 rows.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig5": "The caption, for a picture of the sacred bull Apis from Kircher's Sphinx Mystagoga, refers back to the chapter on the zodiac for the bull as the sun at the spring equinox. The bull and ox stand for the element earth and the planet, and for man's animal nature, which is why they were sacrificed in the Jewish and Druid rites. It quotes Plutarch that the Apis is a fair image of the soul of Osiris. Osiris is the spirit of the lower world, killed and scattered through physical matter, and Apis is the material world that holds him. Apis also stands for the outer, public teaching, as against the inner teaching shown by the uraeus on the priests' brows. Hence Serapis combines Osiris and the world he dwells in, and also the Mysteries, the earthly bodies that hold the secret teaching.",
  "modern.hall.18.fig6": "The caption, for an illustration of Aeneas and the Harpies from Dryden's Virgil, says the harpies were among the Mysteries' creatures, unseen beings of nature given material form. The Greeks described them with maidens' heads and birds' bodies, with metal wings that clanged as they flew. Aeneas landed on their island and fought them in vain, and one harpy on a cliff foretold that his attack would bring disaster on the Trojans.",
  "modern.hall.19.p1": "Hall says nearly all ancient peoples honoured the female and male organs as fitting symbols of God's creative power. Eden, the Ark, the temple gate, the veil of the Mysteries, the oval vesica piscis and the Holy Grail are female symbols; the pyramid, obelisk, cone, candle, tower, standing stone, spire, bell tower, maypole and sacred spear are male. He says many modern writers judge pagans by their own vulgar minds, while the Eleusinian Mysteries set very high moral standards, and he answers critics with 'Honi soit qui mal y pense', shame on him who thinks evil of it. The obscene rites of later Bacchic festivals no more represented the original Mysteries than occasional Christian orgies represented early Christianity; Sir William Hamilton reported that in 1780 Christians at Isernia in Italy worshipped Priapus as 'St. Cosmo'. Father, mother and child are the natural trinity, and the Mysteries honoured the home; Pythagoras said the hearth is to the home what the central fire is to the universe. God shows himself as Father (spirit), Mother (matter) and Child (living nature), whose symbol is Euclid's 47th problem. Hall sees this as the true meaning of the Madonna and Child, and calls the mystery of life nature's greatest achievement.",
  "modern.hall.19.p2": "Hall says modern prudishness calls this mystery unfit for religious people, preferring innocence born of ignorance to virtue born of knowledge, but people must learn never to be ashamed of truth. He says Christianity has failed here: it calls the body God's temple and in the same breath calls its parts and functions unclean. Yet the cross is the oldest phallic emblem, the lozenge-shaped windows of cathedrals are female symbols, and the very church building is full of such emblems; remove them and nothing is left, since even the fertile earth it stands on was the first female symbol. Few notice the irony, and only those who know the secret language of antiquity see the sacred meaning. Flowers were chosen as symbols for many reasons, since there is one to fit almost any quality: a myth about its origin, like Daphne and Narcissus; where it grows, like the orchid and the fungus; its shape, like the passion flower and the Easter lily; its colour or scent, like verbena and lavender; its lasting form, like the everlasting flower; or some unusual trait, like the sunflower and heliotrope, sacred for turning toward the sun.",
  "modern.hall.19.p3": "Hall says plants were also honoured because their leaves, petals, stems or roots gave healing ointments, essences or drugs that affect the mind, like the poppy and the old herbs of prophecy, or because they looked like body parts and so were thought to heal them: fern juice, oak moss and thistledown to grow hair, the tooth-shaped dentaria for toothache, and the hand-shaped palma Christi for the hands. The flower is the plant's reproductive organ, so it fittingly stands for sexual purity, which the Mysteries required, and for beauty and rebirth replacing lust. The most important symbolic flowers are the lotus of India and Egypt and the rose of the Rosicrucians, which mean the same: the teachings of the Eastern lotus live on in Europe as the rose. Both are female emblems of the mother's creative mystery, while the Easter lily is male. Hall says Indian and Egyptian initiates, who knew how to awaken man's hidden energy centres, used lotuses for the spinning chakras along the spine, linked to nerve centres: four petals at the sacrum, six at the prostatic plexus, ten at the navel, twelve at the heart, sixteen at the throat, two at the cavernous plexus, and a thousand at or near the pineal gland. Aaron's budding rod and the Pope's flowering staff in Tannhauser point to the same unfolding along the spine.",
  "modern.hall.19.p4": "Hall says the Rosicrucians used a garland of roses for the same energy centres, which the Bible calls the seven lamps of the candlestick and the seven churches of Asia; a 1642 frontispiece to Bacon's History of Henry the Seventh shows him with Rosicrucian roses on his shoe buckles. In Hindu thought each lotus petal bears a symbol. The lotus also stands for the three stages of human awareness, ignorance, effort and understanding, and as it lives in earth, water and air, man lives in the material, intellectual and spiritual worlds. Rooted in mud, growing up through water and flowering in the light, it shows the soul rising from base desire through the shifting world of illusion into truth; so Eastern gods sit on open lotuses. The lotus was everywhere in Egyptian art: lotus columns held up temple roofs as eternal wisdom, and lotus-topped sceptres were carried in processions. Nine petals meant man; twelve, the universe and the gods; seven, the planets and the law; five, the senses and the Mysteries; three, the chief gods and the worlds. The medieval heraldic rose usually has five or ten petals, linking it with man through the Pythagorean five and ten.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p1": "Hall says tree worship was common throughout the ancient world. Temples stood in sacred groves, night rites were held under great trees decorated for their gods, and the trees themselves were often thought to have divine power and were prayed to. The size and strength of oaks, elms and cedars made them symbols of power, integrity, permanence, virility and protection. Hindus and Norsemen saw the universe as a divine tree grown from one seed sown in space, and Greeks, Persians, Chaldeans and Japanese told of an axle-tree or reed on which the earth turns. Kapila called the universe the eternal tree Brahma, sprung from an invisible seed. Medieval Kabbalists drew creation as a tree rooted in spirit with its branches in physical illusion, so their tree of the sephiroth hangs upside down, roots in heaven; Blavatsky says the Great Pyramid was seen as this inverted tree, its root at the apex. The Norse world-tree Yggdrasil holds nine worlds, which the Egyptians showed as the nine stamens of the persea, all inside a tenth sphere, the cosmic egg; the Kabbalah's tree likewise has nine branches inside the Crown. The trunk is the single source, the roots draw divine nourishment, and the branches are the endless effects of one cause.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p2": "Hall says the tree also stands for man: he exists first unseen within the world-tree and later blossoms on its branches, and a Greek myth says Zeus made the third race of men from ash trees. The serpent wound around the tree is the mind, the tempter that drives every reasoning being to discover reality and so overthrow the gods' rule; in the world-tree it is the cosmic mind, in the human tree the individual intellect. Since all life comes from seeds, grain and plants stood for human seed, and the tree for life unfolding from its germ, as the oak holds every branch and leaf in the acorn. Wise and upright people were called trees: the Druids' name may mean 'oak men', some Syrian initiates were 'cedars', and Hall thinks the cedars of Lebanon cut for Solomon's Temple were really initiated sages, the true supports of God's house. Trees fill the scriptures: the trees of life and of knowledge, the burning bush, the vine and fig tree, the olives of Gethsemane and the healing tree of Revelation. Hall says the Buddha was enlightened under the bodhi tree near Madras, and that sages carried rods from sacred trees, like Moses and Aaron, Odin's spear Gungnir from the Tree of Life, and Hermes's serpent staff.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p3": "Hall says trees were also worshipped for their usefulness, and quotes J. P. Lundy: they hold moisture, shade springs and soil, and give shade, fruit, medicine, fuel, timber for houses and ships, and furniture, so it is no wonder the oak, pine, palm and sycamore became sacred. Early church fathers sometimes used the tree for Christ, believing Christianity would grow like an oak over all other faiths. Shedding and regrowing its leaves, the tree stands for resurrection and reincarnation. The Tree of Life and the Tree of the Knowledge of Good and Evil hide the great secret of balance: the Tree of Life is the point of balance and the secret of immortality, the other is polarity and the secret of mortality. The Kabbalists show this by giving the middle column of their tree to the Tree of Life and the side columns to the other, and a secret text says 'unbalanced forces perish in the void'. The apple is knowledge of procreation, by which the material world began. Hall calls the literal Eden story absurd but its creative meaning profound. The Ophites honoured the Eden serpent as the cause of individual existence. Humanity still wanders among good and evil but will at last eat from the Tree of Life, the emblem of the Mysteries, and become immortal.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p4": "Hall names five trees of greatest symbolic weight: the oak for the Father God, the pine for the saviour or world martyr, the ash for the world axis and the divine in man, the cypress for the goddesses, and the flower of the male date palm for the male pole of generation. The pine cone is a very old phallic symbol, and the thyrsus of Bacchus, a staff topped by a cone or grapes and wound with ivy or vine, means nature's wonders need the sun's virility. In the Phrygian Mysteries the sun-saviour Atys dies under a pine, the sun at midwinter, so the pine was sacred to him, and to Dionysus and Apollo. Egyptians and Jews revered the acacia or tamarisk, and Masons still treat sprigs of acacia, cypress, cedar or evergreen as key emblems; the shittim wood of the Tabernacle and Ark was a kind of acacia. Albert Pike says the true acacia is the thorny tamarisk that grew around Osiris's body, was the Arabs' idol Al-Uzza destroyed by Muhammad, made the crown of thorns, and is so hardy that a doorpost of it can take root and sprout. Hall thinks some of the acacia's honour comes from the sensitive plant, the mimosa, often confused with it; a Coptic legend says it was the first plant to worship Christ. Its fast growth and beauty also made it a sign of fertility.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p5": "Hall gives four meanings for the acacia: the spring equinox and the sun god's yearly rising; purity and innocence, as the sensitive plant that shrinks from touch and as one Greek sense of its name; human immortality and rebirth, as the evergreen part of man that outlives the body; and the emblem of the Mysteries, whose candidates carried sacred branches or flowers through the winding passages. Albert Mackey notes that each Mystery had its own sacred plant, later used as the sign of its degrees: lettuce for Adonis, the lotus for Brahmins and Egyptians, mistletoe for the Druids and myrtle in some Greek rites. Since the CHiram legend comes from the Osiris rite, the acacia stands for CHiram's rising: Osiris's coffin washed ashore near Byblos and was enclosed by a growing tamarisk, the source of the acacia sprig on CHiram's grave, and the evergreen on the dead sun god's grave lives on as the Christmas tree. Apricot and quince are female symbols, grapes and figs male. The pomegranate belongs to Eleusis: eating it bound Proserpine to Pluto's realm, as tasting the life of the senses costs immortality. With its many seeds it meant fertility and, Bryant says, the Ark; the Cabiri called it 'the forbidden secret'. Gods hold it as givers of plenty, and it crowned Solomon's pillars and adorned the High Priest's robe.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p6": "Hall says strong wine stood for the false life and false light of the world, because it comes from an unnatural process, fermentation. Drink clouds reason and frees the animal nature, so the grape and wine stood for the Adversary, the lower nature tempting man to excess. The Egyptians thought grape juice the closest thing to human blood and believed the vine drew its life from the buried dead. Plutarch says the sun priests at Heliopolis brought no wine into their temples and poured it only as the blood of enemies of the gods, believing the first vines grew from earth fattened by those who fell in the war against the gods, which is why heavy drinking drives men mad, filling them with their ancestors' blood. Some cults saw drunkenness as close to ecstasy, possession by the spirit of life whose vehicle is the vine. In the Mysteries the grape often meant lust and debauchery, but since fermentation shows the presence of the solar fire, it was also the symbol of the solar spirit, giver of divine enthusiasm. Likewise Christians take wine as Christ's blood in communion; Christ, who said 'I am the vine', was honoured with the wine of ecstasy like Bacchus, Dionysus, Atys and Adonis before him.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p7": "Hall says the mandrake was credited with remarkable magical powers. The Greeks used it as an anaesthetic in surgery, and it has been identified with baaras, the herb the Jews used to drive out demons; Josephus says baaras flashes like lightning and kills anyone who touches it except by rules said to come from Solomon. Its little-understood powers made it a charm for increasing whatever it was kept with, a sure cure for sterility, and one of the phallic objects the Templars were accused of worshipping. Its root looks strikingly like a human body, often with a head, arms or legs, which Hall calls a puzzle of science and the real reason it was revered; Blavatsky says it seems to stand on land where plants and animals meet, as polyps do in the sea. Popular belief said the mandrake shrank from touch and screamed with a human voice when pulled up, killing or maddening anyone who heard it. So people dug around it, tied it to a dog, and called the dog, which pulled it out and suffered the curse; once uprooted, it could be handled safely.",
  "modern.hall.19.s1.p8": "Hall says mandrake charms fetched high prices in the Middle Ages, and people learned to carve the roots to look even more human. Like most superstitions, the belief rested on an old secret teaching about the plant's nature. He quotes Eliphas Levi: the mandrake is mildly narcotic and was thought to be an aphrodisiac sought by Thessalian witches for love potions; some mystics suggest it is a trace of man's earthly origin, and Levi, without affirming it, says man did come from the earth's slime and may first have been a rough sketch, giant sun-quickened 'mandragores' who pulled themselves up out of the soil. The Egyptians honoured the onion as a symbol of the universe, its layers matching the levels of creation, and as a strong medicine. Garlic was a powerful agent in magic; Hall says nothing better has been found against obsession, and that vampirism and some kinds of madness, especially from mediumship and elemental 'larvae', respond to it at once; in the Middle Ages it was thought to keep evil from a house. Three-leaved plants like the shamrock stood for the Trinity, as St Patrick is said to have taught, and a fourth leaf stands for man, so it means humanity redeemed. Wreaths were worn at initiations, and Richard Payne Knight says evergreens on coins were Dionysiac symbols of lasting youth and vigour.",
  "modern.hall.19.fig1": "The caption describes a carved tree at the Chateau de Pierrefonds in northern France. Its eight side branches end in cup-like flowers, each with a knight rising from it holding a ribbon with his name, and the central stem ends in a larger flower from which King Arthur rises. The tree is a favourite motif in heraldry, and its single trunk with many branches made it the usual way to draw family descent, hence 'family trees'.",
  "modern.hall.19.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from the 'Breeches' Bible of 1599, says most Bibles of that period include family tables tracing humanity from Adam to Christ. Here a tree growing from the roof of the Ark stands for Noah, its three branches for his sons Shem, Ham and Japheth, and circles on the branches for the nations descended from them. Hall calls such tables hopelessly wrong as history but very valuable to the student of symbols.",
  "modern.hall.19.fig3": "The caption, for a diagram from Kircher's book on magnetism, says it shows an experiment in plant magnetism. Egyptians, Greeks and Hindus held some plants sacred because of the sun's effect on them. Since people cannot look at the sun without being blinded, plants that turned to face it were thought to stand for very advanced souls, and since the sun stood for the supreme god, plants under its strong influence were sacred. The sunflower, plainly drawn to the sun, ranked high among them.",
  "modern.hall.19.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from the Museum Hermeticum, says alchemists drew their metals as a tree to show that all seven were branches of one trunk of solar life, as the seven spirits are branches of God, who is root, trunk and soil. It quotes the Gloria Mundi: trees, herbs, stones and metals all grow to perfection without human hands. Metals grow in their ores inside the earth through the four elements, warmed by the sun and moon like grain in a field, and each mountain bears its own ore as each tree bears its own fruit.",
  "modern.hall.20.p1": "Hall says each of the four elements has its match in man: rock and earth in bones and flesh, water in the body's fluids, air in its gases, and fire in its heat. Bones, the frame of the body, stand for the spirit that holds up mind, soul and body, and the skeleton with a scythe is Saturn, father of the gods, with the sickle he used on his own father Ouranos. In the Mysteries men's spirits are 'the powdered bones of Saturn', who was worshipped as the base of creation. Hall says the Saturn myth rests on Greek and Phoenician memories of a king of that name who ruled ancient Hyperborea, and that lost lands like Polaris, Hyperborea and Atlantis, buried under today's continents and oceans, were pictured as rocks bearing new lands and races. In Norse myth stones and cliffs came from the bones of the giant Ymir; to the Greeks rocks were the bones of Gaia. After the flood that ended the Iron Age, an oracle told Deucalion and Pyrrha to throw their mother's bones behind them; they threw stones, which became men and women. Hall reads this as human evolution: spirit, living in matter, raises mineral to plant, plant to animal, animal to man and man to god.",
  "modern.hall.20.p2": "Hall says the solar system was shaped by forces working inward from Saturn's ring, and since Saturn ruled the beginning of everything, the first worship was probably of him and his symbol, the stone. Saturn is the spiritual rock under the solar temple, and the earth is its lower counterpart. Stone worship is one of the oldest forms of religion; Godfrey Higgins says the first idol everywhere was a plain upright stone, an emblem of nature's procreative power. Its remains are found across the world, notably the thousands of standing stones at Carnac in Brittany, set in eleven rows, some over twenty feet high and weighing as much as 250,000 pounds; Hall thinks they most likely record ancient astronomy. Such cairns, dolmens and menhirs across Britain and Europe witness to vanished peoples. Rocking or logan stones show their skill: huge boulders balanced so a touch moves them but no force can topple them. The Greeks and Romans called them living stones, and the most famous, the Gygorian stone near Gibraltar, could be moved by a daffodil stalk; Hercules is said to have set one over the sons of Boreas. Traces of one have been found at Stonehenge, whose inner green stones were said to have come from Africa.",
  "modern.hall.20.p3": "Hall says many standing stones are uncarved because they are older than tools and writing. Some were shaped into columns or obelisks, like rune stones and Hindu lingams; others into rough human figures, like the Easter Island statues, or the carved figures of Central America and Cambodia. The first rough images were not of particular gods but early attempts to show God's procreative power in lasting stone. The sense that God is steady like stone survives in Christianity, in the rock of refuge, the rock on which the church is built, the rejected cornerstone, Jacob's anointed stone pillow, David's sling stone, the rock of Moriah under Solomon's altar, the white stone of Revelation and the Rock of Ages. Early people valued stones chiefly for their use: as the first weapons, as cliff fortresses, as shelters, as markers and monuments, and on graves to keep animals away. Migrating peoples carried stones from their homeland, which was sacred to them. Finding that striking stones made fire increased their reverence, but in the end fire worship replaced stone worship: the dark, cold father stone gave birth to the bright son, fire, which became the most impressive of all sacred symbols.",
  "modern.hall.20.p4": "Hall says the body of anything was likened to a stone, squared into a cube or carved into a pedestal, and its spirit to the figure on top; so altars stood for the lower world and the fires on them for the spirit lighting the body. The square is one face of a cube and its symbol, which is why Greeks, Brahmins and Egyptians spoke of the earth's four corners when they meant the element earth, though they knew the planet was round. Since the Mysteries' teachings were the foundation of knowledge and the first step to conscious immortality, they were shown as cubic or pyramid-shaped stones, which in turn stood for godhood won by one's own effort. The stone's changelessness made it an emblem of God and of the divine sciences. Hermes, the rational mind on which life rests, was shown the same way, as pillars topped with his bearded head, called herms, set up in public places. Terminus, god of boundaries and roads, whose name gives us 'terminal', was an upright stone at borders and crossroads. Hall says the philosopher's stone is really the philosophical stone: philosophy, like a magic jewel, turns base things into gems like itself, and wisdom turns many times its weight of ignorance into enlightenment.",
  "modern.hall.20.s1.p1": "Hall retells a legend from the Zohar: on Sinai Moses received two tablets of the Ten Commandments written by God's finger, cut from the divine sapphire Schethiya, which God had taken from his throne and thrown into the abyss as the foundation of the worlds. Made of heavenly dew, the stone was split by God's breath, the Law was written on it in black fire, and it was given to Moses on the Sabbath, who could read it from behind because the jewel was clear. The commandments are ten shining gems in the sapphire sea of being, reflected below as the laws of the lower world; the Pythagoreans honoured the same ten as the tetractys, ten being the number of perfection and the symbol of God, man and the universe. Because the Israelites worshipped idols, Moses judged them unworthy and broke the sapphire tablets, replacing them with rough stone ones cut with ten ancient letters: the first, of the Tree of Life, held eternal truths; the second, of the Tree of Good and Evil, only temporal ones, so Israel's ancient tradition went back to heaven, leaving its shadow. One stone tablet stood for the oral and the other for the written tradition of the rabbis. Accounts of their size vary from palm-sized to ten or twelve cubits long, and some say they were of a wood from Paradise called sedr.",
  "modern.hall.20.s1.p2": "Hall says the two tablets stand for the upper and lower worlds, the father and mother principles; whole, they are the cosmic androgyne, and breaking them means the parting of the upper and lower spheres and of the sexes. Greek and Egyptian processions carried an ark holding stone tablets, cones and vessels standing for procreation. The Israelites' Ark, modelled on the sacred chests of Isis, held three objects with phallic meanings: the pot of manna, the budding rod and the tablets, the three principles of the creative trinity, and also the Kabbalah, the Mishnah and the written Law, the spirit, soul and body of Judaism. When placed in Solomon's Temple it held only the tablets, and Hall asks whether the secret tradition had already been lost, leaving only the letter. The stone tablets were sacred to Jehovah, maker of the lower world, as the sapphire ones were to the power behind the heavenly world. Hall traces them to the paired pillars or obelisks at pagan temple doors, perhaps from an age when God was worshipped through Gemini, the twins. Hargrave Jennings says the commandments are written in two columns of five, the right meaning the Law and masculine, the left the Prophets and feminine, matching the two towers in front of every cathedral and pagan temple.",
  "modern.hall.20.s1.p3": "Hall says the right tablet also stands for Jachin, the white pillar of light, and the left for Boaz, the dark pillar, the two brass pillars on the porch of Solomon's Temple, eighteen cubits high and decorated with chains, nets and pomegranates. On top of each was a bowl, wrongly called a globe today, probably one of fire and one of water: the heavenly globe on Jachin stands for divine man, the earthly one on Boaz for earthly man. The pair also stand for active and passive divine energy, sun and moon, sulphur and salt, good and bad, light and dark, and standing at God's door they show that Jehovah is both male-female and human in form. As twin columns they are Cancer and Capricorn, birth and death, the summer and winter solstices, which Masons now call the 'two St. Johns'. On the Kabbalists' tree they are Mercy and Severity, like the obelisks before Egyptian temples, and their names together mean 'In strength shall My House be established.' The High Priest stood between them as the point of balance, the divine in man amid his twofold nature, the One before the Two, with the intellect on one side and the flesh on the other; he reaches that state only after suffering on the cross made by joining the pillars. Early Jews sometimes drew them as Jehovah's legs, wisdom and love holding up creation.",
  "modern.hall.20.s2.p1": "Hall tells how the Lapis Exilis, the green crown jewel of Lucifer, fell from heaven like the sapphire Schethiya: when Michael, archangel of the sun, led the angels against Lucifer's rebels, his flaming sword struck the stone from Lucifer's crown and it fell into the abyss. From it was made the Holy Grail, from which Christ is said to have drunk at the Last Supper. Accounts differ on whether the Grail was a cup or a dish, but art shows a large, beautiful chalice. Legend says Joseph of Arimathea caught Christ's blood in it at the cross, and later carried it and the spear of Longinus to a far country; one version puts them at Glastonbury Abbey, another in a castle built by angels in one night on Mount Salvat in Spain. Parsifal, the last Grail king, took the cup to India under the name Prester John, and it vanished from the West; the quest for it drove the knightly adventures of the Arthurian legends. Hall says no adequate explanation of the Grail Mysteries exists. Some see its knights as Christian mystics keeping the ancient wisdom alive; Mackey links the quest to the Masonic Lost Word; others see an old pagan nature myth grafted onto Christianity, the Grail being the vessel of the world's life, the body of Mother Nature, green like Venus and Islam.",
  "modern.hall.20.s2.p2": "Hall says the Grail stands for the lower world and for the human body, both vessels for the life of the higher worlds; the redeeming blood that descends into death overcomes it by filling all matter with its own immortality. For Christians it is the heart, filled with the living water of eternal life, and the quest for it is the search for the real self, the completion of the Great Work. Only those who have risen above sensual life can find it. In James Russell Lowell's poem The Vision of Sir Launfal, the old, broken knight, returning from his proud quest, sees the glowing chalice in a leper's cup, showing that the Grail is seen only in a certain state of spirit. Some writers link it to the stories of slain sun gods whose blood falls into the cup of matter and is freed by initiation. It may also be the seed pod used in the Mysteries for germination and resurrection, and if its cup shape comes from a flower it means the spiritualizing of the generative forces. Hall adds that many accounts tell of stone statues, made of chosen materials with special rites, that were inhabited by the gods they portrayed and could speak, think or move; some priests cheated, as the apocryphal Bel and the Dragon shows, but he thinks many such events can be explained only by supernatural agency.",
  "modern.hall.20.s2.p3": "Hall says history tells of stones whose sound threw hearers into ecstasy, of statues that echoed whispers for hours, and of musical stones. Greeks and Romans laid their hands on sacred pillars to swear oaths, and juries gave verdicts by dropping pebbles into a bag. The Greeks practised divination by stones, and Helen is said to have foretold the fall of Troy this way. Superstitions about stones survived the Middle Ages, chief among them the black stone under the coronation chair in Westminster Abbey, said to be Jacob's pillow. Black stones appear often in religion: one, called Heliogabalus after the Syrian sun god Elagabal, was sacred to the sun and credited with many powers. The black stone in the Kaaba at Mecca is still revered by Muslims; it is said to have been white at first, so bright it could be seen days away, and to have been darkened over the ages by pilgrims' tears and the world's sins.",
  "modern.hall.20.s3.p1": "Hall says the Mysteries taught that the rays of the heavenly bodies, meeting the crystallizing forces of the lower world, become the elements, which carry their source's virtues, balance unbalanced heavenly forces and, rightly combined, help human health. Little is known of this today, but he thinks the early philosophers' findings deserve attention. From such study came the idea that metals were the bones of the gods: Manetho says the Egyptians called iron the bone of Mars and the lodestone the bone of Horus, so lead would be Saturn's skeleton, copper Venus's, quicksilver Mercury's, gold the sun's, silver the moon's and antimony the earth's; Hall suggests uranium may prove to be Uranus's metal and radium Neptune's. The Greek Golden, Silver, Bronze and Iron Ages are the four stages of every life, dawn, noon, sunset and midnight, or birth, growth, maturity and decay, matching the four Hindu yugas. He quotes a calculation: 1,800 minutes in each of 12 signs make 21,600, which times 80, 60, 40 and 20 gives the yugas of 1,728,000, 1,296,000, 864,000 and 432,000 years, multipliers that he notes run in the reverse of the tetractys, 4, 3, 2, 1.",
  "modern.hall.20.s3.p2": "Hall says Blavatsky claims Orpheus taught how to sway a whole audience with a lodestone and Pythagoras studied the colour and nature of gems; she says Buddhists hold the sapphire brings peace of mind, drives off evil thoughts, opens barred doors for the spirit and stirs the wish to pray, but must be worn only by those who live purely. Myths are full of magic rings and jewels. Plato tells of the ring of Gyges, which made its wearer invisible when its setting was turned inward and won him the throne of Lydia. Josephus describes magic rings made by Moses and Solomon, and Aristotle one that brought love and honour. Agrippa, citing Philostratus, says Apollonius of Tyana lengthened his life with seven rings from an Indian prince, each set with the gem of one planet of the week and changed daily to guard him from sickness and death, and that Apollonius taught their virtues as essential to the theurgist. Agrippa explains how to make such a ring: when a planet rises favourably with the moon, take a stone and herb belonging to it, make a ring of its metal, set the stone with the herb beneath, and add the proper inscriptions, names, symbols and incense.",
  "modern.hall.20.s3.p3": "Hall says the ring stands for attainment, perfection and immortality, having no beginning or end. Initiates wore rings shaped as a serpent biting its tail to show their rank, hierophants wore signet rings with secret emblems, and a messenger proved he spoke for a prince by bringing his master's seal or ring. The wedding ring first meant that its wearer had reached balance and completeness: the plain gold band witnessed the union of the higher self (God) with the lower self (nature), the 'Hermetic marriage' of the Mysteries. Eliphas Levi describes a magician's regalia for each day: on Sunday a gold wand with a ruby or chrysolite; on Monday a three-strand collar of pearls, crystals and selenites; on Tuesday a magnetized steel wand and a steel ring with an amethyst; on Wednesday a necklace of pearls or glass beads holding mercury and an agate ring; on Thursday a glass or resin wand and an emerald or sapphire ring; on Friday a polished copper wand, a turquoise ring and a crown with lapis lazuli and beryl; on Saturday an onyx wand, an onyx ring and a lead chain. Hall then lists the gems that magicians and astrologers gave to the planets, for example the ruby and fiery stones to the sun, pearl and crystal to the moon, onyx and jasper to Saturn, sapphire and emerald to Jupiter, amethyst and lodestone to Mars, turquoise and beryl to Venus, and chrysolite and agate to Mercury.",
  "modern.hall.20.s3.p4": "Hall lists the gems the same writers gave to the twelve signs, from Aries (sardonyx, bloodstone, amethyst, diamond) through Taurus, Gemini, Cancer, Leo, Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius, Capricorn and Aquarius, to Pisces (sapphire, jasper, chrysolite, moonstone, amethyst). He warns that the magic mirror and crystal ball are little understood, and that anyone who takes the stories about them literally will learn, often at the cost of health and sanity, that sorcery and philosophy have nothing in common. The Persian Magi carried mirrors as an emblem of the material world, which reflects God from every part. The crystal ball, long misused to develop psychic powers, has three meanings: the clear world-egg in which creation exists; God before entering matter; and the ethereal sphere of the world, in which a perfect image of everything that happens on earth is recorded. Meteorites, rocks from heaven, were enshrined as signs of a pact between the gods and the place where they fell. Oddly marked natural stones are also prized, like a Chinese marble slab whose grain forms a perfect dragon, and the Oberammergau stone, shaped by nature like the face of Christ, which even European royalty asked to see.",
  "modern.hall.20.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Cartari's Images of the Gods of the Ancients, says Saturn, warned that one of his children would overthrow him, swallowed each at birth. To save Jupiter, her sixth child, Rhea gave him a stone wrapped in swaddling clothes, which he swallowed unaware. Jupiter grew up hidden on Crete and then forced his father to bring up the five children he had eaten. Jupiter set the swallowed stone at Delphi, where it was greatly honoured and anointed daily.",
  "modern.hall.20.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from an old Bible, says Maimonides, the twelfth-century Jewish philosopher, writing in his Guide for the Perplexed about the tablets written by God's finger, divides all things made into works of nature and works of art: God works through nature and man through art, so God's word is the hand by which the Creator's will is traced on creation. Only the Tannaim, the initiates of the Jewish mystery school, fully understood the Ten Commandments, which secretly correspond to the ten steps of contemplation on the 'Path of Ecstasy', which climbs through the four worlds and ends in the radiance of the Ain Soph.",
  "modern.hall.20.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Christie's book on painted Greek vases, says the early custom of worshipping gods as heaps of stones gave way to phallic pillars or cones. Some were huge and richly carved with inscriptions or images of gods and heroes; others, like Babylonian votive cones, were a few inches high and bore only a short dedication or hymn. These small clay cones meant the same as the large herms set up by roads and in public places. Later the pillars were topped with a human head, often with shoulder-like pegs to hold garlands, food offerings were left beside them, and some held up roofs or decorated the villas of rich Romans.",
  "modern.hall.20.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Cartari, says the Pythagoreans linked the number five with healing and made the five-pointed star their symbol of health. The picture shows a magic ring with a gem bearing the pentalpha, a star made of five Greek letter Alphas. Mackey says the Pythagoreans, its real inventors, put a letter of the Greek word for health (or the Latin SALUS) in each of its inner angles, used it as a health charm and as a greeting at the head of their letters, and that across the East it was used as a charm against evil spirits.",
  "modern.hall.21.p1": "Hall defines ceremonial magic as the old art of calling up and controlling spirits through set formulas: a magician in consecrated robes, holding a wand marked with symbols, could command the unseen inhabitants of the elements and the astral world by the power of words and signs. Such magic was not evil in itself, but its corruption produced schools of sorcery or black magic. Hall says that in Egypt the black magicians of Atlantis went on using their powers until they corrupted the original Mysteries: they formed a priestly caste, took the place of the initiates, seized spiritual rule, made black magic the state religion, and demanded blind acceptance of priestly dogma, turning Pharaoh into the puppet of a 'Scarlet Council' of arch-sorcerers. They set out to destroy every key to the ancient wisdom so no one could reach mastery outside their order, mangled the rituals, encouraged worship of images that the wise had set up only for study, gave false meanings to the symbols and built confusing theologies. The masses, robbed of understanding, became slaves of these impostors, and Hall says humanity still suffers from the priestcraft of Atlantis and Egypt. Many medieval Kabbalists practised ceremonial magic, believing their scriptures allowed it, following the formulas credited to Solomon, the Jews' prince of magicians.",
  "modern.hall.21.p2": "Hall says many medieval Kabbalists became black magicians, straying from the noble ideas of the Sepher Yetzirah into demon worship and witchcraft, and tried to replace a virtuous life, which alone brings true completion, with magic mirrors, consecrated daggers and circles of coffin nails. Those who sought to command elemental spirits mostly wanted rare knowledge or supernatural power. Napoleon's 'little red demon' and the de Medici oracle heads show the disaster of letting elementals steer human affairs. The wise daemon of Socrates seems an exception, but proves that the magician's own character decides what kind of spirit he can call, and even it left Socrates when he was condemned. Hall calls all magic of marvels a blind alley grown out of Atlantean sorcery, whose followers nearly always come to grief: a man who cannot rule his own appetites cannot rule fiery elemental spirits. Many magicians have died from letting such creatures into their lives. He asks what Eliphas Levi hoped to gain by calling up Apollonius of Tyana, who had refused his secrets to the curious while alive; Levi himself did not claim the apparition was really Apollonius, knowing that elementals impersonate the dead. Hall says most seance apparitions are elementals wearing bodies made of thought-stuff supplied by the sitters.",
  "modern.hall.21.s1": "Hall sets out the premises of ceremonial magic. First, the visible world has an invisible counterpart, whose higher levels hold good spirits and whose lower levels hold evil spirits and demons under the fallen angel and his ten princes. Second, secret rites can contact these beings and win their help; good spirits help any worthy aim, evil ones only those bent on corruption and destruction. Third, a magician can make a contract that makes him master of an elemental for a fixed time. Fourth, true black magic uses a demon who serves the sorcerer for life on condition that the sorcerer serves the demon after death, which is why black magicians go to any length to live longer. The worst black magic is the deliberate misuse of occult power for personal desire; its commonest form is ordinary selfishness, the root of all evil. It covers most ceremonial magic, necromancy, witchcraft, sorcery and vampirism, and mesmerism and hypnotism except for medical use, which is still risky. Hall says it lives on in 'prosperity' psychology, willpower metaphysics and high-pressure salesmanship. He tells of Dr Johannes Faust, a real sixteenth-century magician, whose elemental served him for years; he hatched eggs by throwing his cloak over them and was pulled dry from the water, but was found stabbed in the back, supposedly by his familiar. Faust wrote a book on spirits, extracted next, and is not Fust the printer.",
  "modern.hall.21.s2.p1": "Hall gives a shortened translation of a book attributed to Faust, ordered destroyed. Faust says he read tirelessly from youth and found a book of invocations teaching that spirits of fire, water, earth and air can be forced to obey a magician strong enough, each suited to different tasks. Doubtful at first, he tried an invocation, and a mighty spirit appeared at once asking why he had called. Amazed, he asked it to serve him; it agreed on condition of a pact. He did not want one, but having foolishly not protected himself with a circle, he dared not refuse, and agreed to sign himself over in return for service for a set time. The spirit, Asteroth, introduced him to Marbuel as his servant; asked how fast he was, Marbuel said 'as swift as the winds', and Faust sent him away. Aniguel, 'as swift as a bird', was also too slow. Aciel said 'as swift as human thought' and was accepted, serving faithfully for a long time. Faust says he will not tell how Aciel served him, but only how to call spirits and make protective circles, and begins a list of spirits that will serve men.",
  "modern.hall.21.s2.p2": "Hall continues Faust's list. Aciel is the mightiest, appears in pleasant human form about three feet tall, must be called three times, brings riches and fetches things instantly from afar, as swift as thought. Aniguel comes as a ten-year-old boy, is called three times, and finds buried treasure and minerals. Marbuel, lord of the mountains, swift as a bird, is stubborn and hard to control, must be called four times, appears as Mars in heavy armour, supplies what grows above and below ground, and is lord of the spring-root. Hall's note explains this as a magic herb that opens whatever it touches; magicians blocked a woodpecker's nest so the bird would fetch the root to pull out the stopper, then took it, and some held that elementals worked through it. Aciebel rules the sea and recovers sunken ships and treasure, the faster the more sharply he is called. Machiel comes as a beautiful maiden, brings honour and dignity, makes her masters noble and gracious, helps in lawsuits, and must be called twice. Baruel, master of all arts, appears as a craftsman in an apron, can teach more in a moment than all master workmen in twenty years, and must be called three times.",
  "modern.hall.21.s2.p3": "Hall ends Faust's extract: to get help, first draw the sign of the spirit wanted, Aciel for money, in front of a circle made before sunrise. Mark a great cross with a sword that has never hurt anyone, then make three circles: the inner of virgin parchment hung on twelve crosses of cross-thorn wood and written with names and symbols; the second of new white paper written with an unused pen, wound with red silk spun to the left and pinned on twelve crosses of laurel leaves; the third of virgin parchment on twelve crosses of consecrated palm. Stand on a pentagram in the middle of the cross, read the invocation, speak the spirit's name clearly, and mind the day and hour, since each spirit can be called only at certain times. Hall then says the black magician who signs a pact believes he can control these powers, but soon has to spend all his strength on survival, living on the edge of a whirlpool of horrors his greed has drawn near. Afraid to die because he will serve his demon, he commits crime after crime to stay alive, often becoming an occult vampire who steals the universal life force from others; medieval belief said such magicians became werewolves, attacking victims at night for the life in their blood.",
  "modern.hall.21.s3.p1": "Hall reproduces, as an example of ceremonial magic, a shortened extract from The Complete Book of Magic Science, an unpublished manuscript in the British Museum mentioned by Francis Barrett. The opening prayer asks God to send a spirit of the order of Jupiter, a messenger of Zadkiel, the angel then governing the heavens, to show the magician what he asks and carry out his wishes, ending 'thy will and not mine be done'. Then, with robes and tools consecrated and standing in his circle, the magician calls on the spirits: he shows them the sign and holy names of God, orders them out of their caves and dark places to stop tormenting mortals, come to the place, attend to his orders and not think resistance will make him give up, commanding them by the names Elohe, Agla, Elohim, Adonay, Gibor. He calls on Zadkiel in the name of the Trinity, begs him to attend by further holy names, and conjures him by many more divine names, Greek and Hebrew, to send Pabiel or another serving spirit to show him whatever he asks.",
  "modern.hall.21.s3.p2": "Hall continues the manuscript's ritual. The magician begs Pabiel, by all the orders of angels and further angel names, by his star Jupiter and the constellations, and by the seal he has given, to come at once, show himself, speak audibly and fulfil the magician's wishes, and to send a serving spirit in the form of a beautiful angel who will come gently and courteously, let no evil spirit harm or frighten him, and not deceive him, all in Christ's name, ending 'Fiat, fiat, fiat. Amen.' Then come the questions. The magician asks whether the spirit comes in peace in the name of the Trinity, and it answers yes; asks its name, and it answers Pabiel; reminds it that he called it in the name of Jesus, before whom every knee bows; asks whether it is Zadkiel's messenger, and it answers yes; and asks whether it will reveal whatever he wishes to know, teach him wisdom and show him the secrets of magic and of all the liberal sciences for God's glory, and it answers yes. He then asks it to give him its seal so he can call it at any time and to swear an oath, promising in turn to keep his vow to God and to receive the spirit courteously. A 'License to Depart' follows.",
  "modern.hall.21.s3.p3": "Hall ends the ritual: the magician thanks God that the spirit came in peace and answered, lets it go in peace back to its order, to return whenever he calls it by its oath, name, order or office, and closes with the Gloria. A note advises staying in the circle a few minutes after the dismissal, and outdoors removing all traces of the circle and going quietly home; indoors the circle may be kept for later use but the room must be locked against strangers. Hall says this agreement belongs to ceremonial magic. In black magic it is the magician, not the demon, who signs the pact, in his own blood, since 'he controls the soul who controls the blood of another'. The demon keeps its side to the letter while the magician keeps his, but tries every way to stop him keeping it. Hall gives sample terms: the spirit's body and soul after fifty years; or alms given every Friday in Lucifer's name, forfeit at the first failure; or, at last, a pact promising Lucifuge, prince of demons, one human soul each year in return for the earth's treasures and every wish for life, with the magician's own soul forfeit if he fails, signed in blood.",
  "modern.hall.21.s4": "Hall says that in symbolism an upside-down figure always means a perverted power. Few people suspect the occult powers of magical pentacles; Paracelsus wrote that many would mock the power of metals and written signs, though no one has proved them dead, and the salts, sulphur and quintessences of metals are the best preservers of life. A black magician cannot use white magic's symbols without calling its forces down on himself, so he distorts them, showing that he distorts what they stand for. Black magic has no symbols of its own because it is only the misuse of an art; it takes white magic's figures and reverses them to show it is 'left-handed'. The pentagram, a five-pointed star of five joined lines, is the old symbol of magic: the five properties of the great magical agent, the five senses, the five elements and the five extremities of the body, and through it a person can master lower beings and earn the respect of higher ones. When black magic uses it, it is broken at one point, turned with one point down and two up, or drawn with uneven points. So used, it is called the 'sign of the cloven hoof' or the Devil's footprint; with two points up it is the 'Goat of Mendes', shaped like a goat's head; and the upright star falling means the fall of the Morning Star.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig1": "The caption, for Baphomet, the Goat of Mendes, from Levi's Transcendental Magic, says all magic, white or black, depends on the adept's control of the universal life force, which Levi calls the great magical agent or astral light, and works its wonders by handling that fluid. The hermaphrodite Goat of Mendes is a composite figure made to stand for this astral light. It is the same as Baphomet, the mystic all-god of the Templars, who probably got it from the Arabs.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig2": "The caption, for a magic sword from Levi's Magical Ritual, gives Levi's directions: forge the steel blade in the hour of Mars with new tools; make the pommel of hollow silver filled with mercury, engraved with the signs of Mercury and the moon and the signatures of Gabriel and Samael; cover the hilt with tin bearing the sign of Jupiter and Michael's signature; run a copper triangle a little way along each side of the blade with the signs of Mercury and Venus; engrave five sephiroth on the handle; write Malchut on one side of the blade and Quis ut Deus on the other; and consecrate the sword on a Sunday.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig3": "The caption, for a magic circle from The Complete Book of Magic Science, says it faithfully shows a circle designed by medieval conjurers for calling spirits. The magician and his assistant stand where the central lines cross, marked MAGISTER. The words around the circle are names of unseen intelligences, and the small crosses mark where certain prayers are said. The small circle outside is for the spirit being called, and while in use the sign of that spirit is drawn in its triangle.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig4": "The caption, for the pentagram from Levi's Transcendental Magic, calls it the figure of the microcosm, the magical formula of man: the one rising out of the four, the human soul rising from its animal nature, the true 'star of the morning'. It marks five hidden centres of force whose awakening is the highest secret of white magic.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig5": "The caption, for a form of pact with the spirit of Jupiter from The Complete Book of Magic Science, says the bond, with the seal and sign of the planet's angel, must be written on virgin parchment and laid before the spirit to sign when it appears. The magician must not lose confidence but be patient, firm, bold and persistent, and ask nothing except for God's glory and the good of others. Once he has what he wants, he may dismiss the spirit.",
  "modern.hall.21.fig6": "The caption, for a page from an unpublished medieval book of spirits, says the seven large circles are the planets, and the two small circles under each hold the seal and the sign of the intelligence that rules that planet.",
  "modern.hall.22.p1": "Hall says the clearest account of the spirit-world of nature comes from Paracelsus, whom he calls the prince of alchemists and holder of the philosopher's stone and elixir of life. Paracelsus held that each of the four elements has a fine, vaporous side and a coarse, bodily side: ordinary air and an unseen 'spiritual air'; visible fire and a spiritual flame working through it; dense water and a fluid essence; and fixed earth and a fine, mobile earth. 'Elements' names the physical side and 'elemental essences' the invisible side. Minerals, plants, animals and people live in the coarse elements and build their bodies from them. Hall quotes Henry Drummond and Huxley that all life starts from protoplasm, a clear jelly of carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen, 'the clay of the Potter', and matches the old elements with these: water with hydrogen, air with oxygen, fire with nitrogen and earth with carbon. As visible nature teems with life, Paracelsus taught that its invisible counterpart is inhabited by beings he called elementals, later called nature spirits, in four groups: gnomes, undines, sylphs and salamanders. They are real and living, many shaped like people, with worlds of their own that our undeveloped senses cannot perceive.",
  "modern.hall.22.p2": "Hall says the Greeks, Romans, Egyptians, Chinese and Indians firmly believed in satyrs, sprites and goblins, filling the sea with mermaids, rivers and springs with nymphs, the air with fairies, the fire with household spirits, and the earth with fauns and tree nymphs. They honoured these spirits with offerings, and sometimes, through weather conditions or a person's sensitivity, the spirits became visible; many writers spoke of them as if they had seen them, and some think many pagan gods were elementals of great size and dignity. The Greeks called the higher ones daemons and worshipped them, the most famous being the spirit that guided Socrates. Hall thinks Socrates' daemon and Jakob Boehme's angel were probably not elementals but these men's own higher divine natures. He quotes Thomas Taylor that Socrates' daemon was of the highest order and rightly called a god, as Socrates himself suggests in the First Alcibiades and the Apology. The idea that the unseen elements are full of intelligent beings may seem absurd today, but great minds have held it, and history records the sylphs of Cardan, the salamander Cellini saw, St Anthony's pan, and Napoleon's 'little red man'.",
  "modern.hall.22.p3": "Hall says literature keeps the idea alive: Shakespeare's Puck, the spirits in Pope's Rape of the Lock, the beings in Bulwer-Lytton's Zanoni, Barrie's Tinker Bell, and the bowlers Rip Van Winkle met in the Catskills. Folklore everywhere tells of little people who haunt castles, guard buried treasure and live under toadstools; children give them up reluctantly, and Hall says it is still open whether Plato, Socrates and Iamblichus were wrong to affirm them. Paracelsus distinguished two kinds of flesh: the visible flesh inherited from Adam, and a finer flesh not from Adam, free of its limits, of which elementals are made; the difference is as great as between matter and spirit. Yet, he says, they are not spirits, since they have flesh, blood and bones, live, breed, eat, talk and sleep; they stand between men and spirits, like humans in form and like spirits in speed. He called them 'composita', a blend of spirit and matter, as purple is made of blue and red and resembles neither. Man has several natures, spirit, soul, mind and body, while an elemental has only one, the ether of its element. Each element has its own ether and its own family of spirits, cut off from the others, but man has centres sensitive to all four, so any of them can reach him under the right conditions.",
  "modern.hall.22.p4": "Hall says nature spirits cannot be harmed by physical fire, earth, air or water, since they vibrate at a higher rate. Made of a single element, they have no immortal spirit and at death simply dissolve back into it, keeping no individual awareness. With no friction between bodies they wear out slowly and live long: earth spirits the shortest, air spirits the longest, generally three hundred to a thousand years. Paracelsus said they live in conditions like ours and can fall ill. They are thought unable to grow spiritually, but most have high moral character. Paracelsus wrote that nymphs live in water, sylphs in air, pygmies in earth and salamanders in fire, each confined to its own element, which is to it as air is to us or water to fish, transparent and breathable. Hall warns against confusing these spirits with the higher beings evolving in the unseen worlds: angels and archangels have composite natures with a chain of bodies like man's, except the physical one. He says the teaching about nature spirits probably came from the East, from the Brahmins, and that Paracelsus learned it from Eastern sages on his travels, as the Egyptians and Greeks had. He then takes the four kinds in turn, following Paracelsus and the Abbe de Villars.",
  "modern.hall.22.s1.p1": "Hall says the spirits of the fine 'earth ether' are called gnomes, a name perhaps from a Greek word meaning earth dweller. As there are many kinds of people, there are many kinds of gnomes, and since their element is so close to physical earth they have great power over rocks and plants and over the minerals in animal and human bodies. Some, the pygmies, work with stones, gems and metals and guard hidden treasure in deep caves, the Norse land of the Nibelungs; in Wagner's Ring, Alberich makes himself their king and forces them to dig treasure for him. Others are tree and forest spirits: sylvestres, satyrs, pans, dryads, hamadryads, elves, brownies and the little old men of the woods. Paracelsus says gnomes build houses of materials like alabaster, marble and cement, with no physical equivalent. Some live in communities; others live in what they work with, as hamadryads live and die with their trees, and every shrub and flower has its own spirit. Ancient thinkers believed the choices of creatures without minds were really made by these spirits. Hall quotes C. M. Gayley on the pagan love of seeing a god at work in every natural event. The spirits fed and coloured their plants and protected their seed; those of poisonous plants looked repulsive, and the spirits of hemlock are said to look like tiny skeletons that stay with the plant until it dies.",
  "modern.hall.22.s1.p2": "Hall says great trees have spirits much larger than those of small plants. Pygmies cut crystals in rocks and build veins of ore. In animals and people gnomes work only with matching tissues, the bones, and the ancients thought a broken bone could not heal without them. Gnomes vary in size, most much smaller than people, and some can change their size at will because their element is so mobile. The Abbe de Villars says the earth is filled almost to its centre with small gnomes who guard treasures, are clever, friendly to man and easy to govern. Others call them tricky, spiteful and treacherous, but all agree that once their trust is won they are faithful. Ancient initiates were taught to communicate with them and gain their help, but warned never to betray them: working through a person's inner nature, they could bring endless grief and ruin. They served the mystic as long as he served others, but turned on him fiercely if he used them selfishly or deceived them. Earth spirits gather at certain seasons, as in Midsummer Night's Dream, to rejoice in nature and the harvest. Their beloved king is Gob, hence 'goblins'. Medieval mystics gave them the north, the place of darkness and death, and the melancholy temperament, meaning not their own mood but their power over things of that nature.",
  "modern.hall.22.s1.p3": "Hall says gnomes marry and have families; the females are called gnomides. Some wear clothes woven from their element, while others have garments that grow with them like fur. They are said to have huge appetites and spend much of their time eating, but earn their food by honest work, and most are miserly, fond of hiding things away. He says small children often see them, being not yet fully tied to the material world. Paracelsus says man lives in the outer elements and elementals in the inner ones, with their own homes, clothes, customs, languages and governments, as bees have queens and herds have leaders. Paracelsus made them of fine invisible ethers, visible only at times and to those in tune with them, while the Greeks seem to have thought many had physical bodies; Hall suggests the difficulty of telling where physical sight ends and etheric sight begins, as with vivid dreams, may explain the difference. That does not explain the satyr St Jerome says was caught alive under Constantine and shown to the people, human in form with a goat's horns and feet, and preserved in salt for the emperor to see; Hall allows it may have been what science calls a monstrosity.",
  "modern.hall.22.s2": "Hall says the undines, the water elementals, live in the 'humid ether', close in vibration to water, so they largely control its course in nature. Their keynote is beauty; art always shows them graceful, and since water is a feminine symbol they are usually shown as female. There are many groups: some live in waterfalls and can be seen in the spray, others in fast rivers, marshes or clear mountain lakes; every spring had its nymph and every ocean wave its oceanid, under names such as naiads, nereids, sea maids and mermaids, often named after their waters. Most looked like humans in size and form, though those of small streams were smaller, and they could sometimes take human shape and mix with people; legends tell of undines adopted by fishermen's families who always returned to the sea at last. Almost nothing is known of male undines. They did not build homes but lived in coral caves or among reeds; tiny ones lived under lily pads or in moss houses by waterfalls. A Celtic legend says Ireland was first peopled by a half-divine race who withdrew into the marshes when the Celts came. Undines work with the fluids of plants, animals and people, rise from the water draped in mist and cannot stay long away from it. Their beloved ruler is Necksa, their temperament vital, their quarter the west; they are emotional, friendly to people, love flowers, and their songs were heard in the west wind.",
  "modern.hall.22.s3": "Hall says the salamanders, the fire spirits, live in the invisible fire ether, and medieval mystics believed no fire could exist without them: a match will not light nor flint spark unless a salamander, called up by the friction, appears. People cannot easily communicate with them because everything near them turns to ash, but the incense of the ancients gave them a vapour through which they could make themselves felt. There are many kinds, of different looks, sizes and rank. Paracelsus says they have been seen as balls of fire or tongues of flame running over fields or peering into houses. The commonest form was thought to be a glowing lizard a foot or more long writhing in the fire; another kind looked like huge flaming giants in fiery armour. Some, including the Abbe de Villars, held that Zoroaster was the son of Vesta, said to be Noah's wife, and the great salamander Oromasis, which is why the Persians keep undying fires. One branch, the Acthnici, appear as dim globes floating over water at night or as St Elmo's fire on ships' masts. Salamanders are the strongest elementals, ruled by Djin, a terrifying fiery spirit, and the sages were warned to stay away from them. They were given the south and the fiery temperament, and work through body heat, the liver and the blood; without them there would be no warmth.",
  "modern.hall.22.s4.p1": "Hall says the sylphs live in 'air', meaning not the atmosphere but an unseen, far finer medium like it. He quotes Socrates in Plato's Phaedo: some beings live about the air as we live about the sea, the air is to them as water is to us and the ether as air is to us; they have no disease (which Paracelsus disputes), live much longer, have far keener senses, have temples where the gods truly dwell and speak with them, and see the sun, moon and stars as they really are. Sylphs were thought to live among the clouds, but their true home was on mountain tops. Anthony Todd Thomson says fays and fairies are of Norse origin; 'fairy' is sometimes traced to the Persian peri, a kindly being who guarded men from evil spirits, but more likely to the Gothic fagur, as 'elves' comes from alfa. If so, belief in British fairies dates from the Danish conquest. Fairies were thought to be tiny, beautiful, lively and kind beings from Fairy Land who visited earth at times, leaving green rings in the dewy grass where they had danced in the moonlight.",
  "modern.hall.22.s4.p2": "Hall says the ancients gave the sylphs the work of shaping snowflakes and gathering clouds, with the undines supplying the moisture. The winds were their vehicle. They are the highest elementals, their element having the highest vibration, and they live hundreds of years, often a thousand, without seeming to age. Their leader, Paralda, lives on the highest mountain, and the females are called sylphids. Sylphs, salamanders and nymphs are thought to have spoken through the ancient oracles from deep in the earth and from the air. Sylphs sometimes take human form briefly; most are human-sized or smaller. They are said to have taken people into their communities for long periods, as Paracelsus reports, though not in the physical body. Some think the Greek Muses were sylphs, since sylphs gather round dreamers, poets and artists and inspire them with knowledge of nature's beauty. They were given the east, and their temperament is merry, changeable and odd; the quirks of genius come from their help, along with their fickleness. They work with the body's gases and indirectly the nerves, and have no fixed home, wandering everywhere as invisible but ever-present forces.",
  "modern.hall.22.s5.p1": "Hall says some ancients, unlike Paracelsus, thought the elemental kingdoms made war on each other, and saw the clash of elements as their quarrels: lightning splitting a rock was salamanders attacking gnomes. Since the four ethers have nothing in common, they had to fight through physical matter, over which they had some power. There were also wars within each group, gnome army against gnome army. Hall says ancient thinkers explained nature's inconsistencies by personifying its forces with human-like moods. The four fixed signs of the zodiac were given to the elementals: Taurus to gnomes, Scorpio to undines, Leo to salamanders and Aquarius to sylphs. The Church lumped all elementals together as demons, a misleading name, since nature spirits are no more evil than minerals, plants or animals, and several church fathers said they had met and debated with them. Nature spirits have no hope of immortality, though some say adepts have occasionally given it to them. At death an elemental dissolves back into its element, and whatever it gained is kept only in that element's consciousness. Lacking man's complex make-up, they are below him in reasoning, but working in one element they have a specialized intelligence far beyond his in that field.",
  "modern.hall.22.s5.p2": "Hall says the church fathers wrongly applied the terms incubus and succubus to elementals. Incubi and succubi, male and female, are evil, unnatural creations: Paracelsus says they are parasites living on the evil thoughts and feelings of the astral body, and the terms are also used of the subtle bodies of sorcerers. These 'larvae' are real but born of imagination; the ancients saw them as the unseen cause of vice, hovering around the morally weak, urging them to degrading excess. So they haunt drug dens, low bars and brothels, and those who dull their senses with drugs or drink come into contact with them; the visions of hashish and opium users and the monsters of delirium tremens are such beings, seen only by those whose habits draw them. Unlike these is the vampire, which Paracelsus defines as the astral body of a living or, usually, dead person that prolongs its life by stealing the vital energy of the living. Paracelsus says a healthy, pure person cannot be possessed by them, since they can act only on those who make room for them; a healthy mind is a castle they cannot enter uninvited, but once let in they stir up passions, cravings and bad thoughts, sharpen the animal intellect and stifle the moral sense. Hall adds that the Count de Gabalis oddly explained virgin births as unions of humans with elementals, listing Hercules, Achilles, Aeneas, Theseus, Melchizedek, Plato, Apollonius and Merlin among their offspring.",
  "modern.hall.22.fig1": "The caption, for a salamander from Paracelsus' explanation of thirty magic figures, says the Egyptians, Chaldeans and Persians often took salamanders for gods because of their brilliance and power, and the Greeks, following them, deified the fire spirits and kept incense and altar fires burning for them perpetually.",
  "modern.hall.22.fig2": "The caption, for gnomes from Gjellerup's edition of the Elder Edda, says the commonest kind is the brownie or elf, a mischievous, odd little being twelve to eighteen inches tall, usually dressed in green or russet brown. Most look very old, often with long white beards and round bodies, and they can be seen scampering out of holes in tree stumps, sometimes vanishing by melting into the tree.",
  "modern.hall.22.fig3": "The caption, for a mermaid from Lycosthenes' chronicle of prodigies, says mermaids, the most famous undines, were placed in every sea by early sailors. The belief in creatures human above and fish below may have come from flocks of penguins seen far off, or from seals. Medieval accounts add that their hair was green like seaweed and that they wore wreaths of underwater flowers and sea anemones.",
  "modern.hall.22.fig4": "The caption, for a sylph from a sketch by Howard Wookey, says sylphs are changeable beings that dart about as fast as lightning. They work through the gases and ethers of the earth, are kindly toward people, and are nearly always shown winged, sometimes as tiny cherubs and sometimes as delicate fairies.",
  "modern.hall.23.p0": "Hall says healing began as one of the priesthood's secret sciences, its origin as hidden as that of religion. All higher knowledge first belonged to the priests: the temple was the cradle of civilization, and the priests made and enforced laws, appointed and controlled rulers, cared for the living and guided the dead, admitting to their ranks only those fit in mind and morals. He quotes Plato's Statesman that in Egypt a king could not reign without priestly powers, and one who seized the throne had to be enrolled among the priests. Candidates passed hard tests, called initiations, and then learned the secret teachings. The ancients never separated philosophy, science and religion: each was part of the others, and perfect wisdom came only from uniting all three. Modern doctors call Hippocrates the father of medicine, but the ancient healers gave that honour to Hermes; Clement of Alexandria lists among the books of Hermes one on medicine. The Emerald Tablet, said to have been found in the valley of Ebron and credited to Hermes, Hall calls a chemical formula of a high, secret order. He says Hippocrates, in the fifth century BC, split medicine off from the other temple sciences, leading eventually to today's crude materialism, and that modern science is hampered by refusing what lies beyond the five senses.",
  "modern.hall.23.s1.p1": "Hall says the neglected teachings of Hermetic wisdom were gathered again in the Middle Ages and put to the test, and that much of what is known of ancient medicine comes from Theophrastus of Hohenheim, who called himself Paracelsus, 'greater than Celsus'. Paracelsus spent his life studying and teaching Hermetic philosophy and took ideas from everywhere. Doctors opposed him then and belittle him now, but Hall says the occult world knows he will one day be recognized as the greatest physician of all time. His enemies held his unorthodox temper and wanderings against him, yet he was one of the few who tried to reconcile healing with pagan and Christian philosophy and religion. Paracelsus defended his travels: whoever would study nature must walk through her books with his feet; writing is studied by its letters, but nature land by land, each land a page. He was a keen observer, called the 'Second Hermes' and the 'Trismegistus of Switzerland'. He crossed Europe and may have reached the East, learning about herbs from the gypsies and, apparently, about talismans and the stars from the Arabs. Caring more for curing the sick than for professional standing, he gave up a respectable career and, at the cost of lifelong persecution, attacked the medicine of his day.",
  "modern.hall.23.s1.p2": "Hall says Paracelsus held that everything in the universe is good for something, which is why he scraped fungus from tombstones and collected dew on glass plates at midnight; many think he discovered mesmerism and that Mesmer learned it from his writings. Paracelsus scorned the medicine of his time. He wrote that most diseases come from unknown causes that doctors cannot remove because they do not know them; they can only watch and guess, and the patient should be glad if the medicine does no serious harm. The best popular doctors are those who do least harm; some poison patients with mercury, others purge or bleed them to death, some have learned so much that they have lost their common sense, and others care more for profit than for health. A disease does not adapt itself to the doctor's knowledge; the doctor must understand its causes, be nature's servant and not her enemy, and help her fight for life rather than obstruct recovery. Hall says a basic rule of Hermetic medicine is that nearly all diseases begin in the invisible nature of man, the 'Astrum', since the Hermetists saw the body as an outgrowth of the unseen spiritual principles, and he gives a summary of Paracelsus' principles.",
  "modern.hall.23.s1.p3": "Hall says there is one vital substance on which everything lives, called the archaeus or vital force, the same as the astral light or spiritual air of the ancients. He quotes Eliphas Levi: light, the creative agent whose vibrations are the movement and life of all things, latent in the ether, gathered in centres that send it out again, starry in stars, animal in animals, human in humans, growing plants, shining in metals, shaping all forms and balancing everything by universal sympathy; it shows itself in magnetism, as Paracelsus guessed, and colours the blood, released from the air we breathe. This energy comes from the spiritual body of the earth. Everything has two bodies, a visible one and an invisible ethereal copy that carries the archaeus, a 'vital body'. This etheric shadow is not lost at death but lasts until the physical body has fully decayed, and such 'etheric doubles' seen in graveyards gave rise to belief in ghosts. Being finer, it is more easily disturbed, and its disorders cause much disease: Paracelsus taught that a morbid state of mind could poison the etheric nature and divert the flow of life force, later showing as physical illness. All plants and minerals have this archaeus, each showing it differently.",
  "modern.hall.23.s1.p4": "Hall quotes James Gaffarel, writing in 1650, on the astral bodies of flowers: even chopped, ground or burned to ash, plants keep their form invisibly in their juice and ashes, and an expert can make it visible. Gaffarel cites du Chesne, who said he saw a Polish doctor in Krakow keep the ashes of nearly every known herb in glasses; held over a candle, the ashes of a rose would stir, rise into a little dark cloud, and take the shape of a rose so fresh and perfect it seemed as real and fragrant as one on the bush. Hall says Paracelsus saw disorders of the etheric double as the main cause of disease and tried to set it right by bringing it into contact with other bodies whose vital energy could supply what was missing or overcome the disorder in the patient's aura; once the invisible cause was gone, the illness soon vanished. Paracelsus called the vehicle of the life force the 'mumia'. Vaccine, carrying a half-astral virus, is a physical example, and anything that carries the archaeus, living or not, is a mumia; the most universal is the ether, which science then accepted as a medium between vital energy and matter.",
  "modern.hall.23.s1.p5": "Hall says the universal energy can be controlled only through its vehicle, the mumia. Food is an example: people are not fed by dead plants and animals as such, but by taking over their mumia, or etheric double, and turning the flow of life force to their own use. Paracelsus says that life is in the mumia, and to give the mumia is to give life; that is why talismans and amulets work, since the mumia of their materials connects the wearer to the universal life force. Paracelsus taught that just as plants take in the carbon dioxide animals breathe out, plants and animals can take in disease passed to them from people. Being different, they often absorb it unharmed, but sometimes they die in place of the more useful human. Either way the patient slowly recovers, fully once the plant or animal has absorbed the foreign mumia or died of it. It took many years to learn which herb or animal best took the mumia of each disease, and Paracelsus found that plants often show by their shape which organs they serve. His system rested on moving the diseased mumia out of the patient into some distant, unimportant thing, so that the life force that had been feeding the illness followed it, and the patient got well.",
  "modern.hall.23.s2.p1": "Hall gives the Hermetic philosophers' seven causes of disease. First, evil spirits, born of degenerate acts and living on the vital energy of those they cling to. Second, a mismatch between a person's spiritual and material natures, causing mental and physical weakness. Third, an unhealthy state of mind: melancholy, morbid feelings, passions, lusts, greed and hatred act on the mumia and then on the body as ulcers, tumours, cancers, fevers and tuberculosis; the ancients saw a germ as a bit of mumia tainted by evil, a tiny creature born of man's evil thoughts and deeds. Fourth, karma, the law of compensation, with which a doctor must interfere carefully so as not to thwart eternal justice. Fifth, the movements and aspects of the planets, which incline rather than compel; the wise man rules his stars, the weak are ruled by them. These five are beyond the physical and must be judged by reasoning and study of the patient's life and temperament. Sixth, misuse of a faculty or organ, such as overstrain. Seventh, foreign matter or obstruction in the body, including diet, air and sunlight. Accidents are not disease and are often karma at work. Hall then lists seven ways of healing, the first being spells and commands ordering the evil spirit out, as Jesus sent devils into swine, or back to whoever sent them; spirits were said to leave as smoke, flame, birds or insects.",
  "modern.hall.23.s2.p2": "Hall continues the seven ways of healing. Second, vibration: chanting spells and sacred names, playing music and singing, and showing the sick coloured objects, an early form of colour therapy now being rediscovered. Third, talismans and amulets: since the planets ruled the body, metal charms countered harmful stars; an anaemic person, lacking iron, the metal of Mars, wore an iron talisman inscribed to call on Mars, while one with too much iron wore the metal of a planet opposed to Mars. Fourth, herbs: most ancient doctors used metals only outwardly and did not give minerals internally; each herb had its planet, and after diagnosing by the stars they gave the herbal remedy. Fifth, prayer: all peoples believed in God's compassion, and Paracelsus said faith would cure every disease, though few have enough. Sixth, prevention, by regulating diet and daily habits, since health is man's natural state and disease comes from ignoring nature. Seventh, 'practical medicine', mainly bleeding and purging, useful in moderation but deadly in excess, killing many people decades early. Paracelsus used all seven, and even his enemies admitted his near-miraculous results. Near Hohenheim, he gathered the heavy dew under certain planetary positions and found it a powerful medicine, having absorbed the virtues of the heavens.",
  "modern.hall.23.s3.p1": "Hall says the early pagans held the herbs of the field sacred, made by the gods to cure human ills, and that every plant, rightly prepared, could relieve suffering or develop spiritual, mental, moral or physical powers. He quotes P. Davidson: poets have long talked with flowers and kings have prized their scents, but to the true physician, nature's high priest, they speak far more deeply; no plant or mineral has yet told science all its properties, and many powers may lie hidden within; flowers are well called the 'stars of earth', having come from a more spiritual world, since God made every plant 'before it was in the earth'. Many early peoples cured with herbs; the Chinese, Egyptians and American Indians cured diseases for which modern science has no remedy. Nicholas Culpeper, who died in 1654, was probably the most famous herbalist; disgusted with the medicine of his day, he turned to field plants and won national fame. He placed each plant under a planet and held that diseases too were governed by the heavens: diseases of one planet could be treated with herbs of its opposite (Jupiter with Mercury, the sun and moon with Saturn, Mars with Venus), or by sympathy, each planet curing its own organs, the sun and moon the eyes, Saturn the spleen, Jupiter the liver, Mars the gall and Venus the reproductive organs.",
  "modern.hall.23.s3.p2": "Hall says medieval herbalists recovered only part of the old Hermetic secrets of Egypt and Greece, which laid the foundations of nearly all modern arts and sciences. Healing was taught to initiates of the Mysteries, and ointments, eye salves, love potions and draughts were made with strange rites; their effectiveness is on record, and incense and perfume were much used. Barrett's Magus explains that since our spirit is a pure, fine, airy vapour of the blood, similar vapours suit it best and can stir, attract and change it. Poisons were studied closely, and some communities gave condemned prisoners extracts of deadly herbs, as with Socrates; the Borgias carried the art of poisoning to its height, quietly disposing of many gifted people with chemical knowledge kept in their family for centuries. Egyptian priests found herb extracts that brought on temporary clairvoyance and used them in initiation, mixed into the candidates' food or given as sacred drinks. Soon the candidate felt dizzy and seemed to float through space while his body lay senseless under the priests' guard, and he went through strange experiences he could later describe. Hall says it is hard now to imagine an art able to bring on any desired state of mind almost at once with drinks, perfumes and incense, yet the early pagan priests had one.",
  "modern.hall.23.s3.p3": "Hall quotes Blavatsky: plants have wonderful mystical properties; the secrets of the herbs of dreams and enchantment are lost to and unknown by European science, apart from opium and hashish, whose effects are treated as mere mental disorder; the women of Thessaly and Epirus, priestesses of Sabazius, did not take their secrets with them when their temples fell, and those who know the nature of soma know other plants too. Herbal mixtures induced trance at the oracles, especially Delphi, and words spoken in it were taken as prophecy. Modern mediums in self-induced trance give similar messages but are usually far less accurate, lacking knowledge of nature's hidden forces. The Mysteries taught that in the higher degrees the gods took part in instructing candidates, or at least were present; since the gods came only in spiritual bodies, the candidate could not perceive them without drugs that roused his clairvoyant centre, probably the pineal gland, and many initiates insisted they had spoken with the immortals. When pagan standards declined the Mysteries split: the truly enlightened withdrew with their chief secrets and vanished without trace, while the rest drifted into decay, and lesser formulas fell into profane hands and were misused, as in the Bacchanalia, where drugged wine caused the orgies. Some springs and wells were thought sacred because of minerals in their water, and temples were built beside them.",
  "modern.hall.23.s3.p4": "Hall quotes Salverte: those seeking initiation or prophetic dreams fasted, then ate specially prepared meals and drank mysterious drinks, such as the waters of Lethe and Mnemosyne in the cave of Trophonius or the kykeon at Eleusis, into which drugs could easily be mixed to produce the state of mind and the visions wanted; some early Christian sects were accused of the same. Hall says the Assassins, whom he calls the Yezidees, show another side of drugs: in the eleventh century they took the fortress of Alamut, and their founder Hassan Sabbah, the 'Old Man of the Mountain', is suspected of controlling his followers with narcotics, making them believe they had been in Paradise and would stay there forever if they obeyed him. De Quincey's Confessions of an English Opium-Eater describes the effects of opium, and a similar drug may lie behind the Assassins' idea of Paradise. Philosophers have always taught that the visible universe is a small part of the whole, and the body the least important part of man. Most modern medicine ignores the superphysical man and treats effects rather than causes; Paracelsus remarked that removing the invisible causes of disease is magic, while merely making outward symptoms disappear is physic, sorcery and quackery.",
  "modern.hall.23.s3.p5": "Hall says disease is unnatural, a sign of disharmony within or between organs, and lasting health returns only with harmony. The great merit of Hermetic medicine was that it saw spiritual and mental disorders as largely behind physical illness. Ancient priest-physicians used suggestion with great success, and American Indian medicine men cured many people with dances, invocations and charms despite knowing nothing of modern medicine. Egyptian healing rites rested on a deep grasp of how the mind acts on the body, and the Egyptians and Brahmins understood healing by vibration: chants and mantras stressing certain sounds cleared congestion and helped mend broken bones and weakened organs, and they also used sound to awaken hidden centres of consciousness. The Book of Coming Forth by Day preserves many Egyptian secrets, but few understand its magical passages. Eastern peoples know that every word has power and that arranged words can create whirls of force that affect matter; the Word that made the world, the Lost Word of Masonry and AUM all honour sound. Many 'new discoveries' are rediscoveries of what ancient priests knew, and cruelty has destroyed records that might have solved today's problems; races burn their predecessors' records and then perish for lack of that wisdom.",
  "modern.hall.23.fig1": "The caption, for the title page of the Book of Alze from the Museum Hermeticum, calls it another example of alchemical symbolism. The seven-pointed star of the sacred metals has one black point downward, standing for Saturn the destroyer. Reading clockwise from the space left of the black point, the first letters of the seven Latin words in the outer ring spell VITRIOL.",
  "modern.hall.23.fig2": "The caption, for a portrait of Johann Baptista van Helmont from one of his books, says this Belgian alchemist of the early seventeenth century, who coined the word 'gas', once touched the root of a plant he names only as 'A---' to his tongue without swallowing. He describes his head feeling bound with a string, and then that he no longer thought and felt with his head but with the pit of his stomach, as if consciousness had moved there. Alarmed, he examined himself and found his perception clearer and wider, with great pleasure; he was awake, sober and healthy, and the state, unlike his earlier ecstasies, lasted two hours before passing with some dizziness. His friends feared madness, but his faith calmed him, and though he tasted the root often afterwards, it never happened again. Hall says many have stumbled on the old priestly secrets like this without understanding them, and thinks the herb briefly paralysed the brain and spinal nerves, forcing consciousness to work through the sympathetic nervous system and its 'brain', the solar plexus.",
  "modern.hall.23.fig3": "The caption, for a portrait of Nicholas Culpeper from his Semeiotica Uranica, says this physician, herbalist and astrologer spent most of his life walking the hills and woods of England, cataloguing hundreds of medicinal herbs. Rejecting the unnatural methods of the doctors of his day, he wrote that he consulted 'Dr Reason' and 'Dr Experience', visited 'Mother Nature', with 'Dr Diligence' found what he sought, and, urged by 'Mr Honesty', published it. Samuel Johnson said Culpeper deserved the gratitude of posterity.",
  "modern.hall.23.fig4": "The caption, for a set of chemical signs from De Monte-Snyders' Metamorphosis of the Planets, says each sign is one syllable of a seven-syllable word that names the first matter of the universe. Since all substance is made of seven powers combined by cosmic law, a great mystery lies in the sevenfold make-up of man and the universe. De Monte-Snyders writes that whoever wants the true name of the first matter should know that these figures, combined, make syllables, and from them the meaningful word.",
  "modern.hall.24.p1": "Hall quotes Albert Pike, who in turn quotes Eliphas Levi, on the Kabbalah as a key to Masonry: entering it, one admires a doctrine so logical, simple and complete, uniting ideas and signs, words, letters and numbers; a philosophy as simple as the alphabet and as deep as the Word, with theorems clearer than Pythagoras's and a theology one can count on one's fingers; ten numbers, twenty-two letters, a triangle, a square and a circle are all its elements. Hall says Hebrew theology had three parts: the Law, taught to all Israel; the Mishnah, the soul of the Law, taught to rabbis and teachers; and the Kabbalah, the soul of the soul of the Law, carefully hidden and taught only to the highest initiates. Some Jewish mystics say Moses went up Sinai three times for forty days each: first he received the written Law, then its soul, and finally the mysteries of the Kabbalah, which he hid in the first four books of the Torah, where students have long sought it. As the spirit hides in the body, the unwritten law hides in the written one. Kabbalah means the hidden tradition, given, an early rabbi said, so that man might understand the universe around and within him.",
  "modern.hall.24.p2": "Hall says the Kabbalah's origin is disputed. Early initiates believed God first taught it to a school of angels before the Fall, and the angels then taught Adam, so fallen humanity could regain its lost state: the angel Raziel taught Adam, Tophiel Shem, Raphael Isaac, Metatron Moses and Michael David. Christian D. Ginsburg recounts the tradition that it passed from Adam to Noah and Abraham, who took it to Egypt and let some of it leak out, which is how the Egyptians and other Eastern nations learned of it; Moses, schooled in Egypt's wisdom, mastered it in the wilderness, studying it for forty years and taught by an angel, then passed it to the seventy elders, and of its line of keepers David and Solomon knew it best. Eliphas Levi names the three great Kabbalistic books as the Sepher Yetzirah (Book of Formation), the Zohar (Book of Splendour) and the Apocalypse (Revelation). Their dates are uncertain; Kabbalists say Abraham wrote the Sepher Yetzirah, which is by far the oldest, but Hall thinks it was probably written by Rabbi Akiba about AD 120.",
  "modern.hall.24.p3": "Hall says the Zohar is credited to Simeon ben Jochai, Akiba's pupil, who was condemned to death about AD 161, escaped with his son to a cave, and wrote it there over twelve years with the help of the prophet Elijah, developing the symbolism of the 'Greater' and 'Lesser Face'; he died while teaching, amid wonders, and was the first allowed to write down doctrines said to exist since creation. Twelve hundred years later Moses de Leon found and published the books, but Hall thinks de Leon most likely compiled the Zohar himself about 1305 from older unwritten teachings. The date and author of the Apocalypse are also unproven. He prints the whole Sepher Yetzirah in this chapter because it is short and the key to Kabbalistic thought; the Zohar, he says, has never been fully put into English, though it exists in French and Mathers translated three of its books, and he paraphrases its main points. The Kabbalah deeply shaped medieval Christian and Jewish thought by teaching that scripture holds a hidden key, which Hall sees symbolized by the crossed keys of the papal arms, and many scholars searched for it with fruitful results. It is bound up with alchemy, Hermeticism, Rosicrucianism and Freemasonry, and its early simplicity grew into a system almost too involved to understand.",
  "modern.hall.24.p4": "Hall says the Kabbalists divided their science into five uses: the Natural, for studying nature's mysteries; the Analogical, showing that all things are one in essence and that man, the small universe, is a copy of God, the great one; the Contemplative, for knowing the heavenly spheres and their beings through the higher intellect; the Astrological, for the power and substance of the stars and the hidden nature of the earth; and the Magical, for controlling demons and lower beings and healing with talismans, charms and invocations. Adolphe Franck says the Sepher Yetzirah differs from other sacred books by revealing God through his works rather than starting from the idea of God. Hall explains that his version compares five English translations and is not a direct translation of any one text: it aims at the spirit rather than the letter but does not stray far. Its base is Isidor Kalisch's 1877 translation, the first in English, with material from others and passages rewritten for simplicity. He also used an eighteenth-century manuscript of John Pistor's Book of the Cabalistick Art, William Wynn Westcott's version, the one in Horne's Sacred Books and Early Literature of the East, Knut Stenring's Book of Formation, and four more copies in German, Hebrew and Latin. Doubtful passages are kept where they naturally fall.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p1": "In Hall's version of the Sepher Yetzirah, chapter one says that God, the Lord of Hosts, living and eternal, created the universe through thirty-two mysterious paths of wisdom, by three means, numbers, letters and sounds, which in him are one. Its foundation is ten Sephiroth, or properties, and twenty-two letters: three 'mothers', seven 'doubles' and twelve 'simples'. The ten Sephiroth are like the ten fingers and ten toes, five against five, with the covenant of the One God between them, in the spirit the covenant of the word and in the body circumcision. They are ten, not nine and not eleven; the reader is told to understand this, examine all things by them, and restore the Creator to his throne, since he alone is the maker. The ten have ten infinities: beginning and end, good and evil, height and depth, east and west, north and south, with the one faithful King over them all. They appear like a flash of lightning, without beginning or end; God's word is in them as they go out and return like a whirlwind and bow before his throne. Their end is joined to their beginning, as flame to coal, for God is one and has no second. On them one should keep silent and guard the heart, as the living creatures in Ezekiel 'ran and returned'.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p2": "In Hall's version, the ten emanations are listed. First, the spirit of the living God, whose voice, spirit and word are the Holy Spirit. Second, air from spirit, in which he formed the twenty-two letters, three mothers, seven doubles and twelve simples. Third, water from air, in which he set the letters out of mud and clay like a wall and rampart, and poured snow on them to make earth. Fourth, fire from water, from which he made the throne of glory, the seraphim, ophanim and holy living creatures, his ministering angels. Fifth to tenth, he took three letters, I, H and V, made them a great name and sealed the six directions with its different orders: height, depth, east, west, south and north, following Kalisch's arrangement. So from God's spirit came air, from air water, from water fire, and from fire the six directions. Chapter two says there are twenty-two letters: the three mothers, A, M and Sh, rest on a balance, with virtues on one side, vices on the other and the tongue keeping them level; M is silent like water, Sh hisses like fire, and A is a reconciling breath between them. God combined, weighed and exchanged the twenty-two letters and formed from them everything that exists or will exist.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p3": "In Hall's version, God fixed the twenty-two letters to be spoken by the voice in five parts of the mouth: throat, palate, tongue, teeth and lips. He set them in a ring like a wall with 231 gates and turned it forward and back, forward meaning good and back meaning evil, as the same three letters spell 'pleasure' one way and 'plague' the other. He did this by combining each letter with every other in turn, giving 231 gates through which the letters' powers go out, so that every creature and language comes from one name. He made something real out of nothing, carved great pillars out of thin air, and created every creature and word by speaking, the twenty-two letters being parts of one living body. Chapter three says the three mother letters are like a balance between virtue and vice held level by the tongue. They hold a great, unknown mystery, sealed by six wings, air, water and fire, each active and passive, and from them came the fathers and from those the generations. God set the three mothers in the universe, the year and man: in the universe as fire (heaven), water (earth and sea) and air, which balances them.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p4": "In Hall's version, the three mothers make heat, cold and the temperate season in the year, and in man the head from fire, the belly from water and the chest from air, which balances them. God let A rule in air and sealed with it the air, the temperate season and the chest; M in water, sealing the earth, cold and the belly; and Sh in fire, sealing heaven, heat and the head. Chapter four says the seven double letters, B, G, D, K, P, R and Th, each have two sounds, hard and soft, and stand for strength and weakness. They mean wisdom, riches, fertility, life, power, peace and grace, and their opposites foolishness, poverty, barrenness, death, servitude, war and ugliness. They also mark the six directions with the holy temple in the middle holding them up. They are seven, not six or eight, and the reader is told to search out why and set the Creator on his throne again. From them God formed seven planets, seven days and seven gates of the senses in man, and also seven heavens, seven earths and seven Sabbaths, so he loves seven above all numbers. The planets are Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the sun, Venus, Mercury and the moon; the days are the days of the week; and the gates are two eyes, two ears, two nostrils and the mouth.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p5": "In Hall's version, a note says Knut Stenring and Kircher order the planets and days differently, while Kalisch, Westcott and Horne's edition follow the order given. God made each double letter rule a quality and formed from it a planet, a day and a body part: B in wisdom, the moon, the first day and the right eye; G in riches, Mars, the second day and the right ear; D in fertility, the sun, the third day and the right nostril; K in life, Venus, the fourth day and the left eye; P in power, Mercury, the fifth day and the left ear; R in peace, Saturn, the sixth day and the left nostril; and Th in grace, Jupiter, the seventh day and the mouth. With the seven he also made seven earths, heavens, continents, seas, rivers, deserts, days and weeks from Passover to Pentecost, with his holy palace among them, and the cycle of seven years ending in the release year, with the Jubilee after seven of those, which is why God loves seven above all. The letters combine like stones building houses: two make two, three make six, four make 24, five 120, six 720 and seven 5,040, and from there on beyond what the mouth can say or the ear hear.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p6": "In Hall's version, chapter five says the twelve simple letters stand for twelve basic faculties: speech, thought, movement, sight, hearing, work, sexual union, smell, sleep, anger, taste and mirth. They match twelve diagonal directions, from east-height round to north-depth, which reach out forever as the arms of the universe. From them God made the twelve signs of the zodiac, the twelve months and twelve chief organs: two hands, two feet, two kidneys, gall bladder, small intestine, liver, oesophagus, stomach and spleen. A note says Stenring orders the faculties differently. Then each letter is assigned: H rules speech and makes Aries, the month Nisan and the right foot; V thought, Taurus, Iyar and the right kidney; Z movement, Gemini, Sivan and the left foot; Ch sight, Cancer, Tammuz and the right hand; T hearing, Leo, Ab and the left kidney; and I work, Virgo, Elul and the left hand.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p7": "In Hall's version, the list continues: L rules sexual union and makes Libra, the month Tishri and the gall; N smell, Scorpio, Marcheshvan and the small intestine; S sleep, Sagittarius, Kislev and the stomach; O anger, Capricorn, Tebet and the liver; Tz taste, Aquarius, Shebat and the oesophagus; and Q mirth, Pisces, Adar and the spleen. God set them against one another like provinces and walls, armed like opposing armies, and the Elohim did the same in the other spheres. Chapter six says that from the three mothers came three fathers, spiritual air, water and fire, and from them the seven planets with their angels and the twelve signs. The witnesses are the universe, the year and man; the twelve, the balance and the seven; above is the Dragon, below the world, and last the heart of man, with God ruling all. Fire is above, water below, and air balances them; 'fire carries water'; M is silent, Sh hisses, A is the breath between. The Dragon is in the universe like a king on his throne, the heavenly sphere in the year like a king in his realm, and the heart in the body like a king at war.",
  "modern.hall.24.s1.p8": "In Hall's version, God set opposites against each other, good against evil and evil against good; each comes from its own kind, each tests the other, and good is kept for the good and evil for the wicked. Of three, one is good, one evil and the third balances them; of seven, three stand against three with one between; of twelve, three cause love (the heart and two ears), three hatred (the liver, gall and tongue), three give life (the two nostrils and spleen) and three destroy (the mouth and the two lower openings). God rules over all: one above three, three above seven, seven above twelve, all linked. By the twenty-two letters God designed his universe through numbers, letters and sounds. When Abraham had understood all this, God appeared to him, called him his friend, kissed his head and made a covenant with him: between the ten fingers, the covenant of the tongue, and between the ten toes, the covenant of circumcision. God bound the twenty-two letters on his tongue and revealed their secrets, steeped them in water, burned them in fire, stamped them on the winds, and shared them among the seven planets and twelve signs.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The sentence beginning 'and God said of him, \"Before Abraham' breaks off in the text, so what God said of Abraham is lost; the passage may mean only that God bound the letters on his tongue.",
  "modern.hall.24.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus, says it shows the name of God in seventy-two languages on the petals of a symbolic sunflower, the seventy-two powers of God from the Kabbalah above, and two trees below bearing the planets and the zodiac with the tribes of Israel. Hall says the Kabbalah agrees with the secret teachings of every school, but hides and reveals its secrets in an unusual way. As scripture is studied with modern learning, he says, it becomes clearer that the sacred books are not histories, and that their kings, sages, prophets and saviours are really personified qualities of man himself.",
  "modern.hall.24.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram of the Tetragrammaton, says that arranging the four letters of the Great Name, I, H, V, H, as a Pythagorean tetractys yields the seventy-two powers of God. The rows read I (10), H I (5+10=15), V H I (6+5+10=21) and H V H I (5+6+5+10=26), which add up to 72.",
  "modern.hall.24.fig3": "The caption, for a diagram of the Hebrew letters according to the Sepher Yetzirah, says the three mother letters are in the central triangle and the seven double letters, the planets and heavens, come from them. Around the black star are the zodiac signs, shown by the twelve simple letters. At the star's centre is the invisible throne of the Most Ancient of the Ancients, the supreme Creator beyond definition.",
  "modern.hall.25.p1": "Hall says the Kabbalists think of God as a principle beyond understanding, found only by stripping away every knowable quality in turn; what is left is Ain Soph, eternal being. It cannot be defined but fills all space; it is the unconditioned state of all things, and while substances, essences and minds come out of it, it has none of them itself. Hall likens it to rich dark soil from which countless different plants grow, universes, gods and men, all rooted in it, unlike it, and bound to return to it, the only immortal. The Kabbalists called it the Most Ancient of all the Ancients, held it to be sexless, and symbolized it by a closed eye. To define it is to defile it, yet the rabbis offered theories of how it brought forth creation and gave it partial symbols, above all a circle, the sign of eternity, enclosing a boundless area of incomprehensible life. So God is not only a centre but the whole area. Any centre is a limit and so finite, bound to dissolve back into its cause, while Ain Soph is infinite. Space is pictured inside a vast crystal globe with nothing outside it, not even a vacuum, holding every element ever to be used in cosmic birth, growth and decay: the Cosmic Egg, unbroken until the great day 'Be With Us' ends the cycle of necessity.",
  "modern.hall.25.p2": "Hall says that in creation the diffused life of Ain Soph withdraws from the edge of the circle to its centre and forms a point, the first manifest One. Its withdrawal leaves behind the abyss, the 'Great Privation', so there are now two conditions: the central point of radiance and the darkness around it, and Ain Soph shines on space from that point rather than through it. Isaac Myer says Ain Soph first filled all, then drew into itself, producing the abyss or primal air, not a true vacuum but a crystalline chaotic sea with a dimmer light than that by which the worlds were made. The Kabbalah teaches that man's body is wrapped in a bubble-like, iridescent egg, the 'auric egg', his causal sphere, which is to him what the globe of Ain Soph is to the universe. Man's highest consciousness is in this aura; as the cosmic consciousness gathers into a point called God, man's gathers into a point called the ego. As the universes are formed from powers latent in the cosmic egg, everything man uses in all his lives comes from powers latent in his auric egg. He never leaves it, even at death; all his births, deaths and rebirths happen inside it, and it will not break until the lesser day 'Be With Us', when humanity, like the universe, is freed from the wheel of necessity.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p1": "Hall explains a circular chart whose rings stand for the forty levels of vibration, or 'spheres', that come out of Ain Soph. The outer circle X1 is the edge of space. Ain Soph itself has three layers: Ain, the void of pure spirit; Ain Soph, the boundless; and Ain Soph Aur, the boundless light. At first Ain alone filled the circle; as the divine essence drew in on itself, Ain Soph and then the Light appeared as successive limits, so the Supreme is threefold, and from it the powers of creation were reflected into the abyss left behind. Its steady movement inward set the point in the circle, called God, the highest individualizing of the universal essence. The Zohar says that when the most hidden wished to reveal himself, he first made a single point, before which the Infinite gave no light. The point is called I AM, Eheieh, and also the first crown, the aged, the smooth point, the white head and the Long Face. Once the shining point appeared as Kether, the Crown, nine great globes radiated from it in the shape of a tree, making the first set of ten Sephiroth, called shining sapphires, which some rabbis link with the word Sephira. They do not hold Ain Soph's power but reflect it, as planets reflect the sun. The emptied space filled with four nested worlds, each reflecting the ten globes as a tree, making forty spheres in all.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p2": "Hall names the four worlds: Atziluth, the world of divine names (A1 to A10); Briah, the archangelic world of creation (B); Yetzirah, the world of formation and its orders of beings (C); and Assiah, the elemental world of substance (D). Each has a parent globe and nine more, each born from the one before. In Atziluth, the highest world, the first point, A1, holds all creation but was not seen by the Kabbalists as a person, rather as a divine foundation, the First Crown; in the lower worlds the globes become intelligences, planets and elements, but here they are the rings of the sacred names. He lists the ten globes and their divine names: the Crown, Eheieh, 'I Am'; Wisdom, Jehovah, 'essence of being'; Understanding, Jehovah Elohim; Mercy, El; Severity, Elohim Gibor; Beauty, Eloah Vadaath; Victory, Jehovah Tzabaoth, 'God of hosts'; Glory, Elohim Tzabaoth; Foundation, Shaddai El Chai, 'the almighty'; and Kingdom, Adonai Melekh. From the tenth came B1, the second Crown, and the world of Briah began.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p3": "Hall says the ten globes of Atziluth are the foundations of all creation, the ten roots of the Tree of Life, arranged as a great human figure, Adam Kadmon, the archetypal universal man. Here God's powers are purest. They do not descend and take form in the lower worlds but are reflected on them, and each reflection is like the original but smaller and dimmer. So in Briah the order is the same, but the ten lights are fainter and more tangible and are called ten great spirits who help bring order to the universe. B1, born from A10, rules this world and contains all the rings of the third and fourth worlds as well. The ten archangels of Briah are: the Crown, Metatron, angel of the presence; Wisdom, Raziel, who revealed the Kabbalah to Adam; Understanding, Tsaphkiel, contemplation of God; Mercy, Tsadkiel, justice of God; Severity, Samael; Beauty, Michael, 'like unto God'; Victory, Haniel, grace of God; Glory, Raphael, the divine physician; Foundation, Gabriel, the man-God; and Kingdom, Sandalphon, the Messiah. From the tenth came C1, the third Crown, and the world of Yetzirah began.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p4": "Hall says the ten archangels of Briah show forth the ten powers of God's name present in Atziluth, which surrounds and runs through all creation. Everything in the lower worlds first exists in the unseen rings above, so creation is the making tangible of the intangible at different rates of vibration. The ten Briatic globes, themselves reflections, are mirrored into Yetzirah, where they become the invisible spiritual zodiac behind the visible constellations; still dimmer, they are yet immensely powerful compared with our world. Here they become the choirs of Yetzirah, all contained in C1, which also contains the whole fourth world. The orders are: the holy living creatures; the Wheels (Ophanim); the Thrones (Aralim); the Dominations (Chashmalim); the Powers (Seraphim, flaming serpents); the Virtues (Melachim, kings); the Principalities (Elohim, gods); the Archangels (Ben Elohim, sons of God); the Angels (Cherubim); and humanity (Ishim, the souls of the just). From the tenth came D1, the fourth Crown, and the world of Assiah began.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p5": "Hall says the light of the ten spheres is reflected from Yetzirah into Assiah, the lowest world, where the globes take on physical matter and become the star system. Assiah, the elemental world of substance, is where humanity fell with Adam: Eden is the three upper worlds, and for his sins man was forced into matter and given 'coats of skin', bodies. When the spiritual forces of the upper worlds strike the elements of the lowest, they are twisted, producing orders of demons to match the good spirits above. All the ancient Mysteries saw matter as the source of evil and spirit as the source of good, because matter limits man and clouds his inner sight so he cannot see his divine possibilities; so matter is called the Adversary. Assiah includes all the solar systems in the universe. Opinions differ on its order, but Hall gives: the Primum Mobile, the fiery mist where the material universe begins; the zodiac, or fixed stars; then the spheres of Saturn, Jupiter, Mars, the sun, Venus, Mercury and the moon; and last the sphere of the four elements. Kircher adds an Empyrean before the Primum Mobile, moving the others down so the moon becomes the tenth.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p6": "Hall says demons and tempters live in Assiah. They too are reflections of the ten globes of Atziluth, but distorted by the base matter they fall on into evil beings the Kabbalists call 'shells'. There are ten orders of demons, matching the ten orders of good spirits, and ten archdemons, matching the ten archangels. Black magicians use these inverted spirits, but in time the demon destroys whoever binds himself to it. Hall lists them, from the evil Crown, Thaumiel, the two-headed 'doubles of God', with Satan and Moloch, down through orders such as the obstructers, the concealment of God, the disturbers, the burners, the wranglers, the 'dispensing raven', the embroiler and the obscene, with archdemons including Adam Belial, Lucifuge, Astaroth, Asmodeus, Belphegor, Baal Chanan, Adramelek and Lilith, to the evil Kingdom, Nahemoth, the impure, with Nahema. The Kabbalists say the worlds follow Ezekiel's vision: the man is Atziluth, the throne Briah, the firmament Yetzirah and the living creatures Assiah, and these spheres are his 'wheels within wheels'. They set a human figure in each world, head at the first globe and feet at the tenth; these four androgynous 'World Men' are the prototypes of humanity.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p7": "Hall says the human body, like the universe, is the material form of ten globes of light, so man is the microcosm, made in the image of the great world. The Kabbalists also pictured a universal man stretching from the top of Atziluth to the bottom of Assiah, which Hall thinks is the secret of the giant figure in Nebuchadnezzar's dream, head in Atziluth, arms in Briah, loins in Yetzirah and legs in Assiah: the Grand Man of the Zohar. He quotes Eliphas Levi: the Zohar opens with the teaching that the science of balance is the key to occult science and that unbalanced forces perish in the void, as the kings of the elder world fell like rootless trees; the earth lay empty until God's spirit found its place, and the face of God rose over the waters and was reflected in them, his brow and eyes forming one triangle in heaven and another in the water, revealing six, the number of creation. Levi says the human form given to God is only an image, since God is beyond any figure. Pascal called God a circle whose centre is everywhere and circumference nowhere; the Zohar rather says the circumference is everywhere and the centre nowhere, and likens the universe to a balance whose pivot is everywhere. For Levi the Zohar is a genesis of light and the Sepher Yetzirah a ladder of truth, whose 32 symbols, numbers and letters, give a method for reaching truth.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p8": "Hall says that placing man at the very centre, D10, shows his true make-up: he exists in four worlds, only one of them visible, and his bodily parts correspond to orders of beings in the higher worlds. The whole universe, all the spheres, runs through him, yet he does not know it, because he cannot control what is greater than himself; still they control him, as his bodily functions show, or he would be lifeless matter. Death is simply the higher rings turning their life away from the lower body. Their control over their material reflection is called life, and a person's 'spirit' is really the name for this host of intelligences focused on matter through a point called the ego. Read this way, the diagram is a cross-section of man, its outer circle his auric egg, or of the cosmos. The Kabbalah's secret training teaches man to climb the rings, unfolding his consciousness until he returns to Ain Soph, through what are called the Fifty Gates of Light; Kircher says Moses passed forty-nine and Christ alone the fiftieth. Westcott's Sepher Yetzirah, from Kircher, lists fifty gates of intelligence from Binah in six orders: the first, 'Elementary', from chaos and the abyss through the elements to mixture; the second, 'Evolution', from minerals and plants through insects, fishes and birds to four-footed animals.",
  "modern.hall.25.s1.p9": "Hall continues the fifty gates. The third order, 'Humanity', runs from the appearance of man and the human body through the gift of the soul, the mystery of Adam and Eve, man as microcosm, the five outer and five inner powers, Adam Kadmon and the angelic beings, to man in the image of God. The fourth, the 'World of Spheres', runs through the heavens of the moon, Mercury, Venus, the sun, Mars, Jupiter and Saturn to the firmament, the Primum Mobile and the Empyrean. The fifth, the 'Angelic World', lists nine orders of angels, an arrangement Hall notes differs from others in his book since the rabbis disagree on their order. The sixth, the 'Archetype', is a single gate: God, Ain Soph, whom no mortal eye has seen. Passing the fiftieth gate leads out of creation into the creative principle, back into the boundless, undivided All. The gates describe an evolution, and the rabbis taught that whoever would reach the highest understanding must pass through every order of life in turn, each a gate because the spirit, rising from lower to higher, finds in each better body new ways to express itself.",
  "modern.hall.25.fig1": "The caption, for the Hebrew letter Shin, says the Kabbalists used it for the trinity of the first three Sephiroth. The middle circle, slightly higher than the others, is Kether, the white head or crown; the other two are Chochmah, the Father, and Binah, the Mother, whose union produces the worlds and all living generations. The letter's three flame-like points have long hidden this creative triad.",
  "modern.hall.25.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram of divine activity, says that for the Kabbalists the Creator's life fills all substance, space and time, but for the diagram it is bounded by Circle 3, the 'boundary line of divine existence'. That life is focused at Point 1, which personifies the impersonal and is called the First Crown, and the creative forces pouring through it appear as the visible universe in the space between, Circle 2.",
  "modern.hall.25.fig3": "The caption, for the chart of the four worlds, says the dark line between the outer rings and A1 is the edge of the original point, and the forty rings inside it are the emanations and worlds that came out of it, all still contained in the First Crown, God, in whom the divine powers, heavenly beings, star worlds and man live and move, and which is itself enclosed by Ain Soph's outer ring, its 'auric egg'. Each ring contains all the rings inside it and is contained by those outside. Power is measured by what one controls, so power decreases toward the centre: A1 rules thirty-nine rings besides itself, B1 only twenty-nine, so A1 is stronger. Spiritual permanence is greatest at the edge and material density at the centre, so the rings grow more material and slower in vibration inward until D10 stands for the chemical elements of the earth. A1, ruler of creation, is still less than the three rings of Ain Soph and bows before the throne of the Creator from whom it came.",
  "modern.hall.26.p1": "Hall calls the Tree of the Sephiroth a priceless summary of the secret philosophy that was the soul of Hasidism, and the Kabbalah Israel's precious heritage, though fewer understand it every year. He says the modern Jew who lacks a sense of its depth is often steeped in 'modernism' and sees the Kabbalah as either an evil or a silly superstition, yet without it the spiritual mysteries of both Testaments stay unsolved. The tree has ten shining globes, the Sephiroth, numbered 1 to 10, in three columns, Mercy on the right, Severity on the left and Mildness between, also read as Wisdom, Strength and Beauty, linked by 22 channels, the Hebrew letters, to which the major Tarot trumps are assigned. Eliphas Levi said that by arranging the Tarot in the right order one could learn all that can be known of God, the universe and oneself. Ten globes plus 22 letters make 32, the paths of wisdom, also called the 32 teeth of the Vast Countenance or the 32 nerves of the divine brain, which Hall likens to the first 32 degrees of Freemasonry. The Kabbalists note that God's name appears 32 times in the first chapter of Genesis (33 in English), and the rabbis count 32 spinal segments leading up to the skull, the temple of wisdom.",
  "modern.hall.26.p2": "Hall says later Jewish scholars combined the four trees into one diagram, called the Sephirothic, Archetypal or Heavenly Adam; some say it is this heavenly Adam, not an earthly man, whose creation Genesis describes. The universe was made from his substance and remains in him. God is never actually inside the Sephiroth, which are only notional vessels marking the limits of the creative essence; Adolphe Franck likens them to coloured glass bowls filled with pure light, which seems to take their colour but stays unchanged. Hall gives a table of the ten, from Kether, the Crown (the Primum Mobile), through Chochmah, Wisdom (the zodiac), Binah, Understanding (Saturn), Chesed, Mercy (Jupiter), Geburah, Severity (Mars), Tiphereth, Beauty (the sun), Netsah, Victory (Venus), Hod, Glory (Mercury), and Jesod, Foundation (the moon), to Malchuth, the Kingdom (the elements), with an alternative scheme shifted by one. He stresses that these are only symbols whose fuller meaning cannot be written or spoken but must be grasped through study and meditation. The Zohar says the written teaching is a garment everyone sees, the wise look at the body beneath it, and the wisest look only at the soul. Levi says no one enters the House of Wisdom without the cloak of Apollonius, self-reliance, and the lamp of Hermes, an illumined, balanced mind.",
  "modern.hall.26.p3": "Hall says the tree is sometimes drawn as a human body, the heavenly man Adam Kadmon, the idea of the universe. Kether is the crown of the head, perhaps the pineal gland; Chochmah and Binah the right and left halves of the brain; Chesed and Geburah the right and left arms; Tiphereth the heart or the whole trunk; Netsah and Hod the right and left legs; Jesod the generative organs; and Malchuth the feet, the base. Sometimes Jesod is the male and Malchuth the female power. This Grand Man is the giant statue of Nebuchadnezzar's dream, with head of gold, chest of silver, body of brass, legs of iron and feet of clay. Medieval Kabbalists also gave each Sephira one of the Ten Commandments and a tenth of the Lord's Prayer. He quotes Blavatsky on the three triads of the Zohar's 'three heads': Kether, the first androgyne, contains the other nine, which together form Adam Kadmon, the dual prototype of humanity. Kether gives forth Chochmah, masculine Wisdom, and Binah, feminine Intelligence, the first trinity; these give forth Chesed, masculine Mercy, and Geburah, feminine Justice, whose union produces Tiphereth, Beauty, the spiritual sun; then Netzah, Firmness, and Hod, Splendour, produce Jesod, Foundation. Malchuth, the Kingdom or Shekinah, is a tenth, dual sphere. The three heads are the intellectual, the sensuous and the physical worlds.",
  "modern.hall.26.p4": "Hall says later Kabbalists also split the tree into five parts. First, Macroprosopus, the Great Face, is Kether together with the nine that issue from it. Second, Abba, the Great Father, is usually Chochmah, Wisdom, the first emanation of Kether, though Ibn Gebirol makes Chochmah the Son, the Word born of Kether and Binah. Third, Aima, the Great Mother, is Binah, the Holy Spirit from whose body the generations come, corresponding to Jehovah, the Demiurge. Fourth, Microprosopus, the Lesser Face or Lesser Adam (Zauir Anpin), as against the Greater (Arikh Anpin), is made of the six Sephiroth from Chesed to Jesod, symbolized by the six-pointed star and the six faces of the cube, the six directions and the six days of creation; Mathers adds Binah as its head, making it sevenfold, and if it is sixfold, Malchuth is the Sabbath. Fifth, the Bride of Microprosopus is Malchuth, made of the four elements, the divine Eve taken from his side, who gathers all the powers of the tree in one sphere, which may be called man. Hall then turns to the order of creation, the divine 'lightning flash' zigzagging through the four worlds.",
  "modern.hall.26.p5": "Hall says that from Ain Soph, the nothing and all, comes Macroprosopus, the Long Face, of whom it is written that thirteen thousand myriads of worlds exist in his skull, drawing their being from him. He is the directed will of Ain Soph, Kether, the crown, and brings forth the nine lesser spheres, of which he is the sum and cause; the 22 Hebrew letters, whose combinations set the laws of the universe, are the sceptre he wields from his flaming throne in Atziluth. From this ancient androgyne come Chochmah, the great Father, and Binah, the great Mother, Abba and Aima, the first male and female and the prototypes of sex, matching the first two letters of JHVH, J and H, and set in the creative world of Briah. The Zohar says all things are established in the balance of male and female, since otherwise they could not last, Wisdom being the Father and Understanding the Mother, bound together, each path shining into the other. Some Kabbalists, including Ibn Gebirol, instead make Kether the Father, Binah the Mother and Chochmah the Son, so that Wisdom, the Son, creates the lower spheres. Binah's symbol is the dove, fit for the brooding love of the universal Mother.",
  "modern.hall.26.p6": "Hall says that because their triad so resembled the Christian Trinity, later Kabbalists rearranged the first three Sephiroth and added a hidden eleventh point, Daath, where the line from Chochmah to Binah crosses the line from Kether to Tiphereth. The first Kabbalists do not mention it, but it was added deliberately: if Chochmah is Kether's active intelligence and Binah its receptive capacity, Daath is the thought Chochmah creates and pours into Binah, so the trinity becomes Chochmah the Father, Binah the Mother or Holy Spirit, and Daath the Word by which the worlds were made. Isaac Myer dismisses Daath as a cover for the fact that Kether, not Chochmah, is the true Father, without explaining its symbolism. In the original scheme the union of Father and Mother produces Microprosopus, the Lesser Countenance, in the world of formation, matching the letter V of the Name; its six powers flow from Binah, its mother, and are the spheres of the sacred planets, the Elohim moving on the face of the deep. Malchuth, the Kingdom, is the Lesser Adam's bride, created back to back with him, matching the final H, and lives in Assiah, made of all the higher powers reflected into the elements. So the tree runs through four worlds, branches in matter and roots in the Ancient of Ancients.",
  "modern.hall.26.p7": "Hall says three columns hold up the tree. The middle one rises from Kether through Daath, Tiphereth and Jesod to Malchuth, and means balance: God sends out opposite poles from within himself while staying free of polarity; its Sephiroth, 1, 6, 9 and 10, add up to 26, the number of Jehovah. The right column, Jachin, rests on Chochmah, outpouring Wisdom, and its three globes are masculine; the left, Boaz, rests on Understanding, which receives, and its globes are feminine. All three meet in Malchuth, where every higher power shows itself. The four globes of the middle column show the creative power in each world: Will, the one divine cause, in the first; Daath, the Word from the divine thought, in the second; Tiphereth, the sun, the focus between God and nature, in the third; and in the fourth the male and female poles of reproduction, Jesod and Malchuth. In Kircher's tree the furnishings of the Tabernacle appear in its parts, showing that God's house and the universe correspond, as the world is the house of the God who makes it. Hall says that if modern science grasped this, it would see that the Kabbalah's makers knew the heavenly plan as well as any modern scholar.",
  "modern.hall.26.p8": "Hall says the four-letter Name, the Tetragrammaton, is pronounced Jehovah. Its first letter, Yod, is the seed, life, flame, cause and the One, the most basic Jewish phallic emblem; its value is 10, the one containing the ten, and the Kabbalah says it is really three Yods, beginning, middle and end. Its throne is Chochmah (for Ibn Gebirol, Kether), from which it fertilizes Binah, the first He; their child is Tiphereth, the Vau, whose number is 6, the six members of the Lesser Adam; the final He is Malchuth, the lower mother. Written top to bottom, the four letters look like a human body: Yod the head, He the arms and shoulders, Vau the trunk, and He the hips and legs, and the same holds roughly in English letters. Inserting Shin in the middle turns Jehovah into Jehoshua, Jesus. Eliphas Levi says the name was sometimes drawn with 24 dots, the 24 powers before the throne, and that the sign of the evil power is Jehovah's sign upside down. Albert Pike writes that the true Masonic Word lies in the hidden meaning of the Name God gave Moses, a meaning lost through the very care taken to hide it, and which holds all we can know of God's nature.",
  "modern.hall.26.fig1": "The caption, for four Sephirothic trees, says the forty circles of the previous chapter's chart are here set out as four trees of ten circles, showing the orders of beings that govern all creation. The trees are the same in each of the four worlds, but the powers of their globes show differently through each world's substance, giving endless variety.",
  "modern.hall.26.fig2": "The caption, for a table of correspondences from Fludd's collected works, says it was specially translated from Latin for students of the Kabbalah and as an example of Robert Fludd's skill at such tables. Hall calls Fludd one of the most eminent Rosicrucians and Freemasons, often called 'the first English Rosicrucian', and notes that his main works came out at the same time as those of Bacon, Shakespeare and the first Rosicrucian writers.",
  "modern.hall.26.fig3": "The caption, for the tree of the later Kabbalists translated from Kircher, says that after dividing the universe into four worlds of ten spheres, the Kabbalists arranged each world's spheres as the Sephirothic Tree: ten circles for the numbers, joined by twenty-two channels for the Hebrew letters, together making the 32 paths of wisdom. Letters and numbers are the keys to all knowledge, since their secret arrangement reveals creation; Hall says this is hidden in the 32nd degree of Freemasonry. There is a tree in each world: the first, in Atziluth, is ten globes of light in the midst of Ain Soph, reflected in the three lower worlds in the same form but with less power. The Kabbalists also made a single composite tree of ten globes gathering all their scattered lore.",
  "modern.hall.26.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Maurice's Indian Antiquities, says it is a simplified version of the elaborate tree before it. The Sephiroth are drawn one over another, shrinking as they lose power and rank: the Crown is the largest and contains all, and the Kingdom, the physical universe, is the smallest and least important.",
  "modern.hall.27.p1": "Hall says Henri Estienne's A World of Wonders (1607) tells of a monk who claimed the patriarch of Jerusalem had shown him a rib of Christ, rays from the star of Bethlehem, a seraph's snout, a cherub's fingernail, the horns of Moses and a box holding Christ's breath. Hall says people who believe a seraph could have a preserved nose cannot grasp the deeper Jewish philosophy; an ancient sage would be startled to hear that a cherub, which Augustine took for the Evangelists, Philo for the outer circle of the heavens and some church fathers for God's wisdom, had grown fingernails. Confusing divine principles with the figures made to represent them has bred terrible misunderstandings, and similar absurdities still block a true reading of the Bible. The Old Testament, especially the Torah, holds not only the story of creation but, locked inside it, the Egyptian teachings Moses received about the birth of the god-man, the initiate, and his rebirth through philosophy. The so-called sixth and seventh books of Moses are spurious medieval black magic. Hall says hardly any Bible readers have sensed the sublime hidden teaching of Sod, the Jewish Mysteries, but the three Kabbalistic methods, Gematria, Notarikon and Temurah, unlock many deep truths.",
  "modern.hall.27.p2": "Hall explains Gematria: swapping letters for their number values, and also reading the hidden purpose of a building or object from its measurements. Mathers gives the example that 'and lo, three men' in Genesis 18:2 has the same value, 701, as the names Michael, Gabriel and Raphael. A triangle with sides of 11, 9 and 6 inches would fit Jehovah, since the sides add up to 26, the value of IHVH. Gematria also reads meaning from the shape and arrangement of a letter's strokes. The Greeks used it as well, and the New Testament, especially the writings credited to John, contains many examples. Nicephorus Callistus said John's Gospel was found in a cave under the Temple, hidden long before Christ; Hall says added material in it supports the view that it was first written without reference to Jesus the man, his sayings originally being mystical speeches of the personified universal mind, and that John's letters and Revelation are similarly veiled. Notarikon makes each letter of a word the first of a new word: from the first word of Genesis come six words meaning that God saw Israel would accept the Law, and Augustine expanded ICHTHYS into 'Jesus Christ, Son of God, Saviour'. Done in reverse, the initials of a phrase form a word, as Amen from 'the Lord is the faithful King'. Because of such devices, priests forbade translating or rewriting the sacred books.",
  "modern.hall.27.p3": "Hall says Temurah covers systems of swapping letters by set tables or patterns: for example, writing the alphabet in two rows and exchanging upper and lower letters, which turns the word Kuzu into IHVH. Another form simply rearranges letters, as when the name of the stone at the centre of the world, from which the earth spread out, is split to mean 'the placing of God'. Temurah can also be a simple anagram, like 'live' reversed into 'evil'. These are among the most complex devices of the ancient rabbis. Hall notes a growing view among scholars that accepted translations do not convey the original scriptures. He quotes Blavatsky: after Hilkiah's first copy of the Book of God vanished, Ezra made a new Bible finished by Judas Maccabeus; copying it from 'horned' into square letters corrupted it, the Masorah finished the damage, and we now have a text under 900 years old full of gaps, additions and deliberate distortions. He also quotes Crawford Howell Toy of Harvard: scribes copying manuscripts made errors, added new material and merged writings by different authors without marking them, as in Micah, Jeremiah and the prophecies under the names of Isaiah and Zechariah.",
  "modern.hall.27.p4": "Hall asks whether the damaged state of the Bible, partly accidental, also served to confuse uninitiated readers and hide the secrets of the Jewish sages. Christians never had the hidden scrolls of Israel's secret teaching, and if the Kabbalists were right that the lost books of the Mosaic Mysteries are woven into the Torah, the scriptures are books within books. Many rabbis feel Christians have never understood the Old Testament and that, having persecuted the Jews, they take liberties in claiming Jewish writings; one rabbi said that if Christians must use them, they should at least do so intelligently. In Genesis the seven Elohim separate light from darkness and the waters above from those below, in line with Hindu, Egyptian and Greek teachings, then make plants, animals and finally man: 'Let us make man in our image... male and female created he them... be fruitful, and multiply, and replenish the earth.' Hall points to the plural 'us' and 'our': translating the plural, male-female Elohim as a single, sexless 'God' made these chapters nearly meaningless, perhaps for fear Christians would be charged with polytheism. The verse shows the Elohim as plural and androgynous, created man male-female before the sexes divided, and so refutes the picture of God as a man, as on the Sistine ceiling. And 'replenish', meaning to fill again, points to a humanity before this creation.",
  "modern.hall.27.p5": "Hall says Bible dictionaries and encyclopedias cannot explain the plural Elohim. The Schaff-Herzog encyclopedia asks whether it meant a plurality of divine beings; Hastings' dictionary calls it 'difficult to explain'; Dr Havernick says it means the abundance of the divine being, which Hall calls typical of efforts to get round it; the International Standard Bible dictionary finds such explanations too clever for the early Hebrews and sees a survival of polytheism; and the Jewish Encyclopedia says the evidence shows the Semites leaned toward polytheism. Many schools have explained Adam. The Neoplatonists saw him as Plato's idea of humanity, the pattern of the human race. Philo saw him as the human mind, able to name the creatures around it but not to understand its own nature. Others likened him to the Pythagorean One, at home in Eden while undivided; when it split into two, the symbol of discord and illusion, the divided being was driven out, and cherubim with a flaming sword guarded the gate of the causal world. So man can return to his first spiritual state only when he becomes one within himself again.",
  "modern.hall.27.p6": "Hall says the secret teaching of Israel, according to the Isarim, spoke of four Adams, one in each world. The first, heavenly Adam lived alone in Atziluth and held every spiritual and material possibility. The second, in Briah, was androgynous, its heel, Malchuth, being the church of Israel that will crush the serpent's head. The third, also androgynous, wore a body of light in Yetzirah. The fourth was the third after his fall into Assiah, when he put on the animal 'coat of skins'; still one being, he was divided inside into two bodies, one male and one female. Adam's universal nature appears in the stories of what he was made from: the dust was to come from the seven worlds, and when they refused, God took it by force. Augustine found that the letters A-D-A-M begin the Greek names of the four directions, east, west, north and south, and saw Adam as a figure of Christ: Eve was formed from Adam's side as he slept, and when Christ died his side was pierced so the sacraments flowed out to form the church. George Foote Moore says the Jewish tradition made Adam a huge mass filling the world, formed of dust from every part of it or from the site of the future altar, and created androgynous: Rabbi Samuel bar Nahman said God made him facing both ways and then sawed him in two, giving each half a back.",
  "modern.hall.27.p7": "Hall says the Zohar speaks of two Adams: a divine one who came out of the highest darkness and made the second, earthly Adam in his own image. The heavenly man was the causal sphere with all its divine powers pictured as a giant person, whose limbs, the Gnostics said, were the basic elements of existence; he may have been shown facing both ways, one face toward his cause and the other toward the sea of the cosmos he was to enter. Philosophically, Adam is man's full spiritual nature, androgynous and not subject to decay, which mortal man barely grasps. As spirit contains matter, Eve stands for the lower, mortal part taken out of the greater spiritual being. She is the temptress who, with the serpent of mortal knowledge, put Adam into a trance in which he forgot his higher self; when he seemed to wake, he actually fell asleep, living now in the body, not the spirit, while the true Adam rested in Paradise and his lesser part wandered in mortal darkness. Hall says Muslims understood better that Paradise was not an earthly garden but a higher, angelic sphere watered by four streams. After his fall Adam landed on Ceylon, which some Hindus hold was Eden; the Arabian Nights says his footprint is still on a mountain there, and Islamic legend says he rejoined his wife and was later buried in Jerusalem by Melchizedek.",
  "modern.hall.27.p8": "Hall says ADM means a species or race, and only through misunderstanding has Adam been taken for one man. As the macrocosm, Adam is the giant androgyne, even the Demiurge; as the microcosm, he is the Demiurge's chief work, given all the Demiurge's powers but not immortality, which the Demiurge lacked. The Gnostic Christians said humanity's redemption was secured when Nous, the universal mind, a being higher than the Demiurge, entered man and gave him conscious immortality. Phallic symbolism clearly mattered in early Jewish mysticism: Hargrave Jennings sees Adam as a form of Shiva's lingam and quotes Gregorie that Noah prayed daily in the Ark before 'the Body of Adam', by which Jennings understands the phallus; an Eastern tradition says Adam's embalmed body was kept above ground and handed down from father to son to Noah, to be buried at last at the centre of the earth, which Jennings identifies with Mount Moriah and Mount Meru. The Golden Legend says Adam was buried with three seeds of the Tree of Knowledge in his mouth. By Notarikon the letters ADM stand for Adam, David and the Messiah, said to share one soul: Adam the soul descending, the Messiah the soul rising, and David the stage between.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The sentence on the Gnostic Nous breaks off ('conferred conscious immortality upon the') where the e-text has lost some words; the object is probably humanity or the elect.",
  "modern.hall.27.p9": "Hall says the Jewish Mysteries, like some Asian schools, taught a doctrine of the shadows of the gods: looking into the abyss, the Elohim saw their own shadows and modelled the lower creation on them. An anonymous Master of Balliol College says that in the Mysteries' drama of creation the Elohim were played by men who traced the outline of a human figure on their own shadows on the wall, which is how drawing began in Egypt and why Egyptian carvings are so flat they still look like shadows. In the early Jewish rites the creation was acted out, with actors playing the creative powers. The red earth Adam was made from may mean fire, since Adam is linked with Yod, the flame, first letter of Jehovah. John says the Temple took forty-six years to build, and Augustine saw a secret Gematria here, since in Greek the name Adam adds up to 46, making Adam a figure of the Temple, both being small copies of the universe. In the Mysteries Adam reproduced spiritually: he cast a shadow of himself onto matter and gave it a soul, but these shadows last only as long as their original. Hall says this is the key to Eve's creation from Adam's side: Adam, the pattern, is reflected into the material world as many ensouled images, together called Eve. Another view puts the division of the sexes in the archetypal world, and Plato saw in the attraction of the sexes an urge to reunite the severed halves of that being.",
  "modern.hall.27.p10": "Hall says the meaning of the division of the sexes in Genesis is much debated, but it is widely agreed that man was first androgynous and will probably become so again. There are two views of how. One says the soul was actually split into male and female halves and remains incomplete until they reunite through love; from this came the much-abused idea of 'soul mates' searching through the ages for each other, and modern marriage rests partly on it. The other says one pole of the androgynous being was suppressed so its energies could go into developing reason; on this view each person is still spiritually complete, but in the material world a man's feminine side and a woman's masculine side lie dormant, and spiritual growth and the teaching of the Mysteries gradually awaken them until the person regains balance. This view makes woman man's full equal rather than his stray half, and sees marriage as a partnership of two complete people of opposite polarity, each awakening what is latent in the other toward individual completeness. The first view treats marriage as an end, the second as a means, and Hall says the deeper schools lean to the second.",
  "modern.hall.27.p11": "Hall says the Christian Church is fundamentally opposed to marriage, holding that the highest spirituality belongs to those who stay virgin. He traces this to early Gnostic sects who taught that having children increased the power of the Demiurge, since the lower world was an evil trap for souls; at the last judgment children would accuse their parents of causing their earthly miseries, as humanity will accuse Adam, whose sin is generally taken to concern generation. More reasonable Gnostics said that the very existence of the lower worlds shows the Creator had a purpose for them, and that doubting his judgment was a grave error. Hall says the Church presumed to correct God by imposing celibacy wherever it could, producing many neurotics; in the Mysteries celibacy is reserved for those of advanced spiritual development and, urged on ordinary people, becomes a dangerous heresy. As Christendom blamed every Jew for the crucifixion, it has slandered every woman for Eve, whereas philosophy reads the story as man being tempted by his emotions away from reason. Church fathers linked Adam with Christ, which softens his sinfulness, since when Augustine likens Eve to the Church he surely does not blame the Church for the Fall. Yet religion has always treated knowledge as fatal to spiritual growth, the Ignorantine Friars being a clear example.",
  "modern.hall.27.p12": "Hall describes a ritual drama, perhaps of Egyptian origin, in which Adam, banished from Eden, is man exiled from truth: he falls through ignorance and redeems himself through wisdom. Eden is the house of the Mysteries, with the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge in its midst. The exiled man tries to pass from the outer court into the inner sanctuary, but a huge creature with a slowly sweeping flaming sword keeps a circle he cannot cross. The cherubim tell him he is dust, made by the builder of forms, that his breath of life will flicker out, and that he belongs outside. Adam answers that he has often begged entry to his Father's house and been sent back into darkness; he admits he was made from dirt by a maker who could not give him immortality, but says that in his wanderings he learned that God sent his only Son into the Demiurge's world, who was crucified on its elements and whose blood saves him, opening a road back to God. Immortality, he says, was already in the very dust he was made of, stamped on the cosmos by the eternal life before the world was made, its sign the cross, and he asks if he can still be refused, now that he knows himself. A voice answers 'He who is aware, IS! Behold!' and Adam finds himself in a radiant place before a tree hung with jewels, round which coils a flaming, winged serpent crowned with stars; it was the serpent that spoke.",
  "modern.hall.27.p13": "Hall continues the drama. Adam asks who the serpent is, and it says it is Satan, the Adversary, who has opposed and accused him since his making, tempted and slandered him, and sworn that none it can mislead shall eat from the Tree of Knowledge. Adam replies that he has served it for ages, and its words brought him only sorrow: its dreams of power gave him pain, its desires agony, its false prophets and false reasoning dismay. He is done with it and its world of illusion and will no longer work in its vineyards; there is no happiness or good in selfishness, hatred and passion, and he renounces its rule forever. The serpent says 'Behold the nature of thy Adversary!' and vanishes in a blaze of light, and in its place stands an angel in gold with great scarlet wings spanning the sky, before whom Adam falls in awe. The angel says it is the Lord who is against him and so brings about his salvation: Adam has hated it, but will bless it in ages to come, for it has led him out of the Demiurge's world, turned him against worldly illusion, weaned him from desire and awakened the immortality it shares, and it bids him follow, for it is the Way, the Life and the Truth.",
  "modern.hall.27.fig1": "The caption, for a plate from the 'Bear' Bible, the first Protestant Bible in Spanish, shows the Merkavah, the chariot of God that Ezekiel saw by the river Chebar: four creatures each with four heads, four wings and calves' hooves, four wheels full of eyes that went wherever the creatures went, coals of fire between them, and above a throne with the likeness of a man. Ezekiel kneels amid a whirlwind of cloud and flame as a hand from the clouds offers him a scroll to eat. Mystics say the wheels are the planets' orbits and the whole solar system is God's chariot, and the branch of the Kabbalah dealing with the sciences of the lower heavens is called the Merkavah. The Zohar says the throne means the traditional law and the man on it the written law. Philo says the two cherubim on the Ark stand for the revolutions of the heavens, the outer circle and the inner sphere, and, facing each other, the two hemispheres. The flaming sword of the cherubim in Genesis is the motion of the heavenly bodies, and probably also the sun's ray.",
  "modern.hall.27.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram of Noah's ark from Myer's Qabbalah, says church fathers including Tertullian, Cyprian, Augustine and Chrysostom saw the ark as a figure of the Church. Bede said Noah was in every way a figure of Christ: he alone of his generation was just, as Christ alone was sinless; Christ had a sevenfold spirit of grace and Noah seven righteous people with him; Noah saved his family by water and wood, Christ saves Christians by baptism and the cross; and the ark's lasting wood is the Church of people who will live forever. Hargrave Jennings adds the zodiac to the diagram, from Aries at the head through Leo, with the door panel for the joined sign Virgo-Libra-Scorpio and the rest for Sagittarius to Pisces. The ark has eleven sections each with three subdivisions, making 33; a cross on the central door sits where the generative organs would be in a body. The main door is where animal lives come down into physical existence, and a small window near the 'crown' is where the spirit escapes. Myer says the twelve signs appeared when the joined Scorpio-Virgo was split and Libra placed between them, and that the three storeys may stand for heaven, man and earth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text's quotation from Myer says '32 constellations or signs'; since the subject is the division into the present zodiac, this is probably a misprint for 12.",
  "modern.hall.28.p1": "Hall says opinions differ widely on where playing cards came from and when they reached Europe. Samuel Weller Singer thought they came to southern Europe from India by way of Arabia. Hall thinks the Tarot was probably part of the magical lore the Knights Templars brought back from the Saracens or Syrian mystical sects, and that to avoid persecution they disguised the leaves of their magic book as a game. Mrs John King Van Rensselaer agrees that returning crusaders brought cards back with other Eastern customs, and that the gypsies, who began roaming Europe at about the same time, also brought cards for fortune-telling. Through the gypsies Hall traces the Tarot to Egyptian religious symbolism, and he cites Samuel Roberts's book The Gypsies as proving their Egyptian origin; English laws under Henry VIII describe them as 'an outlandish people, calling themselves Egyptians'. A legend says that after the Serapeum in Alexandria was destroyed, its priests banded together to keep the rites of Serapis, and their descendants, the gypsies, wandered the earth carrying the most precious book saved from the burning library, the Book of Thoth, the Tarot.",
  "modern.hall.28.p2": "Hall says Court de Gebelin derived 'Tarot' from Egyptian words meaning 'royal road', the royal road to wisdom. P. Christian, speaking for a French secret society, describes an Egyptian initiation in which the 22 major cards were huge panels lining a gallery, each explained to the candidate in turn, and Schure hints at the same. Hall notes that these French mystics offer no evidence beyond their own word, and that the so-called Egyptian Tarots in circulation are unproven, modern in drawing and French rather than Egyptian in feeling. He thinks the Tarot is central to Rosicrucian symbolism, perhaps the very book of universal knowledge the order claimed; the early Rosicrucian manifestos speak of the 'Rota Mundi', and 'Rota' rearranged spells 'Taro'. W. F. C. Wigston found that Bacon used Tarot symbolism in his ciphers: the numbers 21, 56 and 78, all tied to the divisions of the deck, recur in his cryptograms, and Bacon's first name appears 21 times on page 56 of the Histories in the 1623 Shakespeare Folio. The cards also interest Masons, numerologists, Kabbalists and alchemists, and, as the Greeks placed their letters on the body of the Logos, the Tarot cards correspond to the parts of the universe and of the human body, the key to man's magical make-up.",
  "modern.hall.28.p3": "Hall says the Tarot cards should be read in three ways: each as a complete symbol of one principle, law, power or element in nature; in relation to one another, as one force acting on another; and as the vowels and consonants of a philosophical alphabet. Their symbols stand for the laws behind all phenomena, with matching numbers, and as every structure has basic parts, the cards stand for the parts of philosophy, so that whatever the subject, each card can be matched to one of its essential elements by mathematical and philosophical law. Eliphas Levi says a prisoner with only the Tarot, if he knew how to use it, could gain universal knowledge in a few years and speak on every subject with unrivalled learning. The experts disagree hopelessly: Court de Gebelin and Etteilla, the first authorities, contradict each other; Levi discredits both; Waite and Paul Case reject Levi's order of the trumps as meant to mislead; and the 'reformed Tarotists' regard Levi's followers, Papus, Christian, Westcott and Schure, as honest but in the dark for lack of Pamela Colman Smith's new deck as revised by Waite.",
  "modern.hall.28.p4": "Hall says most writers, Waite excepted, assume the 22 major trumps match the 22 Hebrew letters, merely because both number 22. Postel, Saint-Martin and Levi all wrote books in 22 parts, and the trumps show scenes from Revelation, which also has 22 chapters. Assuming the Kabbalah holds the answer has led people to neglect other lines of research, and matching trumps to letters has not been very successful. It depends first on placing the unnumbered card, the Fool: Levi puts it between the 20th and 21st cards, giving it the letter Shin, followed by Papus, Christian and Waite, though Waite says this is wrong; Westcott makes it the 22nd; Court de Gebelin and Paul Case put it first, since zero naturally comes before one. Even then, assigning letters in order is unconvincing; Waite says he is not satisfied there is any valid correspondence. Hall suggests the trumps may no longer be in the order they had as the leaves of Hermes's book, perhaps deliberately shuffled by the Egyptians or Arabs. Case's system is better than most but rests on two doubtful points, Waite's revised deck and giving Aleph to the zero card, which in effect makes zero equal to Aleph and so to one.",
  "modern.hall.28.p5": "Hall says Court de Gebelin wisely gave the zero card to Ain Soph, the unknowable first cause. As the centre of the Bembine Table shows the creative power ringed by seven triads of gods, the zero card may be the eternal power of which the other 21 trumps are limited expressions, their common denominator; the Hebrew letter from which all others come is Yod. He adds another guess: the Fool has been likened to the material world, the world of unreality, a motley costume over the divine substance, a Mardi Gras of divine sparks dressed as fools, perhaps placed in the deck to deceive those who cannot see through illusion. The initiates gave the cards to the foolish and ignorant, so they became toys and even tools of vice, and man's bad habits unwittingly preserved his philosophy; Papus admires the initiates for making vice do more good than virtue. If knowledge was entrusted to fools, Hall asks, should it not be sought in the Fool's card? Laid before the others in a row, the Fool seems to walk toward them, like a blindfolded candidate about to pass through the gates of wisdom. If the Fool is left out, one Hebrew letter has no card and must be given to an imagined card of the elements, thought to have been broken up into the 56 minor cards; the major trumps may have been divided similarly.",
  "modern.hall.28.p6": "Hall describes the first trump, the Juggler or Magician. Court de Gebelin says it shows creation as a dream, existence as a juggling of divine elements and life as a game of chance; nature's wonders are cosmic sleight of hand, and man is like the ball that seems to vanish but is hidden in the juggler's palm. He is also the adept, Omar Khayyam's 'master of the show', teaching that the wise direct nature's phenomena and are never fooled by them. On his table lie a cup (the Grail and Joseph's cup in Benjamin's sack), a coin (the tribute money and a Master Builder's wages) and a sword (Goliath's, and the philosopher's blade that divides false from true). His hat is the figure-eight lemniscate, the first motion of creation; his right hand points to the earth, his left holds up Jacob's rod or the budding staff, the spine topped by the globe of intelligence. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he wears a uraeus, his table is a cube and his belt a serpent biting its tail. The second trump, the Female Pope, recalls the legend of Pope Joan, who reigned disguised as a man and was stoned when found out. She sits crowned with a tiara and crescent, holding the Torah, half closed, and gold and silver keys, between the two pillars with a veil hung between them, on a chequered floor; sometimes Juno replaces her. She stands for the Shekinah, divine wisdom; in the pseudo-Egyptian deck she is veiled, as is half her book, since truth is only half revealed.",
  "modern.hall.28.p7": "Hall says the third trump, the Empress, is likened to the 'woman clothed with the sun' in Revelation: a winged woman on a throne, holding a shield with a phoenix and a sceptre topped with an orb or three-leaved flower, sometimes with a crescent under her foot, crowned or ringed with stars. Called Generation, she is the threefold spiritual world from which the fourfold material world comes, and to the initiate she is the 'alma mater' from whose body he is born again. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck she sits on a cube covered with eyes, a bird on her finger, in a golden halo, and she is often shown pregnant. The fourth trump, the Emperor, is linked by his number with the Pythagorean four; he is the Demiurge, king of the lower world, in armour on a cubic throne marked with a phoenix, legs crossed, holding a sceptre and orb as ruler of the world, with the sun and moon, the great king's eyes, on his breast. His posture forms the sign of sulphur, the alchemical king. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he is in profile, wears a Masonic apron whose skirt forms a right-angled triangle, the crown of the north and a uraeus.",
  "modern.hall.28.p8": "Hall says the fifth trump, the Pope, is the high priest of a pagan or Christian mystery school: he wears the tiara, holds a triple cross over the globe, makes 'the ecclesiastic sign of esotericism' with a stigmatized hand, and two kneeling acolytes are before him, his throne backed by a heavenly and an earthly column. He is the master of the mystery of life, the Pythagoreans' spiritual physician; the two kneeling figures are the illusory world of polarity bowing before the initiate who has risen to spiritual reality. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he wears the uraeus, and a white and a black figure kneel before him, life and death, good and evil; the tiara and triple cross show his rule over the three worlds. The sixth trump, the Lovers, has two forms: a priest marrying a youth and maiden, perhaps Adam and Eve, sometimes with a winged figure shooting them with an arrow; or a youth between a crowned, winged woman and a woman crowned with vine leaves like a bacchante, his guardian angel and his ever-present demon. The youth stands at the parting of the ways, choosing between virtue and vice, the eternal and the passing. Above him is the genius of Fate, his star, mistaken for Cupid, whose blind arrow strikes him if he chooses badly; in the pseudo-Egyptian deck the arrow points at vice, whose road ends in ruin. The card teaches that the price of choice is responsibility.",
  "modern.hall.28.p9": "Hall says the seventh trump, the Chariot, shows a crowned conqueror in a chariot drawn by black and white sphinxes or horses, under a starry canopy on four columns. He is the Exalted One riding the chariot of creation; its number seven shows the seven planets as the chariots of the sun, the four columns as the four great ones holding up the worlds. He holds the sun's sceptre, has crescent moons on his shoulders, the Urim and Thummim, and the sphinxes are the hidden power that moves him through his universe. In some decks he is the reborn man, rising out of the cubic stone of the chariot body, the three rising out of the four, like the raised flap of the Master Mason's apron. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he carries the curved sword of the moon, is bearded, wears a collar of planetary orbits, and his sceptre bears a square, circle and triangle. The eighth trump, Justice, shows a crowned woman on a throne whose back rises as two columns, holding a sword and scales: it recalls the weighing of the soul before Osiris and teaches that only balanced forces last, while eternal justice cuts down the unbalanced. Sometimes her own braid is twisted round her neck like a noose, suggesting that man's own actions undo him. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck she sits on three steps and is blindfolded. Hall notes that Waite swapped the eighth and eleventh trumps.",
  "modern.hall.28.p10": "Hall says the ninth trump, the Hermit, shows an old man in a monk's habit leaning on a staff, popularly Diogenes looking for an honest man. He half hides a lamp in his cloak, standing for the secret orders that have long hidden the light of the ancient wisdom from the profane; his staff is knowledge, man's only lasting support, sometimes divided into seven parts for the seven centres of the spine. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he shields the lamp with a rectangular cloak, since wisdom exposed to ignorance would be snuffed out like a flame in a storm, and man's bodies are a cloak through which his divine nature faintly shows. Through renunciation, the Hermetic life, man gains depth and peace. The tenth trump, the Wheel of Fortune, shows an eight-spoked wheel, the Buddhist cycle of necessity, with Anubis rising and Typhon falling on its rim, good and evil, each in turn rising and sinking, and above them a motionless sphinx with the sword of justice, the perfect balance of universal wisdom. The wheel is the lower universe with divine wisdom as eternal judge; in India the chakra is linked with the life centres of a world or person. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck the sphinx holds a javelin and Typhon is thrown off; the wheel's supports are the world's axis with the sphinx at the north pole, and sometimes the wheel sits in a boat on the ocean of illusion, the only foundation of the cycle of necessity.",
  "modern.hall.28.p11": "Hall says the eleventh trump, Strength, shows a girl in a lemniscate hat with her hands on the jaws of a lion. Writers argue whether she is closing or opening its mouth; most say closing, but Hall thinks she looks to be opening it. She is spiritual strength, the lion either the animal world she masters or the secret wisdom she commands; astronomically, the lion is the summer solstice and the girl Virgo, who robs the lion of his strength when the sun enters her sign. Solomon's throne had lions, and he was likened to a lion holding the key of wisdom in its teeth, so she may be opening the mouth to find the key, since courage is needed for knowledge. The twelfth trump, the Hanged Man, shows a youth hanging by his left leg from a beam set on two trunks, each with six branches cut off; his right leg crosses behind the left and his arms behind his back, forming a cross over a downward triangle, an inverted sign of sulphur, which Levi says means the completion of the Great Work. Levi likens him to Prometheus, the feet raised showing the lower nature spiritualized, but the head below the body may also mean lost spiritual powers. The twelve stumps are the zodiac in two groups, polarity briefly ruling over balance, and man must turn his life upside down, giving up the rule of gold for the golden rule. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck he hangs between palms as the sun god who dies each year.",
  "modern.hall.28.p12": "Hall says the thirteenth trump, Death, shows a skeleton reaping heads, hands and feet rising from the ground, and in some decks it has cut off one of its own feet, showing that imbalance and destruction are the same. The skeleton, the body's foundation, fits the first God, the foundation of creation; physically it means death, philosophically the urge that draws every being back into the divine state it had before the illusory world appeared. The scythe blade is the moon with its crystallizing power, the field the universe, and all that grows from the earth will be cut down and return to it. Kings, queens, courtesans and knaves are all alike to death; some decks show an armoured rider on a white horse trampling young and old. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck a rainbow behind Death shows that the body's mortality wins the spirit's immortality; death destroys form, never life, and the card stands for constant renewal, breaking down so that things can be rebuilt higher. The fourteenth trump, Temperance, shows an angel with the sun on her brow pouring from a full urn into an empty one, without losing a drop, then reversing them: life flowing from the invisible into the visible and back. She is an agent of Jehovah, the Demiurge; the sun on her brow draws water up as vapour to fall as rain, and the card also shows the life forces passing between the poles of the creative system. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck the figure is male, pouring from a gold urn into a silver one.",
  "modern.hall.28.p13": "Hall says the fifteenth trump, the Devil, shows a Pan-like figure with ram's or deer's horns, a man's body and goat's or dragon's legs, standing on a cube to which two satyrs are chained, holding a torch as sceptre. It stands for the magic powers of the astral light, the universal mirror in which divine forces appear upside down, in hellish form; its bat wings show it belongs to the shadowy lower world, the chained figures are man's animal natures, and the torch is the false light that leads the unenlightened to ruin. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck it is Typhon, a winged mix of hog, man, bat, crocodile and hippopotamus, holding up a firebrand amid its own destruction, created by man's misdeeds, which turn on their maker. The sixteenth trump, the Fire of Heaven, shows lightning from the sun striking the crown-shaped battlements of a tower; the crown is smaller than the tower, perhaps falling because it was inadequate. The bolt is sometimes shaped like the sign of Scorpio, and the tower may be a phallic emblem. Two figures fall from it, and the card is linked with the Fall: man's divine nature is the tower, and when his crown is destroyed he falls into material illusion; it is also a key to the mystery of sex. Gold coins pour from the breach, suggesting hidden powers. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck the tower is a pyramid with its top shattered, pointing to the missing capstone, and the falling figure resembles the Hebrew letter Ayin, which Levi links with the card.",
  "modern.hall.28.p14": "Hall says the seventeenth trump, the Stars, shows a naked girl kneeling with one foot in water and one on land, pouring two urns over land and sea, her body somewhat like a swastika. Above her are eight stars, one large and bright, which Court de Gebelin takes for Sirius, with the other seven as the sacred planets; he sees the girl as Isis bringing the Nile flood that came with the rising of the Dog Star. Her nakedness may mean nature gets her green garment only when the flood frees the seeds, and the bush and bird or butterfly mean growth and resurrection. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck the great star holds a black-and-white diamond, the bush is a tall three-headed plant with a butterfly, Isis forms an upright triangle, and the water and earth under her feet are nature's opposites sharing in the divine bounty. The eighteenth trump, the Moon, shows the moon rising between a light tower and a dark one, a dog and a wolf howling at it, and a crayfish crawling out of a pool, with a path winding into the distance. Court de Gebelin, citing Pausanias, says the Egyptians believed the Nile rose from the moon goddess's tears, shown falling from the moon's face; the towers are the Pillars of Hercules, beyond which sun and moon never went; the dogs are the tropics, guarding the sun and moon from straying near the poles; and the crayfish is the moon's backward motion.",
  "modern.hall.28.p15": "Hall says the Moon card also shows the path of wisdom: man emerges from the pool of illusion, gets past the guardians of wisdom's gates, passes between the fortresses of science and theology and follows the winding road to spiritual freedom, lit faintly by reason, the moon, which only reflects divine wisdom. The nineteenth trump, the Sun, shows two children, probably the Twins, in a garden ringed with flowers before a brick wall, one of them properly male and the other female, with the sun rising above in straight and wavy rays and thirteen drops falling from it. Levi sees the children as Faith and Reason, which must coexist while the world lasts, since balance needs two feet, the worlds two forces and generation two sexes. The sun of truth shines on the world's garden, over which the two preside; the world's harmony depends on uniting mind and heart. Gemini, ruled by Mercury, makes them the two serpents of the caduceus. The twentieth trump, Judgement, shows three figures rising, though only one coffin is shown, as an angel, probably Gabriel, sounds a trumpet: the freeing of man's threefold spirit from the body, only a third of which actually enters the body. Court de Gebelin thinks it may show creation rather than resurrection, which Hall calls nearly the same in philosophy; the trumpet is the creative Word. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck three mummies rise from one case.",
  "modern.hall.28.p16": "Hall says the twenty-first trump, the World, shows a woman in a scarf blown into the shape of the Hebrew letter Kaph, a wand in each hand and her left leg crossed behind the right, forming the alchemical sign of sulphur, inside an oval wreath which Levi likens to Kether, with Ezekiel's four creatures in the corners. Called the microcosm and macrocosm, it sums up everything that builds creation: the figure is the divine fire at the heart of the mystery, the wreath is nature around it, the creatures are the elements, worlds and forces coming from that centre, and the wreath is also the initiate's crown, won by mastering the four guardians and coming before unveiled truth. In the pseudo-Egyptian deck the creatures surround a wreath of twelve three-leaved flowers, the decans, and a kneeling figure plays a three-stringed harp, since the spirit must harmonize its threefold lower nature before winning immortality. The four suits of the minor cards correspond to the four elements, directions and Kabbalistic worlds, with the four-letter name IHVH as their key. They also stand for the classes of society: cups the priesthood, swords the military, coins the merchants and rods the farmers, and in Court de Gebelin's 'political geography' the north, east, west and south; the pip cards are nations, the kings governments, the queens religions, the knights histories and the pages arts and sciences. Hall declines to discuss fortune-telling with the cards, which goes against their purpose.",
  "modern.hall.28.p17": "Hall says European museums and private collections hold many early playing cards, some beautifully hand-painted with portraits of rulers and their families. England favoured engraved cards, and the British Museum has stencilled ones; Chatto shows heraldic cards with the arms of Pope Clement IX on the king of clubs. There were philosophical decks with Greek and Roman myths, educational decks with maps and history, cards found bound into old book covers, Japanese games needing knowledge of the national literature, round Indian cards with Eastern myths, cards of wood, ivory and metal, satirical cards, commemorative cards, and an American Civil War deck with stars, eagles, anchors and flags for suits and generals as court cards. Modern cards are the Tarot's minor suits without the page, leaving 13 per suit, and still follow the year: red and black for the halves of the year north and south of the equator; four suits for the seasons, the Greek ages and the Hindu yugas; twelve court cards for the zodiac, in triads like the top of the Bembine Table; ten pips per suit for the Sephirothic trees in the four worlds; 13 cards for the lunar months and 52 for the weeks. Counting pips and the court cards as 11, 12 and 13 gives 364, and the joker makes 365. Milton Pottenger thought the United States was planned on the deck, to end as 52 states governed from a 53rd district, Washington, D.C.",
  "modern.hall.28.p18": "Hall says the court cards hold Masonic symbols: nine face forward and three are in profile, the broken 'Wheel of the Law', for nine months before birth and three degrees of spiritual growth. The four armed kings are the Egyptian architects who carved out the universe with knives, and the cardinal signs; the four queens, holding eight-petalled flowers of Christ, the fixed signs; and the four jacks, two with acacia sprigs, the mutable signs. The spade court cards turn away from the spade, as if fearing this sign of death, and the king of clubs, holding the orb, is the grand master of the cards. Hall calls chess the most meaningful of games, 'the royal game', older in India and China than in Europe. The 64 black and white squares are the floor of the house of the Mysteries, and the white king is Ormuzd and the black Ahriman, fighting the age-long war of light and darkness. In man the kings are the spirit, the queens the mind, the bishops the emotions, the knights the vitality, the rooks the body, and the pawns the senses, the eight parts of the soul; the king's side is positive, the queen's negative. White is the self, black the false self, so the game is each part of man fighting its own shadow. Each piece's move reveals its nature: the rook, the body, moves square; the bishop, the emotions, on the slant; and the king, the spirit, cannot be taken, but loses when it cannot escape.",
  "modern.hall.28.fig1": "The caption, for four early Portuguese jacks from Chatto's history of playing cards, quotes Chatto that some Portuguese cards look as if they were copied from an Eastern model, especially the money and club suits: the round 'money' sign looks more like the chakra, the discus of Vishnu in Indian drawings, than a coin, and the club is topped with a diamond, another of Vishnu's attributes. Hall adds that Rosicrucian and Masonic emblems appear on various medieval decks, and that since these orders often hid their secrets in cryptic pictures, the strange designs on cards probably served to hide and preserve their political and philosophical secrets; the frontispiece of Chatto's book shows a knave of hearts with a shield bearing a crowned Rosicrucian rose.",
  "modern.hall.28.fig2": "The caption, for a card from the Mantegna deck from Taylor's History of Playing Cards, says a complete set of fifty cards sold in 1820 for the then amazing sum of eighty pounds. The fifty subjects run from the ranks of society (beggar, page, goldsmith, merchant, gentleman, knight, doge, king, emperor and pope), through Apollo and the nine Muses, the liberal arts and sciences with philosophy, astrology, theology, astronomy, chronology and cosmogony, the virtues, and the seven planets, to the eighth sphere, the Primum Mobile and the First Cause. Hall says their Kabbalistic meaning is plain and that they may correspond to the fifty gates of light of the Kabbalah.",
  "modern.hall.29.p1": "Hall says much of the first five books of the Bible clearly comes from the initiation rites of the Egyptian Mysteries: the priests of Isis knew much occult lore, and the Israelites learned from them during their captivity about God and how to worship him. The books are traditionally credited to Moses, but this is disputed, and there is much evidence that they were compiled much later from oral tradition. Thomas Inman says that books claiming to be by Moses prove no more than those claiming to be by Homer, Orpheus, Enoch, Mormon or Junius, and that the mentions of Moses in the time of the early kings were added later to make it seem David and Solomon knew of him. Hall thinks this too sweeping. Inman doubted that Moses existed at all, because the name resembles an old name for the sun, and showed that by swapping two letters Moses becomes Shemmah, a name for the heavenly globe, so making him another sun myth. Hall says Inman overlooked that initiates were often given sun names to show that the solar power had been reborn in them. He thinks it far more likely that Moses was a real envoy of the secret schools, working like many others to teach early peoples the mysteries of their immortal souls.",
  "modern.hall.29.p2": "Hall says Moses's real name will probably never be known. In its secret Egyptian sense, 'Moses' means someone admitted to the Mystery schools who has gone out to teach the ignorant what the temples of Isis, Osiris and Serapis taught. People argue over whether he was a Jew raised by Egypt's royal house or a full Egyptian, and a few identify him with Hermes, since both received tablets written by the finger of God. His story, from the basket in the bulrushes to his revolt against Egypt, matches the ceremonies Egyptian candidates went through, and also the movements of the heavens. So it is no surprise that Moses, initiated in Egypt, taught the Jews Egyptian esoteric principles. Hall claims Egypt's religion was older than the Egyptians knew: their divine ancestors were Atlanteans who fled their seven islands after volcanic disasters and settled Egypt, then an Atlantean colony. As Odin did in Scandinavia and Quetzalcoatl in Mexico, Moses founded a secret school among the twelve tribes, the Tabernacle Mysteries. The Tabernacle was a portable copy of an Egyptian temple, every part of it symbolic: to the ignorant a place of sacrifice, to the wise a temple of learning.",
  "modern.hall.29.p3": "Hall says the Bible is understood by its best minds to be allegory, but few study its symbols. Moses is said to have given a chosen few an oral teaching, passed on by word of mouth, that unlocked the hidden meaning of the allegories; the Jews called these keys the Qabbalah. The pagans made two-faced Janus keeper of the key to wisdom's temple; Janus became St. Peter with the key of heaven, and the Pope's gold and silver keys stand for the same secret doctrine. Egyptian temples, from which the Tabernacle was copied, were models of the universe: candidates entered by birth, wound through earthly life and passed through the veil of Death back into the unseen world. Socrates's last words, about owing a cock to Asclepius, hinted that death was his great initiation, since a cock was sacrificed when a candidate entered the Mysteries. The Tabernacle's furnishings also followed Egyptian models: the Ark of the Covenant, down to the kneeling figures on its lid, copies the Egyptian ark that priests on reliefs at Philae carry on poles. Hall's account follows Josephus's Antiquities, Book Three, and quotes the 1599 'Breeches' Bible in its original spelling.",
  "modern.hall.29.s1.p1": "Hall says Moses, speaking for Jehovah, named two architects for the Tabernacle, Besaleel of the tribe of Judah and Aholiab of the tribe of Dan, and the people chose them too. In Jacob's deathbed blessing Judah's symbol is a lion and Dan's a serpent or bird, possibly an eagle. The lion and eagle are two of the four cherubic beasts, the fixed signs of the zodiac, and the Rosicrucian alchemists made the Stone of the Wise from the Blood of the Red Lion and the Gluten of the White Eagle; Hall suspects a hidden link between these alchemical fire and water and the two tribes. As the Tabernacle was God's dwelling among men, the soul body in man houses his divine nature, with a twelvefold material constitution gathered round it as the tribes camped round the sacred enclosure. Hebrews calls the Tabernacle a 'paterne and shadowe of heavenly things', a physical symbol of an invisible spiritual institution. Hall then quotes Exodus 25, where God asks for a freewill offering of gold, silver, brass, coloured cloth, linen, goats' hair, skins, shittim wood, oil, spices and stones, so that a sanctuary may be built to his pattern for him to dwell among them.",
  "modern.hall.29.s1.p2": "Hall describes the layout. The court was fifty by a hundred cubits, a cubit being the length from elbow to fingertip, about eighteen inches. It was walled by linen curtains hung on brass pillars five cubits apart, twenty on each long side and ten on each short side, with brass bases and silver capitals. The long sides faced north and south and the entrance faced east, showing the influence of sun worship. The court's main purpose was to set apart the tent in its middle. Inside the eastern entrance stood the Altar of Burnt Offerings, brass over wood with horns of bulls and rams, and beyond it the Laver, a two-part vessel: an upper bowl feeding a lower basin where the priests washed, said to be covered with the mirrors of the women of the twelve tribes. Josephus gives the tent itself as thirty cubits long, ten wide and ten high. Bible scholars divide it into two rooms: the Holy Place, twenty cubits long, holding the seven-branched Candlestick, the Table of Shewbread and the Altar of Incense; and the Holy of Holies, ten cubits each way, holding only the Ark of the Covenant. A veil embroidered with flowers, but no animal or human figures, divided them.",
  "modern.hall.29.s1.p3": "Hall says Josephus hints at a third room made by dividing the Holy Place, and states firmly that Moses divided the tent into three, though most writers ignore this. The veil before the Holy of Holies hung on four pillars, probably for the four elements, and seven pillars stood at the tent's entrance for the seven senses and the seven vowels of the Sacred Name; that only five are mentioned later may be because man now has only five developed senses and five active vowels. He quotes the Baraitha on the curtains: ten of blue, purple, scarlet and fine linen; eleven of goats' hair, thirty cubits long; and, says Rabbi Judah, two covers, the lower of red-dyed rams' skins and the upper of badgers' skins. Calmet thinks 'badger' really means dark purple, probably a heavy waterproof cloth. In the wilderness a pillar of fire was said to hang over the Tabernacle by night and smoke by day; the Jews called this cloud the Shechinah, the sign of the Lord's presence. A quoted early Jewish text describes the clouds of glory around Israel on every side for forty years, levelling the way, killing serpents and scorpions, burning thorns and guiding them straight.",
  "modern.hall.29.s2.p1": "Hall says the Tabernacle, its furniture and its rites, read esoterically, match the structure, organs and functions of the human body. The bronze Altar of Burnt Offerings at the entrance, five cubits square and three high, with a grill over the fire, meant that a candidate must sacrifice not an innocent bull or ram but its counterpart in himself: the bull stands for his earthy nature, to be burned up by the fire of his divinity. Pagan sacrifice of beasts and sometimes of people came from misunderstanding this. The Laver beyond it told the priest to clean soul as well as body, for no one unclean in body and mind can enter God's presence and live. The tent faced east to catch the first sunlight, and its walls were hung with drapes embroidered with cherubs. On the south side of the Holy Place stood the gold lampstand, said to weigh about a hundred pounds, with a central stem and six arms bearing seven lamps, decorated with seventy-two almonds, knops and flowers; Josephus says seventy, but Hall says the Hebrews' round seventy means seventy-two. Opposite, on the north, a table held twelve loaves of shewbread in two stacks or rows of six, topped by two incense burners whose smoke carried the soul of the bread up to the Lord.",
  "modern.hall.29.s2.p2": "Hall says the gold-covered Altar of Incense, a cubit square and two cubits high, stood near the partition to the Holy of Holies. It stands for the human larynx, which lies between the Holy Place, the trunk of the body, and the Holy of Holies, the head, so that a man's words rise from it as an offering to the Lord. Only the High Priest could enter the Holy of Holies, and only at set times. It held nothing but the Ark of the Covenant against the western wall, made of shittim wood plated with gold inside and out, two and a half cubits by one and a half by one and a half, and holding the tablets of the Law. On its gold lid knelt two Cherubim facing each other with wings arched over, and between their wings, on the mercy seat, the Lord came down to speak with his High Priest. All the larger furnishings had rings and poles so four or more men could carry them, and the poles stayed in the Ark until it was set in the Holy of Holies of Solomon's Temple. Hall says the early Jews knew at least partly that the Tabernacle was symbolic, and Josephus, though criticized for reading it through Egyptian and Greek paganism, gave an explanation worth considering, which follows.",
  "modern.hall.29.s2.p3": "Hall quotes Josephus. Moses divided the Tabernacle into three parts: two open to the priests stood for land and sea, open to everyone, and the third, kept for God, for heaven, which men cannot reach. The twelve loaves meant the twelve months; the lampstand's seventy parts the planetary divisions called Decani; its seven lamps the seven planets. The four materials of the veils were the four elements: linen the earth, where flax grows; purple the sea, since the dye comes from a shellfish; blue the air; and scarlet fire. The High Priest's linen robe meant the earth, its blue the sky, its pomegranates lightning and its bells thunder. The Ephod showed that God made the universe from four elements, and its gold the light that lights all things. The breastplate in its middle stood for the earth at the world's centre, the girdle for the ocean surrounding everything, the two sardonyx shoulder buttons for the sun and moon, and the twelve stones for the months or the zodiac signs. The blue mitre meant heaven, with the name of God on it and a gold crown for God's splendour. Hall adds that the Tabernacle took seven months to build and was dedicated at the new moon.",
  "modern.hall.29.s2.p4": "Hall says the metals were symbolic. Gold stands for spirituality, so gold plating over shittim wood shows the spiritual nature glorifying the human nature. Mystics teach that man's body is surrounded by invisible bodies of many colours; in most people the spiritual nature is shut inside the material, but in a few it has come outside and surrounds them with radiance. The silver capitals refer to the moon, sacred to Jews and Egyptians alike; the priests held secret rites at new and full moon, both sacred to Jehovah. The ancients said silver is gold with its sun-ray turned inward: gold is the spiritual soul, silver the purified human nature. Brass, an alloy of precious and base metal, stands for ordinary people, a mix of higher and lower elements. The three divisions of the Tabernacle should interest Freemasons, as they are the three degrees of the Blue Lodge, and the three orders of priests survive as Entered Apprentice, Fellow Craft and Master Mason. The Hawaiians built a similar tabernacle with its rooms stacked one above another, and the three rooms also match the three main chambers of the Great Pyramid.",
  "modern.hall.29.s3.p1": "Hall says priestly robes, Jewish, Christian and pagan, still carry a hidden language in their colours and shapes. The ordinary priest's garments were called Cahanaeae and the High Priest's Cahanaeae Rabbae. The priest wore short trousers called Machanese, over them the Chethone, a fine full-length linen robe with long sleeves tied to the arms, a brightly embroidered sash wound high round the waist with one end hanging in front, and a close linen cap called Masnaemphthes. The High Priest wore the same with additions. Over the linen robe went the Meeir, a seamless, sleeveless sky-blue garment almost to the feet, fringed with alternating gold bells and pomegranates, which Ecclesiasticus says sounded as he walked as a memorial for his people; it was bound with an embroidered girdle threaded with gold. Over the upper part went the Ephod, a short coat woven of many colours, probably red, blue, purple and white, with gold wire. It was fastened at each shoulder by a large onyx button engraved with six of the names of Jacob's twelve sons. These stones were thought to be oracles: when the High Priest asked a question, a light in the right one meant yes from Jehovah and in the left one no.",
  "modern.hall.29.s3.p2": "Hall says the front of the Ephod held the Essen, the Breastplate of Righteousness and Prophecy, a square frame of embroidery set with twelve large, valuable stones in gold sockets and held up by gold chains because of its weight. Like the onyx buttons, its stones could light up with divine glory as an oracle. He quotes Josephus that the stones shone before a battle to show victory, so that Greeks who respected Jewish law called the breastplate 'the Oracle'; Josephus adds that they stopped shining two hundred years before he wrote because the Jews had broken God's laws. Since the Jews learned astronomy from Egypt, Hall thinks the twelve stones probably stood for the zodiac, the jewels on the breast of the Universal Man, the Ancient of Days of the Zohar. Many peoples had twelve gods under a supreme one, and Jewish and Christian writings have twelve prophets, patriarchs, tribes and apostles, all pointing to a twelvefold deity working through twelve channels. The secret doctrine taught that the jewels were centres of life in the priest's own body that could absorb and radiate divine light, like the Indian lotus. The rabbis said each linen thread had twenty-four strands: the experience of the day's twenty-four hours, the Mason's twenty-four-inch rule, weaves the Garments of Glory.",
  "modern.hall.29.s4": "Hall says a pocket behind the breastplate held the Urim and Thummim, used for divination but otherwise little understood. Some think they were small sacred stones brought from Egypt, others dice cast on the ground, others holy names on gold plates carried as charms; Gardner says the names mean 'lights and perfections' or 'light and truth', like the figures of Ra and Themi on Egyptian breastplates. The High Priest's headdress added a blue cloth and a triple gold crown, like the Persian Magi's mitre, over the plain white cap, showing his rule over heaven, earth and hell and over the spiritual, intellectual and material worlds, also shown by the Tabernacle's three rooms. On top was a tiny gold flower-shaped cup, meaning his soul could receive the waters of life from above, like the Templar rose growing from a skull; since the spirit was thought to leave through the crown of the head, it also meant spiritual consciousness. The crown bore the words 'Holiness unto the Lord'. But finery meant nothing to Jehovah, so the High Priest entered the Holy of Holies unclothed, robed only in his virtues. Legend said anyone unclean there, even by one selfish thought, would be struck dead by fire from the Mercy Seat, so the priests tied a chain to his foot to drag his body out if needed.",
  "modern.hall.29.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Montfaucon's Antiquities, says this is how the Qabbalists usually picture Jehovah. It is meant to show the Demiurge of the Greeks and Gnostics, whom the Greeks called Zeus, the Immortal Mortal, and the Hebrews IHVH.",
  "modern.hall.29.fig2": "The caption, for a diagram from Calmet's Dictionary of the Holy Bible, says it gives Calmet's order of the breastplate's stones and the tribe each stood for. According to the Rosicrucians the gems stood for twelve qualities and virtues: Illumination, Love, Wisdom, Truth, Justice, Peace, Equilibrium, Humility, Faith, Strength, Joy and Victory.",
  "modern.hall.29.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Mosaize Historie der Hebreeuwse Kerke, says the High Priest's robes were called 'the Garments of Glory' because they stood for man's regenerated, spiritual nature, a garment everyone must weave from the threads of character and virtue before becoming a High Priest after the order of Melchizedek.",
  "modern.hall.29.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Mosaize Historie der Hebreeuwse Kerke, says the High Priest wore a blue overcloth and a gold band over the ordinary priests' plain white cap, with 'Holiness unto the Lord' in Hebrew on the front of the band. The picture shows the headdress with and without the gold crown.",
  "modern.hall.29.fig5": "The caption, for a picture from Calmet's Dictionary of the Holy Bible, says Josephus describes the Cherubim as flying creatures unlike anything on earth and so impossible to describe. Moses is supposed to have seen them kneeling at God's footstool when he was taken into Jehovah's presence. Hall thinks they probably looked roughly like Ezekiel's Cherubim.",
  "modern.hall.30.p0": "Hall opens with a string of questions. Who were the Rosicrucians: an organized body of thinkers rebelling against the religious and philosophical limits of their time, or scattered mystics who simply thought alike? Where was the 'House of the Holy Spirit' where their manifestos say they met yearly? Who was 'our illustrious Father and Brother C.R.C.', and did the letters mean Christian Rosencreutz, supposed author of the Chymical Wedding? How were the Rosicrucians related to medieval Freemasonry, and are they the link between Masonry and ancient mysticism, with their secrets carried on by modern Masons? Did the order die out in the late eighteenth century or does it still exist in secret? Was its real aim religious and philosophical, as it said, or was that a cover for a plan to control Europe politically? Hall says there are four distinct theories, each held by scholars who have spent their lives in Hermetic archives, and that their conclusions show how inadequate the records are on the order's origin and early history.",
  "modern.hall.30.s1.p1": "Hall gives the first theory: that the order really existed as its manifesto, the Fama Fraternitatis, describes. The Fama is thought to have been written about 1610 and printed in 1614, perhaps earlier. It begins by reminding the world of God's mercy and warning learned men that pride and greed lead them after false prophets, so a reformation is needed and God has raised up sages for it. To help, 'the Highly Illuminated Father C.R.C.', a poor German of noble family, founded the secret Society of the Rose Cross. Put in a cloister at five, he later left with a monk on pilgrimage to the Holy Land; the monk died on Cyprus, and C.R.C. went on alone to Damascus, where ill health stopped him reaching Jerusalem, so he studied with the philosophers there. Hearing of mystics and Qabbalists in the Arabian city of Damcar, he went there at sixteen, was welcomed as someone long expected, learned Arabic and the adepts' secrets, and translated the book M into Latin, bringing it back to Europe. After three years he went to Fez, where he learned to communicate with the elemental beings, probably the nature spirits, who told him more of Nature's secrets; the sages there were lesser, but his experience let him sort truth from error.",
  "modern.hall.30.s1.p2": "Hall continues the Fama's story. After two years in Fez, C.R.C. sailed to Spain with treasures including rare plants and animals, hoping Europe's scholars would welcome what he brought; instead they mocked him, afraid to admit their ignorance. Here the Fama adds that Paracelsus, though not a member, had read the book M and drew from it the knowledge that made him the leading physician of medieval Europe. Undiscouraged, C.R.C. returned to Germany, built a house for quiet study and made scientific instruments, preferring God's company to fame. After five years he called three brothers from his old cloister, bound them by oath to keep his secrets and write down what he dictated, and the four founded the Fraternity of the Rose Cross. They devised a secret cipher and, the Fama says, a great dictionary of all wisdom, and began copying the book M, but the crowds of sick people coming to be healed left them too little time. They built a larger house, the 'House of the Holy Spirit', and took in four more members, making eight, seven of them German and all unmarried, and together finished the order's documents and instructions.",
  "modern.hall.30.s1.p3": "Hall says the brothers then decided to scatter to other countries, to share their wisdom with the worthy and to correct their own system. They swore to six rules: claim no honour except to heal the sick free; wear no special dress but follow local custom; meet yearly on a set day at the House of the Holy Spirit or send a letter; each find a worthy successor; use 'R.C.' as their seal and mark; and keep the fraternity unknown for a hundred years. Five left at once and two a year later, leaving C.R.C. alone, and they met yearly with joy at their quiet success. When the first brother died, in England, they decided their burial places should be secret; C.R.C. is thought to have prepared his own symbolic tomb, and the Fama says no brother living when it was written knew when or where he died. His body was found by accident 120 years later, when a brother skilled in building was altering what Hall says is only suspected to be the House of the Holy Spirit. Moving a memorial tablet held by a large nail broke away some plaster and revealed a hidden door into a vault, inscribed POST CXX ANNOS PATEBO, which the brothers read as 'After 120 years I shall come forth.'",
  "modern.hall.30.s1.p4": "Hall says the next morning the brothers entered a seven-sided vault, each side five feet wide and eight high, lit by a mysterious light in the ceiling though no sun reached it. A round altar in the middle bore brass plates with strange characters, and doors in the seven walls hid boxes of books and the order's lost secret teachings. Under a brass cover beneath the altar lay a body, presumably C.R.C.'s, perfectly preserved after 120 years, dressed in the order's robes and holding a parchment valued next to the Bible. They put everything back, sealed the vault and left with their faith strengthened. The Fama ends by saying it is sent to the learned of Europe in five languages at C.R.C.'s wish, invites sincere seekers to contact the brothers and promises they will be heard, and warns that selfish seekers will find only misery. Literalists date the founding to about 1400. Against them, none of the Fama's key points has ever been confirmed: there is no record of C.R.C. in Spain, Damcar cannot be found, and no German place of mass healing is known. A picture of him in Waite's book proves nothing, since no outsider ever saw him, and Hall thinks the name Christian Rosencreutz most unlikely, as it was only attached to him by the Chymical Wedding.",
  "modern.hall.30.s2.p1": "Hall gives the second theory, held by Masonic investigators: the order really existed but its origin is disputed. Some think it grew out of medieval alchemy; Robert Macoy thinks its founder was the German theologian Johann Valentin Andreae, who may have reformed a society founded by Cornelius Agrippa. Others see it as the first arrival in Europe of Buddhist and Brahmin culture, or as founded in Egypt and carrying on the Mysteries of Persia and Chaldea. Godfrey Higgins writes that the German Rosicrucians do not know their origin but believe they descend from the Egyptians, Chaldeans, Magi and Gymnosophists, the naked sages Alexander's men found in India. These groups agree that C.R.C.'s story, like the Masonic legend of Hiram Abiff, is allegory, much as Bible students have mostly failed to prove the Scriptures as history. If the order was real and had political as well as philosophical aims, Hall finds it remarkable that a society spread across Europe kept its secret for centuries. Bacon, Goethe and many others were suspected of belonging but nothing has been proved; there were many false Rosicrucians, but the true 'Unknown Philosophers' remain unknown to this day.",
  "modern.hall.30.s2.p2": "Hall says many medieval tracts claiming to be Rosicrucian were fakes put out by people seeking power through the name, which has made research harder; one false group gave members a black cord as a sign and threatened to strangle them with it if they broke their vow. The real fraternity published only fragments of its teaching. He quotes Franz Hartmann's description of the order as men said to have more than human powers: to prophesy, to penetrate Nature's mysteries, to turn base metals into gold, to make an elixir of youth, to command the elemental spirits and to hold the Philosopher's Stone, which made its owner all-powerful, immortal and wise. Hartmann also says a true Rosicrucian is one who has awakened spiritually to the real meaning of the rose and the cross, and that, just as calling someone a Christian does not make him a Christ, no one can be made a Rosicrucian or Mason: he must grow into one from within, and forgetting this is why churches and societies fall short of their names. Hall says their charts deal with profound cosmic principles. The brothers seem to have been bound by shared aims rather than rules, worked quietly at ordinary trades, hid their membership even from their families, had no central meeting place after C.R.C.'s death, and kept their initiation, probably in chemical language, entirely secret.",
  "modern.hall.30.s2.p3": "Hall says no one could join by asking, for the Rosicrucians chose their own pupils. A chosen person might get a letter, unsigned or sealed with 'C.R.C.' or 'R.C.', telling him to go to a certain place at a certain time; he never told what happened there, though his later writings often showed a deeper understanding. Alchemists told of strangers who came to their laboratories, explained secret processes and vanished without trace, and others said the brothers taught them in dreams; either way they were sworn to secrecy about both the formulas and how they got them, and could only guess who their visitors were. Many think the Rosicrucian rose is a version of the Egyptian and Hindu lotus, with the same meaning. Dante's Divine Comedy shows he knew Rosicrucian ideas: Albert Pike says Dante's heaven is a set of Kabbalistic circles divided by a cross, with a rose blooming at its centre, the first public explanation of the Rose-Croix symbol. It has always been doubted whether the name comes from the rose and cross or whether that is a blind. Godfrey Higgins derives it from ros, dew; Hall adds that ras means wisdom and rus concealment, and thinks all these meanings play a part.",
  "modern.hall.30.s2.p4": "Hall says Waite agrees with Higgins that the name hides the secret of making the Philosopher's Stone with dew, and Hall suggests the dew may be a substance in the human brain resembling the alchemists' heavenly dew that redeemed the earth. The cross is the human body, and the rose on the cross is the soul crucified on the body, held by three nails. Hall thinks Rosicrucian symbolism probably carries on the secret teaching of the Egyptian Hermes, making the order the true link between modern Masonry and Egyptian Hermeticism, where its symbols came from. Waite says there are signs of a connection between Masonry and Rosicrucianism, but the evidence is inconclusive, and Masonry itself has never shown a mystical character or known where its symbols came from. Many early Freemasons were suspected Rosicrucians, and Robert Fludd even wrote in the order's defence. Frank C. Higgins says Masons honour the Rosicrucian Elias Ashmole as a founder of the first Grand Lodge in London, and the Britannica says Ashmole was initiated in 1646, the first gentleman to be 'accepted'. Papus says the Rosicrucians initiated Leibniz and founded Freemasonry through Ashmole. If Masonry's founders were taught Egypt's great secret, as its symbols suggest, Hall says they must have learned it from a society qualified to teach it.",
  "modern.hall.30.s2.p5": "Hall says one theory holds that Freemasonry grew out of Rosicrucianism, an outward body the Unknown Philosophers created for their work in the world. The story goes that the adepts grew dissatisfied with it and quietly withdrew, leaving their symbols and allegories behind but taking the keys to them. Some say Masonry has wholly absorbed the order; others equally learned say the Rosicrucians still exist apart. Hartmann gives a tradition that the order's headquarters is near Carlsbad, and another says a similar school, 'the Bohemian Brothers', survives in the Black Forest. What is certain, Hall says, is that the order in Europe practically vanished as Masonry rose, and that the eighteenth degree, the Rose-Croix, keeps many symbols of the Rosicrucian fire alchemists. An unpublished eighteenth-century manuscript says wise men founded schools of wisdom called Brothers of the Rosy Cross, that false schools corrupted them so the order no longer exists in the usual sense, and that in a coming age of the Holy Spirit they will call themselves Brothers of the Lily Cross and Knights of the White Lion, when the schools will flower again. Politically, Hall says, the Rosicrucians worked through Bacon, St.-Germain and Cagliostro, the last suspected of being a Templar agent, and are popularly thought to have helped start the French Revolution, as the introduction to Bulwer-Lytton's Zanoni suggests.",
  "modern.hall.30.s3": "Hall gives the third theory: the original order never existed at all and was pure invention. Its supporters ask whether the fraternity was a myth made up by a cynic to mock alchemy and Hermeticism, whether the House of the Holy Spirit existed only in a mystic's imagination, whether the whole story was a satire on Europe's credulous scholars, and whether C.R.C. was invented by Johann Valentin Andreae or someone like him who, meaning to ridicule Hermetic philosophy, accidentally spread it. Hall says there is little doubt that at least one early document was written by Andreae, but his purpose is unknown; he asks whether Andreae was acting on someone else's instructions, and whether, if he wrote the Chymical Wedding at fifteen, he was inspired by a higher power. No answers exist. Many took Andreae's 'magnificent imposture' as true, and many pseudo-societies sprang up claiming to be the order of the Fama and Confessio, though few existing today date back past the early nineteenth century. Philalethes, Maier, Heydon and Fludd defended the order's existence. Some think Bacon helped write the Fama and Confessio, whose style resembles his New Atlantis. The order inspired literature, above all Bulwer-Lytton's Zanoni. Hall concludes it is very probable that a secret society of illuminated scholars existed in Germany and then elsewhere in Europe, contributing greatly to knowledge while hiding their identities.",
  "modern.hall.30.s4.p1": "Hall gives the fourth theory, a purely spiritual one known to early writers but popularized by Theosophy. It says the Rosicrucians really had the supernatural powers credited to them and lived in two worlds: besides their physical bodies, their training let them act in an ethereal 'astral form' free of time and distance, and their temple lay in that invisible realm, out of reach of outsiders. On this view the true brotherhood was a small number of highly developed adepts, the higher degrees no longer subject to death, who took candidates only after long probation. They had the Philosopher's Stone and could make gold, but taught that these were allegories for human regeneration, turning the 'base elements' of the lower nature into the 'gold' of mental and spiritual realization; historians have failed because they looked at the subject only materially. These adepts were said to teach a person to leave the body at will by 'removing the rose from the cross'. The spirit is fastened to the body at points symbolized by the nails of the crucifixion, and through three alchemical initiations in the true Temple of the Rose Cross in the spiritual world the nails could be drawn and the divine nature come down from its cross. They hid these stages under three phrases: 'the casting of the molten sea', 'the making of the rose diamond' and 'the achieving of the Philosopher's Stone'.",
  "modern.hall.30.s4.p2": "Hall says that while intellectuals flounder among theories, the mystic believes the true fraternity is a school of supermen, like the Mahatmas of India, existing not in the visible world but on the 'inner planes of Nature', reachable only by those who can transcend matter. Such mystics quote the Confessio: the unworthy may clamour a thousand times, but God has closed the brothers' ears to them and hidden them in clouds, so that no human eyes see them unless they have borrowed strength from the eagle. The eagle is a symbol of initiation, the spinal spirit fire, which explains why the unregenerate world cannot understand the order. Holders of this view regard the Comte de St.-Germain as their highest adept and say he and Christian Rosencreutz were the same person. Their universal symbol is fire, the element that controls the metals; they claim descent from Tubal-cain and Hiram Abiff and say their purpose is to keep man's spiritual nature alive through materialistic ages. Papus names the Gnostics, Arabs, alchemists, Templars, Rosicrucians and Freemasons as the Western chain of occult tradition. Max Heindel described the Rosicrucian temple as an etheric building in and around a European country gentleman's home, which he believed would eventually move to America, and called its initiates so advanced that 'death had forgotten them'.",
  "modern.hall.30.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Geheime Figuren der Rosenkreuzer, says this cross is said to be made of spiritual gold and worn on the breast by every brother. It bears the alchemical signs of salt, sulphur and mercury, a star of the planets, and the words Faith, Hope, Love and Patience. The double-headed eagle or phoenix hints at the final androgynous state of humanity. Rosicrucian alchemy was not only about metals: a man's own body was the laboratory, and no one became an adept until he had turned the base metals of ignorance into the gold of wisdom.",
  "modern.hall.30.fig2": "The caption says the original Rosicrucian symbol was a rose crucified on a cross, often set on a three-stepped Calvary, and sometimes a cross rising from a rose. The Rosicrucian rose was painted on King Arthur's Round Table and forms the links of the chain from which the 'Great George' of the Order of the Garter hangs; Hargrave Jennings suspects the Garter had some link with the Rosicrucians.",
  "modern.hall.30.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Geheime Figuren der Rosenkreuzer, says the rose is a female symbol of generation, fertility and purity, and, because flowers open out, of spiritual unfolding. Its red is Christ's blood, its golden heart the spiritual gold hidden in human nature, and its ten petals the perfect Pythagorean number. It also stands for the heart, which Christians take as the emblem of love, compassion and Christ himself. The rose is an ancient religious symbol: the Greeks made it the sign of dawn, and in Apuleius's Golden Ass the hero, turned into a donkey for his folly, regains human shape by eating a sacred rose given him by Egyptian priests. The rose on Martin Luther's coat of arms has led to speculation about a link between the Reformation and the Rose Cross.",
  "modern.hall.30.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from the Chymische Hochzeit, says the mention of four red roses and a white cross in the Chymical Wedding of Christian Rosencreutz identified Johann Valentin Andreae as its author, since his family crest, shown here, was four red roses and a white cross.",
  "modern.hall.31.p0": "Hall says nothing reliable is known about the Rosicrucians' real beliefs, political aims or charitable work: their mysteries remain protected by their very nature, and every interpretation is speculation. The evidence suggests two bodies, an inner order that never revealed its members or teachings and an outer one run by it. Hall thinks the symbolic tomb of Christian Rosencreutz was really this outer body, whose spirit is in a higher sphere. For over a century after 1614 the outer body put out tracts and manifestos under its own name or members' names, apparently to confuse investigators and hide the order's real plans. When Rosicrucianism became the fashion of the seventeenth century, impostors also put out documents to cash in. The order's own deliberate tricks and the clumsy forgeries of charlatans made a double veil behind which the inner order worked quite differently from what was publicly stated; the brothers call these tolerated misunderstandings the 'clouds' they work behind. Something of the inner teaching can still be glimpsed in its shadow, the public writings, and Hall summarizes one of the most important, the Confessio Fraternitatis, in its fourteen chapters.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p1": "Hall summarizes the Confessio, chapters I to III. I: Do not misjudge what the earlier Fama said about the fraternity. God, seeing civilization decline, is revealing to the willing, and forcing on the unwilling, secrets once kept for his elect; the godly will be saved by this wisdom and the ungodly will suffer more. Misunderstandings of the Fama have led to false charges of heresy and treason, and this document hopes to clear them up so that Europe's learned will join in spreading divine knowledge. II: The philosophy of the age, though many call it sound, is false and will die of its own weakness, but as Nature provides a cure for each disease, the fraternity offers a cure for the world's philosophy. Its secret teaching sums up all sciences and arts; it draws much on theology and medicine and little on law, studies heaven and earth, but above all studies man, in whom the supreme secret lies. Those who join will be shown undreamed-of wonders of Nature. III: The fraternity does not hold these secrets lightly; it cannot describe them fully, lest the ignorant be overwhelmed and the vulgar mock them. Many, not understanding the wonders of this sixth age, are like blind men in a world full of light who know things only by touch; Hall explains that sight here means spiritual knowing and touch the bodily senses.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p2": "Hall summarizes chapters IV and V. IV: Through meditation, the help of angels and spirits, and long experience, Father C.R.C. was so filled with God's wisdom that if every book in the world were lost and science overturned, the fraternity could rebuild human thought on divine truth. Because its knowledge is so deep, seekers must grow into it step by step through grades up to the Great Arcanum, which is better than wandering in worldly ignorance now that God has lit his 'sixth candelabrum'. Those who receive this knowledge will master every art, nothing will be hidden from them, the world will become one book, and science and theology will be reconciled. God has decreed that the fraternity grow, and its doors are open, but only to those who have earned it: the brothers may not reveal their knowledge even to their own children, because the right to spiritual truth cannot be inherited but must grow in each soul. V: The brothers may seem rash to offer their treasure to all, but only the worthy will understand; the Fama was published in five languages so that the righteous of every nation, scholars or not, could hear of them. God has closed their ears to the clamouring unworthy and hidden them in his clouds, so that they cannot be seen unless by eyes strengthened by the eagle. They will reform Europe's governments on the model of the sages of Damcar, give everyone as much knowledge as they can grasp, and overthrow false theology.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p3": "Hall summarizes the next chapters, which the e-text numbers VII twice and which are presumably VI to VIII. First: C.R.C. was born in 1378 and died at 106, leaving the brothers to spread a philosophic religion worldwide. God protects the order, and its treasures are kept for 'the Lion' to use in founding his kingdom. Next: before the world ends God will send a great flood of spiritual light. The falsehood that has crept into the arts, sciences, religions and governments will be removed and one standard set up. People will credit the change to the progress of the age, not to the fraternity, which claims no glory, since many good men outside it will help. VIII: As signs of a coming great council of the elect, God has set new stars in Serpentarius and Cygnus, showing that visible nature holds signs of what is coming for the few who can read them. Man has two eyes, ears and nostrils to take wisdom in but only one tongue to give it out; seers have long perceived God's will, and soon they will speak. But first the world must sleep off the drunkenness of its poisoned cup, the false wine of theology, and open its heart to the rising sun of truth.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text heads both of the first two chapters here 'Chapter VII'; the first is presumably Chapter VI.\n- Before 'new stars' the e-text has a stray 'i', which may stand for 'two'.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p4": "Hall summarizes chapters IX to XI. IX: The brothers have a magic writing copied from the divine alphabet in which God writes his will on heaven and earth. With it they foresee the church's darkenings as astronomers foresee eclipses. Their language is like that of Adam and Enoch before the Fall; they can explain their mysteries in it but not in Latin, a language corrupted by the confusion of Babel. X: Because powerful enemies still force them to hide, they urge would-be members to study the Bible constantly, not by quoting it all the time but by seeking its true, eternal meaning, which theologians, scientists and mathematicians seldom find because sectarian opinion blinds them. No better book has ever been given to man: blessed is he who has it, more blessed who reads it, most blessed who understands it, and most godlike who obeys it. XI: What the Fama said about turning metals into gold and the universal medicine should be taken lightly. Both are possible, but great minds may be led astray by fixing on gold-making. A man given power to heal, to escape poverty and to win high rank faces many temptations, and without true understanding becomes a menace; an alchemist who can make gold can do great evil unless his understanding equals his wealth. So knowledge, virtue and understanding must come first. The Christian church has sinned by holding power and using it unwisely, and will fall under the weight of its own wrongs.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p5": "Hall summarizes chapters XII to XIV. XII: Throw away the worthless books of false alchemists and philosophers, who mock the Trinity and fool the credulous with empty riddles; one of the worst is a stage player clever enough to deceive. The Enemy mixes such men among those doing good so that truth is harder to find. Truth is simple and open, while falsehood is complex, hidden and proud, its worldly knowledge glittering like divine wisdom. The wise will turn to the brothers, who want not their money but to share their own greater treasure, who do not mock parables but explain them, and who invite seekers into their 'kingly houses and palaces' because God's Spirit, Father C.R.C.'s wish and the great need of the times require it. XIII: Having declared that they confess Christ, reject the Papacy, devote themselves to true philosophy and good living, and admit the worthy of every nation, they ask the reader to join them for self-perfection, the growth of the arts and the service of the world; then the treasures of the whole earth will be given and the darkness over human knowledge lifted. XIV: Those dazzled by gold, or who might be turned to idleness and display by wealth, should not disturb their silence. Though a medicine exists to cure all disease and give wisdom, God wills that understanding come only through virtue, work and integrity. The brothers can show themselves only by God's will, and those who seek them against it will lose their lives sooner than find happiness.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p6": "Hall says Johann Valentin Andreae is generally named as the Confessio's author, but it is much debated whether Francis Bacon used Andreae's name as a pseudonym. He points to two passages in the introduction to The Anatomy of Melancholy, published in 1621 under the name Democritus Junior, later identified as Robert Burton, a suspected intimate of Bacon. One slyly suggests that the founder of the fraternity was still alive in 1621, a point most students have missed; the other is a footnote reading only 'Job. Valent. Andreas, Lord Verulam', which Hall says ties Andreae to Bacon, Lord Verulam, and by its punctuation suggests they were the same person. He then turns to John Heydon, who called himself 'a Servant of God, and a Secretary of Nature'. In The Rosie Cross Uncovered Heydon describes the Rosicrucians as a divine fraternity living in 'the suburbs of heaven', the eyes and ears of the great King, illuminated like Moses, able to take any form at will. He says Moses was their father, that some call them of the order of Elijah or disciples of Ezekiel, and that like the prophets they work miracles and are carried wherever they wish, as Habakkuk was to Babylon. One of them, he says, carried a message to a friend in Devonshire and brought the answer back to London the same day, a four days' journey. They taught him excellent predictions, he says; they check plague, calm winds, storms, seas and rivers, walk in the air, thwart witches and cure all diseases.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p7": "Hall says John Heydon's writings matter greatly. He was probably related to Sir Christopher Heydon, called a 'seraphically illuminated Rosie Crucian', whom F. Leigh Gardner of the English Rosicrucian society thought was his source; Gardner judged from his writings that Heydon had passed through the order's lower grade and published much of it. Heydon, a great traveller, claimed that his book The Wise-Mans Crown, or The Glory of the Rosie Cross, was an English translation of the book M that Christian Rosencreutz brought from Arabia. Thomas Vaughan (Eugenius Philalethes) confirms that the brothers could become invisible at will, moving in a 'white mist' that only those who can see in that light can see through. Hall calls the fraternity a sovereign body that uses the symbols of alchemy, Qabbalah, astrology and magic for its own ends while being independent of all those cults. Its first of three main aims was to abolish monarchy and put the philosophic elect in charge. He claims modern democracies grew from Rosicrucian efforts to free the masses from despotism; in the early eighteenth century they turned to the American colonies, the War of Independence was their first great political experiment, founding a government on divine and natural law, and they left the Great Seal of the United States as a reminder. He also says they started the French Revolution, which failed partly because the revolutionaries' fanaticism could not be controlled and the Terror followed.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p8": "Hall gives the fraternity's second aim, the reform of science, philosophy and ethics. The Rosicrucians held that the material arts and sciences are shadows of divine wisdom, reached only by going to the depths of Nature. Though they called themselves Christians, Hall says they were plainly Platonists and deeply versed in early Hebrew and Hindu theology, and wanted to restore the ancient Mysteries as the main way of teaching the eternal doctrine. Probably the heirs of those Mysteries, they survived dogmatic Christianity only by total secrecy and clever disguises, guarding the Supreme Mystery, the identity and relation of the Three Selves. Hall says the outer order is slowly building a body or setting in which Brother C.R.C. may finally be reborn and complete the fraternity's work. The third aim was the universal medicine, and Hall says there is ample evidence they found the elixir of life. Ashmole wrote that the Rosicrucians were unappreciated in England but welcome on the Continent, that they twice cured Queen Elizabeth of smallpox, and that a Rosicrucian physician cured the Earl of Norfolk of leprosy. Heydon hints that the brothers could prolong life indefinitely, though not past God's appointed time: four founders died and rose again until Christ, brought him gifts at Bethlehem, grew young again for centuries, and decided to take in more members while Brother C.R. was 'in a proper womb quickening'.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p9": "Hall says the 'womb' was apparently the glass coffin in which the brothers were buried, also called the philosophical egg: the philosopher broke out of it, lived for a set time, then went back into his glass shell. The Rosicrucian medicine can be read as a real chemical substance or as spiritual understanding, which heals by showing a man the truth; ignorance is the worst disease, so whatever cures it is the strongest medicine, and the perfect medicine was meant to heal nations and races as well as individuals. An unknown philosopher's manuscript says alchemy, Qabbalah, astrology and magic were once divine sciences but have been perverted into false teachings that lead seekers astray. He divides the path into three schools, or mountains, climbed in order: Mount Sophia, philosophy, the knowledge of Nature's workings that lifts man above the senses; Mount Qabbalah, the language of the angels, which lets man speak with God's messengers; and Mount Magia, divine magic, the language of God, where God himself teaches the true nature of things. Hall says the Rosicrucian meaning of their symbols is still unrevealed, hidden in clouds. Michael Maier says what the Fama and Confessio say is true, that it is childish to complain the brothers promised much and did little, since many are called but few chosen, and that the masters hold out the rose as the distant prize but lay the cross on beginners.",
  "modern.hall.31.s1.p10": "Hall says the rose and cross appear in the stained glass of the Lichfield Chapter House, where Walter Conrad Arensberg believes Bacon and his mother are buried, and a crucified rose inside a heart is watermarked on the dedication page of the 1628 Anatomy of Melancholy. The rose, female, and the cross, male, are universal sexual emblems. Inman, Jennings and Payne Knight rightly saw that they stand for generation, but did not see that physical creation is only a shadow of divine creation in the spiritual world. Because of this sexual symbolism the Rosicrucians and Templars were falsely accused of obscene rites. The alchemical retort does stand for the womb, but also, more deeply, for the second birth. Since generation is the key to physical existence, the fraternity naturally took reproductive symbols; since regeneration is the key to spiritual existence, they chose the rose and cross, which stand for man's redemption through uniting his lower, temporary nature with his higher, eternal one. The rosy cross is also a hieroglyph for the formula of the universal medicine.",
  "modern.hall.31.fig1": "The caption, for a portrait from a rare print, says some esoteric circles hint that the modest Johann Valentin Andreae masked a high emissary of the Rose Cross. Andreae certainly existed, but his biography has unresolved gaps. Hall says this face looks strikingly like Francis Bacon's, allowing for age, and suggests that if Bacon borrowed Shakespeare's identity he could also, after a mock funeral in England, have taken on Andreae's. The crescent below the bust also appears in Bacon's crest, marking him as Sir Nicholas Bacon's second son, and the letters O MDC in the lower right corner can be turned by a simple Baconian cipher into numbers totalling 33, the number of the name Bacon. Hall says these points together go far to explain who wrote the first Rosicrucian manifestos.",
  "modern.hall.31.fig2": "The caption, for de Bry's engraving in Fludd's Collected Works, calls it the most famous diagram of Robert Fludd's philosophy. Hall names Michael Maier, Elias Ashmole and Fludd as the outstanding links between Rosicrucianism and Freemasonry; De Quincey calls Fludd the immediate father of Masonry, and Waite ranks him above all of Paracelsus's followers, even Paracelsus himself, and calls him the central figure of Rosicrucian literature, a man of immense learning and great personal holiness. Fludd lived from 1574 to 1637. In the diagram, beyond the starry heavens lie three fiery rings, the triple fire of the Creator, home of the celestial beings. Inside the stars come the planets and elements, then the earth, animals, plants, minerals and human crafts, and at the centre an ape sitting on the globe measuring a sphere with compasses, standing for the animal creation. At the top, God's name in clouds sends down a hand holding a chain to a woman between God and the ape. The chain to the world below is merely held in her hand, but the chain from above ends in a shackle on her wrist. She may stand for humanity hung between God and beast, for Nature linking God to the lower world, or for the human soul, the common ground between higher and lower.",
  "modern.hall.31.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from the Turba Philosophorum, says this is one of the earliest Latin alchemical texts, of unknown origin, sometimes called the Third Pythagorean Synod; as its name says, it is an assembly of sages setting out the alchemical views of early Greek philosophers. The symbol comes from a rare German edition of 1750 and shows the completion of the Great Work as a hermaphrodite. Alchemists often drew Nature's active and passive principles as a man and a woman, and when the two were perfectly balanced in one body they showed it with a combined figure like this.",
  "modern.hall.31.fig4": "The caption, for the title page of Maier's Viatorium, says Count Michael Maier, physician to the Emperor Rudolf II, was a leading figure in the Rosicrucian debate and almost certainly an initiate authorized to pass on its secrets to Europe's philosophic elite. The page shows the seven planets as figures, each with a smaller emblem behind it for the zodiac sign where that planet is enthroned, and a portrait of Maier in the arch above the title. The book analyses the nature and effects of the seven planets in alchemical language throughout. Maier hid his knowledge so cleverly that it is very hard to extract, and much of it lies in the engravings in his books.",
  "modern.hall.31.fig5": "The caption, for a diagram from the Museum Hermeticum, describes its rings from the outside in. The first holds the zodiac figures; the second their signs and the parts of the body they rule; the third the months, with notes on temperaments; the fourth the elements and their symbols. The next seven are the planetary orbits, with the planetary angels, the seven chief members of the Universal Man and the seven metals, each placed under its element. The twelfth reads 'There are Three Principles, Three Worlds, Three Ages, and Three Kingdoms'; the thirteenth names the twelve arts and sciences needed for spiritual growth; the fourteenth says 'Nature'; and the fifteenth says it is the great honour of faithful souls that an angel is appointed from birth to guard each one.",
  "modern.hall.32.p1": "Hall says Hargrave Jennings reproduced five Qabbalistic charts as genuine Rosicrucian drawings without saying where they came from or explaining them, and a later writer linked one to the symbolic tomb of C.R.C., exposing what Christian Rosencreutz really was. The five are part of a series of fifteen in Georg von Welling's Magical, Qabbalistical and Theosophical Writings on Salt, Sulphur and Mercury, a very rare book published at Frankfurt and Leipzig in 1735 and 1760, whose chapter and section numbers the figures refer to. Hall calls them a valuable addition to the few genuine Qabbalistic and Rosicrucian diagrams. Lucifer, he says, is the greatest mystery of symbolism, and these plates almost openly reveal the Rosicrucians' closely guarded knowledge of his identity; Lucifer is given the number 741. Von Welling did not fully explain the charts, which would have been against Qabbalistic principle; their deeper meaning comes only from long study. Hall begins with Table I, Figure 1, a Ptolemaic chart of the primordial elements. Its outer ring, between lines A and B, is Schamayim, a Qabbalistic name for God meaning the expanse of heaven or a spiritual fiery water: the 'Ocean of Spirit' in which everything created and uncreated exists and lives, which in the lower worlds becomes the astral light.",
  "modern.hall.32.p2": "Hall continues Figure 1. Between B and C lie the orbits of seven spiritual intelligences called the Divine Planets, not the visible ones; human souls enter the lower worlds through ring B, the fixed stars, and all creation reflects Schamayim's energy, which filters into the elements through the stars and planets. C to D is the subtle spiritual air; D to E the surface of earth and sea, also grades of ether; E to F the region called the gathering of the waters and the virgin earth, or 'Ares', the alchemists' 'quicksand', the real mystical foundation of the earth. F to G is the denser underground air, where starry influences harden into bodily forms. G is the central fire of the earth, a coarse fire opposed to divine Schamayim. Each upper sphere has a lower counterpart, and the middle zone, D to E, 'the Reservoir', takes impressions from both. In the other figures, 2, 3, 4 and 5 are the signs of elemental water, air, earth and fire, and 9, 7, 8 and 6 the spiritual, invisible water, air, earth and fire. Figures 6 to 9 show the elements before Lucifer's fall, the four rivers of Genesis flowing from one river, with Schamayim as a golden ball at the centre. Figure 11 is the sign of the beginning and end of all creatures, from which all come and to which all return to become one with the fiery water of divine understanding.",
  "modern.hall.32.p3": "Hall turns to Table II, Figures 12 to 51. Figures 12 to 14 show the sphere, a symbol of motion, standing for fire, water and air, and the cube, a symbol of weight, for earth; since the sphere rests on a point and the cube on a face, the sphere means spirit and the cube matter. Figure 15 is the sign of salt. Von Welling says the cube's six faces are the six days of creation, with the seventh day of rest as the point at its centre, and each face bears the element signs. Alchemists called salt the first substance made by the fire of Schamayim flowing from God, holding the beginning and end of all things. The cube is made of twelve six-sided bodies, the twelve pillars of the true invisible church, and twelve times six gives the magic 72. It also divides into six pyramids, each of four triangles, giving 24, the Elders before the throne; six faces and the centre make 7. Multiplying 7 by itself seven times gives 5,764,801, the ancients' measure of the periods of eternity, not earth years. The 5,000,000 is the great 'hall' year; the 700,000 the great Sabbath year, in which all people regain their lost inheritance; the 64,800 the number of fallen angels; and the final one the year of Lucifer's release and return to his first state.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'Great hall year' is the e-text's wording; it may be a misprint, perhaps for 'great half year'.",
  "modern.hall.32.p4": "Hall explains more of Table II. Figure 16 is another sign of salt, and Figure 17, a dot, is the sign of spirit, gold, the sun or the seed of life. A dot moving forward makes a line, Figure 18, the first motion, and every line begins and ends in a dot. The circle, Figure 19, is the second motion and the most perfect line. Figure 20 is the outpouring of spiritual life into manifestation, and day; Figure 21 the release of the destructive underground principle, darkness and night. Figure 22 is water, and Figure 23 the full sign of light and darkness: the upright triangle Schamayim, the inverted one the dark earth holding the underground fire, the first day of creation. Figure 24 shows the six days and that the elements flow out of the divine fire, which breaks up into the substances of the world. Figure 26 is air, born of the eternal light and the ethereal water, and Figure 27, its inversion, is water, which comes from the lower fire but still mirrors heaven. Other figures are salt, fire and water in one. Figure 33 is gold and the sun, identical in nature, both made from the first fire of Schamayim and perfect, since nothing is more perfect than a circle round a dot. Figure 34 is the great and little worlds: as the dot is ringed by its circle, the world is ringed by Schamayim, and man's inner nature is potential gold, his eternal spiritual body; gold is the male principle. Figure 35 is silver and the moon, a perfect metal with its red nature turned inward, the female principle.",
  "modern.hall.32.p5": "Hall lists the rest of Table II: Figures 36 to 40 are the signs of copper and Venus, iron and Mars, tin and Jupiter, lead and Saturn, and Mercury as planet and element; then come antimony, the key metal of the earth, arsenic, sulphur, cinnabar, quicklime, nitre and vitriol. Figure 48 is sal ammoniac, named from the temple of Jupiter Ammon in the Egyptian desert where it was found, and Figures 49 to 51 are alum, alkali (an Arabic word) and salt of tartar, which has great hidden virtue. Table III, Figure 52, shows the seven days of creation as eight globes and a central square inside three rings for the three worlds where creation happens, with German words from Genesis 1 in the outer ring and 'The First Day' around it. Four small globes inside the outer ring show the abstract stages of creation: 'Heaven and Earth' in the top one with a triangle, 'Light' on the right, 'Jehovah Elohim' over 'Darkness' on the left, and 'Day' over 'Night' at the bottom. Four globes in the second ring are the second to fifth days: a white globe split by a dotted line, a globe with mountains, one with planetary rings, and one split by a dotted line. The central square with a human figure is the sixth day. The chart shows the three layers of the 'auric egg' of the universe and of man and the forces at work in them.",
  "modern.hall.32.p6": "Hall says Table IV, Figure 53, has been called the symbolic tomb of Christian Rosencreutz. The upper circle is the first world, God's divine sphere, with God's throne as the central triangle; small circles at the star's points are the seven Spirits before the throne in Revelation, among whom walks the Alpha and Omega, the Son of God. The triangle holds three flames, the Trinity. From the lowest flame the first divine outflow descends as two parallel lines through the throne of Saturn, the spirit Orifelis through whom God showed himself, past the edge of the heavens and the 22 spheres below, to point B, the throne of Lucifer, where the outpouring is gathered and reflected. From Lucifer the light shines in turn through the twelve signs, from Capricorn round to Sagittarius and back; the zodiac circles are twelve orders of great and good spirits, and the small circles inside the fixed stars mark the orbits of the sacred planets. Table V, Figure 54, is similar but shows the universe when God showed himself through Jupiter, the spirit Sachasiel. It shows the universe after Lucifer's fall: when Lucifer sought to control the power, the divine light stopped at once, his world, later the solar system, with its spirits reflecting his ideas and inverting the light, went dark, his Schamayim shrank into a solid disc, and Chaos began.",
  "modern.hall.32.p7": "Hall describes Table VI, Figures 55 to 59. Figure 55 is Lucifer's Chaos; 56 the separation of light from darkness; 57 light within darkness; and 58 the regions of the elements and their inhabitants. The four A's are the Abyss around everything, and A B is Lucifer's fiery throne. Moving outward come the underground air (g), underground water (f), the earth (c), the outer water (d), the outer air (e), and Schamayim (W and X). The elemental beings of each layer are better the farther they are from the centre of evil, A B: above the earth's surface they rise in virtue, and below it they sink in depravity. Air is a partial exception: near the light and full of beautiful spirits, it is also home to Beelzebub, the evil spirit of the air, and his demons. The air takes the imprint of the stars, of human thoughts, words and deeds, and of many unseen influences, which people breathe in with varied effects on their minds; it also carries the seeds that make water fertile. The air spirits are great and wise but treacherous and confused, open to both good and evil. The mighty beings of the fiery region X cannot be fooled by the dark spirits and love the creatures of the waters, since water came from their fiery water. Figure 59 is the solar system, with W and X as the site of Eden.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text lists 'd, c, and X' for the outer water, air and fire; from the key above, 'c' is probably 'e', the outer air.",
  "modern.hall.32.p8": "Hall describes Table VII (and Figure 6 from Table VIII). Figure 1 is threefold divine sulphur, the all-perfect soul of creatures, shown as three interlocking circles labelled salt, sulphur and mercury, with the divine name Ehieh in the central triangle; Geist means spirit. Figure 2 is common destructive sulphur, which becomes the sign of earth with a bar in its triangle. Figure 3 is true oil of vitriol, a circle with two diameters and two half-circles hanging below, which hides the signs of all the metals. Figure 4 is tin and Figure 5 iron. Figure 7 is the Copernican solar system and Figure 8 the Last Judgment, in which the earth replaces the sun at the centre, shifting every planet except Mars, Jupiter and Saturn; inside sphere h are the great circles of damnation. In Figure 6, a is the centre of eternity, and the rays moving out to b, d and c, the first divine act, form an equilateral triangle. The eternal world became manifest in the water (salt), light (mercury) and fire (sulphur) of the archetypal world, three circles named understanding, wisdom and reason inside a triangle of circles named Father, Son and Spirit, surrounded by the circle of the high throne. Seven outer circles are the spirits before the throne. Below, as in Figures 53 and 54, the angelic world ends in the knowable world of the Sons of God, then come the fixed stars and the sun-centred solar system; 'Ungrund' means the Abyss.",
  "modern.hall.32.p9": "Hall describes Tables IX to XI. Table IX, Figure 9, combines the Old and New Testaments to show the interwoven planes of being. Seven outer circles on the right name the planetary angels, and graded circles from the top read: Abyss of Compassion, Zion, the New Heaven and Earth, the New Jerusalem, Paradise, the Bosom of Abraham, and the Outer Courts of the Lord. Circles of darkness reach up from below, each divine principle with its infernal opposite; small circles on the left and right are the Tree of Life and the Tree of Knowledge, and the Trinity sits in the centre. Table X, Figure 10, shows the New Jerusalem as a cube with the twelve tribes on its twelve edges and God's eye at the centre, ringed by words from Revelation; Figures 11 to 15 may be ciphers for names in Revelation. In Table XI, Figure 1 is the solar system according to Genesis: the dot at the top is eternity, the beginning of beginnings, and the diameter is God's outflow, first into Schamayim, beyond human understanding. Figure 2 is the earth with the houses and signs of the zodiac; 3 the Universal Mercury, divine life, as mercury, sulphur and salt; 5 the positions of the planets and stars; and 6 the Microcosm, with planets and signs matched to the body, inscribed 'Know thyself. In words, herbs, and stones lies a great power.' Figure 7 is the universal sign from which all signs come; 8 to 10 are left for the reader to solve; 11 is the radiating Universal Mercury.",
  "modern.hall.32.p10": "Hall describes the last tables. In Table XII, Figures 12 and 13 are 'mirrors' of astrological aspects with an astrologer's wheel; 14 is a secret alchemical formula inscribed 'Out of one in all is all'; and 16 the solar system with its inner spiritual heavens up to the throne of God and the immeasurable beyond. Figure 17 shows the solar system created out of the ring of divine eternity. Figure 18 shows Genesis's division of the waters above the heavens from those below, and Figure 19 the philosophers' mercury, needed for material existence. In Table XIII, Figure 1 is Ain Soph, the incomprehensible abyss of divine majesty welling up without limit; Figure 2 the Father, Son and Holy Ghost as a triangle inscribed 'I shall be that I shall be', with Crown, Wisdom and Understanding at its points; Figure 3 the Trinity's outflow with the words 'Lucifer the Son of the Aurora of the morning'; and Figure 4 the home of Lucifer and his angels, the Chaos of Genesis. Table XIV shows the triune God in a cross between 'the Secrets of Elohim' and 'the Secrets of Nature', the two Trees on its arms; 'the Road to Paradise', probably the planets at their birth; and the earth before the Flood. Table XV ends with a dot in a triangle enclosing the twelve tribes, the completion of regeneration and of the Great Work.",
  "modern.hall.33.p1": "Hall asks whether turning base metals into gold is possible, and whether modern scholars can afford to laugh at the idea. Alchemy, he says, was a practical art as well as a speculative one, and since Hermes alchemists have claimed, with some supporting evidence, to make gold from tin, silver, lead and mercury. He finds it untenable that the brilliant minds who affirmed transmutation for two thousand years were sane on everything else but wrong on this, or that the hundreds who said they saw or did it were all dupes, fools or liars. To call all alchemists unsound would condemn nearly every ancient and medieval philosopher and scientist; emperors, princes, priests and ordinary townspeople saw what seemed to be the change of metals. Anyone may remain unconvinced, but scoffers ignore evidence that deserves respect, and many alchemists are honoured while their critics are forgotten. He lists some: Thomas Norton, Isaac of Holland, Basil Valentine, supposed discoverer of antimony, Jean de Meung, Roger Bacon, Albertus Magnus, Quercetanus, Geber, the Arab whose writings brought alchemy to Europe, Paracelsus, Nicolas Flamel, Helvetius, Raymond Lully, Alexander Seton, Sendivogius, Bernard of Treviso, George Ripley, Pico della Mirandola, John Dee, Khunrath, Maier, Thomas Vaughan, van Helmont, John Heydon, Lascaris, Thomas Charnock, Synesius, Morienus, Cagliostro and St.-Germain. Legend makes Solomon and Pythagoras alchemists too, Solomon making his temple's gold by alchemy.",
  "modern.hall.33.p2": "Hall says Albert Pike sided with the alchemists, writing that Hermetic science is mathematically provable and that Hermetic gold is both a true doctrine and real material gold, the purest in the earth; so much, Hall says, for the Masonic view. When William and Mary came to the English throne in 1689, alchemists must have been plentiful, because in their first year they repealed Henry IV's law making the multiplying of metals a felony. A handwritten copy of the repeal is in Dr. Sigismund Bacstrom's collection of alchemical manuscripts, and Hall quotes it in full. It recites Henry IV's ban on multiplying gold and silver, and says that since then many people have become skilled at melting, refining, improving and multiplying metals and their ores, which are plentiful in the kingdom, and at extracting gold and silver from them, but practise abroad for fear of the penalty, to England's loss. It therefore repeals that part of the old act entirely, on condition that all gold and silver produced this way be used only for coinage and taken to the royal mint in the Tower of London, where the makers will be paid its full assayed value, and that none of it be used or sold anywhere else in the realm. Hall adds that after the repeal William and Mary encouraged the further study of alchemy.",
  "modern.hall.33.p3": "Hall says Franz Hartmann gathered reliable evidence about four alchemists who turned base metals into gold many times. One was Wenzel Seiler, an Augustinian monk who found a little mysterious red powder in his convent and, before the Emperor Leopold I, turned tin into gold. He also dipped a large silver medal in it, and the part that touched the powder became pure gold while the rest stayed silver. Hartmann calls this medal, kept in the imperial treasury in Vienna, the most indisputable proof of transmutation, 'if appearances can prove anything', and adds that Seiler was knighted by the Emperor as Wenzeslaus Ritter von Reinburg. Hall says there is no room for a long account of the alchemists, but brief lives of four great masters will show how they worked, how they gained their knowledge and how they used it, and their stories, told by themselves and their pupils, are as gripping as any novel.",
  "modern.hall.33.s1.p1": "Hall says the most famous alchemist was Philippus Aureolus Theophrastus Bombastus von Hohenheim, who called himself Paracelsus and predicted that all Europe's doctors would one day follow him. He was born, by the accepted date, on 17 December 1493, an only child; his father was a physician and his mother ran a hospital. As a youth he was inspired by Isaac of Holland and resolved to reform medicine. From twenty he travelled for about twelve years through Europe, including Russia and possibly Asia. Hall says Arab adepts in Constantinople gave him the great Hermetic secret, and he probably learned about nature spirits and invisible beings from Indian Brahmins or their pupils. He was a successful army surgeon. Back in Germany he began his reform of medicine and was attacked on every side, his violent temper and strong personality bringing on many avoidable storms. He accused apothecaries of using the wrong ingredients and caring only for high fees. His remarkable cures, said to include leprosy, cholera and cancer, only made rivals who could not match them hate him more, and they drove him again and again to move on. He was certainly irascible, with a near-mania of hatred for physicians and women, apparently never had a love affair, and his odd appearance and excessive way of life were always held against him; his physical abnormalities may explain much of his bitterness.",
  "modern.hall.33.s1.p2": "Hall says Paracelsus was accused of being seldom sober even as a professor at Basel, but his constant mental clarity and his huge output, three large volumes in the Strasbourg edition, contradict the stories. Many charges were probably invented by enemies who, not content with hiring assassins to kill him, also blackened his memory. How he died is uncertain, but the likeliest account is that he died from the effects of a fight with assassins hired by rivals whose trickery he had exposed. Few manuscripts in his hand survive, because he dictated most of his works. Hall quotes Professor John Maxson Stillman of Stanford: whatever his final place in medical history, Paracelsus came to Basel sure he was inspired with a great truth. He was a keen, open-minded observer, though not a very critical one, and a self-reliant thinker of debatable originality. Having rejected the authority of Aristotle, Galen and Avicenna for his own version of Neoplatonism, he burned his boats. He taught that future medicine must rest on studying nature, observing the patient, experiment and experience, not the dogmas of dead authors. He underestimated the force of conservatism, and after Basel he wandered again, sometimes poor, sometimes comfortable, disillusioned about quick success but never doubting final victory, since he believed his medicine agreed with nature, the expression of God's will.",
  "modern.hall.33.s1.p3": "Hall calls Paracelsus a mass of contradictions whose genius shone like a star in medieval darkness, fighting both jealous colleagues and his own temper for the good of the many against the few; he was the first to write scientific books in the common language so everyone could read them. Even dead he found no rest, his bones being dug up and reburied again and again. His gravestone says he cured wounds, leprosy, gout, dropsy and other incurable diseases, gave his goods to the poor, and died on 24 September 1541. A. M. Stoddart writes that the poor still pray at his tomb and have made him a saint: when cholera threatened Salzburg in 1830 they made a pilgrimage there, and it passed them by while raging elsewhere in Germany and Austria. One of his early teachers is supposed to have been a mysterious alchemist called Solomon Trismosin, of whom nothing is known except that he found the secret of transmutation after years of wandering, claimed to have made vast amounts of gold and to have lived to 150, and wrote Splendor Solis, a beautifully illuminated manuscript of 1582 now in the British Museum. In his Alchemical Wanderings he wrote: 'Study what thou art, whereof thou art a part ... All that is without thee also is within.'",
  "modern.hall.33.s2.p1": "Hall says Raymond Lully, the most famous Spanish alchemist, was born about 1235, son of the seneschal of James I of Aragon. He grew up amid the temptations of court, later held his father's post, married money and lived like a grandee. He became infatuated with Donna Ambrosia Eleanora di Castello, a married lady famous for her beauty and virtue, followed her everywhere and finally sent her passionate verses. She invited him to visit, said it was only fair he should see more of the beauty he praised, and uncovered one side of her body, almost eaten away by cancer. The shock changed his life: he gave up the court and became a recluse. Later, doing penance, he twice saw Christ in a vision telling him to follow. He divided his property among his family, lived in a hut on a hillside and learned Arabic to convert the Muslims. After six years he set out with a Muslim servant who, learning that Raymond meant to attack his people's faith, stabbed him in the back. Raymond would not let him be executed, but the man later hanged himself in prison.",
  "modern.hall.33.s2.p2": "Hall says that after recovering Lully taught Arabic to people going to the Holy Land, and met Arnold of Villanova, who taught him alchemy, so that he learned to transmute and multiply metals. On his travels he reached Tunis, debated with Muslim teachers, nearly died for his fierce attacks on their faith, and was banished on pain of death; he went back anyway, and was merely deported to Italy. Hall then quotes an unsigned article in Dickens's magazine Household Words. In Vienna, it says, Lully received letters from Edward II of England and Robert Bruce of Scotland inviting him to visit. He had become close friends with John Cremer, Abbot of Westminster, and went to England more to please Cremer than the king; Hall notes that a tract by Cremer exists but Westminster has no record of such an abbot. Cremer longed to learn how to make the powder of transmutation, which Lully had never told him. Knowing that Lully's dearest wish was to convert the Muslims, Cremer told the king tales of Lully's gold and persuaded Lully that Edward would readily launch a crusade if he had the money.",
  "modern.hall.33.s2.p3": "Hall continues the Household Words account. Though Lully had lost faith in popes and kings, he went with Cremer to England as a last hope, stayed at his abbey and at last taught him the powder. Presented to the king, he made one condition: the gold must not be spent on court luxury or on war with Christians, and Edward must lead an army against the Muslims in person; Edward promised anything. Lodged in the Tower, Lully says he turned fifty thousand pounds' weight of quicksilver, lead and tin into gold, coined into six million nobles, and some coins said to be from this gold survive in collections; Hall notes that the evidence for and against is still about evenly balanced. Lully sent Robert Bruce a short work on transmuting metals, and workmen later found some of the powder in his old cloister at Westminster and grew rich from it. In England he befriended Roger Bacon. Edward had no intention of a crusade, and Lully realized his rooms in the Tower were an honourable prison; he predicted misfortune for the king, escaped in 1315, and went to Egypt, Jerusalem and Tunis a third time. There he was stoned. Genoese merchants carried him, barely alive, to their ship, and he died in sight of Majorca on 28 June 1315, aged eighty-one, and was buried with great honour in his family chapel.",
  "modern.hall.33.s3.p1": "Hall says Nicolas Flamel was a Paris scrivener and illuminator of manuscripts in the late fourteenth century, to whom the world owes its knowledge of the Book of Abraham the Jew, and he quotes Flamel's own account from the Hieroglyphical Figures. Flamel, earning his living by drawing up inventories and accounts, bought for two florins a large, very old gilded book made not of paper or parchment but of thin bark of young trees, with a brass cover engraved with strange letters he could not read, perhaps Greek. Inside, the bark leaves were written with an iron point in neat coloured Latin letters. There were three sets of seven leaves: every seventh leaf of the first showed a virgin being swallowed by a serpent, the second a serpent crucified, and the last a desert with fountains from which serpents ran about. The first leaf said in gold capitals that Abraham the Jew, prince, priest, Levite, astrologer and philosopher, sent greeting to the Jewish nation scattered by God's wrath among the Gauls, and it was full of curses, with the word Maranatha, on anyone not a priest or scribe who read it. The seller did not know its value; Flamel thought it had been stolen from the persecuted Jews. On the second leaf the author comforted his people, urging them to avoid vice and idolatry and wait patiently for the Messiah.",
  "modern.hall.33.s3.p2": "Hall continues Flamel's account. From the third leaf on, to help his captive nation pay tribute to the Romans, Abraham taught transmutation in plain words, drawing the vessels and describing the colours, except for the 'first agent', which he only painted, with great skill, on the fourth and fifth leaves, so that no one could understand it without knowing the Kabbalah. On the fourth leaf was a young man with winged ankles and a caduceus twined with two serpents, striking his helmet, whom Flamel took for Mercury; against him rushed an old winged man with an hourglass on his head and a scythe-like blade, trying to cut off Mercury's feet. On the other side was a flower on a high mountain shaken by the north wind, with dragons and griffins nesting round it. On the fifth leaf a rose tree flowered in a garden, climbing a hollow oak, at whose foot a spring of very white water ran down into the depths, sought by countless blind diggers. On the last side a king with a great sword had soldiers kill many small children while their mothers wept, and other soldiers gathered the blood into a great vessel where the sun and moon came to bathe. Because this resembled Herod's massacre of the Innocents, Flamel later put these symbols in the churchyard of the Innocents.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The weapon is given in the e-text as 'a book (or syrhe)'; 'syrhe' is probably a misprint, perhaps for an old spelling of 'scythe'.",
  "modern.hall.33.s3.p3": "Hall finishes Flamel's account: he will not reveal what the rest of the book said in plain Latin, for God would punish him worse than the man who wished all mankind had one head to cut off at a blow. He studied it day and night and understood the operations but not what material to start with, which made him sad and full of sighs. His new wife Perrenelle, whom he loved as himself, asked what troubled him; he showed her the book, and she loved it as much as he did, though she understood it no better, and it comforted him to discuss it with her. Hall says Flamel studied the book for years, painted its pictures on the walls of his house and showed copies to scholars, but no one could explain them. At last he set out to find an adept, and met a physician, Master Canches, who was eager to see the original. On the way back to Paris Canches explained many of the hieroglyphs, but fell ill and died, and Flamel buried him at Orleans. By reflecting on what he had learned, and with his wife's help, Flamel worked out the formula, transmuted metals successfully several times, and before his death had hieroglyphic figures painted on an arch of the churchyard of the Holy Innocents in Paris, hiding the whole formula from the book in them.",
  "modern.hall.33.s4": "Hall says few seekers suffered so many disappointments as Count Bernard of Treviso, born in Padua in 1406, died 1490. He began searching for the Philosopher's Stone at fourteen and spent a lifetime and a fortune on it, going from one alchemist to another and trying each one's pet theory, always without success. His family thought him mad and a disgrace as his experiments reduced him to poverty. He travelled widely looking for wise men, and at last, nearly seventy-six, he succeeded and learned the secrets of the elixir of life, the Stone and transmutation. He wrote a short book about his work, and though he lived only a few more years, he was sure the treasure was worth a lifetime's search. As an example of his patience, one charlatan persuaded him to spend twenty years calcining eggshells and almost as long distilling alcohol and other substances. Hall says no one ever pursued the Great Work more patiently. The passage ends by saying that Bernard declared dissolution, done not with fire but with mercury, to be the supreme secret of alchemy.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The final sentence breaks off after 'the supreme secret of alchemy,' in the e-text, and both scanned copies end the same way, so whatever followed is missing.",
  "modern.hall.33.fig1": "The caption, for a portrait from The Complete Writings of Paracelsus, says that in his Biographia Antiqua Francis Barrett gives Paracelsus these titles: Prince of Physicians and Philosophers by Fire, Grand Paradoxical Physician, the Trismegistus of Switzerland, First Reformer of Chemical Philosophy, Adept in Alchemy, Cabala and Magic, Nature's Faithful Secretary, Master of the Elixir of Life and the Philosopher's Stone, and Great Monarch of Chemical Secrets.",
  "modern.hall.33.fig2": "The caption, for a portrait from Jovius's Lives of Illustrious Men, says Albert de Groot, Albertus Magnus, was born about 1206 and died at 74, and was called great in magic, greater in philosophy and greatest in theology. A Dominican and Bishop of Regensburg, he taught Thomas Aquinas alchemy and philosophy and was beatified in 1622. He was an Aristotelian, an astrologer and a deep student of medicine and physics. Thought dull as a youth, he was rewarded for his devotion by a vision of the Virgin Mary, who gave him great mental powers. Mastering magic, he spent over thirty years building a speaking, thinking automaton, the Android, of metals and unknown substances chosen by the stars and given spiritual qualities by spells; Aquinas, thinking it diabolical, destroyed it, yet Albertus left him his alchemical formulas, including, legend says, the Philosopher's Stone. Once he invited William II, Count of Holland and King of the Romans, to a midwinter banquet in the snowy grounds of his monastery at Cologne. As the astonished guests sat down, he spoke a few words: the snow vanished, flowers and singing birds appeared and the air grew warm, and when the feast ended the snow returned.",
  "modern.hall.33.fig3": "The caption, for a title page from the Museum Hermeticum, says John Cremer, the 'mythical Abbot of Westminster', is an intriguing figure, since no abbot of that name seems to have existed, which raises the question of who hid behind the name. Hall says such invented figures show two medieval practices. First, many high-ranking people secretly did Hermetic research and, fearing persecution and ridicule, published under pseudonyms. Second, for thousands of years initiates who held the key to the Hermetic secret passed it on by inventing imaginary people, setting them in real historical events and sometimes giving them whole family trees. Their names meant nothing to outsiders but told initiates that the person was purely symbolic, and the secrets were hidden in the lives, words and deeds given to these figures, so that what looked like biography carried the deepest occult teachings.",
  "modern.hall.33.fig4": "The caption, for a picture from Flamel's Hieroglyphical Figures, quotes Robert H. Fryar's note to his reprint: what seems to prove the story beyond dispute is that the Book of Abraham the Jew itself, with Flamel's notes based on the physician's teaching, was later owned by Cardinal Richelieu, as the Count de Cabrines, who had seen and examined it, told Borel.",
  "modern.hall.34.p1": "Hall says alchemy, the secret art of the land of Khem, is one of the two oldest sciences, with astrology, both reaching back before history and regarded in the earliest records as revealed by God to help man regain what he lost. A rabbinic legend says the angel at Eden's gate taught Adam the Qabbalah and alchemy, promising that when humanity had mastered them the curse of the forbidden fruit would be lifted. As man put on 'coats of skins', physical bodies, at the Fall, these sciences too took on dense bodies through which their spiritual nature could no longer show, and so were thought dead or lost. Alchemy's earthly body is chemistry, whose practitioners do not see that half the book of the Law is hidden behind the veil of Isis; astrology has hardened into astronomy, which mocks the old seers. Yet modern thinkers can pass behind the veil only by the appointed way, the Mysteries. The ancients built their temples to answer what life, intelligence and force are; Hall asks who can say they failed, or would recognize the answers. Chaldeans, Phoenicians, Babylonians, Greeks and Romans knew alchemy, and it was Egypt's master science; Khem was a name for Egypt, and the words alchemy and chemistry recall Egypt's lead. To these peoples it was no speculation but a firm belief in multiplying metals, and Hall says scholars should judge it more kindly. Evolutionists trace the arts to growing human intelligence; others see them as revelations from God.",
  "modern.hall.34.p2": "Hall says one explanation of alchemy's origin is that Hermes Trismegistus revealed it. The Egyptians credited him with all the arts and sciences, so all science was called the Hermetic arts, and he is pictured holding the immortal Emerald. When he was buried in the Valley of Ebron, or Hebron, the Emerald was buried with him, and centuries later it was found, by an Arab initiate in one version and by Alexander the Great in another. By the power of its inscription of thirteen sentences Alexander conquered the known world, but, not having conquered himself, he fell as the talking trees foretold; Hall mentions rumours that Alexander was a high initiate undone by the temptations of power. He quotes Kenealy, citing Gobelin Persona's Cosmodromium. In India Alexander marched over mountains and through dark valleys full of serpents and beasts for three hundred days, until he reached a beautiful mountain hung with golden chains, with 2,050 sapphire steps. With his twelve princes he climbed to the Palace of the Sun at the top, with twelve gates, seventy golden windows and a golden temple whose vines bore carbuncles and pearls. Inside a stately old man with snow-white hair and beard lay on a golden bed. He told Alexander he would see what no mortal had seen, said he had known Alexander's deeds since before the Flood, and offered to show him the sacred Trees of the Sun and Moon, which foretell the future.",
  "modern.hall.34.p3": "Hall finishes the quotation. The sage told Alexander to take off his rings, ornaments and shoes; Alexander took three princes and followed him to the great trees, the Tree of the Sun with leaves of red gold and the Tree of the Moon with leaves of silver. Asked whether he would return to Macedon in triumph, the trees said no: he would live a year and eight months more and die by poison. They would not say who would poison him, and the Tree of the Moon added that his mother would die shamefully and lie long unburied, but his sisters would be happy. Hall thinks the talking trees were really wooden tablets with tables of letters used to consult oracles; books written on wood were once called talking trees. He says the origin of alchemy is hard to find because Atlantis is ignored: the Great Arcanum was the Atlantean priests' most prized secret, and when Atlantis sank the hierophants of the Fire Mystery brought it to Egypt, whence it passed to Europe, where it is still kept intact. Those who reject the legend of Hermes and his Emerald Tablet point instead to the two hundred angels who, in Enoch, came down on the mountains, as the first teachers of alchemy. Whoever began it, Egypt's priests kept it for the modern world. Egypt, from its dark soil, was called 'the black empire' and in the Old Testament 'the land of darkness', so alchemy became 'the black art', meaning not evil but the darkness hiding its methods.",
  "modern.hall.34.p4": "Hall says that in the Middle Ages alchemy was a religion as well as a philosophy and science: people who rebelled against the religious limits of the time hid their teachings in the allegory of gold-making and were laughed at rather than persecuted. Alchemy is threefold, well symbolized by a triangle, and its number is three times three, three processes in three worlds. This is part of the mystery of the Masonic 33rd degree, for three times three is nine, the number of esoteric man, of the emanations from the root of the divine tree, and of the worlds watered by the four rivers from God's mouth. Under the derided craft lies the triple key to eternal life, and since it works in three worlds, divine, human and elemental, the sages hid it in elaborate allegory. Alchemy is the science of multiplication, based on growth. 'Nothing comes from nothing': alchemy does not make something from nothing but increases and improves what exists. The ignorant would call it impossible to make a man from a stone, but the wise know the seed of man is in every stone, and that a man is a seed from which a universe can come. God is the within and without of everything and shows himself through growth, an urge from within outward. A mustard seed grows into a bush thousands of times its size in a different substance, the earth; so why should not the seed of gold, planted in its earth, the base metals, and fed by the secret process, be multiplied a hundred thousand times?",
  "modern.hall.34.p5": "Hall says alchemy teaches that God is in everything, one universal spirit in endless forms, a spiritual seed planted in the dark earth of the material universe. By art this seed can be grown until all substance is tinctured by it and becomes like it, pure gold: in man's spirit this is called regeneration, in material elements transmutation. The same holds in the mind. An idiot cannot be taught wisdom because its seed is not in him, but anyone ignorant can, because the seed is there and can be grown by training; a philosopher is just an ignorant man in whom a 'projection' has taken place, his base metals of ignorance tinctured into the gold of wisdom. If a person's consciousness can be changed by faith from animal desire into golden, godly awareness, and if mental ignorance can be turned into genius by training, Hall asks why the same process should not work in the third, material world. By analogy, if spiritual and mental elements can be multiplied, so can material ones, once the method is found. What is true above is true below: if alchemy happens in the universe it happens in man, and if in man then in plants and minerals; if one thing grows or multiplies, all things do, 'for the superior agrees with the inferior'. But just as the way to save the soul is hidden in the Mysteries, the secrets of saving the metals are hidden so the unworthy cannot misuse them.",
  "modern.hall.34.p6": "Hall says whoever would grow metals must know that metals, like stones, plants, animals and universes, grow from seeds already present in the body of substance, the womb of the World Virgin. As a person's seed exists in the universe before birth and a plant's seed lasts beyond the plant, the seeds of spiritual and material gold are always present in everything. Metals grow over the ages, given life by the sun, slowly and in the form of tiny shrubs. A great axiom says that within everything is the seed of everything, though in Nature it may lie dormant for centuries. So every grain of sand holds the seeds of precious metals and gems and even of sun, moon and stars, for as man reflects the universe in miniature, each grain, drop and speck of dust holds the whole cosmos as seeds trillions of times smaller than an electron, waiting their time to grow; Hall compares Leibniz's monads. Growth happens in two ways: by Nature, an alchemist always doing the seemingly impossible, and by art, which does in a short time what takes Nature endless ages. The true philosopher copies Nature's laws, art being Nature's method shortened by being intensified with secret formulas. Nature works either extensively, as when black carbon hardens into diamond over millions of years, or intensively, through art, her faithful servant. Arthur Dee says Nature and Art should embrace so that Art asks nothing Nature denies and Nature denies nothing Art can perfect.",
  "modern.hall.34.p7": "Hall says that by this art the seed in a stone can be made to sprout so intensely that a diamond grows from it in moments. This works only because the seed of diamond is in marble, granite, sand and indeed everything, though it is easier in some substances whose seeds are already well prepared, just as some people are easier to teach than others whose minds are wholly dormant. Alchemy is the art of bringing things to perfect flower as quickly as possible: Nature may fail through the elements destroying one another, but with true art she always succeeds, since the art is not subject to time's waste or elemental conflict. He quotes James Campbell Brown's History of Chemistry: the alchemists' general aim was to do in the laboratory what Nature did inside the earth, and they had seven main problems. These were the elixir or philosopher's stone, which turned base metals into gold and silver and did other marvels; the making of homunculi, little living beings, of which incredible stories are told; the alkahest or universal solvent; palingenesis, restoring a plant from its ashes, which they hoped would lead to raising the dead (Hall says Brown takes a lot for granted here); spiritus mundi, a mystic substance able above all to dissolve gold; the extraction of the quintessence or active principle of all substances; and aurum potabile, drinkable gold, a supreme remedy because perfect gold could make the body perfect.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p1": "Hall says alchemy has three symbolic substances, mercury, sulphur and salt, plus a fourth mysterious life principle, Azoth. He quotes von Welling: these philosophical substances are not the crude salt, sulphur and mercury of the earth or the chemist's shop. Each is threefold and contains the other two, with its own one predominating, making nine divisions, which with Azoth make ten, the sacred decad of Pythagoras. Azoth is disputed: some call it the invisible eternal fire, some electricity, some magnetism, and transcendentalists the astral light. Von Welling goes on: beyond the sphere of the stars is Schamayim, the divine fiery water, the first outflow of God's Word, a flaming river from the presence of the Eternal. This fiery, two-sexed water divides, the fire becoming the solar fire and the water the lunar water. Schamayim is the universal mercury, sometimes called Azoth, the boundless spirit of life. It comes through Eden, Hebrew for vapour, and pours into four rivers, the elements; it is the river of living water flowing from the throne of God and the Lamb. In this Eden is the spiritual earth, the dust Aphar, from which God formed 'Adam min Haadamah', man's spiritual body, which must one day be revealed.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p2": "Hall says von Welling also writes that there was no material universe until Lucifer, trying to perform the cosmic alchemy, misused Schamayim, the divine fire, and that this universe was made to free it from the dark cloud in which Lucifer's failure had locked it. This shows, Hall says, that early philosophers saw the Bible as a book of chemical and alchemical formulas, and that whoever takes the alchemists' rambling allegories literally can never reach the inner truth. Ashmole says their chief study was to wrap their secrets in fables and veils whose rays seem to point every way but meet in one centre and point at one thing. A parable in the Geheime Figuren der Rosenkreuzer (Altona, 1785) about Solomon's wives, concubines and virgins shows the Bible hiding knowledge: Hartmann explains that the wives are the arts, the concubines the sciences and the virgins Nature's unrevealed secrets, and Solomon ordering the virgins to unveil means that wisdom forces the mystic arts to show their hidden parts to the philosopher, while outsiders see only the garments, the mystery of Isis's veil. Because the alchemist must work in four worlds at once, Hall gives a table of the three principles in four worlds. The old masters never standardized their symbols, and some did not separate spirit from soul, so some rows have alternatives; but scripture shows they differ, since 'the soul that sinneth, it shall die' while 'the spirit shall return unto God'.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p3": "Hall gives his tables. The triune power, as Father, Son and Mother, appears in God as Father, Son and Holy Ghost; in man as spirit, soul and body; in the elements as air, fire and water; and in chemicals as mercury, sulphur and salt. An alternative reading swaps the first two in the last two rows: fire, air, water and sulphur, mercury, salt. Paracelsus, more Aristotelian, leaves out God and gives man as spirit, soul and body, the elements as air, water and earth, and the chemicals as sulphur, mercury and salt. The main point, Hall says, is proved: salt, sulphur and mercury stood for the spiritual principles of God, man and the universe as well as for chemicals. Three substances in four worlds make the sacred twelve, the foundations of the Great Work, which Revelation calls the twelve foundation stones of the holy city; Pythagoras likewise made the twelve-faced dodecahedron the foundation of the universe. Hall asks whether this three times four is linked to the four parties of three in the Masonic third-degree legend, who go to the four corners in search of CHiram. Another table matches the four corners east, south, west and north with Aquarius, Leo, Scorpio and Taurus; man, lion, eagle and bull; spring to winter; childhood to old age; birth, growth, maturity and decay; spirit, soul, mind and body; and air, fire, water and earth. A last table, for Masons, links salt, sulphur and mercury to Masonic symbols.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p4": "Hall's Masonic table matches mercury, sulphur and salt with stellar, solar and lunar fire; with the three Grand Masters Hiram, Solomon and Hiram of Tyre (or alternatively Solomon, Hiram and Hiram of Tyre); and with the sphere, pyramid and cube. He says alchemy carries on the universal mystery of death and rebirth: as Jesus died on the cross, Hiram at the Temple's west gate, Orpheus by the Hebrus, Krishna by the Ganges and Osiris in Typhon's coffin, so in alchemy the elements must first die. As the Bible says a man must be born again to see God's kingdom, alchemy says the Great Work needs putrefaction; Hall asks what it is that dies on the cross, in the Mysteries' tomb and in the retort, and in man, so that he may rise like the phoenix from his ashes. The solution in the retort, digested long enough, becomes a red elixir, the universal medicine, like a luminous fiery water. It passes through many colours, one stage so iridescent it is called the peacock. If its power is increased too far, the vessel explodes into dust, the main danger in making it, or the substance seeps through the glass, since it is no longer matter but a divine essence that can pass through anything. Properly made, this liquid universal solvent dissolves all metals into itself; the universal salt becomes liquid fire, and dissolved with a metal and taken through the stages, it becomes a medicine for transmuting inferior metals.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p5": "Hall quotes The True Way of Nature by Hermes Trismegistus, published by a Freemason signing himself I.C.H., on the danger of over-strengthening the universal salt: it can be multiplied only up to the ninth rotation, after which it cannot be raised further, and at the least touch of material fire it melts and passes through the glass like hot oil through paper. Writers do not agree on the stages of the work: The True Way of Nature gives seven, the Dictionnaire Mytho-Hermétique twelve, matched to the zodiac: Aries calcination, Taurus congelation, Gemini fixation, Cancer dissolution, Leo digestion, Virgo distillation, Libra sublimation, Scorpio separation, Sagittarius incineration, Capricorn fermentation, Aquarius multiplication and Pisces projection. Hall says this opens a useful field of study. The twelve steps recall the twelve degrees of the ancient Rosicrucian Mysteries, Rosicrucianism being partly chemistry made theology and alchemy made philosophy, and the Mysteries taught that man is redeemed by passing in turn through the twelve houses of heaven. The twelve processes for finding the 'secret essence' also recall the twelve Fellow Crafts sent to search for the murdered Builder of the Universe, the universal mercury.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p6": "Hall says Solomon Trismosin divides the stages through which matter passes towards perfection into twenty-two, each with a picture, and these are closely linked to the twenty-two major Tarot cards and the twenty-two Hebrew letters; the Tarot itself, rightly read, is an alchemical formula. Supporting the medieval claim that Solomon was a master alchemist, Hartmann says the Song of Solomon describes alchemical processes, and the 'dark maid of Jerusalem' is a material sacred to the sages, not a woman. Hartmann gives chapter and verse: the subject in 1:5, the lily of the art in 2:1, preparation and purification in 2:4, the fire in 2:7 and 4:16, putrefaction in 3:1, sublimation and distillation in 3:6, coagulation and change of colours in 5:9 to 14, fixation in 2:12 and 8:4, multiplication in 6:7, and augmentation and projection in 8:8. Von Welling's appendix on the universal salt says that a tiny particle of the Stone dropped on water at once replays the history of the universe in miniature: the tincture moves on the water like the spirit of God, and a tiny world forms, which the philosophers say rises from the water into the air, goes through all the stages of cosmic development and finally crumbles to dust. Besides the medicine for metals, there is a tincture for minerals that can turn granite and marble into precious stones and improve poor ones.",
  "modern.hall.34.s1.p7": "Hall says, as one alchemist put it, the search for gold often ruins a man because he takes the processes as purely material, not seeing that the Philosopher's Gold, Stone and Medicine exist in all four worlds and the work succeeds only when done in all four at once by one formula. One ingredient exists only within the alchemist himself, and without it the chemicals will not combine, however much he spends; this subtle element from the nature of an illuminated, regenerated philosopher is why scientists cannot repeat the medieval alchemists' results even by following every step. Hartmann warns readers not to try alchemical recipes unless they are alchemists: he knows from experience that they are literally as well as allegorically true and work for an alchemist, but others will only waste time and money, for an alchemist must have in himself the 'magnesia', the magnetic power to attract and 'coagulate' invisible astral elements. So the formulas that follow, Hall says, work only for a Magus: an initiate and an outsider using the same vessels, substances and method side by side would get different results. The greater alchemy must happen in the soul before the lesser alchemy can happen in the retort; unless a man is 'born again' he cannot do the Great Work. The part concerning the secret life principle in man is forbidden to speak of, since the masters decree that each must find it for himself.",
  "modern.hall.34.fig1": "The caption, for a drawing redrawn from a manuscript of 1577, says that in his Key to Alchemy Samuel Norton divides the work into fourteen stages from the first placing of the substances in the vessel to the finished medicine for plants, minerals or men: solution, the passing into liquid; filtration, separating a liquid from undissolved particles; evaporation by heat; distillation, separating a volatile liquid from what it holds dissolved; separation, decomposing substances; rectification, purifying by repeated distillation; calcination, reducing to powder by heat; commixtion, blending ingredients into new compounds; purification through putrefaction, decay; inhibition, holding back or restraining; fermentation by a ferment; fixation, ceasing to be fluid and becoming firm; multiplication, increase in number; and projection, turning the base metals into gold.",
  "modern.hall.34.fig2": "The caption, for a table from Basil Valentine's Last Will and Testament, says Hermetists used these strange signs for chemical elements and processes, and their full meaning, the hidden spiritual nature of the metals, has never been revealed. In their allegories alchemists combined human, animal and plant emblems and composite creatures such as the dragon, winged serpent, unicorn and phoenix. Gold was nearly always a crowned king, often with a sceptre, sometimes with a sun-disc face; silver was a queen without a crown, often standing on a crescent moon like the Madonna. Mercury was a winged youth, often two-headed, holding serpents or the caduceus; lead an old man with a scythe; iron an armoured soldier. Nitric acid was oddly called 'the ostrich's stomach'; the completed Great Work was a phoenix on a nest of fire; the union of elements a marriage; putrefaction a skull; and antimony a dragon.",
  "modern.hall.35.p0": "Hall says true Hermetic philosophers begin their work with a prayer to the Supreme Alchemist, and he quotes one written centuries ago in a provincial German by an unknown adept. It asks the Holy Trinity to sink the writer into God's eternal fire, where mortal nature is reduced to dust, to melt and transmute him so that the fiery waters of the Holy Spirit may draw him out of the dust with new birth, and through the humility of the Son to rise from the ashes into a pure spiritual body of rainbow colours like transparent paradisal gold, redeemed and purified like the elements in his own glasses and bottles. It asks to be dissolved in the waters of life as in the wine cellar of the eternal Solomon, where the fire of God's love will blaze beyond any stream's power to put it out, and at last to be found worthy of the light of the righteous and sealed with the light of the new world, in immortal glory where light and darkness no longer alternate.",
  "modern.hall.35.s1": "Hall says few medieval alchemists, perhaps none, found the Great Arcanum without a master, whose identity was always hidden. Such sages were called adepts, meaning they had the true secrets of transmutation and multiplication; they went by many names and appeared and vanished without trace, and seem to have been somewhat organized, the strongest bodies being the Rosicrucians, the Illuminati and certain Arab and Syrian sects. The 'Brethren' in the documents that follow are those who had completed the Great Work and knew one another by ciphers and signs; several great European alchemists were initiated in Asia Minor, suggesting that many adepts lived in Arabia. Whoever learned the secret guarded it even from his family. In old age he sought one worthy younger man, his 'philosophical son', to inherit it; sometimes an adept taught a sincere seeker the basics and, if he persevered, quietly initiated him. So the secret survived but spread slowly. In the sixteenth to eighteenth centuries adepts travelled Europe, appearing and vanishing at will, said to be immortal and to live for centuries on a few drops of their elixir; Hall says many witnesses attest such men, who can still be found by those who qualify. He stresses that alchemical formulas are allegories first, worthless taken literally until understood, and that nearly every formula deliberately leaves out one element, so that only those able to find it themselves could gain power over people and Nature's forces.",
  "modern.hall.35.s2.p1": "Hall says the oldest and most revered alchemical formula is the Emerald Tablet of Hermes. Some call it a post-Christian forgery, but he says there is much evidence that it is very old whoever wrote it. It has Masonic meaning, relating to CHiram (Hiram), but it is first an alchemical formula for both the alchemy of metals and the divine alchemy of human regeneration. He quotes Dr. Sigismund Bacstrom's manuscripts, noting that Bacstrom was initiated into the Rose Cross on Mauritius by an unknown adept calling himself the Comte de Chazal. Bacstrom calls the Tablet the most ancient monument of the Chaldeans on the Philosopher's Stone and the source of the allegory of King Hiram, or Chiram. He says the Chaldeans, Egyptians and Hebrews drew on the same source, which Homer followed in his own way, Virgil after Homer, and Hesiod and later Ovid too; their real subject was Nature's secret operations, which ignorance turned into myth and the common people into idolatry. His translation 'from the very ancient Chaldee' is headed 'The secret works of Chiram, one in essence but three in aspect'. It says it is true, certain and reliable that above agrees with below and below with above to perform one wonderful work. As all things exist by the will of the One, all come from one hidden thing; its father is the sun, its mother the moon, the wind carries it in its belly and a spiritual earth nurses it; it is the father of all things, and its power is perfect once united with a spiritual earth.",
  "modern.hall.35.s2.p2": "Hall continues Bacstrom's version, which adds process notes. It says to separate the spiritual earth from the coarse by gentle heat and with great care. It rises from earth to heaven and comes down again newborn, increasing the power of above and below; Bacstrom explains that the Azoth rises from the bottom of the vessel and drops back, growing ever finer and carrying volatilized gold with it, until it becomes the true 'Sophic Mercury' and the earth has absorbed it all, turning into a black, pitch-like matter, 'the Toad' (Hall: the contents of the retort and also man's lower elements), the putrefaction or death of the compound. 'By this thou wilt partake of the honours of the whole world': the black matter must turn white and red, and the perfected red can keep a sound mind in a sound body for a natural lifetime and give endless means for charity. Contemplating the Stone lifts the mind to God and, if one is allowed to possess it, must root out greed and envy, so the philosophers say the Stone either finds a good man or makes one. 'And darkness will fly from thee': strengthening the bodily organs the soul uses gives it greater power to understand and remember, and with prayer and faith all obscurity must vanish; those who lacked this had only themselves to blame, since they cared only for making gold.",
  "modern.hall.35.s2.p3": "Hall finishes Bacstrom's version. 'This is the strength of all powers': the Stone holds all Nature's hidden powers, used not to destroy but to raise and regenerate matter in the three kingdoms. 'With this thou wilt overcome all things and transmute what is fine and what is coarse': it binds the subtlest substance into its fiery nature, gaining tenfold power at each multiplication in less time, until it can penetrate gold, silver, mercury, crystal and glass and give them hardness; Bacstrom cites an artificial diamond, owned by Prince Liechtenstein in his father's time and valued at 500,000 ducats, said to have been fixed by the Stone. 'So the world was created; the ways to follow this road are hidden. Therefore I am called Chiram Telat Mechasot, one in essence but three in aspect; in this trinity is hidden the wisdom of the whole world.' Bacstrom adds that some think Hermes was Moses or Zoroaster, or that the name means a serpent, an emblem of wisdom, and that the serpent, winged globe, sun and moon, dragons and griffins in Egyptian hieroglyphs show the Egyptians' knowledge of the Stone. The Tablet ends: 'What I have said concerning the effects of the sun is finished', which Bacstrom glosses as the perfect seed fit for multiplication, and he calls this the genuine Emerald Tablet of Hermes.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p1": "Hall says Eugenius Philalethes denied being a Rosicrucian but is believed to have headed the order for some years. In Lumen de Lumine (London, 1651) he printed a letter presumably from the order, with an emblem showing the processes of the Philosopher's Stone, a good example of the Rosicrucian mix of theology and chemistry, which Hall invites readers to try to solve. The letter, on 'the invisible magical mountain and the treasure therein', says everyone wants gold and silver, which Hall glosses as intellect and soul, and greatness in the world's eyes. God made everything for man to rule and to praise him for, but people want to enjoy riches without labour or danger and do not look for them where God has stored them, so they are not found; the way and the place have long been hidden, but must still be sought. God does not hide anything from his own, and in this last age before the judgment all will be shown to the worthy, for nothing covered will not be revealed. Moved by God's Spirit, the brothers have published his will in several languages, meaning the Fama and Confessio, but most people mock it or expect to be taught at once how to make gold so they can live in luxury, make war, lend at interest, overeat, drink and sin, against God's will. They should have learned from the parable of the wise and foolish virgins.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p2": "Hall continues the letter. Every man must work for this treasure himself, with God's help. The brothers know the bad motives of the gold-seekers from their own writings, close their ears to them and hide in clouds, and leave God to judge the slanders against them. But having seen how diligently the reader studies scripture and seeks God, they have chosen him above thousands for an answer. There is a mountain in the middle of the earth, both small and great, soft and very hard, far and near, but by God's providence invisible, holding treasures beyond price. The devil has surrounded it with cruel beasts and birds of prey, so the way has been hard and dangerous, and until now it could not be found; now the worthy may find it, but each by his own effort. Go to it on a certain very long, very dark night, prepared by prayer. Keep to the way, but ask no one where it is: follow your Guide, who will meet you unrecognized and bring you to the mountain at midnight when all is silent. You need heroic courage, but no weapons, only sincere prayer. The first wonder will be a violent wind that shakes the mountain and shatters the rocks, with lions, dragons and other beasts; do not fear or turn back, for the Guide will let no harm come to you. The treasure is not yet revealed but is very near. Then an earthquake will level what the wind left; take care not to fall.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p3": "Hall finishes the letter. After the earthquake a fire will burn away the earthly rubbish and uncover the treasure, though it cannot yet be seen. Near daybreak comes a great calm, the morning star rises, and the treasure appears; its chief part is an exalted tincture that could turn the whole world into pure gold if the world served God and deserved it. Used as the Guide teaches, it makes the old young and frees the body from disease, and with it pearls of unimaginable quality can be found. But take no credit, be content with what the Guide gives, praise God, use it not for pride but for works contrary to the world, use it as if you did not have it, live temperately and avoid sin, or the Guide will leave you; whoever abuses it and does not live purely and devoutly will lose it with little hope of regaining it. Hall suggests that if Rosicrucian initiations took place in the invisible worlds, as transcendentalists believe, this allegory may be an initiation ritual as well as an alchemical formula. He then introduces the most nearly complete formula available, noting that Paracelsus used the method of collecting the energies of the heavenly bodies as condensed in dew, and warning that it is only for the properly instructed. It is 'a true revelation of the manual operation for the universal medicine', by the 'celebrated philosopher of Leyden', attested on his deathbed with his own blood in 1662 and addressed to 'my loving cousin and son'.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p4": "Hall quotes the philosopher of Leyden. He had resolved never to write the ancient sages' secret down, but out of love for his kinsman, because life is short and the art obscure, he will set it out, on condition that it is treated in a Christian way, since such a jewel is not for swine. He makes him swear to keep it from the wicked and lustful, not to exalt himself, and to use it for God's honour and his neighbour's good; he has written down part of the kingdom of heaven as he worked it with his own hands, and signs it in blood on his deathbed in Leyden. The process: take the purest sea salt, made by the sun, such as is shipped from Spain (he used salt from St. Uber), dry it in a warm stove and grind it as fine as possible in a stone mortar so the dew-water dissolves it easily. Collect the dew in May or June at full moon, when it falls with an east or south-east wind, by laying square glass plates on sticks about a foot and a half high and draining the dew from them into glass vessels until there is enough; after the full moon it is hard to get. Hall explains that the sun's rays carry solar sulphur, the divine fire, which is crystallized by the moon's rays and by emanations rising from the earth into a partly tangible substance soluble in pure water: the 'magical mountain of the moon' of the Rosicrucian letter. This crystallization in dew yields the 'virgin earth', a pure invisible substance, green when wet and white when dry.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p5": "Hall says von Welling hints at extracting the solar life from stagnant water without naming the essence or describing its refining: seal clean water in a large bottle three-quarters full, leave it in the sun for weeks until it rots and leaves a sediment, and this, properly distilled, gives a clear, fiery, burning oil known only to the wise. The Leyden philosopher goes on. Seal the dew tightly with wax and keep it cool, or its subtle spirit escapes. Dissolve the powdered salt in the dew until it will take no more, the salt lying four days undissolved. Put about a pound and a half of this water in a round short-necked flask, seal it well so the living spirit of the dew cannot escape, without which the soul of the salt will never be stirred, and let it putrefy in a water bath on a slow fire for forty or fifty days, until the matter turns black. Then coagulate it in a dry furnace on a slow, even fire for twelve to fifteen days, until it clings round the glass like a grey salt, and let it cool slowly. Return it to the putrefying furnace for twelve days, when it will dissolve again, taking care each time that the seal and glass are not damaged and the neck is stoppered against moisture.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p6": "Hall continues the Leyden recipe. Repeat putrefying and coagulating, up to five times, until the dissolved water is clean and clear and the calcined matter snow-white; it is then a fixed salt that melts like wax on hot silver. Dissolve it once more by itself and let it cool: the matter is reduced by a third, and in place of salt water there is a sweet, very penetrating water, hidden by the philosophers under wonderful names, the Mercury of the philosophers, from which gold and silver come, whose father is gold and mother silver. Five drops taken inwardly strengthen understanding and memory and open wonders the author's oath forbids him to describe: it is as if all the heavens and stars worked within you, all knowledge and secret arts open as in a dream, and above all you learn the true nature of all creatures and through it the true understanding of God, like David, Moses and Solomon. Hall adds that von Welling's treatise on salt, sulphur and mercury reveals what most alchemical writings do not, that the alchemists had a complete cosmology based on the Qabbalah. Von Welling says the universal salt in watery form cures all bodily ailments; it is in every living thing but easier to get from some, especially virgin earth; it is the universal solvent, the alkahest; and even in its early stages it cures all diseases of the heart.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p7": "Hall continues the Leyden recipe for a tincture for metals. Put the 'paradisiacal water' in a dissolving glass over a gentle ash heat, and add, little by little, gold beaten thin for the red elixir or silver for the white, the process being the same; it melts like ice in warm water until the metal lies four days undissolved. Fill a round glass two-thirds with this solution, seal it hermetically, and keep it in a vapour bath for forty days, when the metal is fully dissolved and turns the deepest black; then move it to the drying furnace. The author explains that the philosophers say there is no true dissolving of the body without a coagulation of the spirit, the two mixing in due proportion so that the body becomes spiritual and penetrating and the spiritual virtue becomes bodily through the fire, as the heavens act in the depths of the earth to bring forth its treasures. To project on metals, melt five parts of gold or silver, throw in the medicine wrapped in wax, heat strongly for an hour, and cast one part of the result on ten parts of base metal, which turns at once into metal purer than mined. Strengthened by repeated dissolving and coagulating, by the fifth time it dissolves in three days and coagulates in twenty-four hours into a shining red stone like a glowing coal, or for the white work like white lightning.",
  "modern.hall.35.s3.p8": "Hall finishes the Leyden recipe. One part of the last coagulation cast on five thousand parts of melted gold or silver turns it into perfect medicine, one part of which turns a hundred thousand parts of base metal into the finest gold or silver. The author went no further, because when he distilled it six times in twelve hours it became so subtle that most of it passed through the glass with an indescribable sweet smell, and he warns his reader to beware of this. He says many more wonders could be told, such as making precious stones, but the art is endless and his aim was only to lead his kinsman into Nature's mysteries. He ends: work as I have, fear God and love your neighbour sincerely, and while you work many of our brethren will privately reveal themselves to you; I have written the truth, found by prayer and the study of Nature, seen with my eyes and done with my hands, and sign it in my blood on my deathbed, Leyden, 27 March 1662.",
  "modern.hall.35.fig1": "The caption says the Egyptian priests used the scarab as a symbol of regeneration and also saw in its habits analogies to the secret process of transmutation, the scarab's egg being the seed of the metals. The figure shows this seed travelling through the planetary bodies until it reaches the centre, is perfected and returns to its source. The small spiral at the top reads 'The spiral progress of the mundane spirit', and after winding to the centre the scarab returns along a path marked 'Return of the spirit to the centre of unity'.",
  "modern.hall.35.fig2": "The caption, for an emblem from Philalethes's Lumen de Lumine, quotes his description of the magical mountain, an emblem he says the muse Thalia gave him in 'the invisible Guiana'. Its upper part shows the Mountains of the Moon, also called the Mountains of India, where the philosophers' secret Lunaria grows, a herb easy to find if men were not blind, which shines at night like a pearl. Their earth is red and indescribably soft and full of crystal rocks, which the philosophers call their glass and their stone, brought to them, they say, by birds and fish. He quotes Hali the Arab telling his son to go to the Mountains of India and take from their caverns the precious stones that melt in water. He adds that the mountains are dangerous at night, haunted by fires and apparitions, spirits that play with the 'sperm of the world' and produce monstrous births, and that the journey there is described by the Brothers of R.C. in the accompanying letter.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.p1": "Hall says the best way to introduce a seeker to the 'Royal Art' is a real alchemical document, and he presents the manuscript of Claudius de Dominico Celentano, as cryptic in its text as in its pictures but rewarding to meditation. An unknown former owner wrote that its drawings make it of the first importance to Rosicrucians and Freemasons: most of the pictures explain Hermetic teachings, mixed with portraits of great teachers and satires of bunglers, and a systematic section shows the colour stages of the work from blue-gold through black to white and rose. It concerns the transformation of human beings, not gold-making; the black stage, a return to chaos through fire from which new creation is possible, is always followed by the new birth of the neophyte. The owner called it unpublished and unique in German collections. Besides the 26 leaves Hall reproduces, there are ten bottles half filled with coloured substances, which he describes rather than reproduces. The first, 'Our Quicksilver', holds a bluish-grey liquid with a golden three-flowered shrub rising from it, and a verse: 'He will have white garments for black and then red.' The second, also quicksilver, has four golden flowers, with the advice to 'make spirit of the body and grace of the gross, that the corporeal may become incorporeal'.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.p2": "Hall describes the other bottles. The third is black except for a golden trunk with six lopped branches and five knobbed ones rising from the neck, called 'blackness showing through the head of the raven', with the note that the philosophers' tincture is hidden in the air as the soul in the body. The fourth, deepest black and with nothing rising from it, is 'the head of the raven', its earth 'submerged in chaos'. The fifth has a spotted bluish-grey liquid under a brick-coloured layer, labelled 'sixth raven's head', and 'at the bottom of the vessel worms are born'. The sixth, bluish-grey below and black above, is the 'seventh raven's head', with a child beside it: 'this newly born black son is called Elixir and will be made perfectly white'. The seventh, black below and black spotted with red above, is 'black blacker than black', many colours appearing as black clouds return to the body and body, soul and spirit are joined and turned to ashes. The eighth, divided by a golden band with a five-leaved stem, is clear: 'the black clouds are past and the great whiteness has been completed'. The ninth holds a clear liquid and a golden-white rose saying 'He who blanches me makes me red'. The tenth, the completed work, is half full of blood-red elixir with a beautiful many-petalled red rose; all the planets were present, and the author says he gave the master, the spirit, so much silver and gold that he can never be poor.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.p3": "Hall says the author's dedication claims to set out all the operations of the Great Work and prays to the Holy Spirit to be counted among those who have pursued this noblest science and kept on the right path; besides his own research, his sources are Thomas Aquinas, Raymond Lully and Arnold of Villanova. Medieval alchemists used Christian language to protect themselves from persecution, though their great secrets came mostly from Egyptian or Arab adepts; Muslims were masters of the Hermetic secrets, Paracelsus learned most of his knowledge from them, and the Rosicrucian manifestos admit an Arab source. So the linking of alchemy to Bible symbolism was a matter of expediency, though the Qabbalists largely confirmed the alchemical reading of scripture, since both are wholly concerned with human regeneration. Hall says the manuscript puts the deepest Hermetic secrets before the student. Deciphering it may seem hopeless and the shallow will mock, not seeing that provoking ridicule is one way it guards its secrets. These few leaves are a lifetime's work by someone devoted to lifting the veil of the World Virgin, years of research, labour, prayer and meditation ending in success. Physical symbols can express life's deepest truths only imperfectly, and only those who have gone through spiritual birth can understand and respect these touching attempts to show others knowledge locked in the knower's heart.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig1": "The caption describes Leaf 1. The top line says the earliest medicine was made of natural things. Around the king and queen it says a son will be born to them 'in two trees of the vine', like his father and without equal in the world. Around the vase: 'Green and white', 'The vase flame colour, the flowers green', 'Our water, our silver'. Below, it says the material of the Philosopher's Stone is a thick, sticky water that heat or cold congeals, mercury boiled down and thickened, cooked in neutral earth with sulphurous heat, called the first matter of the metals. A stone that Nature made from her fruits a thousand years ago, found in dark caves and forbidding mountains, will rescue whoever has it from trouble, and the author says he speaks his verses without veil or deceit.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig2": "The caption describes Leaf 2. At the top is a quotation from Thomas Aquinas describing the Philosopher's Stone as perfectly transparent, with all the forms of the elements and their opposites visible in it. Under his figure a paragraph praises the Stone, saying that from one substance come three and from three, two. To his right Raymond Lully sits in the door of his hermitage, with a quotation that asks 'What is the Philosopher's Stone?', calls it a reddish, fixed mercury, and swears to God that this is the truth and more may not be said. Hall notes that the manuscript is damaged at this point.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig3": "The caption describes Leaf 3. The top reads 'Death of Saturn; life of Mercury', and after describing the use of saturnine substances says the key to the process is shown below. A verse says the Stone is made of four elements, the truth in all Nature, and tells the reader to take it, bright and shining, with great care and bind everything securely together so the fire may do its work. Above the human figure is 'Saturn is almost dead'. Beside the devouring serpent Albertus Magnus says Saturn and Mercury are the first principles of the Stone, and that Nature wisely mixed the elements so earth gives its dryness to fire, fire its heat to air, air its moisture to water and water its coldness to earth. The text about the vase is illegible in the original.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig4": "The caption describes Leaf 4. The top says to believe everything is possible: the art is fleeting, bright and rare, and fools do not believe it. Between the sun and crescent is 'It is hidden', and across the body 'The Book of Life and true Treasury of the World'. The left panel says the soul returns to the body it fled, ripens seven or nine months, and the crowned King appears; the right says there are three Mercuries, animal, vegetable and mineral. Hall says the text below is almost untranslatable; it says that by putting fire under the figure's feet one can draw out the sun and moon, which the figure holds up above its head.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig5": "The caption describes Leaf 5. The upper panel says the sun-bird fights the earth-serpent, which tears out its own entrails and gives them to the bird; the spirit is revived and Lazarus joyfully raised from the dead. Above the bird is 'This is the sun in the form of a bird', and above the dragon 'This is the dragon devouring the bird. The first operation.' The lower left panel, in substance, calls the mixing of 'our sperm', quicksilver, with the mother of elements, earth, coitus; the earth's holding of a little quicksilver conception; its growth impregnation; and its whitening with water into one colour birth, when the King is born from fire. The lower right text was deliberately defaced to hide a secret that was too plain.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig6": "The caption describes Leaf 6, which shows all the secrets of the Stone. In the centre stands the Paschal Virgin, with the prime virtue in her hair, described as a herb growing in wells, and in her hands the symbols of the spiritual and material elements. Upper left: there are four spirits with two faces, called the elements. Upper right: fire lives on air, air on water, water on earth, and so the Stone lives peacefully on all the pure elements. Under the sun is 'Summer', under the moon 'Autumn'. By the left tree with its eyes: 'Turn away your eyes from the fire. There is space'; by the right tree: 'Open your eyes to the fire. There is time.' The lower panel begins 'I am exalted above the circles of the world.'\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Hall marks the left tree's wording as uncertain: 'to [from?]' and 'space [?]'.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig7": "The caption describes Leaf 7. The top verse says the Stone is so noble that Nature hid it in her recesses; its soul is fair and pure, the true sun; keep it apart, and whatever you ask will come to you abundantly, without sin and with delight. The seated man raising a hammer is breaking hard stone, and beside the man with the retort is 'Breaking of stone our replenishment'. Between the standing figures below: 'O Sages, seek and ye shall find my Stone!' Under the man with a basket: 'Draw out sorerem in the bottom', a word Hall cannot read. Under the pool the man on the left is stirring: 'Our healing water.' Hall notes that the four men's faces are very well drawn.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig8": "The caption describes Leaf 8. Under the sun, moon and Mercury are the words 'Three and One', implying the three are one. Under the flower stems: 'Whiteness forty days after ashes'; under the flowers, 'Minor time of the Stone' and 'The selected red'. Between the central figure's arms: 'Let one pound of Mercury be placed.' To the left: whoever understands this figure has the whole science of the Stone; to the right: whoever does not will be stubborn and dull. Above the sun is 'Father', above the satyr 'Ferment of the work'; by the child, 'The son of the moon would throw the Stone into the fire, his mother'; above the flaming basket, 'I am the true Stone'; and under the central figure, 'A moderate fire is the master of the work.'",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig9": "The caption describes Leaf 9. Upper left: without the moon's light the sun does not warm the earth, and the sun puts its fruits into the moon. Upper right describes the true herb of the philosophers, saying whoever believes in it will be spiritually rich, and tells the reader to understand what the man holds in each hand if he wants to be enlightened. Left of the sun: 'Entirely without the Sun and Moon, make dye; dissolve, congeal, and like produces for itself like.' Beside the man with the philosophers' herb, sublimation is called the beginning, middle and end of the Great Work. The last sentence says to make one thing of equal parts out of the sun and moon, and by their union, God willing, the Philosopher's Stone will be made.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig10": "The caption describes Leaf 10. Upper left: 'Some take a recent stone.' By the sign of Mars, iron, the student is told to control his appetites and apply his mind to gaining knowledge, and Hall can find no good translation for the words under the arm of the man holding the top of the tree. The lower panel says that once the Stone is well refined it will seem to penetrate thoroughly; put it in its vessel with its water, close it well over a little fire and await Nature's wonders. The large red oval in the lower half is evidently the egg or vessel of the sages, and the tree symbolizes the growth of metals, which alchemists said grow in the rocks like plants, spreading their veins as branches.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig11": "The caption describes Leaf 11. The fountain is the source of the philosophers' two Mercuries, the white described at upper left and the red at upper right; Saturn gathers the white Mercury, called the water of earth, and the earth gathers the red, called the water of heaven. Beside the frog, the Stone speaks: through him who made heaven and earth it is the Philosopher's Stone and carries in its body what the wise seek, a sweet refreshment if extracted; it is an animal with father and mother, which were created, it holds the four elements, it existed before its father and mother, and it is a poisonous animal. The lines on the right describe distilling and calcining.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig12": "The caption describes Leaf 12. The top says 'This is Nature.' Over the donkey: this is the philosophers' donkey, who wanted to rise to the practice of the Philosopher's Stone. Under it: frogs gather in crowds, but science consists of clear water made from the sun and moon. Under the bird: this is two-winged fortune, and whoever has it knows how fruit is produced; a great philosopher showed the Stone is a white sun that needs a telescope to see, and dissolving it in water needs the sun and moon and 'two hundred telescopes', putting body and soul into one mass; other sages cook the frogs and add nothing if you would enjoy the juice of the wise. Hall adds that to the Greeks the frog stood for both reincarnation and earthly moisture.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig13": "The caption describes Leaf 13, which has two figures: Morienus the philosopher pointing to a salamander that 'lives and grows in fire'. Hall says Morienus, born in the twelfth century, learned the Hermetic arts from the Arab alchemist Adfar, made the elixir for the Sultan of Egypt, inscribing its vase 'He who possesses all has no need of others', and lived many years as a hermit near Jerusalem. The lines under the salamander say: let the fire be perfectly red, the earth white and the water clear; combine them philosophically and calcine them as many times with the body's own water as it takes to whiten it, and you will have the greatest treasure in the world.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig14": "The caption describes Leaf 14. At the top left: 'The man that digs.' Over the birds: only the cocks of Hermes, the two Mercuries, may put hand to the plough, and only after watering will the earth bear fruit. The seated man is Count Bernard of Treviso, saying 'Work the earth with Mercury.' Beside him: go to the fire and wait a month for me with Mercury your brother; crumble the stone I gave you and I will go to the fire; your death is my life; I shall not die but live to tell the works of my master. Hall says Bernard stressed meditating on the writings of the great adepts rather than chemical experiment, and in the end found the Stone.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig15": "The caption describes Leaf 15. The first line: 'The fruit of plants by virtue of the Sun, our Stone.' A boy with a dish invites all who thirst to come to the waters and drink freely, to open their eyes to the wonders of the earth, addressing 'my thirsty twenty and four'. Beneath him: in the beginning God created heaven and earth and divided the waters from the waters; bless the waters above the heavens. The circle reads: 'The earth without form and void. Out of the stars come the rains.' The lower left panel continues the process and ends by urging the reader to renounce the misery of worldly life, and above is a prayer to the Virgin Mary beginning 'Hail Mary, full of grace'.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig16": "The caption describes Leaf 16. The first sentence: the dead bodies remain, and the spirits are freed by the death in the bodies; you will ride with that death with a scythe, with the light of the sun, moon and fixed stars. Over the scythe: 'Subject to the Sun, the Moon and Azoth, complete the Work.' In its blade: 'Man's head, head of raven.' To the right: this figure is called Laton, because it looks black in the vessel and is the beginning of corruption. Under the ladder: this is the ladder of first matter, which turns black in the vessel and then gradually white by the ladder of digestion, according to the heat. Hall says the ladder stands for the natural steps matter must climb to become truly spiritual.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig17": "The caption describes Leaf 17. The top verse says the material must be not only fixed but allowed to enter everything, so it is completed and has infinite virtue; thickened, it turns white at once, and by sublimation from white it becomes shining. Above the sun: 'God and Nature do nothing in vain.' The man on the left is a medieval idea of Hermes, and on the right is Christopher, the philosopher of Paris, above whom is written 'If the Stone is black, it is not useless.' Over the retort: 'There is air, fire, water, and earth', and below: 'A dissolution of the body is the first step.' Hall says the odd apparatus is purely symbolic and, as the author says, only hints at the art.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig18": "The caption describes Leaf 18. Aristotle, 'the most learned of all the Greeks', stands at the left with a book. By the tree topped with the sun and moon: when the Stone is dead, that is, turned to water, it will flower. Under Aristotle and the lying figure from which the flowering tree grows: whoever makes everything come down from heaven to earth and go up again from earth to heaven knows about the Stone, for in Mercury is something the wise seek, called out only by white or red ferment. Hall notes that the first part comes from the Emerald Tablet, and explains that in ordinary people the spirit is swallowed up by the body, but in the true philosopher the spirit grows strong enough to absorb the body and feed on it.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig19": "The caption describes Leaf 19. The top: whoever comes to know this figure will know the Stone. The seated man is probably Paracelsus. To his right, the Stone speaks: I am neither tree nor animal, stone nor plant, but the Philosopher's Stone, trampled by men and thrown into the fire by my father, and in fire I rejoice. To the left: 'In dryness is the Stone.' Below is the philosophic egg inscribed 'It is the end in which the beginning rests', and the capital T stands for Tincture. The right-hand text says that, as Geber learnedly writes, it is formed in stone and turns into clear living water, and has the power to make people rich, content and free of care if their wit can find the secret.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig20": "The caption describes Leaf 20. The top: 'Rains are made by six stars.' Under the upside-down man: 'Receive new spirit. Arise, for you are asleep.' About the large figure: remember, Mercury, that you are ashes and to ashes will return; I thirst and am dead. Over the seven globes: if he thirsts, give him drink and he will live. Over the small man: 'Hermes, the father of philosophers', who points to a line reading 'The measure of the drink'. Under the central pedestal: 'The light of my eyes is a lantern to my feet', and 'If the beginning is unknown, the rest is unknown.' Above the figure rising from the flames: 'He was resurrected after new Moon'; under the eagle: 'Thou shalt not fly further with me.'",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig21": "The caption describes Leaf 21. The top: two things, and double, but at last one is dissolved into the first and they make sperm. The capitals I A A T are the initials of the Latin names of the elements, Ignis, Aer, Aqua and Terra, fire, air, water and earth. Under them: our fire is water; if you can give fire to fire, fire and Mercury will be enough. Along the arm: 'The Art of the Stone is', and on the ribbon: 'Swift, brief, bright, and rare.' Below: every hand is a key, because it was called the herb celandine; under the sun: 'I am the gift of God.' The verse has the Stone describe its body as naked, clean and shining, running like oil, bright as gold, and then succumbing to 'the plague' in its bright little chamber, the retort.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig22": "The caption describes Leaf 22. The upper left verse says this group is made of three stones, lunar, solar and mercurial: white sulphur is in the lunar, red in the solar and both in the mercurial, and this is the strength of all instruction. In the left bottle: 'Dissolving, calcination, sublimation complete the instruction'; at its base: 'Wash, congeal, and coagulate.' Under the central tower: metallic salts are hidden by one letter. Around the red circle: dryness, coldness, moisture, heat and dryness, with the four elements on the points below and the initials I A A T four times, meaning the elements. The three cherub heads in the circle at upper left stand for the three powers of the Stone.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig23": "The caption describes Leaf 23. The upper left is in substance the Lord's Prayer, ending with the names Jesus and Mary. The upside-down words in the banner: you can do nothing without me, for God has promised it, saying 'So be it.' Under the angel: by this plague he will be damned who knows he is dead, cold in a black body; let this be your first comfort, that he will then burn to calcination, and when I have brought him within this door I shall be blessed if I know how to tend the garden. Most of the leaf is an elaborate drawing of apparatus labelled the furnace of distillation, congealing, rectification, perfection and fixation, the quintessence of the philosophers, the 'fifth essence' of the wisest.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig24": "The caption describes Leaf 24. The top: I, the bird, the adept, speak into your ears from the sun, moon and Azoth; the work is completed with little labour. The left panel describes primordial matter and the philosophers' drink. The right: this is my beloved son whom I saw and loved; if he rises he will stay at home, where spirit will be soul and body, for Mercury may be called the son of the sun and moon. Under the child: if he were not dead I should not have been his mother; I bore him after death before he was born into the world; I hold under my feet what was his, and from me, my son and the ground under my feet the Philosopher's Stone is made. At lower left the Stone's three parts stand on a pedestal to show their dignity.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig25": "The caption describes Leaf 25. Above the Queen it says, in substance, that the beginning of the book told how she fed the sun at her breasts, and that whoever could turn her into first matter had rare skill. By her head: 'In the highest mountains this water' and 'I am the light of the Philosophers.' To her left the reader is told to strike the sons she bears. She calls herself the mother of the sun, sister of the moon, and servant and wife of Mercury, and on the right says she cannot be crowned unless these sons of hers become ashes; the sons are shown below. The verse under her continues the process, describing how the substance's exudations should be kept.",
  "modern.hall.celentano.fig26": "The caption describes Leaf 26, the last of the leaves with the symbols of the secret work, which has several unrelated emblems. At the top is the King's head, the commonest alchemical figure; to its right a vessel called the Hermetic Seal; below that the head of a fierce bird called a griffin. To the left of the King a headless figure holds up a sun or spiritual face: this is the world, headless because its spiritual and rational part is not material and so cannot be seen. Below is a circle with no description. Under the King's head is a vase from which rises the philosophers' golden plant, and at the bottom more apparatus, also called a Hermetic Seal.",
  "modern.hall.36.p0": "Hall says Johann Valentin Andreae, born in Wurttemberg in 1586, admitted writing The Chemical Marriage, which appeared when he was twenty-eight and was presumably written about twelve years earlier, at fifteen or sixteen. Hall finds it almost incredible that someone so young could have put such wealth of symbolic philosophy into it. It is the earliest known mention of Christian Rosencreutz and is usually counted the third of the original Rosicrucian manifestos, and as a symbolic work it cannot be squared with what Andreae said about it. It tells of events befalling an old man, presumably the Father C.R.C. of the Fama and Confessio; if he was born in 1378, as the Confessio says, he was made a Knight of the Golden Stone at eighty-one, in 1459, and by Andreae's own account Andreae could not have been Father Rosy Cross. Many figures in symbolic books of the early seventeenth century closely resemble its characters and scenes, and Hall thinks it may be the key to the riddle of Baconian Rosicrucianism; some English words in the German text show that its author knew English. He then summarizes the book's seven days.",
  "modern.hall.36.s1": "Hall summarizes the First Day. On the evening before Easter, as Christian Rosencreutz prayed a violent storm threatened his house and the hill under it. Someone touched his back, and he saw a glorious woman in a sky-blue robe spangled with stars, with wings full of eyes, a trumpet in one hand and letters in every language in the other. She gave him a letter and flew off with a trumpet blast that shook the house. Its seal bore a strange cross and the words 'In hoc signo vinces', and inside, in gold on blue, was an invitation to a royal wedding. It fulfilled a prophecy he had received seven years earlier, but he felt so unworthy that he was frozen with fear. He prayed and slept, and dreamed he was in a filthy dungeon among many chained men stumbling over each other in the dark. Trumpets sounded, the lid was lifted, and a white-haired old man announced that a rope would be let down seven times and whoever held on would be pulled out. In the scramble the rope swung to C.R.C. and he was drawn up, injured. An old woman, 'the Ancient Matron', wrote the names of the rescued in a golden book and gave each a gold piece with the sun and the letters D L S, and told him to thank God for bringing him into so high a light. He woke still feeling his wounds. Strengthened in faith, he put on a white linen coat with a red ribbon crossed over his shoulders, put four roses in his hat, took bread, water and salt, vowed to use whatever he learned to serve his neighbour, and set out joyfully.",
  "modern.hall.36.s2.p1": "Hall summarizes the Second Day. Walking through the forest, C.R.C. felt that all Nature was celebrating the wedding. On a green heath stood three great cedars, one with a tablet describing four roads to the King's palace: one short and dangerous, one roundabout, one pleasant and royal, and one fit only for incorruptible bodies. Tired and unsure, he sat down to eat, but a white dove begged his bread, a raven attacked the dove, and while separating them he ran a long way down the southward road without noticing. A fierce wind kept him from going back, so he gave up his bread and went on until, near sunset, he reached a great gate bearing among other figures the words 'Procul hinc procul ite profani', 'Away, away, you profane'. A gatekeeper in sky-blue asked for his invitation, let him in, and asked him to buy a token; calling himself a Brother of the Red Rosy Cross, C.R.C. gave his water bottle for a gold disc marked S C. At a second gate guarded by a lion, inscribed 'Date et dabitur vobis', 'Give and it shall be given you', he showed a letter from the first gatekeeper and bought a token marked S M with his salt, then hurried on to reach the palace before the gates were locked.",
  "modern.hall.36.s2.p2": "Hall continues the Second Day. A beautiful virgin, Virgo Lucifera, was putting out the castle lights, and C.R.C. barely squeezed through the closing gates, leaving part of his coat caught in them. His name was written in the Bridegroom's vellum book, and he received new shoes and a token marked S P N. Invisible barbers cut the grey hair from his crown, and he was led to a hall full of kings, princes and commoners, where he got a humble seat. Most of the guests were vain pretenders and the banquet became an orgy, until stately music silenced them for half an hour. Then thousands of candles carried by invisible hands entered, followed by Virgo Lucifera on a self-moving throne. She announced that next day scales would be set up to weigh each guest's worth, and that anyone unwilling should stay in the hall. Most were confident, but nine, including C.R.C., felt their faults so keenly that they stayed; they were tied with ropes and left in the dark. C.R.C. dreamed of many men hanging over the earth by threads while an old man flew among them cutting threads, so that the proud who had risen highest fell furthest and were hurt worst, while the humble fell a short way and often landed safely. Taking it as a good sign, he talked it over with a companion until dawn.",
  "modern.hall.36.s3": "Hall summarizes the Third Day. At dawn Virgo Lucifera, in red velvet with a white sash and laurel crown, came with two hundred men in red and white, and hinted to the nine that they might fare better than the self-satisfied guests. Golden scales were hung with seven weights. An emperor so virtuous that it took six weights to tip the scale went first; rich and poor were weighed, and the few who passed got velvet robes and laurel and sat on the steps of the throne, while those who failed were mocked and whipped. A captain asked that the nine be weighed too; one of the first seven passed, and C.R.C., the eighth, outweighed every weight and even three men hanging on the beam, so that a page cried 'That is he!' Freed, he was allowed to release one captive and chose the first emperor, and gave Virgo Lucifera his red roses. At dinner the successful 'artists' got the best seats and were given the Golden Fleece and a Flying Lion in the Bridegroom's name, and all drank from a goblet sent by the King. The failures were punished and sent away with a drink of forgetfulness. Each of the elect got a learned page to show him the castle; C.R.C. alone saw the Royal Sepulchre, where he learned 'more than is extant in all books', a great library and a thirty-foot globe. At supper he solved Virgo Lucifera's riddle about herself. A crowned virgin called the Duchess, taken for the bride, told him he had received more and must give more back.",
  "modern.hall.36.s4.p1": "Hall summarizes the Fourth Day. The guests washed and drank from a garden fountain inscribed among other things 'Drink, brothers, and live', then climbed the 365 steps of the royal winding stair behind Virgo Lucifera. Given laurel wreaths, they found themselves before the King and Queen as a curtain rose; the throne room awed C.R.C., and the Queen's robes were too dazzling to look at. Each guest was presented by a virgin, Virgo Lucifera praised the honest 'artists' and asked whether she had done her duty, and old Atlas welcomed them in the royal couple's name and promised her a reward. The room was five times as long as wide, with a porch to the west holding three thrones, the middle one raised, each with two people: an old king with a young consort, a black king with a veiled matron, and in the middle a young couple under a costly crown, around which a little Cupid shot arrows at the lovers and round the hall. Before the Queen stood a small altar with a black velvet book, a burning candle, a celestial globe, a small striking clock, a crystal pipe running with clear blood-red liquid, and a skull with a white serpent crawling in and out of its eye sockets. The guests then went back down to the great hall.",
  "modern.hall.36.s4.p2": "Hall continues the Fourth Day. Virgo Lucifera announced a comedy for the six royal persons in the House of the Sun, and after the seven-act play with its happy ending all returned to the throne room. C.R.C. noticed that the young King was very sad and kept sending food to the white serpent in the skull. After the feast the young King, holding the black book, asked if they would stay true to him in good times and bad, and when they tremblingly agreed had each sign the book. The royal persons drank from the crystal fountain, then the guests, the 'Draught of Silence', and the royals sadly shook hands with everyone. A bell rang: the kings and queens changed from white into black, the room was hung in black, their eyes were bound with black taffeta, six coffins were set down, and a black-robed Moor beheaded all six with an axe. Each one's blood was caught in a gold goblet and put in the coffin with the body, and the executioner was beheaded too and his head put in a small chest. Virgo Lucifera assured the guests all would be well if they stayed faithful and kept watch with the dead. At midnight C.R.C. saw from his window seven ships on a lake, each with a flame above it, which he took for the spirits of the dead. Virgo Lucifera met them, a covered coffin was put on six of them, the lights went out and the flames passed back over the lake, leaving one watch light on each ship.",
  "modern.hall.36.s5": "Hall summarizes the Fifth Day. At dawn C.R.C. asked his page to show him more treasures and was taken down to an iron door with a strange inscription into the royal treasury, lit only by huge carbuncles, where stood the triangular tomb of Lady Venus. Through a copper door in the floor the page led him to a crypt with a great bed, and lifting the covers showed him the body of Venus. He told his companions nothing. Virgo Lucifera, in black velvet, then led the guests to six coffins in the courtyard; C.R.C. alone suspected the bodies were no longer in them. The coffins were buried under great stones, and she urged everyone to help bring the royal persons back to life by travelling with her to the Tower of Olympus, where alone the necessary medicines could be found. They sailed in seven ships across the lake and out to sea, escorted by sirens, nymphs and sea goddesses who gave a great pearl for the royal couple. The tower's old warden came out to meet them. The tower stood on an exactly square walled island; in its laboratory the guests were made to pound and wash plants, gems and other things, extract their juices and essences and bottle them, working so hard they felt like drudges, and slept on mattresses on the stone floor. Unable to sleep, C.R.C. climbed onto the wall and at midnight saw seven flames come over the sea and gather on top of the central spire. Afraid, he went back and fell asleep to the sound of a fountain in the laboratory.",
  "modern.hall.36.s6.p1": "Hall summarizes the Sixth Day. The old warden, pleased with the laboratory work, had ladders, ropes and wings brought out and told each guest to carry one all day; lots were drawn and C.R.C., to his annoyance, got a heavy ladder. Locked in the lowest room, the guests were then called up through a hole in the ceiling, the winged flying up at once, those with ropes struggling, and C.R.C. making fair speed with his ladder. On the second floor, around a fountain-like device holding the six royal bodies, Virgo Lucifera put the Moor's head in a kettle at the top and poured on it the substances made the day before; heated by lamps, they boiled over onto the bodies and dissolved them, and the liquid was drained into a heavy golden globe. On the third floor, with glass walls, mirrors focused sunlight on the hanging globe until it was very hot; when it had cooled it was cut open with a diamond, revealing a beautiful white egg, which Virgo Lucifera took away. On the fourth floor the egg was warmed in a square kettle of silver sand until it hatched an ugly, bad-tempered bird, fed on the royal blood diluted with prepared water, whose feathers turned from black to white to many colours as its temper improved. On the fifth floor it bathed in water coloured with white powder until the heat of lamps stripped its feathers; the bath was boiled down to a blue stone, which was ground into paint and used to paint the bird all over except its head.",
  "modern.hall.36.s6.p2": "Hall continues the Sixth Day. On the sixth floor, at an altar like the one in the throne room, the bird drank from the little fountain, was fed the white serpent's blood, and when the clock struck three laid its neck on the book and let itself be beheaded; its body was burned and the ashes put in a cypress box. Virgo Lucifera called C.R.C. and three others lazy and had musicians 'blow' them out of the seventh room in mockery, but the musicians led them up to the eighth floor under the roof, where the old warden congratulated them on being chosen for the greater work. Virgo Lucifera, laughing at their confusion, emptied the real ashes into another vessel and filled the box with worthless matter, presumably to keep the others busy on false ashes. The four moistened the ashes into a dough, heated it in two moulds, and found two bright, almost transparent figures about four inches high, a man and a woman. Fed drop by drop with the bird's blood on satin cushions, they grew to full size and great beauty, but had no life, lacking souls. Surrounded by torches, their faces veiled, they received a ray of light that came down through a hole in the dome and through a trumpet placed at each mouth, three times each. Within half an hour the young King and Queen woke, put on white garments brought by Virgo Lucifera, thanked C.R.C. and his friends, and left by ship. The four rejoined the others without a word about what they had seen.",
  "modern.hall.36.s7.p1": "Hall summarizes the Seventh Day. Virgo Lucifera announced that each guest was now a 'Knight of the Golden Stone', and the warden gave each a gold medal inscribed 'At. Nat. Mi.' and 'Tem. Na. F.' They sailed back in twelve ships under the signs of the zodiac, C.R.C. under Libra, and were met by the King and Queen on a golden barge. At the first gate the blue-clad porter begged C.R.C. to ask the King to free him; the King said the porter was a famous astrologer condemned to keep the gate for having looked on Lady Venus on her couch, and could be freed only when someone else was found guilty of the same crime. C.R.C.'s heart sank, but he said nothing. The new knights swore five articles: to credit their order only to God and his handmaid Nature; to hate all uncleanness and vice; to help the worthy and needy; not to use their knowledge for worldly rank; and not to wish to live longer than God decreed. In a small chapel C.R.C. hung up his Golden Fleece and hat and wrote 'Summa Scientia nihil Scire', 'the highest knowledge is to know nothing', signing himself Brother Christian Rosencreutz, Knight of the Golden Stone, 1459. He then confessed that he had seen Venus and must become porter; the King embraced him, and he was given a room with three beds, for himself, the old lord and Atlas. The book stops abruptly in mid-sentence, with an editor's note, implying he would take up his post next morning.",
  "modern.hall.36.s7.p2": "Hall explains the allegory. Under the image of an alchemical marriage medieval philosophers hid a system of spiritual training meant to reunite the scattered limbs of both the individual and society. Society is threefold like man: as man is spirit, mind and body, society is church, state and people, and the bigotry of the church, the tyranny of the state and the fury of the mob are the three murderers of Truth in the Hiram legend. The first six days show the philosophical 'creation' every organism passes through: the three kings are man's threefold spirit, their consorts its vehicles below, and the executioner the mind, whose higher part, the head, is needed for the work. Blended by a divine formula produce two philosophic babes who, fed on the bird's blood, become rulers of the world. The resurrected King and Queen stand for Intelligence and Love, which must one day guide society, corresponding to enlightened spirit and regenerated body. The bridegroom is reality and the bride the regenerated being who becomes one with it, the mortal united with its immortal source; whoever achieves this is a 'Knight of the Golden Stone', a philosophic diamond made from the essence of his sevenfold nature. The Golden Fleece is the Lamb of God, the Flying Lion illumined will, the weighing of souls a parallel to the Book of the Dead. The tower's floors are the planetary orbits, and the ascent recalls the Eleusinian and Mithraic rites of climbing seven steps to escape the planetary governors.",
  "modern.hall.36.fig1": "The caption, for the title page of the 1616 Chymische Hochzeit, calls The Chemical Marriage, published at Strasbourg, the most remarkable of all the Rosicrucian publications. It is very rare, and Hall says it should be reproduced in exact facsimile so students can examine its text for the kinds of cipher used. Probably no other book caused such a stir: from the moment it appeared its purpose was debated, attacked and defended by theologians and philosophers alike, yet its mysteries remain unsolved. Its author was admitted to be very learned, and those with the deepest understanding of Nature were among those most impressed by it.",
  "modern.hall.36.fig2": "The caption, for a plate from Ashmole's Theatrum Chemicum Britannicum, says this plate, the key to mystic Christian alchemy, is missing from almost every copy of Ashmole's collection of about twenty English poems on the Philosopher's Stone, and may have been removed deliberately because it showed the Rosicrucian secrets too plainly. Hall also notes how carefully owners' names have been blotted out of early alchemical and Hermetic books, often damaging them; almost all such books deal with Rosicrucianism or contain suspected Rosicrucian ciphers, and he presumes this was done so that early Rosicrucians could not be traced through their libraries. The plate matches the life of Christ to the four great stages of the alchemical process and shows that the Stone itself is a macrocosm and microcosm, embodying the principles of the universe and of man.",
  "modern.hall.36.fig3": "The caption, for a diagram from Fludd's Philosophia Mosaica, says the small globe at the top is God, half dark and half light: the dark half is the divine darkness in which God hides, the bright half the divine light that pours out as creative power. The large dark globe below the dark half is the darkness on the face of the deep, where the Spirit of God moved; the light globe on the right is God revealed out of the darkness, where the shining Word has scattered the shadows and formed a glorious universe, its power known to man as the sun. The large figure below, half light and half dark, is the created universe, sharing the light and darkness in the Creator's nature: the dark half is the deep, the eternal waters pouring from God, and the light half, with Apollo, is the daytime half of the world, ruled in the Mysteries by Apollo, while the night half is ruled by Dionysus, faintly visible in the gloom.",
  "modern.hall.37.p1": "Hall says he takes up the Bacon-Shakespeare-Rosicrucian controversy not to dig up dead men's bones but in hope of recovering knowledge lost since the oracles fell silent, and quotes W. F. C. Wigston calling the Bard 'phantom Captain Shakespeare, the Rosicrucian mask'. He argues that William Shakspere (as he spells the Stratford man) could not have written the works alone. Stratford had no school capable of the learning they show, his parents were illiterate, and he showed no interest in study as a youth. Only six examples of his handwriting survive, all signatures, three in his will, so shaky that Hall thinks he either copied a prepared signature or had his hand guided. No manuscripts of the plays or sonnets exist, nor any tradition about them except the 'fantastic and impossible' statement in the First Folio's preface. An author who knew the literature of all ages would need a library, but there is no record that Shakspere had one, and his will mentions no books. Ignatius Donnelly notes that his daughter Judith could only make her mark at twenty-seven, and finds it unbelievable that the author of the plays would let his daughter grow up unable to read a line of the works that made her father rich and locally famous.",
  "modern.hall.37.p2": "Hall asks where Shakspere learned French, Italian, Spanish and Danish, let alone Latin and Greek, when Ben Jonson, who knew him well, said he had 'small Latin and less Greek', though the plays use Latin with rare skill. There is no record of him playing a leading part in the plays or any others; his highest acting achievement seems to have been the Ghost in Hamlet, though he may have had a small share in the Globe or Blackfriars. Despite his known greed he made no effort to control or profit from the plays, many first published anonymously, and his heirs had no part in the First Folio and no profit from it. If he had written them, his manuscripts would have been his most valuable possessions, yet his will, which disposes of his second-best bed and a silver-gilt bowl, mentions no writings. The Folios and Quartos are usually signed 'Shakespeare', but all his autographs read 'Shakspere'. Hall asks why the publishers of the First Folio, if they revered him, put what looks like a caricature of him on the title page, as if mocking a hoax. At the height of his supposed career he was buying malt, presumably for brewing, and the supposed author of The Merchant of Venice lent money and sued a neighbour, Philip Rogers, for two shillings. Nothing known of his life, Hall concludes, justifies the genius attributed to him.",
  "modern.hall.37.p3": "Hall says the philosophy of the plays shows an author thoroughly familiar with Rosicrucian doctrine and stamps him as one of the illuminated of all time. Most who have tackled the question have been intellectuals who overlooked the role of the transcendental, which materialists cannot grasp; yet who but a Platonist, Qabbalist or Pythagorean could have written The Tempest, Macbeth, Hamlet or Cymbeline, and who but a student of Paracelsus could have conceived A Midsummer Night's Dream? He calls Francis Bacon father of modern science, reformer of modern law, editor of the modern Bible, patron of modern democracy and a founder of modern Freemasonry. Bacon was a Rosicrucian, some hint the Rosicrucian; if not Father C.R.C. himself, he was certainly a high initiate, and that is what matters most to students of symbolism, philosophy and literature. Many books argue that Bacon wrote the plays and sonnets, and Hall thinks an impartial reader must find the case convincing. Its advocates might have won long ago had they stressed that Bacon wrote into the plays the secret teachings of the Rosicrucians and the true rituals of Freemasonry, which he may prove to have founded. The world dislikes giving up a hero, but if solving the riddle yields useful knowledge the best minds will help, for the controversy touches the deepest questions of science, religion and ethics, and may hold the key to the lost wisdom of antiquity.",
  "modern.hall.37.p4": "Hall says King James, recognizing Bacon's learning, gave him the translators' manuscripts of what became the King James Bible, presumably to check and revise; he kept them nearly a year, and nothing is known of what he did. William T. Smedley says it will be proved that the whole plan of the Authorized Version was Bacon's, and its first edition has a cryptic Baconian headpiece; Hall asks whether Bacon hid in it, by cipher, the Rosicrucian key to mystic and Masonic Christianity. Bacon certainly had the knowledge needed to write the plays and sonnets, being generally accepted as a composer, lawyer and linguist, and his chaplain William Rawley and Ben Jonson both testify to his philosophic and poetic gifts. Rawley wrote that if any man in modern times received a beam of knowledge from God it was Bacon, who, though a great reader, got his knowledge not from books but from within himself. As a barrister and courtier Bacon knew the parliamentary law and court etiquette the plays show, which the Stratford actor could hardly have known; he visited many of the foreign countries where they are set, while Shakspere apparently never left England. Bacon's great library contained the very books the plays quote and borrow from, many of whose plots came from works not yet translated into English, which Bacon could read and Shakspere almost certainly could not.",
  "modern.hall.37.p5": "Hall says there is abundant cipher evidence that Bacon was involved in the plays. Bacon's cipher number was 33, and in the First Part of Henry IV the word 'Francis' appears 33 times on one page, in obviously awkward lines. The Folios and Quartos contain scores of acrostic signatures; in The Tempest, Act 1, Scene 2, the first letters of two lines and the first three of the next spell BACon, and similar acrostics occur in Bacon's acknowledged works. The plays' politics and outlook match Bacon's, and they even share his mistakes, such as identical misquotations of Aristotle. Hall says Bacon, expecting the future to reveal his genius, left in his will his soul to God, his body to be buried obscurely, and his name and memory to men's charitable speeches, to foreign nations, to later ages, and to his own countrymen after some time had passed, the part he later struck out as having said too much. A few knew the secret in his lifetime, and hints appear in many seventeenth-century books: on page 33 of Robert Cawdrey's Treasurie or Storehouse of Similes (1609) is a simile about a poor man who, lent fine clothes to play a nobleman on stage, keeps them afterwards and swaggers about in them as his own.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Hall says the part of the will Bacon deleted appears 'in italics', but the e-text has lost the italics, so it is not clear exactly which words he struck out, probably the closing clause about his countrymen.",
  "modern.hall.37.p6": "Hall says repeated references to 'hog' and cipher passages on page 33 of various books show that the keys to Bacon's ciphers were his name, puns on it, or its number. Examples include Mistress Quickly's line in The Merry Wives of Windsor, 'Hang-hog is latten for Bacon' and certain title pages and emblems; and the word 'honorificabilitudinitatibus' in Love's Labour's Lost is a Rosicrucian signature, its numerical value being 287. The title page of the first edition of Bacon's New Atlantis shows Father Time bringing a woman out of a dark cave, inscribed 'In time the secret truth shall be revealed'. Mistakes in page numbering are cipher keys, since reprints set from new type by different printers repeat them: in both the First and Second Folios, nine years apart, page 153 of the Comedies is numbered 151 and pages 249 and 250 are 250 and 251, and similar errors occur in other books. Hall notes how often pages ending in 50 to 54 are involved. Bacon's biliteral cipher appears in scores of books printed between 1590 and 1650: Leonard Digges's verses to Shakespeare's memory in the First Folio use two fonts for both capitals and small letters, most visibly in certain capitals, and the cipher was removed from later editions.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text names the capitals as '7's, N's, and A's'; '7's' is probably a misprint, perhaps for T's.",
  "modern.hall.37.p7": "Hall says hidden material is often signalled by needlessly tangled wording. In the 1641 Du Bartas a boar sits above a pyramid of meaningless text, marked with Bacon's 'hog' signature. A year after the 1623 First Folio a remarkable book on cryptography by 'Gustavus Selenus' appeared at Luneburg, and Hall thinks it very probably the key to the Folio's ciphers. Certain head- and tail-piece ornaments mark Baconian-Rosicrucian ciphers, notably a light and a dark shaded letter A; given how often the A and the hog recur, he finds significant Bacon's remark about whether a sow that happened to print an A with its snout could therefore write a whole tragedy. Seventeenth-century secret societies also used watermarks, and books thought to hold Baconian ciphers are usually printed on paper with Rosicrucian or Masonic watermarks such as the Rose Cross, urns and bunches of grapes. Hall has an unpublished document that may be the key to a cipher beginning in Cymbeline and applying only to the 1623 Folio: a count of lines and words using punctuation, especially long and short exclamation marks and straight and slanting question marks, found by Henry William Bearse in 1900, to be published once checked.",
  "modern.hall.37.p8": "Hall says there is no reasonable doubt that Freemasonry grew directly out of the secret societies of the Middle Ages and is steeped in ancient and medieval symbolism. Bacon knew the true secret of Masonry's origin and probably hid it in cipher. He should be seen less as one man than as the meeting point between an invisible institution and a world that could not tell the messenger from the message. That secret society, having recovered the lost wisdom of the ages and fearing to lose it again, kept it in two ways: through an organization, Freemasonry, which taught it to initiates in symbols, and by hiding it in the literature of the day in ciphers and riddles. A group of wise brothers seems to have taken on the work of publishing the best secret books of the ancients and their own writings, with a symbolic alphabet of designs so that later members could recognize the books and find the key passages by which a man is 'raised' to an illumined life. Hall calls Bacon a link in the chain that has carried the secret doctrine from the beginning, and says seeking that wisdom is the only legitimate reason to decode his ciphers. Masonic research should study the sixteenth- and seventeenth-century books that bear the mark of the society that founded Masonry and stayed hidden behind it; Masonry is the bright son of a hidden father, and the 1623 Folio is a treasure house of Masonic lore.",
  "modern.hall.37.p9": "Hall says Christianity broke up the pagan Mysteries as institutions but could not destroy their knowledge of supernatural power, which survived in secret through the early church and was later absorbed into Christianity in Christian dress. Bacon was one of those entrusted with carrying on this knowledge, and to do so either founded the Rosicrucian fraternity or joined it and became a leading figure. Some unexplained power has always worked to stop the Baconian riddle being solved, as strongly now as after his death. The world has always persecuted those who understood Nature's secrets: Bacon's political standing was undermined and Sir Walter Ralegh met a shameful death because their knowledge was thought dangerous. Forged Shakspere handwriting, fraudulent portraits and death masks, invented biographies, mutilated books and documents, and destroyed or defaced inscriptions bearing ciphers have all made the riddle harder, and the Ireland forgeries fooled experts for years. Hall says the Rosicrucians' supreme council was made up of people who had died the 'philosophic death': when an initiate was to begin his work for the order he 'died' mysteriously, changed his name and home, and a box of rocks or a substitute body was buried. He believes this happened to Bacon, who, like all servants of the Mysteries, gave up personal credit and let others pass as authors of what he wrote or inspired.",
  "modern.hall.37.p10": "Hall says Bacon's cipher writings are among the most powerful tangible parts of the mysteries of symbolic philosophy, and the world will take many years to appreciate the genius who wrote the Novum Organum, sailed his little ship out through the Pillars of Hercules into the unknown sea of learning, and set out his ideals for a new civilization in The New Atlantis. He asks whether Bacon was a second Prometheus who, out of love and pity for humanity, brought down the divine fire hidden in printed pages. The keys, Hall thinks, will probably be found in classical mythology: whoever understands the secret of the seven-rayed god will understand Bacon's method, for Bacon took aliases according to the planets. One little-known but important key is the third edition (Paris, 1637) of Blaise de Vigenere's French version of the Images of the two Philostrati and the Statues of Callistratus, whose title page is full of Masonic or Rosicrucian symbols and whose author's name, rightly deciphered, shows it was produced by or for Bacon or his society. On page 486 a plate, 'Hercules Furieux', shows a giant shaking a spear amid curious emblems. Alfred Freund's book on 'the image of the spear-shaker' tries to explain this symbolism, presenting Bacon as the philosophical Hercules whom time will reveal as the true 'Spear-Shaker', Shakespeare.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig1": "The caption, for a headpiece from the 1597 quarto of Richard II, says this ornament has long been considered a Baconian or Rosicrucian signature. Its light and dark letter A's appear in several books published by Rosicrucian agents, and comparing it with the figure from Alciati's Emblemata on the following pages shows further how the two A's were used as a cipher.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig2": "The caption, for the title page of Burton's Anatomy of Melancholy, says Baconians regard the book as Bacon's scrapbook of rare knowledge gathered over his eventful life, and the title page has long been thought to hide a message. The key is the pointing madman in the lower right corner; according to Elizabeth Wells Gallup, the celestial globe he points at is a cipher symbol for Bacon. The planetary signs in the clouds beside the marginal figures 4 to 7 show the planetary configurations that cause the kinds of madness pictured, and Baconians say the seated man resting his head on his hand is Bacon.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig3": "The caption, for a picture from Alciati's Emblemata (Paris, 1618), says the hog in the foreground catches a Baconian's eye at once: Bacon often used the hog as a pun on his name, especially since the name came from the beech tree, whose nuts were used to fatten hogs. The two pillars behind have Masonic interest, and the light and shaded A's near the centre are almost conclusive proof of Baconian influence. The most convincing point, Hall says, is that 17, the numerical value of the Latin form of Bacon's name, F. Baco, is also the number of letters in the three words in the picture.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig4": "The caption, for a portrait from Bacon's Advancement of Learning, says Bacon was born in 1561 and history records his death in 1626, but some records suggest his funeral was a sham and that he lived for many years in Germany under another name, serving the secret society to which he had devoted his life. Hall adds that impartial investigators have little doubt that Bacon was the legitimate son of Queen Elizabeth and the Earl of Leicester.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig5": "The caption, for a headpiece from Ralegh's History of the World, says many books influenced by Bacon or meant to hide Baconian or Rosicrucian ciphers use certain ornaments at the start and end of chapters to tell initiates that something is hidden. This one has long been accepted as a sign of Baconian influence and is found only in a few rare books, all containing Baconian ciphers put there by Bacon or by fellow members of his society. Variants adorn the 1623 Shakespeare Folio, Bacon's Novum Organum (1620), the 1611 Bible, the Faerie Queene (1611) and Ralegh's History of the World (1614).",
  "modern.hall.37.fig6": "The caption, for the Droeshout portrait in the 1623 Folio, says there are no authentic portraits of Shakspere: the Droeshout, Chandos, Janssen and other portraits differ so much that the artists cannot have known his face. Baconians think the Droeshout face is a caricature, perhaps Bacon's death mask, and Hall says it matches the structure of Bacon's face in other portraits, differing only in shading. A strange line runs from ear to chin, as if the face were a mask; the head rests on the collar rather than joining the body; and the coat is half on backwards, the right arm showing the back of the shoulder in front. Frank Woodward counts 157 letters on the title page, which Hall calls a major Rosicrucian signature. By a simple number cipher the date 1623 gives A, F, B, C, and adding ON from LONDON gives AFBCON, which rearranged reads F. BACON.",
  "modern.hall.37.fig7": "The caption, for the title page of the first edition of Ralegh's History of the World, asks what secret knowledge Ralegh had that was thought dangerous to the government, why he was executed when the charges were unproved, and whether he belonged to one of the feared secret societies; Hall thinks he has been almost ignored in the Bacon-Shakespeare-Rosicrucian question. Soldier, courtier, statesman, poet, philosopher and explorer, Ralegh shone at Elizabeth's court, but James I, a coward who feared weapons and wept when crossed, was madly jealous of him, and his enemies played on this until he was executed and his body mutilated. They persuaded James that the central figure holding the globe on this title page was a caricature of him, and the furious king ordered every copy destroyed, so the plate is very rare. Hall says it is full of Rosicrucian and Masonic symbols, the figures on the columns probably hide a cipher, and the facing page has the same headpiece as the 1623 Folio and the Novum Organum.",
  "modern.hall.38.p1": "Hall says no book on symbolism is complete without ciphers. Their military and diplomatic use is well known, but their role in literature and philosophy is overlooked, and if deciphering became popular much hidden wisdom of ancient and medieval thinkers would come to light, showing that many apparently rambling authors were wordy in order to hide words. Ciphers can be hidden in watermarks, bound into covers, concealed by faulty page numbers, spelled by first letters of words or first words of sentences, buried in equations or strange characters, hidden in the jargon of clowns or written in invisible ink revealed by heat; they can be word or letter ciphers, ambiguous statements understood only after repeated reading, illuminated initials, or counts of words or letters. Freemasons who studied them in sixteenth- and seventeenth-century books might bridge the gap between the ancient Mysteries and the craft Masonry of the last three centuries. The Mysteries revealed their secrets only through symbols, which both hide truths from outsiders and reveal them to the qualified. Forms are symbols of formless divine principles, and symbolism is Nature's language: the wise look through the veil to reality, the ignorant see only symbols, and Nature reveals the alphabet of her strange writing only to her eldest and wisest sons as a reward for faith and devotion.",
  "modern.hall.38.p2": "Hall says the temples of the Mysteries developed their own sacred languages, spoken only in the sanctuary, since priests thought it sacrilege to discuss divine truths in the language used for everyday quarrels. They also invented secret alphabets, called sacred or Hermetic alphabets, some of which, like the 'angelic writing', survive in higher Masonic degrees. But a secret alphabet shows that something is hidden, and its key could eventually be got by patience or persecution, so subtler methods were developed that hid both the message and the very existence of a cipher. The illuminati then circulated specially prepared books carrying ciphers with the deepest secrets, so medieval philosophers spread their ideas across Europe without suspicion, since the books could be closely examined without revealing anything. In the Middle Ages many writers in secret political or religious groups published books with ciphers; secret writing became a fashion, every court had its diplomatic cipher, and scholars competed in inventing complicated ones. The literature of the fifteenth to seventeenth centuries is full of ciphers, few ever decoded, because many great scientific and philosophical minds dared not publish openly in an intolerant age and hid their discoveries in the hope that kinder future generations would find them.",
  "modern.hall.38.p3": "Hall says many churchmen used ciphers for fear of excommunication or worse. A cipher of Roger Bacon's had recently been decoded, and Dr. William Romaine Newbold told the American Philosophical Society that its drawings so accurately showed spermatozoa, body cells, seminiferous tubules and ova with their nuclei that it was hard not to infer Bacon had seen them through a microscope. Had he not hidden this he would have been persecuted as a heretic. Despite its progress, science still does not know many medieval discoveries recorded only in ciphers. The most useful books on ciphers for students of philosophy and religion, Hall says, are Trithemius's Polygraphia and Steganographia, John Wilkins's Mercury, or the Secret and Swift Messenger, Athanasius Kircher's Oedipus Aegyptiacus and other works, and Gustavus Selenus's Cryptomenytices. He groups ciphers under seven headings. First, the literal cipher, the most famous being Francis Bacon's biliteral cipher, described in his De Augmentis Scientiarum and devised while he was a young man in Paris. It uses two styles of type, one ordinary and one specially cut, differing so slightly that a strong magnifying glass may be needed. At first the messages were hidden only in italic passages, whose ornate letters made the small variations easier to hide, in size, thickness or flourishes; later Bacon is believed to have had two Roman alphabets made so alike that experts can hardly tell them apart.",
  "modern.hall.38.p4": "Hall says the first four Shakespeare Folios use several styles of type with tiny but real differences throughout, and may all contain ciphers, perhaps added to the plays, which are much longer in the Folios than in the quartos, sometimes by whole scenes. The biliteral cipher appears in many books printed in Bacon's lifetime and for nearly a century after. Bacon called it 'omnia per omnia', all through all. It can run through a whole book and be added at printing without the author knowing, since no word or punctuation changes, and it was probably used for political purposes in many seventeenth-century books; ciphers were used for such reasons as early as the Council of Nicaea. It is hard to use now that type is standardized and books are seldom hand-set. Hall reproduces Bacon's biliteral alphabet from the 1640 English De Augmentis: four alphabets, two for capitals and two for small letters, each letter-form standing for either a or b. It takes five letters of text to hide one letter of the message, and works rather like a telegraph code, with a's and b's instead of dots and dashes. He then decodes the message hidden in the sentence 'Wisdom and understanding are more to be desired than riches'.",
  "modern.hall.38.p5": "Hall works through the example. Each letter of the sentence is identified as belonging to the a or b alphabet: in 'wisdom' the W is b, the i and s are a, the d is b, and the o and m are a, giving baabaa, and the other words are treated the same way. All the letters are run together and split into groups of five, and each group is looked up in Bacon's key: baaba is T, aabaa E, aaaab B, ababa L, abbba P, babab X, and so on, while the last five a's do not count because the end of 'riches' is set off. This gives TEEBLPXEE, which is itself a second cipher meant to defeat anyone with only a casual knowledge of the system. These nine letters are then put through a wheel or disc cipher, two alphabets with one turning inside the other, so that in Hall's example A on the inner ring stands opposite H on the outer. Swapping each inner letter for the outer letter opposite it, T becomes A, E becomes L, B becomes I, L becomes S, P becomes W and X becomes E, giving ALLISWELL, 'All is well'.",
  "modern.hall.38.p6": "Hall says other settings of the wheel give other letters, but only one makes sense, and the decoder must keep trying until he finds an intelligible message. Bacon varied the biliteral cipher in many ways, with probably a score of systems in the Shakespeare Folio alone, some perhaps forever unsolvable; sometimes the a's and b's must be swapped, the message read backwards, or only every other letter counted. Other literal ciphers substitute letters by a set sequence, as with two alphabets written one above the other, or hide the message in the text with unimportant words inserted in a set order. Acrostic signatures also belong here, from simple first letters of lines to letters scattered through whole chapters, and two alchemical cryptograms he reproduces use the first letter of each word. Any cipher built on letters is a literal cipher. Second is the pictorial cipher, any picture with a meaning beyond the obvious, common in Egyptian symbolism and early religious art; alchemical and Hermetic diagrams are always pictorial ciphers. A more technical form hides words or letters in the number of stones in a wall, the spread of birds' wings, ripples on water or the length of shading lines, read with a measuring scale, and the shape of a building, the bars of a window, the folds of a robe or the posture of a body could all encode characters for those who knew the code.",
  "modern.hall.38.p7": "Hall says initials were hidden in architectural arches: on the title page of the third edition of Montaigne's Essays two arches form a B and a broken arch an F. Pictorial ciphers sometimes carry their own key, a figure pointing to the starting point or holding a tool that shows the measuring system, and artists deliberately drew figures wrongly, with a hat on backwards, a sword on the wrong side or a shield on the wrong arm. The much-discussed extra finger on the Pope's hand in Raphael's Sistine Madonna and the sixth toe on Joseph's foot in his Marriage of the Virgin are such hidden ciphers. Third is the acroamatic cipher: parables and allegories, which abound in the religious and philosophical writings of every nation and survive translation and reprinting unchanged. They are pictorial ciphers drawn in words, used since antiquity to teach moral truths attractively; the Old and New Testaments, Plato, Aristotle, Homer, Virgil, Apuleius and Aesop all hide the deepest truths of mystical philosophy this way. This is the subtlest cipher because a parable can be read in several ways. Bible students settle for the moral reading, forgetting that every parable has seven interpretations, of which only the seventh and highest is complete. The world's creation myths are such ciphers, and the gods of the pantheons are letters of a divine alphabet: the initiated few understand it, while the many worship its letters as gods.",
  "modern.hall.38.p8": "Hall describes the fourth kind, the numerical cipher, in which numbers stand for letters, words or whole ideas and are usually read with special tables. The Old Testament's number ciphers are so complicated that few rabbinical scholars have tried them; Kircher describes Arab Qabbalistic methods, and much of the Pythagorean mystery was hidden by substituting numbers for letters. In the simplest form A is 1, B 2 and so on, with I and J both 9 and U and V both 20, so 'yes' is 23-5-18, which sums to 46; the alphabet can be reversed, or meaningless numbers inserted, as in 23-16-5-9-18. In this system 138 equals 'Note carefully', so line, page or paragraph 138 of a book might hold a message, and there are many far harder systems. Authors based ciphers on their names, Bacon repeatedly using 33. Page numbering is often involved, and faulty numbering, usually blamed on carelessness, can hide secrets. In Baconian ciphers pages ending in 89 seem important: in the 1623 Folio Comedies page 89 has a '9' from a smaller font, page 189 is missing with two pages numbered 187, page 188 has a half-size second 8, and page 89 of the Histories is missing; several of Bacon's books show similar errors, often involving page 89.",
  "modern.hall.38.p9": "Hall says some numerical ciphers are read by counting every tenth, twentieth or fiftieth word, or irregularly, counting 100 for the first word, 90 for the second, and so down to 10 before starting again. Fifth is the musical cipher. John Wilkins, later Bishop of Chester, in an anonymous essay of 1641, Mercury, or the Secret and Swift Messenger, drawn largely from Trithemius and Selenus, described substituting musical notes for letters so that musicians could converse by playing. Musical ciphers can be extremely involved: a cipher can be hidden in an existing piece without changing it, in the flags on the notes, or by matching notes' sounds to similar syllables. Some of Bacon's musical compositions survive and might hold ciphers, since he surely knew how they were made. Sixth is the arbitrary cipher, swapping letters for invented symbols, which is too easy to break to be popular; Albert Pike describes one based on the parts of the Templar cross. Such alphabets are defeated by letter frequency: Hall cites Poe and other authorities for the order of frequency in English, with E most common, and notes that only A, I and O can stand alone as words, so a single separated character must be one of these, as Poe shows in The Gold Bug.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The two frequency lists are garbled in the e-text (for example the stray 'OL' in Poe's list), so their exact order cannot be relied on.",
  "modern.hall.38.p10": "Hall says arbitrary ciphers are made harder by not dividing them into words and by using several symbols for each letter, which defeats frequency counts; the more symbols per letter, the harder to decipher. Ancient secret alphabets are fairly easy to break with a frequency table, knowledge of the language, patience and ingenuity. Seventh is the code cipher, the newest form, best known as the Morse code; it can be hidden in a document by treating full stops and colons as dots and commas and semicolons as dashes, and businesses use codes readable only with a private code book, far more widely than most people suspect. There are other systems using machines, colours or objects for words and ideas, but the ancients and medieval philosophers seldom used them, so they matter little for religion and philosophy. Medieval mystics borrowed the language of chemistry, biology, astronomy, botany and physiology to hide the secrets of the soul, and such ciphers can only be read by those versed in the philosophy behind them; much about man's invisible nature is hidden in what look like chemical experiments or scientific speculation. Hall urges every student of symbolism to learn the principles of cryptography, which aids research and sharpens the mind, since discrimination and observation are essential to the seeker and nothing trains them better.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig1": "The caption, for the title page of Selenus's Cryptomenytices, says this remarkable book on ciphers appeared in 1624, a year after the First Folio, during the Rosicrucian controversy. Its title promises the cryptography of Gustavus Selenus in nine books with an explanation of the Steganography of Trithemius, and its author was believed to be Augustus, Duke of Brunswick, but Hall says its emblems prove the Rosicrucians were behind it. At the bottom a nobleman, perhaps Bacon, puts his hat on another man's head; the lights in the top oval may be beacons, a pun on Bacon. In the left panel a nobleman, possibly Bacon, hands a paper to a shabby man carrying a spear; on the right the same man appears as an actor in spurs blowing a horn. Hall says the spear-carrier and the actor blowing his own horn suggest much, since 'spear' ends the name Shakespeare.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig2": "The caption, for a sample of biliteral writing, asks the reader to compare each letter of the sentence with the two letter-forms in Bacon's table. The 'd' in 'wisdom' has a large loop at the top, the 'd' in 'and' almost none; the 'i' in 'wisdom' is curved and the one in 'understanding' angular; the two 'r's in 'desired' clearly differ; one 'o' has a tiny extra stroke over the top; the 'a' in 'than' is thinner and more angular than the 'a' in 'are'; and the 'r' in 'riches' ends in a ball rather than a point. These small differences reveal the two alphabets used.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig3": "The caption, for the key from Bacon's De Augmentis Scientiarum, says that once the document to be deciphered has been reduced to its a and b equivalents, it is broken into groups of five letters and read using this table.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig4": "The caption says the diagram shows a wheel cipher, in which the smaller inner alphabet turns so that any of its letters can be set opposite any letter of the larger outer alphabet. In some wheels the inner alphabet runs backwards, but here both run the same way.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig5": "The caption, for a plate from Bacon's De Augmentis Scientiarum, says it shows the two alphabets Bacon designed for his cipher, each capital and small letter having two forms called a and b. In practice the differences were not always as visible as here, but two alphabets were always used, sometimes so alike that a strong magnifying glass is needed to tell them apart.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig6": "The caption, for a cipher James Campbell Brown reprints from Kircher in his History of Chemistry, says the capitals of the seven words in the outer circle, read clockwise, spell SVLPHVR; those of the second circle FIXVM; and those of the six words in the inner circle, rearranged, EST SOL. Together they read 'Sulphur fixum est sol', 'Fixed sulphur is gold'.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig7": "The caption, for a figure from the Geheime Figuren der Rosenkreuzer, says that starting from the word VISITA and reading clockwise, the initials of the seven words in the outer circle spell VITRIOL. It is a very simple alchemical riddle, but Hall says it reminds students of Hermetic, Rosicrucian, alchemical and Masonic works always to look for meanings hidden in parables and allegories or in arrangements of numbers, letters and words.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig8": "The caption, for a plate from Selenus's Cryptomenytices, says its three circles show the divine origins of justice, its universal scope and its human application, dealing with divine principles, worldly affairs and man. At the top sits Themis, spirit of law, with Juno, Minerva and Venus at her feet in robes patterned with geometric figures, and the axis of law links her throne to a throne of human judgment below, where a queen with a sceptre faces the winged Nemesis, angel of judgment. The second circle has a bright upper region of gods, good spirits and heroes, a dark lower region of lust, sin and ignorance, and a large middle region mixing both. In the inner circle is man, a tenfold being of nine parts in one body: thought, speech and action of spirit; memory, intelligence and will of intellect; and understanding, courage and desire of soul. It is divided into a Golden Age of spiritual truth, an Iron Age of spiritual darkness, and a Bronze Age between, itself in three parts, showing ignorant man ruled by force, half-awakened man ruled by law, and illuminated man ruled by love. Both circles turn on the axis of law, but its divine source, heavenly justice, is hidden in clouds.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text reads 'On the at the top of the picture sits Themis', with a word missing, probably 'throne'.",
  "modern.hall.38.fig9": "The caption, for a plate from Barrett's Magus, says early and medieval philosophers invented strange alphabets to hide their teachings, some still used a little in higher Masonic degrees. The most famous is the angelic writing, here called 'the writing called Malachim', whose figures are supposed to come from the constellations, and advanced students will meet many documents using them. The first alphabet has English equivalents beneath each letter, and the other three have their Hebrew equivalents above.",
  "modern.hall.39.p1": "Hall says several early Masonic manuscripts, such as the Harleian, Sloane, Lansdowne and Edinburgh-Kilwinning, claim that the craft of initiated builders existed before the Flood and helped build the Tower of Babel. He quotes a Masonic Constitution of 1701 on how the sciences began. Before the Flood, Lamech had two wives, Adah and Zillah. Adah bore Jabal and Jubal, and Zillah a son and a daughter, and these four children found the beginnings of all crafts: Jabal geometry, herding and building houses of stone and wood; Jubal music, songs, organs and the harp; the third brother, Tubal-cain, the smith's craft of iron and steel; and their sister Naamah weaving. Knowing that God would punish sin by fire or water, they wrote their sciences on two stone pillars so they could be found after the Flood, one of marble, which cannot burn, and one called 'Laturus', which Hall guesses was brass, which cannot drown. The Constitution adds that Hermes later found one of these pillars and passed its secrets on to mankind.",
  "modern.hall.39.p2": "Hall says Josephus writes that Adam warned his descendants of a coming flood, so Seth's children set up two pillars, one of brick and one of stone, inscribed with the keys of their knowledge. Enoch, whose name means 'the initiator', is evidently a personification of the sun, since he lived 365 years. He built an underground temple of nine vaults one below another, placing in the deepest a triangular gold plate bearing the ineffable Name of God; some accounts say he made two golden deltas, putting the larger on a white cubic altar in the lowest vault and giving the smaller to his son Methuselah, who built the brick chambers to the plan God showed Enoch. The vaults stand for the nine spheres of the ancient Mysteries and the nine layers of the earth through which the initiate must pass to reach the flaming spirit at its centre. In Masonic tradition Enoch, fearing the Mysteries would be lost in the Flood, put up the two columns, engraving the secret teaching in symbols on the metal one and on the marble one an inscription saying a priceless treasure lay in a vault nearby. Enoch was then taken up from Mount Moriah. The vaults were lost, until ages later another builder, an initiate of Enoch's order, found them while laying the foundations of another temple to the Great Architect of the Universe.",
  "modern.hall.39.p3": "Hall says John Leland, sent by Henry VIII to save important books and manuscripts from the dissolved religious houses, copied a set of questions and answers about Masonry attributed to Henry VI. Asked how Masonry came to England, it says Peter Gower, a Greek, travelled for knowledge in Egypt, Syria and every land where the Phoenicians had planted Masonry, entered all the lodges, settled in Greater Greece, formed a great lodge at 'Groton' and made many Masons, some of whom took Masonry to France, whence it reached England. Hall says Peter Gower is plainly Pythagoras and Groton Crotona, linking the Greek Mysteries to medieval Masonry. William Preston, commenting, tells from Pliny how Anaxarchus, imprisoned to force out his secrets, bit off his tongue and spat it at the tyrant Nicocreon of Cyprus, and how the Athenians honoured a tongueless bronze statue as a sign of sacred secrecy; the manuscript also says Masonry began in the East and brought civilization westward. Hall then explains the seven liberal arts as Masonic symbols: grammar teaches noble expression of one's thoughts; rhetoric how to veil ideals in figures of speech; logic to order the mind; arithmetic universal order, quantity and proportion; geometry the mathematics of form and organization; music that the universe rests on celestial harmony; and astronomy the vastness of time and space and man's place before the power that drives the stars. A Mason who knows them, Hall says, can face almost any problem.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p1": "Hall says the most famous ancient guild of craftsmen was the Dionysiac Architects, made up solely of initiates of the cult of Bacchus-Dionysus and devoted to building and decoration. Thought to hold a sacred knowledge of architecture, they designed and built public buildings and monuments and were honoured as the master craftsmen of the earth. Since Dionysus was the patron of theatre, which began with dances in his honour, they specialized in theatres, always placing an altar in the orchestra; Hall adds that Aeschylus, accused by an angry audience of revealing a Mystery secret while acting in his own play, had to take refuge at the altar of Dionysus. They guarded their secrets so well that only fragments survive. John A. Weisse sums up what is known: they appeared no later than 1000 BC, had special privileges, secret signs of recognition and private bonds, the richer were bound to support the poorer, they lived in communities under a Master and Wardens, held an annual festival and were highly respected. They are said to have been employed by Solomon at the request of Hiram of Tyre and on the Temple of Diana at Ephesus, had ways of communicating across the known world, and doubtless gave rise to the travelling masons' guilds of the Middle Ages.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text says they erected 'an altar to Aeschylus ... that while appearing', where some words have dropped out; the altar was presumably to Dionysus, with the story of Aeschylus as a separate remark.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p2": "Hall says the Dionysiac Architects spread through Asia Minor to Egypt and India, round the Mediterranean, and with Rome into Central Europe and England, building the grandest and most lasting buildings of Constantinople, Rhodes, Athens and Rome. One of them was Vitruvius, author of the Ten Books on Architecture, which hints at their philosophy of symmetry drawn from the proportions of the human body. Hall quotes him: a temple's design depends on symmetry, which comes from proportion, the agreement of the measures of the parts with each other and with a standard unit, as in a well-shaped man. In the human body the face from chin to hairline is a tenth of the height, as is the hand from wrist to fingertip; the head from chin to crown is an eighth; from the top of the chest to the hairline is a sixth, and from mid-chest to the crown a quarter. The face divides into three equal parts, chin to nostrils, nostrils to brows, and brows to hairline. The foot is a sixth of the height, the forearm and the breadth of the chest each a quarter, and the other members have their own proportions, by which the great painters and sculptors of antiquity won lasting fame.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p3": "Hall calls the Dionysian buildings 'sermons in stone': even the uninitiated were overwhelmed by the majesty of their harmony of pillars, spans, arches and domes. The builders believed that by varying size, material, type, arrangement, decoration and colour they could produce particular mental or emotional reactions. Vitruvius describes placing bronze vases about a room to change the tone of the voice, and each chamber of the Mysteries had its own acoustics: in one the priest's voice made the room shake, in another it was softened to the tinkle of distant silver bells, and in some underground passages the candidate could not hear himself shout, while a few feet further his softest sigh echoed a hundred times. Their highest aim was buildings that create the impression suited to their purpose; like the Pythagoreans, they thought combinations of lines and curves could induce any mood, and they tried to build in perfect harmony with the structure of the universe. They may have believed such a building, at odds with nothing real, would never decay, and would also become an oracle. Early magical works hint that the Ark of the Covenant was oracular because of chambers inside it shaped to catch and amplify the voices of the ages recorded in the astral light.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p4": "Hall says modern architects, ignorant of these subtleties, produce absurdities they would blush at if they understood their symbolism: phallic emblems decorate banks, offices and department stores, and Christian churches get Hindu or Muslim domes or are designed like synagogues or Greek temples of Pluto. To a trained psychologist such discord defeats much of a building's purpose. He quotes Vitruvius on propriety: a perfect style comes from building on approved principles, from rule, custom or nature. Temples open to the sky suit Jupiter the Thunderer, the Heaven, the Sun and the Moon, whose forms we see in the bright sky. Minerva, Mars and Hercules should have Doric temples, since their manly strength makes daintiness unfitting; Venus, Flora, Proserpine, the springs and the nymphs the Corinthian order, whose slender lines, flowers and volutes suit delicate gods; and Juno, Diana, Bacchus and similar gods the Ionic, a mean between the severity of Doric and the delicacy of Corinthian. Joseph Da Costa says the Dionysiac rites were based on astronomy, linked by the initiates to the builder's art; some documents hint that their great buildings were laid out on patterns from the constellations, a temple after Pegasus, perhaps, or a law court after the Scales. They had a code for recognizing each other in the dark, and their symbols and terms came mainly from architecture.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p5": "Hall says that though called pagans, the Dionysiac craftsmen built most of the early Christian abbeys and cathedrals, whose stones still carry their marks. Church facades show compasses, squares, rules, mallets and builders' tools worked into the decoration and even placed in the hands of saints and prophets. The old portals of Notre Dame, destroyed in the Revolution, held a great mystery: many Rosicrucian and Masonic emblems, and, according to alchemists who studied them, the secret of transmutation set out in their grotesque figures. The checkered floor of the Masonic lodge is the old tracing board of the Dionysiac Architects, and though modern Masonry is no longer a workmen's guild, its symbols keep the metaphysics of the society from which it presumably grew. He quotes Charles W. Heckethorn: Freemasonry is a tree with roots in many soils, so its language and ritual keep much of the sects and institutions it passed through, and in Masonry one meets Indian, Egyptian, Jewish and Christian ideas and the terms and symbols drawn from them.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The end of Heckethorn's sentence is garbled in the e-text ('terms therefrom the supreme ambition of their craft and symbols'), where a phrase from elsewhere seems to have crept in; the sense is presumably 'terms therefrom, and symbols'.",
  "modern.hall.39.s1.p6": "Hall says the Roman collegia of architects seem to have been a branch of the Ionian body, with the same principles and organization. The Dionysians may also have influenced early Islam, since some of their symbolism reached the dervishes; they once called themselves Sons of Solomon and used the Seal of Solomon, two interlaced triangles, which is often seen in mosques. The Templars are believed to have met them and brought their symbols and teachings into medieval Europe. But Masonry owes most of its architectural symbols and rites to the Dionysians, together with the legacy of the unfinished Temple of Civilization, an invisible structure that has fallen and been rebuilt many times on unmoving foundations, the true Everlasting House of which Solomon's temple was only a passing symbol. The Dionysians also had a speculative philosophy. Human society was to them a rough stone just cut from the quarry of Nature, which they worked to square, polish and carve into beauty. As mystics freed their souls by meditation and philosophers by thought, these craftsmen won release from the wheel of life and death by swinging their hammers in rhythm with the forces of the cosmos. They worshipped God as the Great Architect who cuts rough stones from space and trues them into universes, held constructiveness to be the soul's highest expression, and believed immortality came from joining Nature's creative work.",
  "modern.hall.39.s2.p1": "Hall divides the name Solomon into SOL-OM-ON, for light, glory and truth, so the Temple of Solomon is first 'the House of Everlasting Light', of which the stone temple on Mount Moriah is the earthly symbol. The Mysteries teach three Temples of Solomon. The first is the universe, where the sun (SOL) sits on a golden throne with the twelve signs as Fellow Crafts around him, lit by stellar, solar and lunar light; CHiram is the sun's active physical light, SOLomon its invisible spiritual and intellectual radiance. The second is the human body, the little house made in the image of the great one, as Paul asks, 'Know ye not that ye are the temple of God?'; Masonry in a temple of stone is only speculative, but in the living body it is operative. The third is the invisible 'Soular House', a supreme Masonic secret, hidden under the image of Paul's spiritual body or wedding garment, the High Priest's robes of glory, the Buddhist monk's yellow robe, and Pike's robe of blue and gold. The soul, made of a fiery golden substance, is cast by CHiram Abiff into the clay mould of the body and called the Molten Sea; it is built by three Master Masons, Wisdom, Love and Service, and when built by the law of life God's spirit dwells in it. Whoever can raise it is a Master Mason indeed. Waite agrees that Solomon's Temple is a symbol of the universe.",
  "modern.hall.39.s2.p2": "Hall says Solomon, the spirit of universal illumination, mental, spiritual, moral and physical, is personified as the king of an earthly nation. A real king may have built a temple, but whoever reads the story only as history will never clear the rubbish from the secret vaults, the rubbish being later symbols, allegories and degrees that do not belong to the original Mysteries. Albert Pike laments that the old phrases have lost their meaning: no one now journeys from the high place of Gibeon to the threshing floor of the Jebusite, sees the Master in blue and gold, pays apprentices at the columns, or passes from the square to the compasses. Hall says the rabbis held that Solomon was an initiate and his temple a house of initiation full of pagan and phallic emblems; its pomegranates, palm-capitalled columns, pillars, Babylonian cherubim and the layout of its rooms and curtains show it was modelled on the sanctuaries of Egypt and Atlantis. Isaac Myer quotes the pseudo-Clement saying God made man male and female, the male being Christ and the female the Church; the Qabbalists called the Holy Spirit the mother and Israel the daughter, and Solomon carved male and female figures on his temple walls to hint at this, such as the cherubim, though against the Law, standing for the upper, active, male principle and the lower, passive, female one made by it.",
  "modern.hall.39.s2.p3": "Hall says Masonry came to North Africa and Asia Minor from Atlantis, under the general name of sun and fire worship. The Mysteries did not die when Christianity triumphed, 'Great Pan did not die!', and Masonry proves it: they simply took on Christian symbols and carried the same truths under them, so Christian symbols can only be truly explained by pagan philosophy, and only the keys of the Egyptian, Brahmin and Persian hierophants open Wisdom's gates. Readers should therefore approach the allegory of the Temple and its builders reverently, knowing a royal secret lies beneath it. The Talmud says Solomon understood the Qabbalah, was an alchemist and necromancer who commanded demons and learned much from them and other unseen beings. S. L. MacGregor Mathers, translating the Key of Solomon, a book of the magical secrets Solomon supposedly used to summon spirits, which Frank C. Higgins says sheds light on Masonic initiation, sees no reason to doubt the tradition that Solomon wrote it, since Josephus mentions his magical works and Eastern traditions and the Arabian Nights confirm his skill. Hall quotes Josephus: God gave Solomon wisdom beyond the ancients and even the Egyptians, and taught him the art of expelling demons, incantations that relieve illness, and exorcisms that drive demons out for good, still in powerful use in Josephus's day.",
  "modern.hall.39.s2.p4": "Hall says medieval alchemists believed Solomon knew Hermes's secret of multiplying metals; Dr. Bacstrom wrote that the Universal Spirit, CHiram, helped Solomon build his temple, because Solomon knew how to command this invisible essence and make the unseen world supply him with vast amounts of gold and silver that people thought were mined. The mysteries of Islam, Hall says, are now kept by the dervishes, who have given up the world and passed a thousand and one days of temptation. Jalal ad-Din Rumi, the great Persian Sufi poet, is said to have founded the Mevlevi order, the 'dancing dervishes', whose whirling outwardly represents the motions of the heavenly bodies and inwardly sets up a rhythm that awakens centres of spiritual consciousness in the dancer's body. He quotes L. M. J. Garnett: according to the mystical tradition there are always holy men on earth in close communion with God. The highest is the 'Axis' or 'Pole' of his age, with two 'Faithful Ones' on his right and left, below them four 'Intermediate Ones', then five 'Lights', seven 'Very Good' and forty 'Absent Ones' or 'Martyrs'; when an Axis dies, the Faithful One on his right succeeds him, and these 'Lords of Souls' hold a spiritual authority over mankind far beyond that of earthly rulers. Hall adds that the Axis mingles with people unknown, and by tradition likes to sit on the roof of the Kaaba.",
  "modern.hall.39.s3": "Hall says the inner sanctuary of Freemasonry is adorned with the wisdom of a thousand ages: a hundred religions have brought gifts to its altar and countless arts and sciences have fed its symbolism. It is a worldwide university teaching the liberal arts of the soul, and those who understand it realize that it hides the long-lost secrets every people has sought. He quotes Albert Pike's letter to Robert Freke Gould: Masonry grew in his vision into something majestic and mysterious, like the lonely pyramids. In its symbols and spirit of brotherhood it is older than any living religion, holding symbols and teachings older even than Zarathustra, and it was a sublime yet pitiful sight to see this ancient faith holding out its once eloquent symbols to the world, asking in vain for an interpreter; its true greatness lies in owning these symbols, which are its soul. Hall ends that though Thebes and Karnak are ruins, the Brahmins' rock temples deserted and the oracles silent, the philosophy of Egypt, the wisdom of the Vedas and the glory of Greece live on, and in Masonry's temple Zoroaster, Hermes, Pythagoras, Plato and Aristotle live again, so that the candidate feels himself face to face with these illumined teachers.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Pike's phrase 'In its symbolism, which and its spirit of brotherhood are its essence' is garbled in the e-text; the sense is that symbolism and brotherhood are Masonry's essence.",
  "modern.hall.39.fig1": "The caption, for a drawing from Cesariano's edition of Vitruvius, quotes Vitruvius: since nature made the human body with its members in proportion to the whole, the ancients rightly required that in perfect building each part be in exact proportion to the whole, and took special care with temples, where merits and faults last forever. If number came from the human fingers and the body's parts correspond to the whole by a standard unit, we must respect those who built temples so that every part and the whole design harmonize. Hall adds that some believe St. Paul was initiated into the Dionysiac Mysteries, since in 1 Corinthians 3:10 he calls himself 'a wise master-builder' who laid the foundation for others to build on, and that Cesariano's two diagrams, as survivals of the Dionysiac rites, are invaluable to the modern mystic architect.",
  "modern.hall.39.fig2": "The caption, for a drawing from Cesariano's edition of Vitruvius, says it shows Plato's Word, crucified in space before the world was founded. The anonymous author of The Canon says the Logos or world-soul of Plato, the Greek Hermes and the Gnostics' Christ are all the same as the Hebrew Adam Kadmon, second person of the Kabbalistic triad, and that the Cyllenian Hermes described by Hippolytus matches the figure in Cesariano so exactly that they may be identical; noting the vine leaves in its hair, he links it to Dionysus and calls it a survival of the Greek cosmic deity, crudely copied by medieval draughtsmen but still recognizable. Agrippa's De Occulta Philosophia has similar figures, with equally inadequate keys, and says man, as the little world, contains all numbers, measures, weights, motions and elements. Hall says the secret doctrine of Masonry, like that of the Dionysiac Architects, is above all the effort to measure the proportions of the microcosm, so as to achieve the craft's highest aim, the making of a perfect man.",
  "modern.hall.40.p1": "Hall says the true story of Jesus has never been told, in the Gospels or the Apocrypha, apart from stray hints in the early Fathers, and is kept in the secret vaults of the 'Houses of the Brethren'. Some Templars, initiated by the Druzes, Nazarenes, Essenes, Johannites and other sects in remote parts of the Holy Land, heard part of it, and this knowledge was a main reason for their destruction. The early Fathers contradict each other so badly that for five centuries they clearly had little more than folklore and hearsay to go on. To the credulous there are no problems, but the fact-seeker meets many, for example Jesus's age. The usual view is that he was crucified at thirty-three, three years after his baptism. But about AD 180 Irenaeus, Bishop of Lyons, in Against Heresies, said on the Apostles' authority that Jesus lived to old age. He attacks those who claim Jesus preached one year and died at thirty, saying they rob him of the more honourable age of a teacher, for how could he teach without having reached a master's age? Luke says he was 'beginning to be about thirty' at his baptism; youth lasts to forty, and from forty or fifty a man declines into the old age which the Lord had while teaching, as the Gospel and the elders in Asia who knew John testify, John having lived until Trajan's time. Some of them saw other apostles too and heard the same, and Irenaeus asks whom we should believe, such men or Ptolemaeus, who never saw an apostle.",
  "modern.hall.40.p2": "Hall says Godfrey Higgins remarks that this passage luckily escaped the censors who deleted such statements to make the Gospels consistent, notes that the crucifixion was a disputed question among Christians even in the second century, and calls Irenaeus's evidence unimpeachable. Irenaeus was contradicting a current claim that Jesus's ministry lasted only one year, and writing within eighty years of St. John's death he should have known. If the disciples said Jesus lived to old age, Hall asks why the mystical number 33 was chosen for his life, and whether its events were changed to fit the pattern of the earlier saviour-gods. That early Christians used such parallels to convert Greeks and Romans is clear from Justin Martyr, who tells the pagans in his Apology that saying the Word was born without sexual union, was crucified, died, rose and ascended is nothing different from what they believe of Jupiter's sons; that a special birth of God's Word should not surprise those who call Mercury the angelic word of God; and that if Jesus was crucified, he is in this too like Jupiter's reputed sons who suffered. Hall concludes that the first Christian missionaries were far more willing to admit the likeness between their faith and paganism than their successors.",
  "modern.hall.40.p3": "Hall says some suggest that two or more teachers named Jesus, Jehoshua or Joshua lived in Syria then and their lives were confused in the Gospels. Bernard H. Springett, a Masonic author, quotes an early book he could not name because it belongs to a sect's ritual: Jehovah prospered the Essenes for generations, then the chief of the angels raised up an heir, Joshua, child of Joseph and Mara, devout Essenes who kept apart from other people; in Nazareth he restored Jehovah's worship and many lost rites, and in his thirty-sixth year he was stoned to death in Jerusalem. In the last century, Hall says, many books have tried to fill out the Gospels. There are persistent rumours that Jesus studied in Greece and India, that an Indian coin was struck in his honour in the first century, that early Christian records exist in Tibet, and that a Buddhist monastery in Ceylon has a record of his staying and learning there. Proof that Jesus was an initiate of the pagan Mysteries would shock conservative believers. If Jesus was God incarnate, Hall asks, why does the New Testament call him a high priest 'after the order of Melchizedek', implying one of a line of equals or superiors? If the Melchizedeks were the priest-kings who ruled before temporal kings, Jesus was either one of these 'philosophic elect' or was trying to restore their rule, and Melchizedek too shared bread and wine as at the Last Supper.",
  "modern.hall.40.p4": "Hall says George Faber gives Jesus's original name as Jescua Hammassiah, and Godfrey Higgins found two early Jewish commentaries saying a man was healed 'in the name of Jesus ben Panther', making Panther the family name. This links Jesus to Bacchus, who was nursed by panthers and rides on one or in a chariot drawn by them. The monogram IHS, now read as 'Iesus Hominum Salvator', Jesus Saviour of Men, comes from the Greek letters YHS, whose number 608 stands for the sun, the secret name of Bacchus. Hall asks whether early Roman Christianity was confused with Bacchus worship because of their many parallels; if so, many New Testament puzzles would be solved. He thinks it quite possible that Jesus himself taught cosmic processes as allegories that were later confused with his own life. The Christos stands for the solar power revered by all ancient nations, and if Jesus taught it under the person of Christos, a god-man, he followed every earlier world-teacher. This god-man is the latent divinity in every person; man becomes divine only by 'at-one-ment' with it, and whoever finds it is 'saved'. As Attis, Adonis, Bacchus and Orpheus were probably illumined men later confused with the symbolic figures they created, so Jesus has been confused with the Christos he preached. The initiate's first duty was to free or 'resurrect' this inner Christos, and whoever did so was called a Christian, a 'christened' man.",
  "modern.hall.40.p5": "Hall says a deep pagan doctrine concerned a universal saviour-god who lifts regenerated souls to heaven through his own nature, which inspired the words 'I am the way, the truth, and the life'. By making Jesus and his Christos one person, Christian writers produced a patchwork that must be separated again to recover Christianity's meaning. In the Gospels the Christos is the perfect man who, having passed through the 'World Mystery' symbolized by thirty-three years, rises to rejoin his Father, and the story of Jesus, like the Masonic Hiram legend, is part of a secret ritual of the early Christian and pagan Mysteries. In the centuries before Christ the Mysteries' secrets had leaked to outsiders, so a few faithful philosophers re-clothed them in new symbols, preserved for centuries as Mystic Christianity. The Essenes are generally thought to have kept this knowledge and to have taught and initiated Jesus, in the same temple of Melchizedek where Pythagoras had studied six centuries before. They were devout men and women living simply, working by day and praying by night; Josephus praises them. Their name is thought to come from a Syrian word for 'physician', and they devoted themselves to healing body, mind and soul. Schure places their centres by Lake Moeris in Egypt and at Engaddi near the Dead Sea. Members served a year's probation, there were three degrees, and there were separate celibate and married communities.",
  "modern.hall.40.p6": "Hall says the Essenes avoided trade and city life, living by farming, keeping sheep, pottery and carpentry; Joseph is called both carpenter and potter, and apocryphal gospels tell of the child Jesus making clay sparrows that flew away when he clapped. They were well educated, sometimes tutors to Roman officers' children, and their many craftsmen and builders' tools make them seen as forerunners of Masonry, secretly building a spiritual temple for the living God. Like the Gnostics they believed in emanation and reinterpreted the Law of Moses by secret keys, so were Qabbalists, and awaited the promised Messiah. Joseph and Mary are believed to have been Essenes. Joseph was much older than Mary: the Protevangelium makes him a widower with grown sons, and in Pseudo-Matthew he calls Mary younger than his grandchildren. Mary was dedicated to God as a baby, with many miracles told of her childhood. When she was twelve, an angel told the high priest Zacharias in the Holy of Holies to gather the widowers, each with a rod, and she would marry the one God marked with a sign. Joseph's rod was half the length of the others and was left behind in the sanctuary; no sign came for the others, and Joseph, thinking himself too old, did not ask for his. An angel told the priest to return the short rod, and as Joseph took it a white dove flew from it onto his head, and the girl was given to him.",
  "modern.hall.40.p7": "Hall says the editor of The Sacred Books and Early Literature of the East notes how oddly the apocryphal books, especially one attributed to Thomas dating from about AD 200, treat the child Jesus, as almost an imp who curses and destroys those who annoy him. Such books were popular in the cruel Middle Ages and were written to outdo pagan miracle stories and frighten unbelievers; Hall says they have no basis in fact, and Christianity's miracles, once an asset, now alienate the intelligent. In the Gospel of Nicodemus the Roman standards bow to Jesus before Pilate despite the soldiers, and in the Letters of Pilate Caesar orders Pilate beheaded for killing a just man, but an angel promises the repentant Pilate that Christendom will remember him and he will be Christ's witness at the second coming. Hall calls these crusts that have formed on Christianity, defended by the popular mind; tradition holds some truth, but the details are badly distorted, and truth, like beauty, is best unadorned. Behind the fantasies the discerning glimpse a noble doctrine taught by a noble soul. Joseph and Mary, devout and holy, longing for a Messiah, obeyed the Essene high priest's instruction to prepare a body for a great soul, and so Jesus was born of an immaculate conception, 'immaculate' meaning clean, not supernatural.",
  "modern.hall.40.p8": "Hall says Jesus was raised and taught by the Essenes and initiated into their deepest Mysteries. Like all great initiates he travelled east, and spent his silent years learning the secret teaching he later gave the world. Completing the ascetic practices of his order, he attained 'the Christening', reunion with his spiritual source, and went out in the name of the One crucified since before the worlds, teaching disciples the secret doctrine partly lost from Israel's teachings. His end is unknown, but he probably suffered the persecution that falls on reformers of their age's ethics, philosophy or religion. He spoke in parables to the crowds and in loftier parables to his disciples. Voltaire said the church should canonize Plato, who as first teacher of the Christos mystery contributed more to its doctrines than anyone. Jesus taught that the lower world is ruled by a great spirit who shaped it by the Father's will, whose mind is both the world-mind and worldliness. So that men should not die of worldliness, the Father sent his highest power, the Divine Mind, which offered itself as a sacrifice, was broken and eaten by the world at a secret supper given to the twelve kinds of rational creatures, and so entered every living thing. Man can use it as a bridge to immortality: whoever lifts his soul to it and serves it becomes righteous and frees the Divine Mind to return in glory to its source. And the disciples, Hall says, came to say that Jesus himself was this Mind personified.",
  "modern.hall.40.s1.p1": "Hall says legend gives the body of the Christos, the spiritual law, into the keeping of two men barely mentioned in the Gospels, Nicodemus and Joseph of Arimathea, devout men chosen above all to guard his remains. Waite calls Joseph, one of the initiated brethren, 'the first bishop of Christendom'. As the visible power of the Holy See came through St. Peter, so the invisible body of the faith passed to the 'Secret Church of the Holy Grail' through Joseph, who held the symbols of the covenant, the ever-flowing cup and the bleeding spear. Supposedly sent by St. Philip, Joseph brought the relics to Britain after many hardships and founded Glastonbury Abbey, where his staff took root as the Glastonbury thorn that flowers twice a year. His end is unknown: some think he was taken up like Enoch, others that he was buried at Glastonbury, where many believe the Grail is hidden in a crypt. Hall doubts the recently found Glastonbury chalice is genuine. The Grail's origin is elusive. British folklore is full of magic cauldrons, cups and horns, and the earliest Grail is a horn of plenty that never empties and keeps anyone who sees it alive for eight days. Some see in it the cup of the rites of Adonis and Attis; cups were used in several Mysteries, and Bacchus was often shown as a vase or urn. In nature worship it is the bounty of the harvest, like Mercury's bottomless pitcher, and Hall says it would be wrong to give the Grail a purely Christian origin.",
  "modern.hall.40.s1.p2": "Hall says Merlin is a strange figure in the Arthurian cycle. One legend says that when Jesus came to free the world, the Devil, to undo his work, took the form of a dragon and overshadowed a young woman who had fled to sanctuary from the evil that destroyed her family. Her child Merlin had both human and demonic natures, but he turned to the light, keeping only two powers from his father, prophecy and miracle-working. Hall reads the demon father as an allegory: Merlin was a 'philosophical son' of the serpent or dragon, as all initiates were, taking Nature as mortal mother and wisdom, the serpent, as immortal father; the confusion of serpent and dragon with evil comes from misreading Genesis. Merlin raised the infant Arthur, taught him the secret doctrine and probably initiated him into natural magic, and helped him become Britain's leading general, a rank later confused with kingship. After Arthur drew the sword from the anvil, proving his right to lead, Merlin helped him get Excalibur from the Lady of the Lake. Once the Round Table was founded, Merlin vanished, in one account into the air, where he still exists as a shadow that talks with mortals at will, in another into a stone vault he sealed from inside.",
  "modern.hall.40.s1.p3": "Hall says many legends of Charlemagne were later attached to Arthur, famous for founding the Round Table at Winchester, of whose rites nothing reliable is known. One story says the table could grow or shrink to seat fifteen or fifteen hundred; most give twelve or twenty-four knights. Twelve stand for the zodiac and the apostles, and the knights' names and arms were on their chairs. Twenty-four split each sign into light and dark halves, since each sign rises for two hours a day, standing for the hours and the twenty-four elders of Revelation. At the centre was the rose of the Passion, for Christ 'rose' again, and there was the Siege Perilous, an empty seat for whoever achieved the Grail. Hall sees in Arthur the old cosmic myth: he is the sun, his knights the zodiac, his sword the sun's ray or the earth's axis, the table the universe and the Siege Perilous the throne of the perfect man. Historically, Arthur was Grand Master of a secret Christian-Masonic brotherhood of mystic knights, earning the post by drawing the sword, spirit, from the anvil of base metals, his lower nature. After his death at Camlann his Mysteries ended; his departure on a black barge, as in Tennyson, and Excalibur's return to the waters picture the coming of cosmic night. He was probably buried at Glastonbury. The Rosicrucians knew the true secret of the Arthurian and Grail legends, the most obvious yet most neglected key to the Christos mystery.",
  "modern.hall.40.fig1": "The caption, for a picture from Jennings's The Rosicrucians, says tradition makes Arthur king of Britain at fifteen in AD 516, soon founding the Order of the Round Table, whose knights met yearly at Caerleon, Winchester or Camelot at Pentecost. The brave came from all Europe, nobility, virtue and valour being required, and Arthur chose twenty-four of the best for his inner circle; they were so equal in dignity that a round table was needed so no seat was higher than another. The order probably had its own rites and symbols, but they are lost: Ashmole's plate of all the orders of knighthood leaves the Round Table's space blank. Hall says the main reason was Arthur's early death at Camlann in AD 542, in his forty-first year, where he killed his enemy Mordred but lost his own life and those of nearly all his knights.",
  "modern.hall.40.fig2": "The caption, for a picture from Ashmole's Order of the Garter, says Edward III probably founded the Order of the Garter in imitation of Arthur's Round Table, scattered after Camlann, and that the story of the Countess of Salisbury's garter is untenable. Its motto is 'Honi soit qui mal y pense', 'Shamed be he who thinks evil of it'. St. George is its patron because he stands for man's higher nature overcoming the dragon of his lower nature, and though he is supposed to have lived in the third century, Hall thinks he was probably borrowed from pagan mythology.",
  "modern.hall.40.fig3": "The caption, for a portrait from William Law's translation of Boehme's works, says Jakob Boehme was born in 1575 near Gorlitz and died in Silesia in 1624. With little schooling he was apprenticed to a shoemaker, became a journeyman, married and had four children. One day a stranger, poor but evidently wise and noble, asked the price of a pair of shoes; Boehme, afraid of displeasing his master, named the highest price the master could hope for, and the stranger paid and left. Down the street he called 'Jakob, Jakob, come forth', and when the frightened youth came out, he took his right hand, looked at him with eyes full of divine light, and told him he was small but would become great, a man the world would wonder at, and must be pious, fear God and study scripture, for he would suffer much poverty and persecution but God loved him. Deeply moved, Boehme searched ever harder for truth, and was rewarded with seven days in a strange state in which the mysteries of the unseen world were revealed to him. It is said he revealed the deepest secrets of alchemy to mankind. He died surrounded by his family, saying 'Now I go hence into Paradise.'",
  "modern.hall.40.fig4": "The caption, for a figure from Audsley's Handbook of Christian Symbolism, says the golden halos of pagan gods and Christian saints mean both that they are bathed in the sun's glory and that a spiritual sun within them radiates around them. A nimbus of straight rays is solar, one of curved rays lunar, and one combining both blends the two principles; the round nimbus is solar and male, the lozenge-shaped vesica piscis lunar and female, as in the round and pointed windows of cathedrals. There is a whole science in the shape, colour and ornament of halos: a canonized saint usually has a plain gold ring, while God the Father and the Son have richer ones, usually bearing a cross with only three arms showing.",
  "modern.hall.41.p1": "Hall retells a legend from the Golden Legend of Jacobus de Voragine. Feeling his death near, Adam asked his son Seth to go to Eden and ask the guardian angel for the Oil of Mercy God had promised. Seth did not know the way, but Adam told him to go east and follow the path where no grass had grown since he and Eve were driven out. Seth found the garden easily and was let in, and saw a great cross-shaped tree reaching to heaven, standing on the edge of a precipice above hell, with the body of his brother Cain trapped among its roots. The angel refused the oil but gave him three seeds of the Tree of Life, or some say of Knowledge. Adam was so glad that he no longer wished to live, and died three days later; the seeds were buried in his mouth as the angel instructed, and grew into a sapling with three trunks in one that drew up Adam's blood, so that his life was in the tree. Noah dug it up and took it into the Ark, and after the Flood buried Adam's skull under Mount Calvary and planted the tree on the top of Mount Lebanon.",
  "modern.hall.41.p2": "Hall continues the legend. Moses saw a being in the tree, the burning bush, and cut from it the rod that brought water from the rock; because he failed to call on the Lord when he struck the rock the second time, he could not take it into the Promised Land and planted it in the hills of Moab. David found the tree, and Solomon tried to use it as a pillar in his Temple, but it was always too long or too short, so it was thrown aside as a footbridge. The Queen of Sheba refused to step on it, knelt, prayed, and waded the stream barefoot instead, which so impressed Solomon that he had it covered in gold and set over the Temple door, until his greedy grandson stole the gold and buried the log. A spring rose there, the healing pool of Bethesda, whose angel guarded the tree, until it floated up and became a bridge between Calvary and Jerusalem, over which Jesus walked to his death. There being no wood on Calvary, it was cut in two to make the cross, which stood where Adam's skull was buried, and when Empress Helena found it, it was made of four kinds of wood, the elements, and healed all who touched it. Hall says reverence for the cross is not only Christian. Early Christians hid the pagan origins of their symbols, doctrines and rites by destroying or locking away other peoples' sacred books and tampering with ancient writers, removing compromising passages or adding material, and the supposedly forged passage about Jesus in Josephus is cited as an example.",
  "modern.hall.41.s1": "Hall says that before the Christian era some 700,000 of the most valuable books, on parchment, papyrus, vellum and wax, and tablets of stone, clay and wood, were gathered from the whole ancient world into special buildings at Alexandria. Three fires destroyed this treasure: what survived the fire Caesar lit to burn the fleet in the harbour was destroyed about AD 389 by Christians obeying Theodosius's edict to destroy the Serapeum, where the books were kept, including the library Mark Antony gave Cleopatra to replace what burned earlier. Blavatsky, in Isis Unveiled, reports a tradition of Palestinian rabbis and sages that not everything burned by Caesar, by the Christian mob in 389 and by the Arab general Amru was lost: during the struggle between Cleopatra and her brother in 51 BC, the Bruchion, with over 700,000 rolls, was being repaired and many of its most precious unique manuscripts were stored in a librarian's house, and in the hours between the burning of the fleet and the spread of the fire, librarians and hundreds of slaves saved the most valuable rolls. Hall thinks the rescued books probably lie buried in Egypt or India, and that until they are found the world will remain ignorant of many great truths, including the continuity of the pagan Mysteries in Christianity.",
  "modern.hall.41.s2": "Hall quotes Thomas Macall Fallow in the Encyclopaedia Britannica: the cross was probably an almost universal religious symbol before Christianity and among non-Christian peoples, often connected with nature worship. It is common among the American Indians of North, Central and South America; William W. Seymour says the Aztec rain goddess held a cross, and the Toltecs said Quetzalcoatl taught them the sign and ritual of the cross, so his staff resembled a crozier and his cloak was covered with red crosses. The Japanese and Chinese revere it, and in both languages the character for ten is a cross; to the Pythagoreans ten, written as an X, was the holiest number. The Buddhist wheel of life is two crosses laid over each other, and its eight points survive in the Templar cross, which Hall calls essentially Buddhist. India keeps the cross in carving, painting and architecture, many temples, like Christian churches, standing on cross-shaped plans. Tibetan mandalas lay out heaven as a cross with a demon king at each of four gates; a very old cross was found in the Elephanta caves near Bombay; and crosses were favourite motifs in Chaldea, Phoenicia, Egypt and Assyria. Eleusinian initiates were given a cross to wear on a cord, and to the Rosicrucians, alchemists and Illuminati the cross meant light, since each letter of LVX can be made from parts of a cross.",
  "modern.hall.41.s3": "Hall says there are three main forms of cross. The first is the Tau, more correctly Tav, shaped like a T with a crossbar on an upright. The Druids' god Hu was represented by an oak cut off some feet above the ground with its top laid across in this shape, and the Egyptian form may have come from the spread horns and upright face of a bull or ram, Taurus or Aries. It is also called the hammer cross, since held by its foot it looks like a mallet, and in a Qabbalistic Masonic legend Tubal-cain gives CHiram Abiff a Tau-shaped hammer; it survives in Masonry as the T-square and seems to be the oldest form of cross. It was marked on the foreheads of initiates of Mithras, pressed to the lips of a king being initiated in Egypt, and tattooed on candidates in some American Indian Mysteries. To the Qabbalist it stood for heaven and the Pythagorean tetractys, and Pike says the caduceus of Hermes grew out of it.",
  "modern.hall.41.s4": "Hall says the second form is the Tau topped by a circle, often an upright oval, the Crux Ansata or cross of life, which was the key to the ancient Mysteries and probably the origin of St. Peter's golden key of heaven. In the Egyptian Mysteries the candidate held it over his head through real and imagined dangers, and the powers of darkness drew back from it, recalling 'In hoc signo vinces'. Payne Knight notes its likeness to the sign of Venus and says such crosses were found in the Serapeum when Theodosius destroyed it, and Christian scholars of the time said they meant the life to come. Le Plongeon says this 'key of the Nile', whole or as a plain Tau, adorned the breasts of statues at Palenque, Copan and elsewhere in Central America, always linked with water: among the Babylonians the sign of the water gods, among the Scandinavians of heaven and immortality, among the Maya of renewal and freedom from bodily pain. Goblet d'Alviella notes that the Egyptians' Nilometer, used to measure the Nile flood, looked like it, which may be why it meant life, since Egypt's crops depended on the flood. In papyri it comes from the mouths of kings pardoning enemies, it was buried with them as a sign of the soul's immortality, and gods carried it as a sign of their life-giving power; the Cairo Museum has many crosses of every kind.",
  "modern.hall.41.s5.p1": "Hall says the third form is the familiar Roman or Greek cross associated with the crucifixion, though the actual cross probably looked different. There are endless variants, and secret orders used compound crosses such as the triple Tau of the Royal Arch and the double and triple crosses of Masonic and Catholic symbolism. To Christians the cross means both their Redeemer's death, through which they share God's glory, and humility, patience and life's burdens, so it is oddly a symbol of both life and death. Hall thinks the Persians, Greeks and Hindus probably saw it as the equinoxes and solstices, where the sun is symbolically crucified at certain seasons, and the many saviours who personify the sun show that the cross is an astronomical element in pagan allegory. Le Plongeon thought veneration for it came partly from the rising of the Southern Cross just before the yearly rains on which crops depended, so it promised life. The four arms also stood for the four elements, with a creature at each end: a bull for earth, a scorpion, serpent or eagle for water, a lion for fire, and a winged human head for air. Pike notes that the four letters fixed to the top of the cross are the initials of Hebrew words for the four elements, water, fire, air and dry earth.",
  "modern.hall.41.s5.p2": "Hall says that because a cube unfolds into a cross, the cross is linked with the earth; a cross in a circle, usually the sign of the planet Earth, really stands for the compound element earth, made of the four triangles of the elements. Alchemists often used a cross for the four ingredients of the Stone, salt, sulphur, mercury and Azoth, and the cross of the four cardinal points has its own secret meaning, as Masonic parties of three still go to the four quarters in search of the Lost Word. The material of a cross mattered: gold meant illumination, silver purification, base metal humiliation, and wood aspiration. Because many peoples prayed with arms spread, the cross came to stand for the human body, whose four main systems, bones, muscles, nerves and arteries, are thought to have shaped its symbolism, especially as the spinal nerves cross at the base of the spine, a reminder that 'Our Lord was crucified also in Egypt'. Man has four vehicles through which the spirit meets the world, the physical, vital, emotional and mental natures, each linked to an element, and the four Qabbalistic creatures assigned to them made the cross a symbol of man's compound nature.",
  "modern.hall.41.s6": "Hall says countless saviours have died for and by the hands of humanity and through their deaths interceded for their killers, and the redemption of the world by a god-man's blood is a central teaching of many religions. Nearly all these stories go back to sun worship, for the sun dies every year for all creatures and rises again from winter's tomb. The doctrine of crucifixion comes from the secret tradition of the Ancient Wisdom and reminds us that man's divine nature is always crucified on his animal body. Some pagan Mysteries crucified the candidate on a cross or laid him on a cross-shaped altar during initiation. It is claimed that Apollonius of Tyana was initiated in the Great Pyramid, hung on a cross until unconscious and laid in the coffer for three days, while his soul was thought to pass into the realm of the immortals, the place of death; having conquered death by realizing that life is eternal, it returned, he rose from the coffer, and the priests hailed him as a brother back from the dead. This, Hall says, was in substance the teaching of the Mysteries.",
  "modern.hall.41.s7.p1": "Hall lists immortal figures linked, historically or allegorically, with a crucifixion: Prometheus, Adonis, Apollo, Attis, Bacchus, Buddha, Krishna, Horus, Indra, Ixion, Mithras, Osiris, Pythagoras, Quetzalcoatl, Semiramis and Jupiter. All gave their lives in service to humanity and most died as martyrs for human progress; the manner of their deaths has often been deliberately hidden, but most may have been crucified on a cross or tree. Prometheus, man's first friend, was fastened to the peak of the Caucasus, with a vulture tearing at his liver for ever, for bringing fire and immortality to man against Zeus's will, until Hercules freed him. J. P. Lundy, citing Dupuis, says Mithras was crucified and rose again on 25 March: in the Persian Mysteries the body of a seemingly dead young man was shown and then 'restored to life', his sufferings were thought to have saved his followers, and his priests kept watch at his tomb in darkness until midnight, when light burst out everywhere and the priest cried, 'Rejoice, O sacred initiated, your God is risen.' Some cases, like the Buddha's, must be allegorical, since his disciples recorded his actual death, but the very association of these heroes with death on a tree shows how universal the crucifixion story is.",
  "modern.hall.41.s7.p2": "Hall says India's equivalent of Christ is Krishna, who charmed birds and beasts with his flute, and who was supposedly crucified on a tree by his enemies, though great care has been taken to destroy the evidence. He quotes Louis Jacolliot's The Bible in India: knowing his hour had come, Krishna forbade his disciples to follow and went to bathe in the Ganges, plunged in three times, then knelt praying and awaiting death. One of those whose crimes he had exposed had followed with a strong band, shot him full of arrows, and hung his body in a tree for the vultures. When the people came, led by his beloved disciple Arjuna, to recover the body, it had vanished, presumably to heaven, and the tree was suddenly covered with great red flowers and filled the air with sweet perfume. Other accounts say he was tied to a cross-shaped tree before he was shot.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text has a stray word in the quotation ('had, with generation. a strong troop'); the sense is simply that the killer followed with a strong troop.",
  "modern.hall.41.s7.p3": "Hall says a plate in Moor's Hindu Pantheon of Krishna with nail wounds in his hands and feet, and one in Inman's Ancient Faiths of an Eastern god with what may be a nail hole in his foot, deserve unbiased study. He quotes Lundy's Monumental Christianity, which asks where the Persians got their idea of a saving crucifixion, seen on all their monuments, and, if from India, how it got there except from the common source of all pure religion. Lundy describes an extraordinary plate from Moor, which he thinks older than Christianity and calls 'a crucifixion in space', asking whether it is the Hindu victim-priest who offered himself before the worlds were, Plato's second God who stamped himself on the universe as a cross, or Plato's just man who would be scourged and finally crucified. Plato, he says, learned his theology in Egypt and the East and must have known of the crucifixion of Krishna, Buddha and Mithras; India had its mythical crucified victim long before Christianity as a type of the real one. Hall says the modern world misjudges the pagan gods, and the slander heaped on Krishna and Bacchus shows the persecution of immortal principles by those who missed the allegory. He asks who the rumoured crucified man of Greece was; Higgins thinks Pythagoras, whose real death Christian writers suppressed. He also asks whether Roman legions really carried standards bearing crosses with a crucified sun-man.",
  "modern.hall.41.s8": "Hall says one of the most remarkable crucified saviours is Quetzalcoatl, Central American god of the winds or the sun, whose name means feathered serpent. He seems to have come out of the sea bringing a mysterious cross, his garments decorated with clouds and red crosses, and great stone serpents were set up for him across Mexico. His cross became sacred to the Maya, whose 'angels' had painted crosses on their foreheads, and crosses were placed over the eyes of their initiates. When Cortes arrived bearing the cross, the natives took him for Quetzalcoatl returned, as he had promised to come back and redeem his people. Higgins writes that the Incas kept a polished marble or jasper cross three-quarters of an ell long in a sacred chamber, which the Spaniards decorated and placed in Cuzco cathedral; Mexican temples are cross-shaped and face the four directions; and the Codex Borgianus shows Quetzalcoatl nailed to a cross, sometimes with two thieves, once crucified in the heavens in a ring of nineteen figures, the Metonic cycle, with a serpent depriving him of his genitals, and once with a red cross and a black face and hands, so that if this came from the Nestorians, Higgins asks, why a black saviour? Hall adds that the crucified Word in space and the crucified dove are traces of pagan symbolism, and that a bird's outspread wings form a cross, one reason the Egyptians showed the soul as a bird over the mummy, holding the signs of life and breath.",
  "modern.hall.41.s9.p1": "Hall says the three nails of the Passion appear in the symbolism of many peoples, with many legends. One says there were four nails but a Hebrew Qabbalist and magician made one vanish just before it was driven, so the feet had to be crossed; another that one nail was beaten into a crown that is still a European imperial diadem; another that Constantine's horse's bit was a Passion nail. Hall thinks the nails were probably not iron, sharpened wooden pegs being usual then. Hargrave Jennings points out that the 'broad arrow' marking British royal property is the three nails grouped together, and that set point to point they form the Egyptian Tau. Frank C. Higgins reproduces the Masonic-style apron of a huge stone figure at Quirigua, Guatemala, whose central ornament is the three nails arranged like the broad arrow. Hall finds it significant that three nails crucified Christ, three murderers killed CHiram Abiff, and three wounds killed Prince Coh, the Mexican Osiris. C. W. King describes a Gnostic gem showing the Pleroma, the sum of the Aeons, as a man with a scroll whose left hand is three bent spikes or nails, the same symbol Belus holds on Babylonian cylinders, which the Kabbalists saw in the three points of the letter Shin and medieval mystics in the three nails of the cross; Jennings adds that the nail resembles an obelisk and that Shin's value, 300, is 100 for each spike.",
  "modern.hall.41.s9.p2": "Hall says the nails matter because esoteric systems teach that there are secret centres of force in the palms and the soles of the feet, and the driving of the nails and the flow of blood and water symbolized secret temple practices. Many Eastern gods have mysterious marks on their hands and feet, and the Buddha's footprints usually have a sunburst where the nail pierced Christ's foot. He quotes Franz Hartmann's notes on Boehme: the cross is earthly life, the crown of thorns the soul's suffering in the body but also the spirit's victory over darkness; the body is naked because the candidate for immortality must strip off all earthly desire; the nailing is the death of self-will, which must do nothing by its own power but serve as an instrument of the divine will. The letters INRI over the head mean above all 'In Nobis Regnat Jesus', 'Within us reigns Jesus', a meaning known in practice only to those who have died to the world of desire and come alive in Christ, in whom the kingdom of the divine love-will is established. Hall adds an interpretation that identifies the man Jesus with a person's own individual consciousness, which lives in the sense of separateness and must be sacrificed so that the universal consciousness is set free and the soul can be reunited with the Father.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig1": "The caption, for pictures from Berjeau's History of the Holy Cross, lists six scenes: Adam telling Seth how to reach Eden; Seth putting the three seeds of the Tree of Life under the dead Adam's tongue; the Queen of Sheba wading the stream rather than step on the sacred tree; the tree being placed over the door of Solomon's Temple; Christ crucified on a cross made from it; and the true cross being told from the other two by its power to bring a corpse back to life.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig2": "The caption says the Crux Ansata was carved on the back of at least one of the mysterious Easter Island statues, showing how far the symbol travelled. The statue, one of the smallest, was brought to London by a sailing ship and is now in the British Museum, with the Crux Ansata plainly visible on its back.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig3": "The caption says the Tau cross was the sign the Lord told the people of Jerusalem to mark on their foreheads, as Ezekiel relates, and was also put on people accused of crimes but acquitted, as a sign of their release.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig4": "The caption says both the cross and the circle were sexual symbols, since the ancients revered Nature's generative powers as expressions of God's creative power, and the Crux Ansata, joining the male Tau with the female oval, stood for generation.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig5": "The caption, for a portrait from Historia Deorum Fatidicorum, quotes Francis Barrett's Biographia Antiqua on Apollonius of Tyana's powers, after telling how he calmed a riot without speaking. He travelled widely and set himself up as a lawgiver, knew all languages without learning them, and could see events far away: when the Emperor Domitian was stabbed, Apollonius, standing in a distant market-place, cried out 'Strike! strike! 'tis time, the tyrant is no more.' He understood the language of birds, condemned dancing and similar amusements, urged charity and piety, travelled through almost every country, and died very old.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig6": "The caption, for a picture from the Codex Borgianus in Kingsborough's Antiquities of Mexico, quotes Lord Kingsborough, who tried to prove the Mexicans were descended from the Jews. He points to page 73 of the Borgian manuscript, which shows Quetzalcoatl both crucified and as if cut up for the cauldron, and asks whether anyone could help thinking that the 'Jews of the New World' applied to their Messiah not only the Old Testament prophecies about Christ but also many incidents from the Gospels.",
  "modern.hall.41.fig7": "The caption, for a drawing from Higgins's Anacalypsis, quotes J. P. Lundy: in some ways it looks like a Christian crucifix and in others it does not. The drawing, posture and nail marks in hands and feet suggest a Christian origin, while the seven-pointed Parthian crown, the absence of wood and of the usual inscription, and the rays of glory above point elsewhere; he asks whether it is the Hindu victim-man, priest and victim in one, who offered himself before the worlds were.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The e-text has the second set of features also 'point to some Christian origin', which contradicts the sentence; Lundy presumably meant some other than Christian origin.",
  "modern.hall.42.p1": "Hall says the Temple of Diana marked Ephesus as sacred to the Mysteries, for the Seven Wonders were built to mark repositories of hidden knowledge. Blavatsky calls Ephesus a focus of the universal secret doctrines, where Buddhist, Zoroastrian and Chaldean philosophy was refined in elegant Greek; its many-breasted, androgynous Artemis, patroness of the 'Ephesian writings', was conquered by Paul, but though converts claimed to burn their books on 'curious arts', enough remained to study once their zeal cooled. Many early Christian myths are set in Ephesus: that it was the Virgin Mary's last home, and that St. John's tomb was there. Legend says John chose his vault, went in alive, closed it behind him and vanished, and Ephesians said he would sleep there until Christ's return, the earth moving like bedclothes when he turned over. Hall calls the Apocalypse, attributed to John, the most criticized, most important and least understood Gnostic Christian book in the New Testament. Justin Martyr credited it to the apostle John, but its authorship was disputed from the second century; in the third, Dionysius of Alexandria and Eusebius are said to have attributed both Revelation and John's Gospel to Cerinthus, who borrowed the apostle's name, Jerome later questioned it, and Luther and Erasmus revived his doubts. Critical scholars now reject the view that it records John's vision on Patmos, and Hall sums up the more reasonable alternative theories.",
  "modern.hall.42.p2": "Hall gives three theories of the Apocalypse. First, its contents suggest it is a pagan text, one of the sacred books of the Eleusinian or Phrygian Mysteries, so its author, setting out Egyptian and Greek mysticism, must have been an initiate bound to write only in the Mysteries' symbolic language. Second, it may have been written to reconcile early Christian and pagan philosophy: when Christians tried to Christianize the pagans, the pagan initiates answered by paganizing Christianity, and they succeeded where the Christians failed, moving their work into the new faith and hiding the eternal truths under its symbols; the Apocalypse's blend of pagan and Christian symbols proves their activity. Third, some say it was written by unscrupulous members of a religious order to undermine the Christian Mysteries by satire, showing the new faith to be old pagan doctrine and turning its own symbols against it; for example, the star Wormwood that fell and poisoned mankind could be the Star of Bethlehem and the 'false' teaching of the church. Hall says this third theory has some popularity but the book's depth makes it the least plausible, and to those who can see through its symbols its inspired source needs no further proof.",
  "modern.hall.42.p3": "Hall says true philosophy cannot be confined by creed, so whether Revelation is pagan or Christian matters little; its value lies in its summary of the universal mystery, which led Jerome to say it has seven different interpretations. Modern theologians, untrained in ancient thought, find it a phantasmagoria and are tempted to doubt its inspiration. Hall sketches only its main features, suggesting that knowledge of the pagan Mysteries will fill the gaps. In the first chapter John describes the Alpha and Omega standing among seven golden candlesticks, surrounded by his flaming planetary regents, a figure summing up humanity's whole evolution, past, present and future. Rudolf Steiner writes that man's first earthly stages took place when the earth was fiery and his first bodies were made of fire, and that at the end he will radiate his inner being creatively by fire; the seer sees this archetype of evolving man on the astral plane, its beginning in the fiery feet, its end in the fiery face, and the creative word to be won in the fiery sword from the mouth. James Morgan Pryse sees the figure as a composite of the seven planets: the white hair of Kronos, the blazing eyes of Zeus, the sword of Ares, the shining face of Helios, the robe and girdle of Aphrodite, feet of mercury, the metal of Hermes, and a voice like many waters, recalling Selene, goddess of the waters.",
  "modern.hall.42.p4": "Hall says the seven stars in the right hand of the Alpha and Omega are the world's governors, and the sword from his mouth is the creative Word that slays the illusion of material permanence. He is also the hierophant of the Phrygian Mysteries, attended by seven lamp-bearing priests; reborn from darkness into wisdom, he says 'I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore.' In chapters two and three John gives messages to the seven churches of Asia, which are like the rungs of the Mithraic ladder: 'in the spirit', John rose through the seven planetary spheres to the edge of the Empyrean. An anonymous author describes the prophet's soul passing from the moon to Saturn, from Cancer, the gate of men, to Capricorn, the gate of the gods, until a new door opens in the highest heaven, the crystal sphere. In Eastern terms the churches are the chakras along the spine, and the 'door in heaven' is the brahmarandhra at the crown of the skull, Golgotha, through which the spinal fire escapes; Ephesus is the base centre. Steiner links the churches to the successive branches of the 'Aryan race', and they also stand for the seven Greek vowels, from Alpha to Omega. Some match Ephesus to Saturn, but Hall argues that since Ephesus was sacred to the moon goddess and the moon is the first sphere above the earth, the churches should rise from the moon to Saturn, beyond which the soul passes through the door in the Empyrean.",
  "modern.hall.42.p5": "Hall describes chapters four and five: God's throne, with twenty-four elders in white and gold crowns on lesser thrones around it, lightnings, thunders and voices coming from it, and seven lamps before it, the seven spirits of God. The One on the throne holds a book sealed with seven seals that no one is worthy to open, until a slain Lamb appears, which Hall glosses as Aries, chief of the zodiac signs, with seven horns, rays, and seven eyes, lights, takes the book, and is worshipped by the four beasts and the elders. The lamb was the universal symbol of Christ in the early church, and only after the Quinisext Synod of AD 692 was the crucified man substituted for the Agnus Dei. One writer notes that the lamb points to a Persian origin, since only the Persians showed the first sign as a lamb. Because pagans offered a lamb for sin, early mystic Christians took it as an emblem of Christ, the world's sin offering. Greeks and Egyptians put ram's horns on their gods, and Thor's hammer was made from ram's horns; the lamb was preferred to the ram for its purity and gentleness, and because if the Creator was Aries his Son was the little ram. The Mason's lambskin apron, worn over the part of the body linked with Typhon or Judas, stands for the purification of the generative powers needed for true spirituality. In the allegory the Lamb is the purified candidate, its seven horns the divisions of illumined reason and its seven eyes the chakras, the perfected senses.",
  "modern.hall.42.p6": "Hall describes the opening of the seals in chapters six to eleven. The first released a crowned rider on a white horse with a bow; the second a rider on a red horse with a great sword; the third a rider on a black horse with scales; the fourth Death on a pale horse, with hell following. Hall reads the four horsemen as the stages of life: birth, the white rider going out to conquer; impetuous youth, the red rider who takes peace from the earth; maturity, the black rider weighing everything in the scales of reason; and death, the pale rider. In Eastern philosophy they are the four yugas, the world ages, each ruling creation in its turn. Dupuis, commenting on Chrysostom, says each element was a horse named for its god. Jupiter's horse, fire and ether, was winged and swift, ran the widest circle round the others, shone with pure light and bore the sun, moon and stars on its body; Juno's, air, was black but bright where the sun touched it, for day and night; Neptune's, water, was heavy and ran a small circle; and Vesta's, earth, stood still champing its bit. They lived in harmony, as the philosophers said the world is kept by the concord of its elements, but Jupiter's horse once scorched the earth-horse's mane, and Neptune's sweat flooded Vesta's horse in Deucalion's flood; at last Jupiter's fiery horse will consume the rest, and the three lower elements, purified in the fiery ether, will come forth renewed as 'a new heaven and a new earth'.",
  "modern.hall.42.p7": "Hall continues. The fifth seal showed those killed for God's word; the sixth brought an earthquake, a darkened sun and a blood-red moon, and an angel sealed 144,000 of Israel on their foreheads to protect them in the great tribulation. Adding its digits gives 9, the number of man and of initiation, for whoever passes the nine degrees of the Mysteries receives the cross as the sign of his rebirth and freedom from his lower nature; the three added noughts raise the sacred 144 to the third sphere. At the seventh seal there was silence for half an hour, then seven angels blew seven trumpets, which Hall glosses as intoning the seven-lettered Name of the Logos, and disasters followed. The star Wormwood fell, meaning the ancients' secret doctrine given to men who profaned it and made God's wisdom destructive. Another falling star, the false light of human reason as opposed to the initiate's divine reason, was given the key to the bottomless pit, Nature, and loosed evil creatures. Then a mighty angel clothed in cloud, his face like the sun and his legs like pillars of fire, one foot on sea and one on land, the Hermetic Anthropos, gave John a little book to eat: the secret doctrine, food for the spirit, by which John, 'in the spirit', satisfied his soul's hunger.",
  "modern.hall.42.p8": "Hall describes chapter twelve: a woman clothed with the sun, the moon under her feet and twelve stars on her head, who is the constellation Virgo and the Egyptian Isis about to bear Horus, attacked by Typhon, who tries to kill the child destined to slay evil. The war in heaven refers to the destruction of a planet Hall calls 'Ragnarok' and the fall of the angels. The woman can also be the secret doctrine and her son the initiate born from the womb of the Mysteries; the dragon tried to rule mankind by destroying their mother, but the Mysteries were given wings and fled into the wilderness, and when the dragon sent a flood of false doctrine, the earth, oblivion, swallowed it and the Mysteries survived. In chapter thirteen a beast with seven heads and ten horns rises from the sea. Faber sees it as the Demiurge rising from the ocean of chaos, and Hall says calling the beasts evil comes from not knowing the ancient doctrines; astronomically the sea monster is Cetus, the whale. Ascetics who thought the universe an evil snare turned the world and its Maker into a beast of destruction. The seven heads are the seven stars of the Great Dipper, the Hindu Rishis or creative spirits, and the ten horns Faber links to the ten patriarchs. The number of the beast, 666, shows Qabbalism in the New Testament, and Hall introduces Kircher's table showing that the names Irenaeus gives for the Antichrist all add up to 666.",
  "modern.hall.42.p9": "Hall's table, from Kircher, gives the letter values of several Greek names of the Antichrist, each totalling 666. Pryse finds the Greek word for the lower mind is also 666, while the name Jesus is 888; adding the digits of 666, and then of the result, gives 9, the number of unregenerate man and of the path of his resurrection. In chapter fourteen the Lamb stands on Mount Zion, the eastern horizon, with the 144,000 bearing God's name on their foreheads, and an angel announces the fall of Babylon, the city of confusion or worldliness; those who do not overcome worldliness perish with it. One like the Son of Man, Perseus, rides a cloud and reaps the earth with a sickle, the Initiator freeing into reality the higher natures of those ripe for liberation; another, Bootes or Death, with the sickle of karma, casts those who lived by the false light into the winepress of wrath, the purgatorial spheres. In chapters fifteen to eighteen seven angels, the Pleiades, pour out seven vials, the loosed energy of the cosmic Bull, the last plagues. The 'harlot of Babylon', riding a seven-headed beast, may be a probably interpolated attack on Cybele or Artemis, the Great Mother, whose worship through feminine symbols led Christians to accuse pagans of worshipping a courtesan; the temptress, the animal soul, is shown as a pagan goddess.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The Greek table of names and letter values is garbled beyond use in the e-text; only the four totals of 666 survive clearly.",
  "modern.hall.42.p10": "Hall finishes his reading of Revelation. Chapters 19 and 20 prepare the marriage of the Lamb: the bride is the neophyte's soul, which gains conscious immortality by uniting with its spiritual source. The rider on the white horse, Faithful and True, is the illumined mind; Armageddon, fought on the plains of heaven like Ahura-Mazda's war with Ahriman, is the last struggle of spirit against flesh, and the judgment is the weighing of the soul borrowed from the Mysteries of Osiris. The dead rising from their graves and from the sea of illusion stand for the completion of human regeneration, and the lake of fire for the fiery sphere of the animal world into which those who fail initiation fall. Chapters 21 and 22 show the New Jerusalem: a perfect cube with 144 foundation stones in twelve rows, the Mason's trued ashlar, a small universe built on the pattern of the great one. Its twelve gates are the zodiac signs, its jewels their stones, its golden streets the paths of spiritual light. It has no temple and no sun or moon, for God and the Lamb are both. The city is the glorified initiate, who will finally be absorbed into God. The river of the Water of Life flows from the First Logos, and the Tree of Life, bearing twelve fruits, is also the year. Jesus calls himself the bright morning star, which Hall glosses as Venus.",
  "modern.hall.42.fig1": "The caption, for a figure from the works of Jacob Boehme (Behmen), explains the throne scene of Revelation. The crystal sea before the throne is the Schamayim, the living waters above the heavens. The four creatures, a bull, a lion, an eagle and a man, are the four corners of creation, and the many eyes covering them are the stars. Hall likens the twenty-four elders to the priests around the statue of Ceres in the Greater Eleusinian rite and to the Persian spirits of the hours of the day; as divisions of time, they throw down their crowns to worship the timeless Spirit in their midst.",
  "modern.hall.42.fig2": "The caption, for a plate from Klauber's Historiae Biblicae, lists the scenes of Revelation it shows. In front, John kneels before the Alpha and Omega among the seven lights, surrounded by flame and smoke. Above, the twenty-four elders bow before the Ancient One, from whose hand the Lamb takes the book with seven seals; the seven spirits of God are shown as cups pouring tongues of fire, and the four beasts kneel at the corners of the throne. Elsewhere are the seven angels with trumpets, the altar and the angel with the censer, the spirits of the winds, the woman clothed with the sun given wings to fly into the wilderness, the serpent hurled into the pit, and John receiving the little book to eat from an angel with legs like pillars of fire and a face like the sun. Hall says such scenes, staged as rituals and dramas, kept the secrets of the Phrygian Mysteries alive, and that when they were opened to everyone, with each soul made its own initiator, humanity received a gift it will only value as it grows more responsive to the spirit.",
  "modern.hall.42.fig3": "The caption, for another Klauber plate, describes its scenes: Babylon destroyed and the angel throwing a millstone into the sea; the rider Faithful and True casting the beast into the pit; the angel binding Satan for a thousand years; the Son of Man with a sickle reaping the world's harvest. In the centre stands the New Jerusalem with twelve gates and the mountain of the Lamb, and from the Lamb's throne flows the crystal river of the spiritual teaching, which makes immortal all who drink of it. John kneels on a cliff looking down at the city, the pattern of a perfect civilization still to come, with the throne of the Ancient One shining above it. Hall says there is a growing, unseen body of the spiritual elect who walk the earth as ordinary people but belong to another world; they are the builders of the New Jerusalem, their bodies its living stones, and through their work the golden age will return. So, he says, the wise hold that good and enlightened people will not go up to heaven but bring heaven down to earth.",
  "modern.hall.42.fig4": "The caption, for a figure from Solis's Biblische Figuren, reads the four horsemen of Revelation as the stages of human existence. In his first, spiritual state man is crowned; as he descends into experience he carries the sword; in physical life, his least spiritual state, he carries the scales; and through the 'philosophic death' he is released again into the highest spheres. Hall adds that in the Roman games the sun's chariot was drawn by four horses of different colours, so the horsemen may also be the sun's energy riding on the four elements through which it works.",
  "modern.hall.43.p1": "Hall opens his chapter on Islam by quoting the abusive postscript Alexander Ross attached to the 1649 English version of Du Ryer's French Koran, calling Mohammed an impostor and the Koran a hodgepodge of contradictions, blasphemy, fables and lies. Hall says the charge of blasphemy rested on Mohammed's denial that God had a Son, and answers it with a verse from the second sura: God owns east and west and is everywhere, and when he wills a thing he only says 'Be,' and it is. In other words, Hall says, Islam's God simply wills things into being, while Ross's God would have to follow the laws of human reproduction. Mohammed was born in Mecca around 570 and died in Medina in 632, the eleventh year after the Hegira. Hall quotes Washington Irving's account of the wonders said to attend his birth: his mother felt no pain, a light lit the country, the newborn proclaimed that there is no God but God, Lake Sawa shrank, the Tigris overflowed, the Persian king's palace shook, the sacred fire of Zoroaster went out after a thousand years, and the idols of the world fell down.",
  "modern.hall.43.p2": "Hall tells the tradition that while Mohammed was a small child the angel Gabriel, with seventy wings, opened his body, cleansed his heart of the black drop of original sin and put it back. As a young man Mohammed travelled with the Meccan caravans, lived among the Bedouins and learned the old traditions of Arabia, and on a journey with his uncle Abu Taleb met Nestorian Christians, from whom Hall thinks he learned much about Christianity. At about twenty-six he married his employer Khadijah, a wealthy widow some fifteen years older, whose ability and loyalty Hall credits with Islam's early success; Mohammed became rich and known in Mecca as 'the faithful and true.' Hall says he could have lived out his life as an honoured Meccan but gave up wealth and position for the God whose voice he heard while meditating in a cave on Mount Hira in Ramadan. Year after year he climbed there to pray for the pure religion of Adam to be restored. At last, in his fortieth year, a great light broke on him and he fainted; when he woke Gabriel stood before him holding a silk cloth written with mysterious characters, from which Mohammed drew the basic teachings of the Koran, and declared him the Prophet of the living God.",
  "modern.hall.43.p3": "Hall says Mohammed, afraid the vision came from evil spirits like those of the pagan magicians he despised, ran to Khadijah, who told him his good life would protect him. When Gabriel did not return he fell into such despair that he tried to throw himself from a cliff, and was stopped only when Gabriel reappeared and promised that revelations would come as they were needed. Hall says Mohammed seems to have had ecstatic swoons: when the suras were dictated he fell unconscious and sweated heavily even in the cold, dictating passages that trusted friends memorized or wrote down, and he later said his beard had gone white from the agony of inspiration. Hall argues that if the Prophet's writings were dismissed as an epileptic's hallucinations, his Christian critics would undermine their own faith too, since many early disciples and saints were known to have nervous disorders. Mohammed's first convert was his wife, then his own household, and Hall quotes Sir William Muir: it strongly supports Mohammed's sincerity that his first followers were upright people who knew his private life well and would have seen through any hypocrisy.",
  "modern.hall.43.p4": "Hall says Abu Bekr, a brilliant man and Mohammed's closest friend, was among the first converts and became leader of the faithful after his death; Abu Bekr's daughter A'isha later married Mohammed. Mohammed spread his teaching quietly among powerful friends, so that when he went public he already led a strong faction. Fearing him, the Meccans set aside the rule against bloodshed in the holy city and all agreed to share in killing him, but he left his friend Ali in his bed and fled with Abu Bekr to Yathrib, later Medina. This flight, the Hegira, begins the Islamic calendar. In the eighth year after it Mohammed took Mecca almost without bloodshed, circled the Caaba seven times, had its 360 idols cut down, cleansed the building and rededicated it to Allah, and pardoned his enemies. Mecca grew in power and became the centre of a great yearly pilgrimage that, Hall says, still crosses the desert more than sixty thousand strong. In the tenth year Mohammed led the farewell pilgrimage, sensing his death was near and wanting it to be the model for all that followed.",
  "modern.hall.43.p5": "Hall quotes Washington Irving on the farewell pilgrimage: knowing his life was ebbing, Mohammed preached often, from the Caaba pulpit or from his camel, telling his hearers he was only a man like them and might not meet them again; the heavens are said to have opened and God declared, 'This day I have perfected your religion,' and the crowd, and even the camel, knelt. In the seventh year after the Hegira someone tried to poison him at Kheibar; he realized it after the first mouthful, by taste or, as the faithful believe, by divine warning, but suffered from the poison for the rest of his life and blamed it for his final illness in A.H. 11. During that illness he visited a burial ground at night and said he had been offered the choice of living on or going to his Lord, and had chosen his Lord. After seeming to recover on 8 June he preached in the courtyard, then had to be helped into A'isha's house, where he spent his last two hours on a pallet on the floor, held in her arms. He prayed for help in the agony of death and whispered three times for Gabriel to come close. Hall ends with Carlyle's words that his last words were a prayer, the broken cries of a heart struggling in trembling hope towards its Maker.",
  "modern.hall.43.p6": "Hall says Mohammed was buried under the floor of the room where he died, and quotes A Dictionary of Islam on the tomb today: a green dome with a gilt crescent stands over the tombs of Mohammed, Abu Bakr and Umar, with a space kept for Jesus, who Muslims say will return, die and be buried at Medina; Fatimah may lie elsewhere in the building. The Prophet's body is said to lie on its right side facing Mecca, with Abu Bakr and Umar behind him. The Christian story that Muslims believed his coffin hung in the air has no basis in Muslim writings and may come from crude pictures sold to visitors. Hall says the charges of cruelty and lust made against Mohammed have no evidence behind them, and that close study shows his finer qualities. He quotes Carlyle: Mohammed was no sensual man; his household was the most frugal, his food barley bread and water, sometimes with no fire on his hearth for months; he mended his own cloak and shoes, fought and counselled among his people, and no emperor was obeyed as this man in a self-patched cloak. Hall says Washington Irving, faced with reconciling the real life with the absurd tales long accepted, also judged him fairly.",
  "modern.hall.43.p7": "Hall quotes Washington Irving: Mohammed's victories brought no pride, he kept the same simple ways at the height of his power as in hardship, and at the deathbed of his infant son Ibrahim he showed resignation to God and hoped to rejoin the child in Paradise; this selflessness and piety, Irving says, make his character hard to judge. Hall then lists details of Mohammed's habits from Muir's Life. A'isha said he mended his clothes, cobbled his shoes and helped with the housework, and loved sewing above all. He ate with slaves and servants, calling himself a servant; he hated lying most of all vices; he freed all his slaves before he died; and he never let his family use alms or tithes. He liked sweets and drank rainwater. He divided his time between God, his family and himself, later giving his own share to his people. He mostly wore white but also red, yellow and green, entered Mecca in a black turban, dressed plainly and prayed with his shoes on. He took great care of his teeth, asking for a toothpick when dying; tied a thread to his ring as a reminder; and threw away a fine gold ring when his followers began copying it.",
  "modern.hall.43.p8": "Hall says the most common charge against Mohammed is polygamy, and replies that anyone who thinks a harem incompatible with spirituality should also strike the Psalms of David and the Proverbs of Solomon from scripture, since Solomon's harem far outstripped the Prophet's. He denies that Mohammed taught that women have no soul and can reach heaven only through marriage. He quotes Mohammed Webb's paper at the 1893 World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago, which says the Koran puts women on full equality with men and the Prophet sometimes placed them above men, citing sura 33, verse 35: Muslims of either sex, believing men and women, devout, truthful, patient, humble, charitable, fasting and chaste men and women, and all who remember God often, are promised forgiveness and a great reward. Hall reads this as making heaven a matter of individual merit. On the day he died, Mohammed told his daughter Fatima and his aunt Safiya to work out their own acceptance with God, since he had no power to save them. He did not tell them to rely on their husbands' virtue.",
  "modern.hall.43.p9": "Hall says Mohammed is not responsible for the contradictions in the Koran, which was not compiled in its present form until more than twenty years after his death. He calls it largely a jumble of hearsay with flashes of true inspiration, and thinks the nobler parts are the Prophet's own while the rest are interpolations, some from misunderstanding and some forged to serve the ambitions of conquering Islam. He quotes Godfrey Higgins contrasting the Koran of Mohammed and the first four patriarchs with the Koran of the proud, conquering Saracens. Hall holds that Mohammed knew the secret doctrine at the heart of every great faith, and may have reached it by one of four routes: direct contact with the Great School in the invisible world; the Nestorian monks; a mysterious holy man who came and went while the suras were being revealed; or a decayed school in Arabia that, though fallen into idolatry, still kept the old wisdom. He suggests Islam's inner teaching may rest on the pagan Mysteries once performed at the Caaba, many of whose ceremonies survive. He notes the feminine principle in Islamic symbols: Friday is sacred to Venus, green, the Prophet's colour, belongs to the World Mother, and the crescent and scimitar may stand for the moon or Venus.",
  "modern.hall.43.p10": "Hall quotes Jennings, via Kenealy's Enoch, that the black stone at Mecca which the faithful kiss is a talisman said to bear an engraved figure of Venus with a crescent, and that the Caaba was once a pagan temple to Al-Uzza, that is, Venus. He quotes Sir William Jones calling Muslims a kind of unorthodox Christians: they believe in the immaculate conception, divine character and miracles of the Messiah, but deny that he is the Son and equal with God, regard the Christian view as blasphemy, and hold that Jews and Christians corrupted the scriptures. Hall says Muslims hold that a verse was cut from the Gospels in which Jesus foretold an apostle named Ahmed coming after him, and that the 'comforter' Jesus promised should be translated 'illustrious' and refers to Mohammed, not to the Pentecost flames. Asked to prove that the original Gospels said this, Muslims ask Christians to produce the original documents of their own faith; until such writings are found, Hall says, the question stays open. He adds that it would be inexcusable to ignore Islam's cultural legacy, since when the crescent triumphed over the cross in southern Europe it brought a civilization unequalled in its day, and introduces a quotation from Stanley Lane-Poole.",
  "modern.hall.43.p11": "Hall quotes Stanley Lane-Poole: for nearly eight centuries Muslim Spain set Europe an example of an enlightened state; art, literature and science flourished as nowhere else, students came from France, Germany and England, its doctors led medicine, women were encouraged to study, and mathematics, astronomy, botany, history, philosophy and law could be learned there alone. He quotes the Library of Original Sources that Islam's achievements have been underrated: within a century of Mohammed's death it took Asia Minor, Africa and Spain from Christianity and built the highest civilization of the Dark Ages, raised the position of women in the East though it kept polygamy, and encouraged progress until the Turks took control. The same work names Gerber or Djafer, who founded chemistry in the ninth century; Ben Musa, who introduced algebra; Alhaze, who studied optics and found the magnifying power of convex lenses; Avicenna, whose medical encyclopedia was the standard of his age; and the Qabbalist Avicebron. It adds that Muslim scholars laid the foundations of chemistry, advanced mathematics and optics, rounded Africa before Vasco da Gama and passed on gunpowder, but Europe was not ready to use their discoveries; the Christian dark ages were the Muslim world's bright ones, and in philosophy the Arabs worked back from Neoplatonism to Aristotle.",
  "modern.hall.43.p12": "Hall closes with a speculation about the phoenix, said to be reborn every six hundred years. Six hundred years before Christ, he says, the phoenix of wisdom, perhaps Pythagoras, died on the altar of humanity; it was reborn in Nazareth and died on the tree rooted in Adam's skull; around A.D. 600 came Ahmed, Mohammed, who suffered from the poison of Kheibar; from those ashes it rose over Mongolia, where Genghis Khan set up the rule of wisdom in the twelfth century, and it now lies buried in a glass coffin under a pyramid marked with the figures of the Mysteries. Six hundred years after Genghis Khan, Hall asks, did Napoleon, who thought himself the man of destiny, meet this legend of the periodic rebirth of wisdom and believe the world's hope had taken flesh in him? The eagle on his standard might have been the phoenix, which would explain why he thought himself destined to found Christ's kingdom on earth and perhaps why he was so friendly to Muslims. Hall poses all of this as questions, not as established history.",
  "modern.hall.43.fig1": "The caption, for a figure from D'Ohsson's Tableau Général de l'Empire Othoman, describes Mohammed's night journey. The seventeenth sura says only that one night he was carried from the temple at Mecca to that of Jerusalem; a later collection, the Mishkat al-Masabih, has him describe rising through the seven heavens to the presence of God and returning to his bed in a single night. Gabriel woke him, removed his heart, washed the cavity with Zamzam water and filled the heart with faith and knowledge. He was carried by Alborak, 'the lightning,' a white creature the size of a mule with a woman's head and a peacock's tail. In some versions he rode Alborak only to Jerusalem, then climbed a golden ladder let down from heaven on Mount Moriah and, with Gabriel, rose through the seven spheres. At each gate stood a patriarch: Adam at the first, John and Jesus at the second, then Joseph, Enoch, Aaron, Moses and Abraham. Another order puts Jesus at the seventh gate, where Mohammed is said to have asked him to intercede for him before God's throne.",
  "modern.hall.43.fig2": "The caption, for a section of D'Ohsson's panorama of Mecca, describes the Caaba, the cube-shaped building in the great mosque court and Islam's holiest place, which Muslims face five times a day; they once faced east, Hall says, until a later decree turned them toward Mecca. Little is known of it before it became a mosque except that it was a pagan temple; Mohammed destroyed 360 idols there. It is an irregular cube about 38 feet long, 35 high and 30 wide. In its southeast corner, about five feet up, is the black stone of Abraham, said to have been dazzling white when Gabriel gave it but blackened by human sin; broken in the seventh century, it is held together by silver. Tradition says the Caaba was first built in heaven 2,000 years before creation, that Adam built the earthly copy from five sacred mountains, and that Abraham and Ishmael rebuilt it after the Flood. Hall thinks the site may once have held a stone ring like Stonehenge. The present building is no older than the seventeenth century, and when the Carmathians returned the stone they took in 930 it is uncertain it was the original. Hall describes the graves of Hagar and Ishmael, the yearly black and gold covering, the colonnade of 360 pillars, the nineteen gates, which he links to the Metonic cycle and Stonehenge, and the seven circuits that may imitate the motion of the heavens.",
  "modern.hall.44.p1": "Hall calls the North American Indian a natural symbolist, mystic and philosopher, in close touch with the forces of nature. He describes beliefs in spirits, or Manitos, who come down among people; clouds as the smoke of the gods' pipes; tribes said to live under lakes or to roam only at night; and Bat people with wings who sleep hanging upside down. Like the early Scandinavians, Hall says, they saw the earth as a middle level between the heaven of the Great Spirit above and a dark underworld below, and like the Chaldeans they divided the space between earth and heaven into layers. The underworld corresponded, for initiates, to the Lesser Mysteries, and creatures at home in two elements were messengers between the levels. The land of the dead lay far off, sometimes across a river, as in Egyptian, Greek and Christian belief. The number four was sacred because the Great Spirit made the world in a square frame, which Hall compares with the Pythagorean tetrad. He argues that stories of heroes entering the land of the dead while alive prove Mystery cults existed among them, since initiation was a rehearsal of death in which the initiate learned to leave and re-enter the body at will; this, he says, is the basis of tales of the Shadow Land.",
  "modern.hall.44.p2": "Hall quotes Hartley Burr Alexander that across the continent the sacred pipe is the Indian's altar and its smoke the offering to heaven, and a description of the pipe ceremony: the leader blows one puff to the sky to thank the Great Spirit for his life, one to the ground to thank Mother Earth for food, and one to the sun for its light. The red stone for pipes had to come from the one quarry where, by legend, the Great Spirit made the first pipe and smoked it to the four corners of the world; tribes travelled thousands of miles to it, and peace was decreed there. Hall says the Indian does not worship the sun but treats it as a symbol of the Great Spirit. He takes serpent symbols as further evidence of the Mysteries in America: the flying serpent as the Atlantean sign of the initiate, the seven-headed snake as seven Atlantean islands, perhaps the cities of Cibola, and seven ancient schools, and the Serpent Mound in Adams County, Ohio, as a serpent releasing the Egg of Existence. Many tribes, he says, believe in reincarnation and name children after their supposed former lives, with a story of a baby that cried until given the right name; he says some aged Eskimos took their own lives to be reborn among loved ones.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- 'the cities of Chibola?' is Hall's own uncertain guess, marked with a question mark in the text; it is usually taken to mean the legendary Seven Cities of Cibola.",
  "modern.hall.44.p3": "Hall says the American Indians distinguish a dead person's ghost from the soul itself, which he calls knowledge confined to initiates. Like the Platonists they held that patterns of all earthly forms exist in a higher sphere, and that group souls watch over each animal species. Their belief in guardian spirits, he says, would have pleased Paracelsus; a guardian protecting a whole clan or tribe is a totem, and in some tribes young men fast and pray in the forest until their guardian appears, and that creature becomes their protector. He then turns to Hiawatha, whose name Lewis Spence renders 'he who seeks the wampum-belt.' Following Schoolcraft, Longfellow confused the historical Iroquois chief Hiawatha with Manabozho, a mythical hero of the Algonquins and Ojibwas. The real Hiawatha, after many setbacks, united five Iroquois nations in the League of the Five Nations, aiming to replace war with arbitration, centuries before Woodrow Wilson's League of Nations. The aim was not fully reached, but the 'Silver Chain' gave the Iroquois unmatched unity. Shamans opposed him and, in one legend, sent a bird that tore his daughter to pieces. After he sailed into the sunset in a self-moving canoe his people made him a demigod, and behind Longfellow's poem Hall sees Hiawatha the initiate.",
  "modern.hall.44.s1.p1": "Hall says no other sacred book sets out the initiation rites of a mystical school as fully as the Popol Vuh, the book of the Quiché Maya, and that it alone shows the philosophical excellence of the native peoples of America. He quotes James Morgan Pryse: these people hold the One God to be wholly impersonal and the forces flowing from it to be personal, the reverse of the Western idea; they honour the Plumed Serpent, the sun's messenger, called Quetzalcoatl in Mexico, Gucumatz among the Quiché, and Amaru in Peru, from which Pryse derives the name America as 'Land of the Plumed Serpent.' Pryse says the priests of this god of peace once ruled both Americas from the Cordilleras, and that the author of the Popol Vuh belonged to their order. The book was found by Father Ximinez in the seventeenth century and published in French by Brasseur de Bourbourg in 1861; Hall bases his account on Kenneth Sylvan Guthrie's English translation in The Word, the only complete one, and notes Pryse's unfinished translation. The second book deals mainly with the initiation rites of the Quiché, which Hall says prove the existence of ancient Mystery schools in America.",
  "modern.hall.44.s1.p2": "Hall lists translations of the title Popol Vuh: Lewis Spence prefers 'The Collection of Written Leaves' over 'The Book of the Mat' or 'The Record of the Community'; Guthrie gives 'The Senate Book' or 'Book of the Holy Assembly'; Brasseur de Bourbourg 'The Sacred Book'; Ximinez 'The National Book'; and Pryse 'The Book of the Azure Veil.' The book itself names its older sources 'The Tale of Human Existence in the Land of Shadows, and How Man Saw Light and Life.' Hall then says the scanty native records show that the later civilizations of Central and South America were dominated by priestly black magic. He describes sorcerers who trapped spirits in magnetized mirrors, neophytes in black robes led down the 'left-hand path' into the underworld to make stones speak and ensnare minds with chants and fetishes, and initiation into the greater Mysteries requiring a human sacrifice before a stone idol. He suggests these bloody rites were remnants of a late Atlantean corruption of the ancient sun Mysteries, claiming that in that era black magic took over the esoteric schools, brought about Atlantis's downfall, and even reached the Aryan world. These are Hall's own claims, not established history.",
  "modern.hall.44.s2.p1": "Hall retells the Popol Vuh. The princes of Xibalba sent four owl messengers to summon the brothers Hunhun-ahpu and Vukub-hunhun-ahpu to the place of initiation in the Guatemalan mountains. They failed the tests and, by the old custom, paid with their lives. They were buried together, but Hunhun-ahpu's head was put in the branches of a calabash tree on the road to Xibalba; the tree at once bore fruit and the head became one of them. Xquiq, the virgin daughter of prince Cuchumaquiq, went alone to see the tree and reached for its fruit; spittle from the head fell into her hand and the head told her it was his posterity, and that it would now fall silent, being only a dead man's head. When her father found she was pregnant she said she had known no man, but he, urged by the princes, demanded her heart in an urn. Her executioners spared her and put in its place the fruit of a tree (rubber) with blood-red sap, and the princes, burning it on the altar, were amazed by its perfume, not knowing it was a fragrant plant.",
  "modern.hall.44.s2.p2": "Hall continues. Xquiq bore twins, Hunahpu and Xbalanque, dedicated to avenging their father and uncle. They excelled at a ball game Hall likens to hockey, and the princes of Xibalba, disturbed by the noise 'over their heads,' summoned them to be destroyed in the seven days of the Mysteries. Before leaving, each planted a cane in their grandmother's house, telling her that while it lived they lived. With their blowpipes they travelled through ravines and past boiling springs, and as a first step crossed a river of mud and a river of blood, using the blowpipes as bridges. At a crossing of four roads, black, white, red and green, they knew their first test would be to tell the princes from wooden figures dressed like them and to name each prince without being told. Hunahpu pulled a hair from his leg and turned it into an insect called Xan, which flew down the black road into the council room and stung each figure in turn; the first two were manikins, but the real princes cried out and called each other by name as they discussed the bites. With all twelve names learned, the twins boldly presented themselves at Xibalba.",
  "modern.hall.44.s2.p3": "Hall continues. Told to bow to the king, the twins laughed, knowing it was a manikin, and greeted the twelve princes by name: Hun-came, Vukub-came, Xiquiripat, Cuchumaquiq, Ahalpuh, Ahalcana, Chamiabak, Chamiaholona, Quiqxic, Patan, Quiqre and Quiqrixqaq. They refused to sit on a heated stone bench that would have burned them, and were sent to spend the night in the House of Shadows; this ended the first degree. The second trial: in the House of Shadows each was given a pine torch and a cigar to keep burning all night yet return unconsumed in the morning. They burned macaw feathers that looked like pine splinters and put fireflies on the cigar tips, and returned everything whole, to the princes' awe. The third ordeal, in the House of Spears, meant fending off the best spearmen hour after hour and producing four vases of rare flowers without leaving the temple. The twins calmed the spearmen and got ants to carry flowers from the temple gardens, so that by morning the vases were full, and the princes recognized blossoms stolen from their own gardens and plotted the next ordeal.",
  "modern.hall.44.s2.p4": "Hall continues. The fourth test was a night in the House of Cold, 'the abode of the frozen winds of the North,' which the princes thought no one could survive; the twins built fires of pine cones, the spirit of cold left, and they came out alive. The fifth was a night in a chamber full of fierce tigers (jaguars); the twins threw them bones, and the princes, hearing the chewing, thought the youths had been devoured, but at dawn they walked out unharmed and the princes asked, 'Of what race are those?' The sixth was a night in the House of Fire, a furnace so hot that no one could survive more than a few moments, yet at sunrise the twins came out unscorched. Afraid that all the secrets of Xibalba would fall into their hands, the princes prepared a seventh ordeal more terrible than any before, sure the youths could not survive it.",
  "modern.hall.44.s2.p5": "Hall continues. The seventh ordeal was the House of Bats, a dark underground maze full of huge bats and ruled by Camazotz, the bat god, with a man's body and a bat's head and wings, who flew through the dark beheading wanderers with his sword. Xbalanque got through, but Hunahpu was caught off guard and beheaded. He was brought back to life by magic, and the twins, to complete their revenge, let themselves be burned on a pyre; their ground bones, thrown into a river, became two man-fishes. Disguised as old wanderers they danced and worked marvels, cutting each other to pieces and reviving with a word, and burning and restoring houses. The princes summoned them, and after they killed and revived the princes' dog and burned and rebuilt the palace, the king of Xibalba asked to be killed and brought back too. The twins killed the princes and did not revive them, avenging their father and uncle, and afterwards rose to heaven and became lights in the sky.",
  "modern.hall.44.s3.p1": "Hall quotes Le Plongeon, who asks whether these initiations do not recall the visions of Enoch: the house of crystal both burning hot and icy cold, the bow and arrows and sword of fire, the stream and the river of fire to be crossed, the ends of the earth full of beasts and birds, the glorious one seated on the sun, and the tree in the middle of the earth said to be the Tree of Knowledge, which he likens to the calabash tree where Hunhun-ahpu's head was placed. Le Plongeon says the Xibalban ordeals seem an exact counterpart, in harsher form, of the Eleusinian Mysteries and the greater Mysteries of Egypt, and that the feats required of candidates recall those credited to the Indian Mahatmas and passages in the book of Daniel, who was initiated into the Chaldean Mysteries. Hall then turns to Guthrie's introduction, which matches the twins' trials to the zodiac signs: Aries the river of mud, Taurus the river of blood, Gemini the two dummy kings, Cancer the House of Darkness, Leo the House of Spears, Virgo the House of Cold, Libra the House of Tigers, Scorpio the House of Fire, Sagittarius the House of Bats, Capricorn the burning, Aquarius the ashes scattered in the river, and Pisces the man-fishes.",
  "modern.hall.44.s3.p2": "Hall suggests the river of blood fits Aries and the river of mud Taurus better, and that the old legend may have had them the other way round. He reports Guthrie's identification of Xibalba with Atlantis, the twelve princes as its rulers and their destruction as the fall of Atlantis, but says that to initiates Atlantis is simply a symbol of the mystery of origins. Pryse, concerned with mystical anatomy, reads the rubber ball as the pineal gland and the twins as the double current of energy along the spine, and takes Xibalba to be the shadowy etheric sphere inside the planet; he never translated the initiation section itself. Hall notes that the fourth book of the Popol Vuh ends with the building of a white temple holding a secret black cubic divining stone. Gucumatz, or Quetzalcoatl, resembles King Solomon, and the temple story recalls Solomon's Temple. Brasseur de Bourbourg was first drawn to religious parallels in the Popol Vuh because this temple with its black stone was called the Caabaha, strikingly like the Caaba with its black stone in Islam.",
  "modern.hall.44.s3.p3": "Hall says the twins' adventures take place before humans were created, so they are spiritual mysteries. Xibalba is the lower universe of Chaldean and Pythagorean thought, its princes the twelve rulers of that world, and the two manikins the two false signs he says were added to the zodiac to hide the astronomical Mysteries from outsiders. The twins' descent across rivers on their blowpipes suggests the spirit's descent into the body through subtle channels, and the blowpipe also stands for the spinal cord and the power in its central canal. They play the 'Game of Life' with the gods of death and win only with help from the 'Sages'; the trials are the soul's wanderings through the realms below the zodiac, and their final victory is the rise of illumined consciousness from the lower nature after it has been purified by spiritual fire. Hall finds signs that the Quiché knew the secret of regeneration in a carved head thought to be Quetzalcoatl, with a sunburst and a serpent of spinal fire on the crown like a Hindu chakra, and in a Central American monolith he says shows two elephants with riders, which he takes as proof of contact with Asia. He ends with the magic mantles the gods wore when appearing to priests: allegory, like the Popol Vuh, is the mantle hiding the secret teaching.",
  "modern.hall.44.fig1": "The caption, for a drawing by the Navajo singer Hasteen Klah, explains that Navajo sand paintings are made by sprinkling coloured powders on smooth sand; this one, ringed by the rainbow goddess, shows an episode from the Navajo creation story. Klah told Hall that the Navajo make no idols but keep only a mental image of their gods, and that, as the gods draw pictures on the moving clouds, the priests draw on sand and wipe the picture away when its work is done. He said the Zuni, Hopi and Navajo shared one origin, emerging from the earth and then separating; the Navajo came up about 3,000 years ago at La Plata Mountain in Colorado. Below the earth were four ranges, white in the east, blue in the south, yellow in the west and black in the north, whose rising and falling made day, dawn, twilight and night. There were seven major gods; one, Bakochiddy, child of the sun ray and the daylight, rose to heaven, and his life in some ways parallels Christ's. A fish god's flood drove the peoples to the surface. This painting belongs to a healing series: the patient is placed on it in a consecrated hogan. The central swastika stands for the four corners of the world, the two hunchbacked gods carry clouds on their backs, and male gods have round heads, female gods square ones.",
  "modern.hall.44.fig2": "The caption describes a fragment of pottery found four feet underground, beneath a heap of broken early pottery near the Casa Grande ruins in Arizona. Hall says it matters because of its striking resemblance to the Masonic compass and square, and adds that Native baskets, pottery and blankets often carry designs of Masonic and philosophical interest. The resemblance is Hall's observation, not an established link.",
  "modern.hall.44.fig3": "The caption describes a birch-bark scroll of the Midewiwin, the Grand Medicine Society of the Ojibwa, one of an initiate's most sacred possessions. Hall quotes Garrick Mallery likening such charts to a Masonic tracing board, which can be shown publicly without revealing secrets yet helps members remember the ceremonies, and W. J. Hoffman's account in the Seventh Annual Report of the Bureau of Ethnology: the society has four degrees, advancement depends on being 'shot at with the medicine sacks' by the priests, and the priests keep incised bark charts of the degrees each owner holds, never shown to the public. On this scroll the two rectangles are two degrees of the lodge, the central line the 'straight and narrow way,' the side lines temptations, and the faces at their ends powerful spirits, manidos. Schoolcraft says the society teaches the higher doctrines of spiritual existence. In legend Manabozho, the Great Rabbit, servant of the Good Spirit, built the first lodge, initiated an otter by shooting the sacred migis shell into its body, and gave it immortality and the society's secrets. Initiation, preceded by sweat baths, centres on overcoming evil spirits and includes healing and, Hall infers from one plate, control of forces in the body's vital centres. He notes that though the cross is important in the rites, Mide priests refused to convert to Christianity.",
  "modern.hall.45.p1": "Hall asks whether the divine knowledge of the pagan priesthoods survived the destruction of their temples, or lies buried in their ruins. He cites Origen that Egyptian philosophers held a sublime secret knowledge of God's nature, and asks what Julian meant by the secret initiations of the Seven-Rayed God who raised souls to salvation, and who were the blessed theurgists who understood depths Julian dared not speak of. If this inner teaching was always hidden from the masses, who were given a simpler code, then, Hall argues, the leaders of modern philosophy, ethics, religion and science may not understand the real meaning of the ideas their beliefs rest on, and the arts and sciences inherited from older nations may hide a mystery only the most enlightened can grasp. Citing Albert Pike's gathering of ancient praise for the Mysteries, he concludes that ancient initiates held the right answers to the problems that still face the twentieth century. He does not mean the ancients foresaw every modern problem, but that the Mysteries trained the mind in the basic truths of life so it could meet any situation; where reason rules, inconsistency cannot exist, and wisdom raises man toward godhood, which is why the Mysteries were said to turn 'roaring beasts into divinities.'\n\nOther possible readings:\n- Origen's quotation opens but is never closed in the e-text; it most likely ends after 'the nature of God,' with the questions about Julian being Hall's own.",
  "modern.hall.45.p2": "Hall says a philosophy is proved by what it produces, and the Mysteries produced minds, visions and lives so great that their teachings still set the world's spiritual, intellectual and moral standards. Their initiates form a 'golden chain' of men and women joining heaven and earth, like the Homeric chain with which Zeus boasted he could bind the universe to Olympus: founders of sciences and philosophies, patrons of the arts, on whose power the world religions rest. But those who stand for ideals the crowd cannot grasp are persecuted, and the lot of the Initiate-Teacher is almost always unhappy. Hall lists Pythagoras, whom he says was crucified and his school burned; Hypatia, torn from her chariot; Jacques de Molay, burned; Savonarola, burned in Florence; Galileo, forced to recant; Giordano Bruno, burned by the Inquisition; Roger Bacon, hiding his knowledge in cipher; Dante, dying in exile; Francis Bacon, bearing persecution; and Cagliostro, the most vilified man of modern times. The world, he says, praises its fools and slanders its thinkers; the Comte de St.-Germain was an exception who outlived his inquisitors, yet even he was called an impostor. From this list Hall chooses three to discuss: Hypatia, the greatest woman philosopher; Cagliostro, the most maligned man of the Christian era; and St.-Germain, the most brilliant and successful modern exponent of the Ancient Wisdom.",
  "modern.hall.45.s1": "Hall says Hypatia succeeded her father, the mathematician Theon, in the chair of philosophy and for many years led the Neoplatonic school of Alexandria; she was famed for learning and charm, loved by the citizens and consulted by the magistrates, and he calls her the greatest of the pagan martyrs. A pupil of Plutarch, she outshone every Christian teacher in northern Egypt in argument and public esteem. Her writings are lost; Hall blames the burning of the Alexandrian library by the Muslims, a claim modern historians reject. She wrote commentaries on Diophantus's Arithmetic, Ptolemy's astronomical tables and Apollonius's Conics, and her friend Synesius, bishop of Ptolemais, asked her help in building an astrolabe and a hydroscope. Hall says she explained Christian doctrines so clearly that many new converts became her pupils, showed their pagan origin, and explained claimed miracles by natural law. He blames Cyril, bishop of Alexandria, whom he calls the founder of the doctrine of the Trinity, for at least indirectly causing her death and doing nothing to prevent it, perhaps believing her a sorceress; he also criticizes Charles Kingsley's novel Hypatia. Monks from the Nitrian desert, led by Peter the Reader, dragged her from her chariot, took her to the Caesareum church, killed her and burned her remains. She died in 415, and with her, Hall says, fell the Alexandrian school; he suggests her memory lives on in St. Catherine of Alexandria.",
  "modern.hall.45.s2.p1": "Hall says the 'divine' Cagliostro passed like a meteor across France, from idol of Paris to prisoner of the Inquisition. In memoirs written in the Bastille, Cagliostro said he was born in Malta of a noble but unknown family and raised in Arabia by Altotas, a master of philosophy, science and the transcendental arts; Hall says biographers mock this but cannot otherwise explain his great store of hidden knowledge. He calls him the most slandered man in modern history and quotes W. H. K. Trowbridge: mystery and magic made Cagliostro an easy target, his character has hung on the gibbet of infamy for over a century, and he is remembered less for what he did than for what was done to him. Popular belief identifies him with Giuseppe Balsamo, a Sicilian of humble birth, born in 1743, with criminal tendencies, who disappeared after a series of escapades; Hall says Trowbridge proves they were different men, removing the worst charge against him. After six months in the Bastille, Cagliostro was cleared at trial of any part in the theft of the 'Queen's Necklace,' and Hall says he had in fact warned Cardinal de Rohan; yet an artist painted him holding the necklace. The trial, Hall says, has been called the prologue to the French Revolution, and Henry R. Evans has also defended him. Whether Cagliostro was Balsamo remains disputed.",
  "modern.hall.45.s2.p2": "Hall says honest investigators trace the stories against Cagliostro to the Inquisition, which needed to justify persecuting him; the original charge was simply that he tried to found a Masonic lodge in Rome, and everything else came later. The Pope commuted his death sentence to life imprisonment, which Hall reads as a sign of respect even from enemies. He doubts that Cagliostro really died years later in the dungeon of San Leo, and repeats rumours that he escaped, one saying he went to India. Cagliostro founded an Egyptian Rite that admitted women, as the ancient Mysteries had; the Princesse de Lamballe became its Mistress of Honour at an initiation attended by the leading members of the French court. The Paris Masonic lodges then asked him, through the orientalist Court de Gébelin, to help clear up questions of Masonic philosophy. Hall says that at the meeting on 10 May 1785 Cagliostro quickly showed himself far beyond Gébelin, declared the Rose-Cross to be the true ancient symbol of the Mysteries, discussed the symbolism of letters, and with an arrangement of the alphabet foretold the Revolution, the fall of the monarchy, the fates of the royal family, and the rise of Napoleon and the First Empire, to show what higher knowledge can do. These are Hall's claims, not established history.",
  "modern.hall.45.s2.p3": "Hall says that in the Bastille Cagliostro wrote a cryptic message on his cell wall which, decoded, foretold that on 14 July 1789 the Bastille would be besieged and pulled down from top to bottom. He calls Cagliostro the secret agent of the Knights Templar and a Rosicrucian initiate, whose learning is shown by his Egyptian Rite. His friends believed he had lived forever and been a guest at the wedding at Cana; his enemies called him the Devil incarnate; Alexandre Dumas described his powers of prophecy in The Queen's Necklace. The world he tried to serve rejected him and has persecuted even his memory; unable to finish the great work at hand, Hall says, he stepped aside for his more successful fellow, the Comte de St.-Germain. These are Hall's claims, not established history.",
  "modern.hall.45.s3.p1": "Hall calls the Comte de St.-Germain, who appeared in European diplomatic circles in the early eighteenth century, the most baffling person in history, whose true identity puzzled his contemporaries as much as later researchers. He describes him as the leading scholar and linguist of his time, skilled in chemistry, history, poetry and music, a composer of a short opera and a painter whose glowing colours, perhaps made with powdered mother-of-pearl, could reproduce the lustre of jewels. He is said to have spoken German, English, Italian, Portuguese, Spanish, French, Greek, Latin, Sanskrit, Arabic and Chinese so well that he passed as a native everywhere, and to have written the same text with both hands at once so that the two sheets matched letter for letter. He seemed to know the events of the previous two thousand years and described past centuries as if he had taken part. Hall says he helped Mesmer develop mesmerism and probably discovered it; that he removed flaws from diamonds for Louis XV in 1757; that he was consulted as an art critic; and that Madame de Pompadour said he gave a court lady an elixir that kept her young and beautiful for twenty-five years beyond the normal span. These are reported claims, many from later or doubtful sources.",
  "modern.hall.45.s3.p2": "Hall says St.-Germain's prophecies brought him fame: he warned Marie Antoinette of the monarchy's fall years before the Revolution. Above all he was a skilful diplomat and served several governments, including France, as a secret agent, with credentials that opened every door. Hall quotes Isabel Cooper-Oakley's list of the names he used between 1710 and 1822: Marquis de Montferrat, Comte Bellamarre or Aymar at Venice, Chevalier Schoening at Pisa, Chevalier Weldon at Milan and Leipzig, Comte Soltikoff at Genoa and Leghorn, Graf Tzarogy at Schwalbach and Triesdorf, Prinz Ragoczy at Dresden, and Comte de St.-Germain at Paris, The Hague, London and St. Petersburg, names Hall links to secret-service work. He was of medium height, well built, dark, usually dressed simply in black but with a passion for diamonds, on his rings, watch, snuffbox and shoe buckles, which a jeweller valued at 200,000 francs. He appeared middle-aged and unwrinkled, ate no meat, drank no wine and seldom ate in company. A few nobles called him a charlatan, but Louis XV rebuked a courtier for mocking him. Hall says he could guess questions before they were asked, sensed when he was needed far away, and was said to enter and leave rooms without using the door.",
  "modern.hall.45.s3.p3": "Hall says St.-Germain travelled widely: in Russia under Peter III and at the Persian Shah's court between 1737 and 1742. He quotes Una Birch: from Persia to France and Calcutta to Rome he was known and respected; Horace Walpole met him in London in 1745, Clive knew him in India in 1756, Madame d'Adhémar claimed to meet him in Paris in 1789, five years after his supposed death, and others claimed to speak with him in the early nineteenth century. Frederick the Great, Voltaire, Madame de Pompadour, Rousseau, Chatham and Walpole all wondered about his origin, and no one learned why he appeared as a Jacobite agent in London, a conspirator in St. Petersburg, an alchemist and art connoisseur in Paris, or a Russian general at Naples; glimpses show him playing the violin at Versailles, gossiping with Walpole, sitting in Frederick's library or leading illuminist meetings in caves by the Rhine. Hall says St.-Germain was important in early Freemasonry and that efforts to deny it, such as A. E. Waite's in The Secret Tradition in Freemasonry, which he says pictures the wrong St.-Germain, a French general, have ulterior motives. He claims Cagliostro's memoirs say St.-Germain initiated him into the Templars, that he was a master of Masonic lore, and that he was probably connected with the Rosicrucians, perhaps as their head.",
  "modern.hall.45.s3.p4": "Hall says St.-Germain knew Eastern esoteric teaching, practised meditation and was seen sitting cross-legged like a Buddha; he had a retreat in the Himalayas and once said he would stay in India eighty-five years before returning to Europe. He admitted obeying a higher power, which Hall identifies as the Mystery school that sent him on his mission, and Hall calls him and Francis Bacon the two greatest emissaries of the Secret Brotherhood in the last thousand years. He reports the view of the Theosophical writer E. Francis Udny that St.-Germain was not the son of Prince Rákóczy of Transylvania but the prince himself, and that he passed through a 'philosophic death' as Francis Bacon in 1626, as Rákóczy in 1735 and as St.-Germain in 1784, and was also the Comte de Gabalis and Count Hompesch, last Grand Master of the Knights of Malta. Hall says members of secret societies often faked their deaths, and repeats the story that Marshal Ney escaped the firing squad and taught school in North Carolina for over thirty years as Peter Stuart Ney, confessing his identity on his deathbed. He quotes Andrew Lang asking whether St.-Germain died in Hesse around 1780-85 or escaped, whether Lord Lytton knew him around 1860, or whether he advises the Dalai Lama: he is a will-o'-the-wisp of the memoir-writers. These are speculations, not established facts.",
  "modern.hall.45.s4.p1": "Hall asks whether Francis Bacon's New Atlantis was a prophetic vision of the civilization about to rise in America, and says there is no doubt that Europe's secret societies worked to found there a new nation 'conceived in liberty' and dedicated to equality. Two episodes in early United States history, he says, show the hand of the silent body that guides nations: it creates nations to carry ideals, and they survive while they stay true and vanish, like Atlantis, when they stop 'knowing the gods.' The first is told in Robert Allen Campbell's pamphlet Our Flag, about the design of the colonial flag of 1775 and a mysterious old man, known to both Washington and Franklin, referred to only as 'the Professor.' Campbell describes him as far past seventy, speaking of events a century earlier as if he had seen them, yet upright, vigorous and clear-minded; tall, dignified, courteous and commanding; and very particular in his diet, eating no meat, fowl or fish, nothing green, raw or unripe, and drinking no alcohol, but living on grains, sun-ripened fruit, nuts, mild tea and sweets. Hall's sole source is Campbell's account, which historians do not accept as fact.",
  "modern.hall.45.s4.p2": "Hall continues Campbell's account. The Professor was well educated and studious, spending much time on rare old books and manuscripts which he seemed to be deciphering or translating; he showed them to no one and locked them, with his own writings, in a heavy iron-bound oak chest whenever he left his room. He took long solitary walks, spent his ample money moderately, was genial and at home on any topic, and no one dared ask where he came from or where he was going. Hall says that, by more than coincidence, the committee the Continental Congress appointed to design a flag stayed in Cambridge with the same family, and Washington joined them there. By signs exchanged between them, both Washington and Franklin evidently recognized the Professor, who was invited onto the committee, treated with deep respect, and had all his suggestions adopted, including the pattern he proposed for the flag, which the other six members accepted at once. After the flag was settled, the Professor quietly vanished and nothing more is known of him. The whole story rests on Campbell's account and is not supported by other evidence.",
  "modern.hall.45.s4.p3": "Hall asks whether Washington and Franklin recognized the Professor as an emissary of the Mystery school he believes has long guided the planet's political destiny. Franklin, he says, was a philosopher and Freemason, possibly a Rosicrucian, and he and Lafayette, another man of mystery, were key links in the chain that made the thirteen colonies independent; Franklin's wisdom shows in Poor Richard's Almanac, published as Richard Saunders, and his interest in Masonry in his reprint of Anderson's Constitutions. The second episode Hall dates to the evening of 4 July 1776 in the State House in Philadelphia, where men debating independence feared for their lives. A stranger, tall and pale, whom no one had seen enter, suddenly spoke with fierce and holy zeal, ending, 'God has given America to be free!' As he sank exhausted into a chair, enthusiasm broke out and the Declaration was signed, but the stranger had vanished and was never identified. Hall says similar episodes are recorded at the founding of every new nation, and asks whether they are coincidences or signs that the divine wisdom of the ancient Mysteries still serves humanity. The episode is a legend, not documented history.",
  "modern.hall.45.fig1": "The caption, for a plate from Vaenius's Theatro Moral de la Vida Humana, explains the Tablet of Cebes, a dialogue attributed to Cebes, a disciple of Socrates, around 390 B.C.; legend says it was based on a picture of all human life set up in a temple of Kronos. The world is a great mountain: crowds of people come out of the earth at its foot and climb in search of truth and immortality, and above the clouds at the top is true happiness. Hall numbers the figures: the gate in the wall of life, the Genius, deceit, opinions, desires and pleasures, fortune, the strong, lust, greed and flattery, sorrow, sadness, misery, grief, rage or despair, the house of misfortune, penitence, true and false opinion, false doctrine, poets, orators and geometers, incontinence, the road of true doctrine, continence and patience, true doctrine, truth and persuasion, science and the virtues, happiness, the highest pleasure of the wise, and the lazy and the strays.\n\nOther possible readings:\n- The caption names the speakers as 'Cebes and Gerundio'; Gerundio is not a figure of the Greek dialogue and is probably a name from the Spanish edition Hall used.",
  "modern.hall.45.fig2": "The caption, for Houdon's bust of Cagliostro, describes him as not tall but broad-shouldered and deep-chested, with a large head of black hair combed back from a broad forehead and brilliant black eyes whose pupils widened when he spoke with feeling, while he shook his head like a lion. His small hands and feet, Hall says, showed noble birth, and he was dignified, studious and tirelessly energetic. He dressed rather fantastically, gave so freely from an apparently endless purse that he was called 'Father of the Poor,' took nothing from anyone, and lived in splendour in a combined temple and palace in the Rue de la Sourdière. By his own account he was initiated by the Comte de St.-Germain, had travelled the whole world, and in the ruins of Babylon and Nineveh had found wise men who knew all the secrets of human life.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p1": "Hall opens his conclusion with Philip of Macedon, who wanted the greatest philosopher to teach his fourteen-year-old son Alexander and wrote to Aristotle that he thanked the gods less for his son's birth than for his being born in Aristotle's time. Aristotle came to Macedon and tutored Alexander for eight years; Alexander said his father had given him being, but Aristotle had given him well-being. Hall says Aristotle taught Alexander the principles of the Ancient Wisdom, bringing him to the threshold of the world of the sages, the one world fate and his own soul would not let him conquer. Aristotle edited and annotated the Iliad for him, and Alexander carried it on all his campaigns, putting it in a jewelled casket taken from Darius as the only case worthy of it. When Aristotle published one of his prized teachings, Alexander wrote reproachfully that knowledge once given only by word of mouth should not be made public, since he would rather excel others in the higher learning than in power and dominion. Aristotle calmly replied that although the discourse had been published, no one who had not heard him give it, that is, who lacked spiritual understanding, could grasp its real meaning.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p2": "Hall says Alexander soon died and his empire crumbled with him; Aristotle died a year later but outdid him in death as in life, for his thought lived on, and Aristotle, 'the master of those who know' as Dante called him, became the real conqueror of the world Alexander tried to take by the sword. This shows, Hall says, that to capture a man you must win his reason, not just enslave his body, and to free him you must free his mind from his own ignorance; physical conquest breeds hatred and must fail, but all men obey an intellect they recognize as better than their own. He claims all must admit that the philosophical culture of ancient Greece, Egypt and India surpassed the modern world's, and that Greek beauty, thought and ethics have never been equalled. The true philosopher belongs to the noblest order of men, and a nation is remembered for its thinkers. In the Pythagorean school at Crotona, anyone who left or was expelled had a gravestone set up for him, because to abandon the life of thought for the illusions of the senses was to be dead to reality; the Pythagoreans held that life enslaved to the senses was spiritual death, and death to the senses spiritual life.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p3": "Hall says philosophy gives life by showing the dignity and purpose of living, while materialism brings death by numbing the soul's response to creative thought and virtue. He contrasts this with the twentieth century: man, a noble being capable of endless growth, turns from his birthright of understanding and plunges into material illusion, spending his short life trying to make himself permanent in a world of impermanent things. Gradually he forgets he is a spiritual being, fixes his half-awakened faculties on the beehive of industry as the only reality, and sinks to the level of a beast, struggling in a hellish turmoil of industry, politics and commerce to grasp the phantoms of success and power. Ignorant of why he lives, what life is for and what lies beyond death, though he holds the answers within himself, he sacrifices the beautiful, true and good to worldly ambition. The garden of philosophy fades and in its place rises an empire of stone, steel, smoke and hate, where millions struggle to survive while keeping running a vast juggernaut rolling toward an unknown end. In this empire everything turns to stone: dazzled by gain, man gazes at the Medusa face of greed and is petrified.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p4": "Hall says that in this commercial age science only classifies physical knowledge and the passing side of nature, and its practical discoveries bind man more tightly to physical limits. Religion has become materialistic, measured by buildings, property and balance sheets. Even philosophy, once a ladder between heaven and earth that the enlightened climbed into the presence of reality, has become a dull jumble of conflicting opinions, made 'practical' to serve the world of stone and steel. He mocks a new 'School of the Worldly Wise Men' who make themselves judges of all knowledge and declare mystics epileptic, saints neurotic, God a primitive superstition, the universe purposeless, immortality imaginary and genius a chance arrangement of cells, calling Pythagoras the victim of a 'bean complex,' Socrates a drunkard, Paul subject to fits, Paracelsus a quack, Cagliostro a mountebank and St.-Germain a crook. Hall says people everywhere, worn down by soulless systems, cry out for the return of an age of beauty and enlightenment, for what is practical in the highest sense. Some see that civilization in its present form is ending, that coldness and commercialism are impractical and only what allows love and ideals to be expressed is worth while. Everyone seeks happiness, which crowns the soul's search for understanding, and something in the human mind reaches out to philosophy in its broadest sense.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p5": "Hall says the great philosophical schools of the past must rise again, because only they can part the veil between the world of causes and the world of effects, and only the Mysteries can show humanity the greater universe that is its true home. Modern philosophy has failed by treating thinking as merely intellectual; materialistic thought is as hopeless as commercialism. The power to think truly saves humanity, and every Redeemer, mythical or historical, personified it. How rational a person is measures how far they have evolved. The purpose of ancient philosophy, he says, was to speed up the growth of the rational nature instead of waiting for nature's slow pace; this unfolding of the god within was the 'philosophic life,' the key to the Great Work and the Philosopher's Stone. Ancient philosophy was first a way of living and only second an intellectual method; you come to know what you live, and a great philosopher is one whose physical, mental and spiritual life is wholly ruled by reason. Body, feelings and mind affect each other, and only a mind in a harmonious, refined body can think rationally, so right action, feeling and thinking come before right knowing. As physical life stresses physical things and monastic life ecstasy, total philosophical absorption carries the thinker into the highest sphere, the pure world of reason.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p6": "Hall says that in a civilization absorbed in extreme worldly activity, the philosopher is a balancing mind able to judge and guide its growth. Establishing the philosophic rhythm in a person takes fifteen to twenty years, and the ancient disciples underwent strict discipline, gradually turning every activity toward reason. The ancients had another factor modern thinkers cannot grasp: initiation into the philosophic Mysteries, through which those who proved fit received an inheritance of secret knowledge handed down through the generations, to which each added his own share. Hall calls philosophy the world's one hope, since all modern sorrows come from lacking a proper philosophy of life; no person, race or nation can be truly great without one and a policy consistent with it. In the World War, he says, civilization not only killed but destroyed the records of human thought by which life can be wisely guided; he quotes the saying attributed to Mohammed that the ink of scholars is more precious than the blood of martyrs. Priceless documents and the knowledge of ages were destroyed with hardly a regret, all for the wish to control a tiny spot of the cosmos for a moment, though every conqueror must soon leave what he seized as a fresh cause of strife.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p7": "Hall says war, the proof of irrationality, will smoulder until selfishness is overcome, and civilization will go on fighting itself with ever more destructive inventions; a fear is dawning that it will destroy itself in one great catastrophe. Then the eternal drama of rebuilding must begin again: from the ruins of a civilization that died when its ideals died, some people not yet born must build a new world, and the philosophers have wanted the best of the past built into it, since by divine law each new order rests on everything achieved before. What is superficial may perish, but what is fundamental must be kept at any cost. He then sets out the Platonists' two kinds of ignorance. Simple ignorance is mere lack of knowledge, shared by everything below the First Cause, which alone knows perfectly; it is a constant spur to learning, since the mind is always surrounded by things it does not yet understand, and wisdom grows from facing the unknown rationally. Only the Ultimate Cause is truly wise, that is, only God is good; Socrates held knowledge, virtue and usefulness to be one with the good, as states of knowing, being and doing. Since only the Whole is complete, every part is to some degree ignorant, knowing itself but not the Self in other parts; evolution moves from many toward one, until the separate minds are reunited in the consciousness of the Whole, and only then is all-knowing real.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p8": "Hall says all creatures are relatively ignorant and relatively wise, nothing and everything by comparison; the microscope shows man his importance, the telescope his smallness. Over the ages his consciousness keeps widening to take in more of the world, and he is beginning to see that he cannot be truly happy until he is perfect, and that reason is the most important faculty for perfecting himself; only the illumined mind can lead the soul through the maze of diversity into the light of unity. The second, more dangerous kind of ignorance is twofold or complex ignorance: ignorance of one's own ignorance. Primitive people worshipped sun, moon and stars and tried to appease unknown gods in a world of wonders they did not understand. Now cities stand where they roamed, and wonder has given way to sophistication: man worships his own achievements and ignores the vastness of time and space. The twentieth century, Hall says, makes a fetish of civilization and forgets how small, temporary and ignorant humanity really is. People laugh at Ptolemy for putting the earth at the centre of the universe, yet modern civilization acts as if the earth were the most important body in the heavens and the gods were fascinated by events on this ant-hill in chaos.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p9": "Hall says men toil endlessly to build cities and rule them, as if a gold crown or ten million subjects could raise a man above the worth of his own thoughts. The earth carries some two billion people who live and die unaware of the immense existence beyond it; measured against infinite time and space, captains of industry are nothing, and a plutocrat who ruled the whole earth would be a petty despot on a grain of cosmic dust. Philosophy shows man his kinship with the All, a brother to the suns, raising him from a taxpayer on a spinning atom to a citizen of the cosmos; though his body belongs to the earth, a diviner Self within him is one with the harmony of the Whole. Ignorance of ignorance is the smug state of one who knows nothing beyond his senses and declares there is nothing more to know; whoever knows only physical life is merely ignorant, but whoever makes it the supreme reality is ignorant of his ignorance. If the Infinite had not wanted man to be wise, virtuous and aware of the vast universe, Hall says, he would not have given him the faculties for these. Philosophy calls everyone to a fellowship of spirit and thought, out of selfishness, ignorance, worldliness, ambition, greed, hate and dead idealism.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p10": "Hall describes the world of philosophy as a land of peace where the best in each soul can be expressed; there even blades of grass are wonders and every stick and stone tells the secret of its being, all life becomes beautiful and real, and the purpose of existence and the wisdom and goodness in the Whole become clear even to an imperfect mind. There the human heart finds companionship that draws out the good hidden in it like metal in a deep vein. The seeker who follows the path of the wise reaches the summit of wisdom's mountain and sees life spread out below, the cities tiny and the horizon lost in the haze of the unknown; he learns that wisdom lies in breadth of vision. As his thoughts rise, streets are lost in cities, cities in nations, nations in continents, the earth in space and space in eternity, until only the Self and the goodness of God remain. Man can live two lives: one from womb to tomb, measured by time, the unheeding life; the other from realization to infinity, beginning with understanding and lasting forever, the philosophic life. Philosophers, once they realize their immortality, are neither born nor die, and on the foundation of the Self they build a civilization that will outlast the sun, moon and stars. The fool lives for today; the philosopher lives forever.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p11": "Hall says that once the rational consciousness rolls away the stone and leaves its tomb, it does not die again; this second, philosophic birth cannot be undone. He does not mean physical immortality, but that the philosopher learns his body is no more his true Self than the earth is his true world, and by knowing that the form will perish while the life goes on, he gains conscious immortality. This is what Socrates meant when he said Anytus and Meletus could kill him but not harm him. To the wise, bodily life is only the outer room of the hall of life, through which the enlightened pass into a fuller existence. The ignorant live in a world bounded by time and space, but to those who grasp the meaning of Being these are illusions of the senses, limits man's ignorance puts on the duration of God, and the philosopher rejoices in that endless duration as the time appointed for all achievement. Man is not as insignificant as he looks: his invisible nature is as vast as his understanding, his mind reaches the stars and his spirit mingles with the life of the cosmos. As understanding grows he takes more of the universe into himself; the unknown is simply what is still to be included. Philosophy develops appreciation and unfolds the powers by which man can master the secrets of the seven spheres.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.p12": "Hall says the ancient initiates called their disciples from physical pursuits into the life of mind and spirit, and the Mysteries have always stood at the threshold between the real and its shadow, with gates always ajar. The world of philosophy is not in any direction but everywhere, and in every person a gate leads from the not-self to the Self: for the mystic it is the heart, through purified emotion; for the philosopher it is reason, the illumined mind bridging body and spirit, so that godhood is born in the one who sees. Today, he says, people mock God and goodness and have forgotten the path beyond the stars; the ancient schools have fallen and their white-robed sages have withdrawn. Yet the sun still shines, babies still face life's mysteries, people still love and dream of a nobler world, and the gates from mortality to immortality are still ajar. Virtue, love and ideals still renew humanity, God still guides creation, and the soul's wings are only folded under its flesh; philosophy can break the vessel of clay and free the soul to rise to its source. The materialist order has failed and, like Frankenstein's monster, is destroying its maker; religion wanders in speculation and science beats against the unknown. Only transcendental philosophy knows the way, teaching man to be born, live and die well and be born again, and Hall ends by inviting the reader, 'YOU,' to join the band of those who choose knowledge, virtue and usefulness.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.fig1": "The caption, for an engraving by Jean Duvet, says Duvet of Langres, born in 1485 and probably dead some time after 1561, when his Apocalypse illustrations were printed as a book, was the oldest and greatest French Renaissance engraver. Little is known of him except that he was goldsmith to the King of France. His engravings for the Book of Revelation, made after he was seventy, were his masterpiece; Hall points to an article by William M. Ivins Jr. in The Arts, May 1926. He says the face of John is a portrait of Duvet himself, and that this plate, like many of Duvet's, is rich in philosophical symbolism.",
  "modern.hall.conclusion.fig2": "The caption, for a figure from Khunrath's Amphitheatrum Sapientiae, gives the substance of Khunrath's own description of the image, the way to everlasting life. It is the gate of the amphitheatre of the one true eternal Wisdom, narrow but august and consecrated to Jehovah. It is reached by seven steps, the philosophic steps of the doctrine of the faithful sons. Beyond them the path follows the way of God the Father, either directly by inspiration or by other means. Under the seven oracular laws shining at the gate, those divinely inspired may enter and study, with the eyes of body and mind, in a Christian-Kabbalistic, divine-magical and physical-chemical way, the wisdom, goodness and power of the Creator, so that they die not as sophists but live as theosophists, and as true philosophers explain the works of the Lord and praise God. Hall calls it one of the most remarkable pictures of the Wise Man's House and the way into it.",
};
